window.AccountingATAR=[{"id":"acf-business","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"What accounting tells a business","subtitle":"Identify users and decisions · Separate performance, position and cash · Compare business activities","scope":"Identify users and decisions · Separate performance, position and cash · Compare business activities","outcomes":["Identify users and decisions","Separate performance, position and cash","Compare business activities"],"paragraphs":["Accounting turns evidence of transactions into information for decisions. An owner needs to know whether prices cover costs; a lender needs evidence of repayment capacity. A useful report answers a defined question, rather than merely listing transactions.","A manufacturing business transforms inputs into products, a retailer resells goods and a service business supplies expertise or labour. The activity affects its cost records. A repairer selling parts may combine retail and service activities.","Profit measures income less expenses over a period. Financial position lists assets, liabilities and equity at a date. Cash is a resource, not a synonym for profit: collecting a previous credit sale increases cash without creating new revenue."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A repairer earns $8,000 service revenue, incurs $5,000 expenses, and collects only $6,000 of the revenue this month. Calculate profit and explain the $2,000 outstanding.","steps":["Profit = 8,000 − 5,000 = $3,000.","The $2,000 uncollected revenue is an account receivable, an asset.","Cash receipts of $6,000 do not replace the revenue figure when measuring earned profit."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":false,"rows":9,"id":"acf-business-q1"},{"prompt":"A retailer sells goods for $12,000 with cost of sales $7,000 and other expenses $3,000. Find profit and identify a useful lender decision.","steps":["Gross profit = 12,000 − 7,000 = $5,000.","Profit = 5,000 − 3,000 = $2,000.","A lender assesses cash forecasts and debt repayment capacity; profit alone does not prove cash is available."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-business-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every cash receipt is sales revenue.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Collecting the receivable swaps one asset for another.","Revenue was earned when the original sale occurred; collection reduces the amount owed by the customer.","Example evidence: Profit = 8,000 − 5,000 = $3,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-business-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does collecting an old credit sale create new revenue?","o":["Collecting the receivable swaps one asset for another.","Every cash receipt is sales revenue."],"a":0,"why":"Revenue was earned when the original sale occurred; collection reduces the amount owed by the customer."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Revenue was earned when the original sale occurred; collection reduces the amount owed by the customer.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Collecting the receivable swaps one asset for another."},{"q":"In the model “Three different accounting questions”, what belongs to “Performance”?","o":["Income less expenses over a period","Resources and claims at a date","Money available to pay debts"],"a":0,"why":"Performance: Income less expenses over a period. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Three different accounting questions”, what belongs to “Position”?","o":["Income less expenses over a period","Resources and claims at a date","Money available to pay debts"],"a":1,"why":"Position: Resources and claims at a date. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Three different accounting questions","rows":[["Performance","Income less expenses over a period"],["Position","Resources and claims at a date"],["Cash","Money available to pay debts"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does collecting an old credit sale create new revenue?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can identify users and decisions.</li><li>I can separate performance, position and cash.</li><li>I can compare business activities.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify users and decisions","html":"<p>Accounting turns evidence of transactions into information for decisions. An owner needs to know whether prices cover costs; a lender needs evidence of repayment capacity. A useful report answers a defined question, rather than merely listing transactions.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate performance, position and cash","html":"<p>A manufacturing business transforms inputs into products, a retailer resells goods and a service business supplies expertise or labour. The activity affects its cost records. A repairer selling parts may combine retail and service activities.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare business activities","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Profit</strong> measures income less expenses over a period. Financial position lists <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> at a date. Cash is a resource, not a synonym for <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>: collecting a previous <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sale increases cash without creating new revenue.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain what accounting tells a business using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-ownership","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Sole traders, partnerships and companies","subtitle":"Compare ownership and liability · Distinguish legal and accounting entities · Recommend a business structure","scope":"Compare ownership and liability · Distinguish legal and accounting entities · Recommend a business structure","outcomes":["Compare ownership and liability","Distinguish legal and accounting entities","Recommend a business structure"],"paragraphs":["A sole trader has one owner and generally unlimited personal liability. A partnership combines owners, skills and capital, but general partners also face unlimited liability. An agreement should clarify contributions, profit sharing and responsibilities.","A small proprietary company is a separate legal entity with shareholders. Shareholders normally risk the unpaid amount on their shares; directors may have separate personal obligations. Formation and reporting requirements are greater than for a sole trader.","The accounting entity convention separates business records from owners under every structure. A sole trader is an accounting entity even though not a separate legal person. Recommend a structure by linking liability, funding, continuity and control to the case."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Two designers want shared skills and low establishment costs, but one fears personal exposure to debts. Compare a partnership and proprietary company.","steps":["A partnership combines skills and capital with relatively simple setup.","General partners have unlimited personal liability for partnership debts.","A company is a separate legal person and shareholders normally have limited liability.","A company may suit the risk concern but brings greater compliance and establishment costs."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-ownership-q1"},{"prompt":"A sole trader introduces $20,000 into the business and later pays a personal holiday from business cash. Explain the entity convention and both entries without GST.","steps":["Capital introduced: debit Bank $20,000; credit Capital $20,000.","The holiday is drawings, not a business expense.","Debit Drawings and credit Bank for the holiday amount.","Separate records preserve the business profit measure."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-ownership-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Only companies can have separate accounting records.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Business records are separated from the owner under the entity convention.","Legal separation and accounting separation are different ideas.","Example evidence: A partnership combines skills and capital with relatively simple setup."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-ownership-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is a sole trader a separate accounting entity?","o":["Business records are separated from the owner under the entity convention.","Only companies can have separate accounting records."],"a":0,"why":"Legal separation and accounting separation are different ideas."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Legal separation and accounting separation are different ideas.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Business records are separated from the owner under the entity convention."},{"q":"In the model “Structure comparison”, what belongs to “Sole trader”?","o":["One owner; unlimited liability","Shared ownership; general partners unlimited liability","Separate legal entity; shareholders normally limited liability"],"a":0,"why":"Sole trader: One owner; unlimited liability. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Structure comparison”, what belongs to “Partnership”?","o":["One owner; unlimited liability","Shared ownership; general partners unlimited liability","Separate legal entity; shareholders normally limited liability"],"a":1,"why":"Partnership: Shared ownership; general partners unlimited liability. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Structure comparison","rows":[["Sole trader","One owner; unlimited liability"],["Partnership","Shared ownership; general partners unlimited liability"],["Proprietary company","Separate legal entity; shareholders normally limited liability"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is a sole trader a separate accounting entity?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare ownership and liability.</li><li>I can distinguish legal and accounting entities.</li><li>I can recommend a business structure.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare ownership and liability","html":"<p>A sole trader has one owner and generally unlimited personal liability. A partnership combines owners, skills and capital, but general partners also face unlimited liability. An agreement should clarify contributions, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> sharing and responsibilities.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish legal and accounting entities","html":"<p>A small proprietary company is a separate legal entity with shareholders. Shareholders normally risk the unpaid amount on their shares; directors may have separate personal obligations. Formation and reporting requirements are greater than for a sole trader.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recommend a business structure","html":"<p>The accounting entity convention separates business records from owners under every structure. A sole trader is an accounting entity even though not a separate legal person. Recommend a structure by linking liability, funding, continuity and control to the case.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain sole traders, partnerships and companies using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-finance","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Funding a small business","subtitle":"Compare equity and debt finance · Match funding to purpose · Explain lender approval factors","scope":"Compare equity and debt finance · Match funding to purpose · Explain lender approval factors","outcomes":["Compare equity and debt finance","Match funding to purpose","Explain lender approval factors"],"paragraphs":["Owners can contribute equity without a contractual interest payment, but their capital is limited. Borrowing increases liabilities and creates repayment commitments. An overdraft is flexible for short cash shortages; a term loan can fund a longer-lived asset.","Trade credit delays payment to suppliers, conserving cash temporarily. Leasing gives access to an asset in exchange for payments. Compare total cost, security, control and duration rather than choosing the option with the smallest first payment.","A lender examines capacity to repay, cash forecasts, past financial information, security and guarantees. Strong profit is useful evidence, but a business can be profitable and unable to meet a repayment on its due date. Interest is the return for lending and compensates for risk and time."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A café needs $30,000 for equipment used for five years and $4,000 for a two-week cash shortfall. Recommend finance for each.","steps":["A term loan or lease matches the equipment duration.","An overdraft may suit the temporary cash shortfall.","Compare interest, fees and security for each offer.","Check forecast repayments rather than relying only on profit."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-finance-q1"},{"prompt":"A $20,000 loan has simple annual interest of 8%. Find one year of interest and give two pieces of evidence a lender should request.","steps":["Interest = 20,000 × 0.08 = $1,600.","A cash forecast shows repayment capacity and timing.","Financial statements and information about collateral/guarantees help assess risk."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-finance-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “The lowest initial payment always proves the best financing choice.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Finance duration and repayment pattern should fit the asset and cash forecast.","Fees, interest, risk, security and future payments must also be compared.","Example evidence: A term loan or lease matches the equipment duration."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-finance-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should all long-term assets be financed by an overdraft?","o":["Finance duration and repayment pattern should fit the asset and cash forecast.","The lowest initial payment always proves the best financing choice."],"a":0,"why":"Fees, interest, risk, security and future payments must also be compared."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Fees, interest, risk, security and future payments must also be compared.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Finance duration and repayment pattern should fit the asset and cash forecast."},{"q":"In the model “Funding evidence”, what belongs to “Need”?","o":["Asset life and amount required","Interest, fees, security and repayments","Cash forecast and financial history"],"a":0,"why":"Need: Asset life and amount required. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Funding evidence”, what belongs to “Terms”?","o":["Asset life and amount required","Interest, fees, security and repayments","Cash forecast and financial history"],"a":1,"why":"Terms: Interest, fees, security and repayments. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Funding evidence","rows":[["Need","Asset life and amount required"],["Terms","Interest, fees, security and repayments"],["Capacity","Cash forecast and financial history"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should all long-term assets be financed by an overdraft?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare equity and debt finance.</li><li>I can match funding to purpose.</li><li>I can explain lender approval factors.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare equity and debt finance","html":"<p>Owners can contribute <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> without a contractual interest payment, but their capital is limited. Borrowing increases <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> and creates repayment commitments. An overdraft is flexible for short cash shortages; a term loan can fund a longer-lived asset.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Match funding to purpose","html":"<p>Trade <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> delays payment to suppliers, conserving cash temporarily. Leasing gives access to an asset in exchange for payments. Compare total cost, security, control and duration rather than choosing the option with the smallest first payment.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain lender approval factors","html":"<p>A lender examines capacity to repay, cash forecasts, past financial information, security and guarantees. Strong <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> is useful evidence, but a business can be profitable and unable to meet a repayment on its due date. Interest is the return for lending and compensates for risk and time.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain funding a small business using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-equation","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"The accounting equation","subtitle":"Classify financial elements · Calculate equity · Track changes without losing balance","scope":"Classify financial elements · Calculate equity · Track changes without losing balance","outcomes":["Classify financial elements","Calculate equity","Track changes without losing balance"],"paragraphs":["Assets are resources controlled by the business; liabilities are obligations; equity is the owners’ residual claim. The accounting equation is assets = liabilities + equity. Equity is not the same as cash, because the business may hold equipment or inventory and owe creditors.","A transaction changes at least two elements or two accounts within one element. Buying equipment for cash changes the asset mix without changing total assets. Borrowing cash increases both assets and liabilities.","Income increases equity and expenses reduce it, apart from owner transactions. Capital contributions increase equity without being income; drawings reduce equity without being expenses. Analyse the event before deciding which side of an account it affects."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A business has cash $9,000, inventory $11,000 and equipment $30,000. It owes suppliers $8,000 and a loan $12,000. Calculate equity, then show the effect of borrowing another $5,000.","steps":["Assets = 9,000 + 11,000 + 30,000 = $50,000.","Liabilities = 8,000 + 12,000 = $20,000.","Equity = 50,000 − 20,000 = $30,000.","After borrowing: assets $55,000 = liabilities $25,000 + equity $30,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-equation-q1"},{"prompt":"Cash $6,000, receivables $4,000 and equipment $15,000 are financed by liabilities $9,000. Find equity and the effect of buying $2,000 equipment for cash.","steps":["Assets total $25,000.","Equity = 25,000 − 9,000 = $16,000.","Cash falls $2,000 and equipment rises $2,000; total assets stay $25,000.","Liabilities and equity do not change."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-equation-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every payment reduces profit and equity.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["An exchange of cash for equipment changes the asset mix.","An asset purchase is not an immediate expense merely because cash was paid.","Example evidence: Assets = 9,000 + 11,000 + 30,000 = $50,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-equation-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does buying equipment for cash necessarily reduce equity?","o":["An exchange of cash for equipment changes the asset mix.","Every payment reduces profit and equity."],"a":0,"why":"An asset purchase is not an immediate expense merely because cash was paid."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["An asset purchase is not an immediate expense merely because cash was paid.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"An exchange of cash for equipment changes the asset mix."},{"q":"In the model “Equation check”, what belongs to “Before”?","o":["50,000 = 20,000 + 30,000","Assets +5,000; liabilities +5,000","55,000 = 25,000 + 30,000"],"a":0,"why":"Before: 50,000 = 20,000 + 30,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Equation check”, what belongs to “Borrow 5,000”?","o":["50,000 = 20,000 + 30,000","Assets +5,000; liabilities +5,000","55,000 = 25,000 + 30,000"],"a":1,"why":"Borrow 5,000: Assets +5,000; liabilities +5,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Equation check","rows":[["Before","50,000 = 20,000 + 30,000"],["Borrow 5,000","Assets +5,000; liabilities +5,000"],["After","55,000 = 25,000 + 30,000"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does buying equipment for cash necessarily reduce equity?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can classify financial elements.</li><li>I can calculate equity.</li><li>I can track changes without losing balance.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Classify financial elements","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Assets</strong> are resources controlled by the business; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> are obligations; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> is the owners’ residual claim. The accounting equation is <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> = <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> + <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong>. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Equity</strong> is not the same as cash, because the business may hold equipment or <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> and owe creditors.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate equity","html":"<p>A transaction changes at least two elements or two accounts within one element. Buying equipment for cash changes the asset mix without changing total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>. Borrowing cash increases both <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Track changes without losing balance","html":"<p>Income increases <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> and expenses reduce it, apart from owner transactions. Capital contributions increase <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> without being income; drawings reduce <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> without being expenses. Analyse the event before deciding which side of an account it affects.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain the accounting equation using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-debits","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Debits and credits","subtitle":"Apply account increase rules · Distinguish account sides from value · Balance a compound entry","scope":"Apply account increase rules · Distinguish account sides from value · Balance a compound entry","outcomes":["Apply account increase rules","Distinguish account sides from value","Balance a compound entry"],"paragraphs":["Debit means the left side and credit means the right side of an account. Neither word means good, bad, increase or decrease in every context. Assets, expenses and drawings normally increase by debit; liabilities, equity and income normally increase by credit.","An increase in Bank is a debit because Bank is an asset. An increase in a loan is a credit because a loan is a liability. Decreases are posted on the opposite side of each account’s increase rule.","Every journal entry has equal total debits and credits, including entries with more than two accounts. Equality is a control check, not proof that the chosen accounts or amounts reflect the transaction correctly."],"questions":[{"prompt":"The owner contributes $10,000 cash and $4,000 equipment. Prepare the journal entry and explain why capital is credited.","steps":["Debit Bank $10,000.","Debit Equipment $4,000.","Credit Capital $14,000.","Capital increases equity; total debits and credits both equal $14,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-debits-q1"},{"prompt":"The business pays $900 wages from Bank, excluding GST. Record the entry and identify the account types.","steps":["Debit Wages expense $900 because an expense increases.","Credit Bank $900 because an asset decreases.","Debits equal credits at $900.","The expense reduces profit and therefore equity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-debits-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Debit means increase for every account.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["A debit increases an asset but decreases a liability.","Account type determines whether a debit is an increase or a decrease.","Example evidence: Debit Bank $10,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-debits-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a debit always increase an account?","o":["A debit increases an asset but decreases a liability.","Debit means increase for every account."],"a":0,"why":"Account type determines whether a debit is an increase or a decrease."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Account type determines whether a debit is an increase or a decrease.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"A debit increases an asset but decreases a liability."},{"q":"In the model “Normal increase side”, what belongs to “Assets, expenses, drawings”?","o":["Debit (left)","Credit (right)","Total debits = total credits"],"a":0,"why":"Assets, expenses, drawings: Debit (left). Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Normal increase side”, what belongs to “Liabilities, capital, income”?","o":["Debit (left)","Credit (right)","Total debits = total credits"],"a":1,"why":"Liabilities, capital, income: Credit (right). Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Normal increase side","rows":[["Assets, expenses, drawings","Debit (left)"],["Liabilities, capital, income","Credit (right)"],["Every entry","Total debits = total credits"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a debit always increase an account?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply account increase rules.</li><li>I can distinguish account sides from value.</li><li>I can balance a compound entry.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply account increase rules","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Debit</strong> means the left side and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> means the right side of an account. Neither word means good, bad, increase or decrease in every context. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Assets</strong>, expenses and drawings normally increase by <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong>; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> and income normally increase by <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish account sides from value","html":"<p>An increase in Bank is a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> because Bank is an asset. An increase in a loan is a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> because a loan is a liability. Decreases are posted on the opposite side of each account’s increase rule.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Balance a compound entry","html":"<p>Every journal entry has equal total debits and credits, including entries with more than two accounts. Equality is a control check, not proof that the chosen accounts or amounts reflect the transaction correctly.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain debits and credits using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cycle","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Documents, journals and the accounting cycle","subtitle":"Trace source evidence · Distinguish journal and ledger roles · Explain the reporting sequence","scope":"Trace source evidence · Distinguish journal and ledger roles · Explain the reporting sequence","outcomes":["Trace source evidence","Distinguish journal and ledger roles","Explain the reporting sequence"],"paragraphs":["Invoices, receipts, credit notes and bank records provide evidence. Identify the date, parties, purpose, gross amount and any GST before recording. A quotation is not proof that a transaction has occurred; a bank receipt may settle a previous transaction.","The journal records transactions chronologically with debit and credit lines and a narration. Posting transfers each line to the relevant ledger account. The ledger gathers the history of an individual account; a running balance or T-account shows its resulting balance.","A trial balance lists ledger balances. Reports then communicate performance and position, and closing entries reset temporary accounts for the next period. Keep the original evidence so another person can trace a reported number back through the records."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Explain how a supplier invoice for equipment travels from evidence to the financial position report.","steps":["Check the invoice date, asset, amount and GST status.","Record the equipment purchase in the journal.","Post equipment and payable/GST lines to ledgers.","Use ledger balances in the trial balance and classified financial statements."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cycle-q1"},{"prompt":"A learner records a supplier quotation as a purchase and treats a credit note as a new sale. Explain both errors.","steps":["A quotation alone does not prove the purchase occurred.","A supplier credit note normally reduces a previous purchase/payable.","Use actual transaction evidence and explain the correction.","Preserve an audit trail rather than deleting evidence."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cycle-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “The ledger is merely a second chronological copy of the journal.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["The journal is chronological; the ledger groups entries by account.","Posting allows each account balance to be calculated and traced.","Example evidence: Check the invoice date, asset, amount and GST status."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cycle-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is a journal grouped by account?","o":["The journal is chronological; the ledger groups entries by account.","The ledger is merely a second chronological copy of the journal."],"a":0,"why":"Posting allows each account balance to be calculated and traced."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Posting allows each account balance to be calculated and traced.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"The journal is chronological; the ledger groups entries by account."},{"q":"In the model “Audit trail”, what belongs to “Source document”?","o":["Evidence and terms","Chronological double entry","Account histories","Balances and communication"],"a":0,"why":"Source document: Evidence and terms. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Audit trail”, what belongs to “Journal”?","o":["Evidence and terms","Chronological double entry","Account histories","Balances and communication"],"a":1,"why":"Journal: Chronological double entry. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Audit trail","rows":[["Source document","Evidence and terms"],["Journal","Chronological double entry"],["Ledger","Account histories"],["Trial balance and reports","Balances and communication"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is a journal grouped by account?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can trace source evidence.</li><li>I can distinguish journal and ledger roles.</li><li>I can explain the reporting sequence.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Trace source evidence","html":"<p>Invoices, receipts, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> notes and bank records provide evidence. Identify the date, parties, purpose, gross amount and any <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> before recording. A quotation is not proof that a transaction has occurred; a bank receipt may settle a previous transaction.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish journal and ledger roles","html":"<p>The journal records transactions chronologically with <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> lines and a narration. Posting transfers each line to the relevant ledger account. The ledger gathers the history of an individual account; a running balance or T-account shows its resulting balance.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the reporting sequence","html":"<p>A trial balance lists ledger balances. Reports then communicate performance and position, and closing entries reset temporary accounts for the next period. Keep the original evidence so another person can trace a reported number back through the records.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain documents, journals and the accounting cycle using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-gst","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"GST categories and inclusive prices","subtitle":"Distinguish taxable, GST-free and input-taxed supplies · Calculate inclusive and exclusive GST · Separate tax from income","scope":"Distinguish taxable, GST-free and input-taxed supplies · Calculate inclusive and exclusive GST · Separate tax from income","outcomes":["Distinguish taxable, GST-free and input-taxed supplies","Calculate inclusive and exclusive GST","Separate tax from income"],"paragraphs":["For a taxable supply at 10% GST, a GST-exclusive price of $100 gives GST $10 and an invoice total $110. When a total already includes GST, the tax component is total ÷ 11, not 10% of the total. Check the wording before calculating.","A GST-registered seller generally records the tax collected as GST payable. Eligible GST on business purchases is GST receivable/input tax credit. Taxable sales attract GST; GST-free sales generally allow related input credits; input-taxed sales generally do not.","GST is administered under the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999. Most ordinary businesses must register when the relevant GST turnover threshold reaches $75,000, subject to rules and exceptions. Turnover is not profit. Use ATO guidance for actual registration decisions."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A GST-registered shop sells taxable goods for $660 including GST. Calculate revenue and GST payable.","steps":["GST = 660 ÷ 11 = $60.","GST-exclusive revenue = 660 − 60 = $600.","Debit Bank/Accounts receivable $660.","Credit Sales $600 and GST payable $60."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-gst-q1"},{"prompt":"An eligible taxable business purchase is $880 including GST. Calculate its net cost and GST receivable. Compare GST-free and input-taxed sales.","steps":["GST receivable = 880 ÷ 11 = $80.","Net asset/expense cost = $800.","GST-free sales do not charge GST but generally allow related input credits.","Input-taxed sales generally do not charge GST or allow related input credits."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-gst-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “GST is 10% of the GST-inclusive invoice total.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["The GST component of a 10% GST-inclusive total is total ÷ 11.","The inclusive total contains eleven equal parts: ten price parts and one GST part.","Example evidence: GST = 660 ÷ 11 = $60."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-gst-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is GST in an inclusive price calculated by multiplying the total by 10%?","o":["The GST component of a 10% GST-inclusive total is total ÷ 11.","GST is 10% of the GST-inclusive invoice total."],"a":0,"why":"The inclusive total contains eleven equal parts: ten price parts and one GST part."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The inclusive total contains eleven equal parts: ten price parts and one GST part.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"The GST component of a 10% GST-inclusive total is total ÷ 11."},{"q":"In the model “Inclusive-price model”, what belongs to “Net value”?","o":["10 parts: $600","1 part: $60","11 parts: $660"],"a":0,"why":"Net value: 10 parts: $600. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Inclusive-price model”, what belongs to “GST”?","o":["10 parts: $600","1 part: $60","11 parts: $660"],"a":1,"why":"GST: 1 part: $60. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Inclusive-price model","rows":[["Net value","10 parts: $600"],["GST","1 part: $60"],["Invoice","11 parts: $660"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is GST in an inclusive price calculated by multiplying the total by 10%?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can distinguish taxable, GST-free and input-taxed supplies.</li><li>I can calculate inclusive and exclusive GST.</li><li>I can separate tax from income.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish taxable, GST-free and input-taxed supplies","html":"<p>For a taxable supply at 10% <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>, a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>-exclusive price of $100 gives <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> $10 and an invoice total $110. When a total already includes <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>, the tax component is total ÷ 11, not 10% of the total. Check the wording before calculating.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate inclusive and exclusive GST","html":"<p>A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>-registered seller generally records the tax collected as <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> payable. Eligible <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> on business purchases is <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> receivable/input tax <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong>. Taxable sales attract <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>-free sales generally allow related input credits; input-taxed sales generally do not.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate tax from income","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> is administered under the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999. Most ordinary businesses must register when the relevant <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> turnover threshold reaches $75,000, subject to rules and exceptions. Turnover is not <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>. Use ATO guidance for actual registration decisions.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain gst categories and inclusive prices using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-gst-report","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"GST accounts and BAS reporting","subtitle":"Reconcile GST payable and receivable · Calculate a net settlement · Explain ABN and BAS roles","scope":"Reconcile GST payable and receivable · Calculate a net settlement · Explain ABN and BAS roles","outcomes":["Reconcile GST payable and receivable","Calculate a net settlement","Explain ABN and BAS roles"],"paragraphs":["GST payable gathers tax on taxable sales; GST receivable gathers eligible input credits. These tax accounts are separate from sales, inventory and expenses. An ABN identifies the business; GST registration and an ABN are related but not the same status.","A Business Activity Statement reports relevant tax amounts to the ATO. For a simplified task with GST only, net GST to pay is GST payable less GST receivable. If credits exceed output tax, the net amount may be a refund.","Reconcile source documents and tax accounts before reporting. A BAS may also include other obligations such as PAYG; do not treat every BAS amount as GST. These classroom examples assume all stated purchase credits are eligible and use the reporting basis supplied."],"questions":[{"prompt":"GST payable is $2,400 and GST receivable is $1,700. Calculate the net GST liability and record settlement using separate accounts.","steps":["Net liability = 2,400 − 1,700 = $700.","Debit GST payable $2,400.","Credit GST receivable $1,700.","Credit Bank $700; the GST accounts are cleared."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-gst-report-q1"},{"prompt":"GST payable $900 and receivable $1,200 are reconciled. Record a $300 refund received.","steps":["Credits exceed output tax by $300.","Debit GST payable $900 and Bank $300.","Credit GST receivable $1,200.","The refund clears tax balances; it is not new sales revenue."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-gst-report-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “A GST refund must increase Sales revenue.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Settlement clears tax balances rather than creating sales revenue.","The credit arose from eligible purchase tax, not a new supply to a customer.","Example evidence: Net liability = 2,400 − 1,700 = $700."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-gst-report-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is a GST refund ordinary sales revenue?","o":["Settlement clears tax balances rather than creating sales revenue.","A GST refund must increase Sales revenue."],"a":0,"why":"The credit arose from eligible purchase tax, not a new supply to a customer."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The credit arose from eligible purchase tax, not a new supply to a customer.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Settlement clears tax balances rather than creating sales revenue."},{"q":"In the model “Net settlement”, what belongs to “Output GST”?","o":["Payable from taxable sales","Eligible purchase credit","Payable less receivable"],"a":0,"why":"Output GST: Payable from taxable sales. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Net settlement”, what belongs to “Input GST”?","o":["Payable from taxable sales","Eligible purchase credit","Payable less receivable"],"a":1,"why":"Input GST: Eligible purchase credit. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Net settlement","rows":[["Output GST","Payable from taxable sales"],["Input GST","Eligible purchase credit"],["Net amount","Payable less receivable"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is a GST refund ordinary sales revenue?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can reconcile GST payable and receivable.</li><li>I can calculate a net settlement.</li><li>I can explain ABN and BAS roles.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile GST payable and receivable","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> payable gathers tax on taxable sales; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> receivable gathers eligible input credits. These tax accounts are separate from sales, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> and expenses. An ABN identifies the business; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> registration and an ABN are related but not the same status.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate a net settlement","html":"<p>A Business Activity Statement reports relevant tax amounts to the ATO. For a simplified task with <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> only, net <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> to pay is <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> payable less <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> receivable. If credits exceed output tax, the net amount may be a refund.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain ABN and BAS roles","html":"<p>Reconcile source documents and tax accounts before reporting. A BAS may also include other obligations such as PAYG; do not treat every BAS amount as <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>. These classroom examples assume all stated purchase credits are eligible and use the reporting basis supplied.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain gst accounts and bas reporting using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-transactions","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Capital, cash purchases and cash expenses","subtitle":"Record commencement entries · Distinguish assets from expenses · Include eligible GST correctly","scope":"Record commencement entries · Distinguish assets from expenses · Include eligible GST correctly","outcomes":["Record commencement entries","Distinguish assets from expenses","Include eligible GST correctly"],"paragraphs":["Commencement entries recognise resources introduced by the owner and the corresponding capital. A cash purchase of equipment creates an asset. An expense is recognised for a resource consumed, such as ordinary advertising; classify the purpose before applying the debit rule.","In Unit 1, taxable purchases include eligible GST receivable. The bank credit is the gross cash paid; the asset or expense debit is the net amount. Wages do not have GST merely because the business is registered.","Narrations describe the transaction and evidence. Write dates and account names clearly; a compound entry has multiple debits or credits but still equal totals. Owner funds are capital rather than revenue because they come from the owner acting as owner."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A registered business buys equipment for $3,300 cash including eligible GST and pays wages $500. Journalise both.","steps":["Equipment: debit Equipment $3,000.","Debit GST receivable $300; credit Bank $3,300.","Wages: debit Wages expense $500.","Credit Bank $500; no GST is added to wages."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-transactions-q1"},{"prompt":"The owner invests cash $8,000. The registered business then pays taxable advertising $220 including eligible GST. Record both entries.","steps":["Debit Bank $8,000; credit Capital $8,000.","Debit Advertising expense $200.","Debit GST receivable $20; credit Bank $220.","Capital is not income; advertising affects profit."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-transactions-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “All cash purchases are expenses.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Equipment held for use is an asset rather than an immediate expense.","Classify what is acquired and whether it has been consumed before recording.","Example evidence: Equipment: debit Equipment $3,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-transactions-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is every purchase debited to an expense?","o":["Equipment held for use is an asset rather than an immediate expense.","All cash purchases are expenses."],"a":0,"why":"Classify what is acquired and whether it has been consumed before recording."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Classify what is acquired and whether it has been consumed before recording.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Equipment held for use is an asset rather than an immediate expense."},{"q":"In the model “Cash purchase entry”, what belongs to “Equipment”?","o":["Debit net asset cost","Debit eligible input GST","Credit gross amount"],"a":0,"why":"Equipment: Debit net asset cost. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Cash purchase entry”, what belongs to “GST receivable”?","o":["Debit net asset cost","Debit eligible input GST","Credit gross amount"],"a":1,"why":"GST receivable: Debit eligible input GST. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Cash purchase entry","rows":[["Equipment","Debit net asset cost"],["GST receivable","Debit eligible input GST"],["Bank","Credit gross amount"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is every purchase debited to an expense?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can record commencement entries.</li><li>I can distinguish assets from expenses.</li><li>I can include eligible GST correctly.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record commencement entries","html":"<p>Commencement entries recognise resources introduced by the owner and the corresponding capital. A cash purchase of equipment creates an asset. An expense is recognised for a resource consumed, such as ordinary advertising; classify the purpose before applying the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> rule.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish assets from expenses","html":"<p>In Unit 1, taxable purchases include eligible <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> receivable. The bank <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> is the gross cash paid; the asset or expense <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> is the net amount. Wages do not have <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> merely because the business is registered.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Include eligible GST correctly","html":"<p>Narrations describe the transaction and evidence. Write dates and account names clearly; a compound entry has multiple debits or credits but still equal totals. Owner funds are capital rather than revenue because they come from the owner acting as owner.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain capital, cash purchases and cash expenses using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-credit-purchases","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Credit purchases and creditor payments","subtitle":"Record inventory bought on credit · Track accounts payable · Separate purchase from payment","scope":"Record inventory bought on credit · Track accounts payable · Separate purchase from payment","outcomes":["Record inventory bought on credit","Track accounts payable","Separate purchase from payment"],"paragraphs":["A credit purchase increases inventory or another acquired resource before cash is paid. Accounts payable records the obligation to the supplier. Under the perpetual system, goods acquired for resale are debited to Inventory rather than a periodic Purchases account.","For an eligible taxable credit purchase, debit Inventory and GST receivable and credit Accounts payable for the gross invoice. Payment later debits the payable and credits Bank; it does not create a second inventory purchase.","Keep supplier records and check invoices against goods received. Payment of a payable reduces an asset and a liability together. It does not reduce profit a second time; inventory becomes cost of sales when sold, using the cost information supplied."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Buy inventory for $1,100 including eligible GST on credit, then pay the invoice in full. Prepare both entries.","steps":["Debit Inventory $1,000 and GST receivable $100.","Credit Accounts payable $1,100.","On payment debit Accounts payable $1,100.","Credit Bank $1,100; do not debit Inventory again."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-credit-purchases-q1"},{"prompt":"Buy inventory on credit for $2,200 including eligible GST. Pay $880 toward it. Calculate and explain the payable remaining.","steps":["Debit Inventory $2,000 and GST receivable $200; credit Accounts payable $2,200.","Payment: debit Accounts payable $880; credit Bank $880.","Remaining payable = 2,200 − 880 = $1,320.","Inventory cost is unaffected by this partial settlement."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-credit-purchases-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Debit Inventory again whenever a supplier is paid.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Supplier payment settles the existing payable.","The purchase was recognised when the goods and obligation were recorded.","Example evidence: Debit Inventory $1,000 and GST receivable $100."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-credit-purchases-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does paying a supplier create a second purchase?","o":["Supplier payment settles the existing payable.","Debit Inventory again whenever a supplier is paid."],"a":0,"why":"The purchase was recognised when the goods and obligation were recorded."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The purchase was recognised when the goods and obligation were recorded.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Supplier payment settles the existing payable."},{"q":"In the model “Creditor ledger movement”, what belongs to “Invoice”?","o":["Credit payable $1,100","Debit payable $1,100","Nil after full payment"],"a":0,"why":"Invoice: Credit payable $1,100. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Creditor ledger movement”, what belongs to “Payment”?","o":["Credit payable $1,100","Debit payable $1,100","Nil after full payment"],"a":1,"why":"Payment: Debit payable $1,100. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Creditor ledger movement","rows":[["Invoice","Credit payable $1,100"],["Payment","Debit payable $1,100"],["Balance","Nil after full payment"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does paying a supplier create a second purchase?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can record inventory bought on credit.</li><li>I can track accounts payable.</li><li>I can separate purchase from payment.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record inventory bought on credit","html":"<p>A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> purchase increases <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> or another acquired resource before cash is paid. Accounts payable records the obligation to the supplier. Under the perpetual system, goods acquired for resale are debited to <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Inventory</strong> rather than a periodic Purchases account.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Track accounts payable","html":"<p>For an eligible taxable <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> purchase, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Inventory</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> receivable and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> Accounts payable for the gross invoice. Payment later debits the payable and credits Bank; it does not create a second <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> purchase.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate purchase from payment","html":"<p>Keep supplier records and check invoices against goods received. Payment of a payable reduces an asset and a liability together. It does not reduce <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> a second time; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> becomes cost of sales when sold, using the cost information supplied.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain credit purchases and creditor payments using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-sales","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Cash and credit sales under perpetual inventory","subtitle":"Record the selling side · Record cost of sales separately · Explain gross profit","scope":"Record the selling side · Record cost of sales separately · Explain gross profit","outcomes":["Record the selling side","Record cost of sales separately","Explain gross profit"],"paragraphs":["A sale has two different amounts: the selling price and the cost of inventory sold. Under the perpetual system, record income and any GST, then record the inventory leaving at its supplied cost. Never use selling price as cost of sales.","A cash sale debits Bank; a credit sale debits Accounts receivable. Both credit Sales for the GST-exclusive value and GST payable for output tax. The second entry debits Cost of sales and credits Inventory, with no second GST entry.","Gross profit is net sales less cost of sales. Inventory records quantities and cost as transactions occur. Unit 1 tasks give the cost of goods sold; detailed FIFO and weighted-average calculations are not required to invent that cost."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Sell goods on credit for $1,650 including GST. Their supplied cost is $900. Record both entries and gross profit.","steps":["Debit Accounts receivable $1,650.","Credit Sales $1,500 and GST payable $150.","Debit Cost of sales $900; credit Inventory $900.","Gross profit = 1,500 − 900 = $600."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-sales-q1"},{"prompt":"A cash sale is $880 including GST; supplied inventory cost is $500. Journalise and find gross profit.","steps":["Debit Bank $880.","Credit Sales $800 and GST payable $80.","Debit Cost of sales $500; credit Inventory $500.","Gross profit is $300, not $380."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-sales-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Credit Inventory with the GST-inclusive selling price.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Inventory is removed at its supplied cost; sales use the selling price.","Selling value and inventory cost serve different accounting purposes.","Example evidence: Debit Accounts receivable $1,650."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-sales-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should inventory leaving be recorded at the selling price?","o":["Inventory is removed at its supplied cost; sales use the selling price.","Credit Inventory with the GST-inclusive selling price."],"a":0,"why":"Selling value and inventory cost serve different accounting purposes."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Selling value and inventory cost serve different accounting purposes.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Inventory is removed at its supplied cost; sales use the selling price."},{"q":"In the model “Two linked entries”, what belongs to “Customer side”?","o":["Gross receivable/cash = net Sales + GST","Cost of sales = inventory cost removed","Net Sales less cost of sales"],"a":0,"why":"Customer side: Gross receivable/cash = net Sales + GST. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Two linked entries”, what belongs to “Inventory side”?","o":["Gross receivable/cash = net Sales + GST","Cost of sales = inventory cost removed","Net Sales less cost of sales"],"a":1,"why":"Inventory side: Cost of sales = inventory cost removed. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Two linked entries","rows":[["Customer side","Gross receivable/cash = net Sales + GST"],["Inventory side","Cost of sales = inventory cost removed"],["Gross profit","Net Sales less cost of sales"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should inventory leaving be recorded at the selling price?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can record the selling side.</li><li>I can record cost of sales separately.</li><li>I can explain gross profit.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record the selling side","html":"<p>A sale has two different amounts: the selling price and the cost of <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> sold. Under the perpetual system, record income and any <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>, then record the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> leaving at its supplied cost. Never use selling price as cost of sales.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record cost of sales separately","html":"<p>A cash sale debits Bank; a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sale debits Accounts receivable. Both <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> Sales for the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>-exclusive value and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> payable for output tax. The second entry debits Cost of sales and credits <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Inventory</strong>, with no second <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> entry.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain gross profit","html":"<p>Gross <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> is net sales less cost of sales. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Inventory</strong> records quantities and cost as transactions occur. Unit 1 tasks give the cost of goods sold; detailed FIFO and weighted-average calculations are not required to invent that cost.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain cash and credit sales under perpetual inventory using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-receivables","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Customer receipts and receivable control","subtitle":"Record collections · Calculate a running balance · Explain why receipts differ from revenue","scope":"Record collections · Calculate a running balance · Explain why receipts differ from revenue","outcomes":["Record collections","Calculate a running balance","Explain why receipts differ from revenue"],"paragraphs":["Accounts receivable is an asset representing amounts owed by customers. A sale creates it; collection reduces it. Customer statements should separate invoices, returns, discounts and receipts so overdue amounts can be identified.","Collecting a previously recorded invoice debits Bank and credits Accounts receivable for the gross amount. It does not create another Sales or GST payable entry. Tax reporting timing can depend on the GST basis, but do not duplicate the underlying accounting sale.","Control measures include credit approval, credit limits, aged receivable reports and follow-up. A growing receivable balance can signal slow collection even if sales are strong. Use due dates and customer history rather than assuming all receivables will become cash immediately."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A customer owes $1,650 and pays $1,000. Record collection and calculate the remaining balance.","steps":["Debit Bank $1,000.","Credit Accounts receivable $1,000.","Remaining receivable = 1,650 − 1,000 = $650.","No additional Sales is recorded on this collection."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-receivables-q1"},{"prompt":"Receivables begin at $2,000. New gross invoices total $3,300 and receipts are $2,500. Find the closing balance and suggest a control.","steps":["Closing receivables = 2,000 + 3,300 − 2,500 = $2,800.","Receipts debit Bank and credit Accounts receivable.","Use an aged receivables report and overdue follow-up.","Check credit limits before allowing further sales."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-receivables-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Cash receipts must equal current sales revenue.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Receipts can collect old invoices and leave new invoices outstanding.","The sale date and collection date need not fall in the same reporting period.","Example evidence: Debit Bank $1,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-receivables-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are customer receipts always equal to current-period sales?","o":["Receipts can collect old invoices and leave new invoices outstanding.","Cash receipts must equal current sales revenue."],"a":0,"why":"The sale date and collection date need not fall in the same reporting period."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The sale date and collection date need not fall in the same reporting period.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Receipts can collect old invoices and leave new invoices outstanding."},{"q":"In the model “Receivable roll-forward”, what belongs to “Opening”?","o":["$2,000","$3,300","$2,500","$2,800"],"a":0,"why":"Opening: $2,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Receivable roll-forward”, what belongs to “Add invoices”?","o":["$2,000","$3,300","$2,500","$2,800"],"a":1,"why":"Add invoices: $3,300. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Receivable roll-forward","rows":[["Opening","$2,000"],["Add invoices","$3,300"],["Less receipts","$2,500"],["Closing","$2,800"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are customer receipts always equal to current-period sales?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can record collections.</li><li>I can calculate a running balance.</li><li>I can explain why receipts differ from revenue.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record collections","html":"<p>Accounts receivable is an asset representing amounts owed by customers. A sale creates it; collection reduces it. Customer statements should separate invoices, returns, discounts and receipts so overdue amounts can be identified.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate a running balance","html":"<p>Collecting a previously recorded invoice debits Bank and credits Accounts receivable for the gross amount. It does not create another Sales or <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> payable entry. Tax reporting timing can depend on the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> basis, but do not duplicate the underlying accounting sale.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain why receipts differ from revenue","html":"<p>Control measures include <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> approval, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> limits, aged receivable reports and follow-up. A growing receivable balance can signal slow collection even if sales are strong. Use due dates and customer history rather than assuming all receivables will become cash immediately.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain customer receipts and receivable control using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-returns","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Sales and purchase returns","subtitle":"Reverse the correct side of a transaction · Restore returned inventory at cost · Adjust GST consistently","scope":"Reverse the correct side of a transaction · Restore returned inventory at cost · Adjust GST consistently","outcomes":["Reverse the correct side of a transaction","Restore returned inventory at cost","Adjust GST consistently"],"paragraphs":["A sales return reverses revenue for the selling value and GST for the related tax. If saleable goods physically return, reverse the original cost of sales entry using their cost. A price allowance with no goods returned does not restore inventory.","A purchase return reduces inventory at its recorded net cost, reduces GST receivable and reduces the payable or creates a cash refund. A credit note provides evidence. Use the gross amount to adjust the supplier or customer account.","Returns accounts help show net sales and maintain an audit trail. Do not confuse a sales return from a customer with a purchase return to a supplier. Check that the inventory adjustment matches the physical movement."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A customer returns saleable goods sold on credit for $220 including GST; original cost was $120. Record the return.","steps":["Debit Sales returns $200 and GST payable $20.","Credit Accounts receivable $220.","Debit Inventory $120.","Credit Cost of sales $120."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-returns-q1"},{"prompt":"Return inventory purchased on credit for $330 including eligible GST. Record the purchase return.","steps":["Debit Accounts payable $330.","Credit Inventory $300.","Credit GST receivable $30.","The return reduces the payable and reverses the original input tax credit."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-returns-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every sales allowance increases Inventory at selling price.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Inventory is restored only when goods return and are recorded at cost.","A price-only adjustment does not bring goods back into the business.","Example evidence: Debit Sales returns $200 and GST payable $20."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-returns-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a price allowance always increase inventory?","o":["Inventory is restored only when goods return and are recorded at cost.","Every sales allowance increases Inventory at selling price."],"a":0,"why":"A price-only adjustment does not bring goods back into the business."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A price-only adjustment does not bring goods back into the business.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Inventory is restored only when goods return and are recorded at cost."},{"q":"In the model “Return evidence”, what belongs to “Sales return”?","o":["Reverse selling value and output GST","Restore inventory at supplied cost","Reduce payable, inventory and input GST"],"a":0,"why":"Sales return: Reverse selling value and output GST. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Return evidence”, what belongs to “Goods returned”?","o":["Reverse selling value and output GST","Restore inventory at supplied cost","Reduce payable, inventory and input GST"],"a":1,"why":"Goods returned: Restore inventory at supplied cost. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Return evidence","rows":[["Sales return","Reverse selling value and output GST"],["Goods returned","Restore inventory at supplied cost"],["Purchase return","Reduce payable, inventory and input GST"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a price allowance always increase inventory?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can reverse the correct side of a transaction.</li><li>I can restore returned inventory at cost.</li><li>I can adjust GST consistently.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reverse the correct side of a transaction","html":"<p>A sales return reverses revenue for the selling value and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> for the related tax. If saleable goods physically return, reverse the original cost of sales entry using their cost. A price allowance with no goods returned does not restore <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Restore returned inventory at cost","html":"<p>A purchase return reduces <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> at its recorded net cost, reduces <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> receivable and reduces the payable or creates a cash refund. A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> note provides evidence. Use the gross amount to adjust the supplier or customer account.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Adjust GST consistently","html":"<p>Returns accounts help show net sales and maintain an audit trail. Do not confuse a sales return from a customer with a purchase return to a supplier. Check that the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> adjustment matches the physical movement.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain sales and purchase returns using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-discounts","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Settlement discounts and GST","subtitle":"Distinguish trade and settlement discounts · Adjust gross debt and tax · Record allowed and received discounts","scope":"Distinguish trade and settlement discounts · Adjust gross debt and tax · Record allowed and received discounts","outcomes":["Distinguish trade and settlement discounts","Adjust gross debt and tax","Record allowed and received discounts"],"paragraphs":["A trade discount reduces the invoice price before recording the transaction. A settlement discount rewards early payment of an already recorded debt. Discount allowed is given to a customer; discount received is obtained from a supplier.","When a settlement discount changes a taxable invoice, adjust the related GST as well as the net discount. For a gross discount of $22 including GST, the net discount is $20 and GST adjustment $2. Apply the stated percentage to the stated invoice base.","The original receivable or payable must be cleared by the full gross amount settled. Bank records the cash actually exchanged. Use the task’s account presentation: the SCSA Year 12 net-sales definition later subtracts discount allowed, and total cost of sales subtracts discount received."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A customer owing $1,100 receives a 2% settlement discount on the gross balance. Record payment.","steps":["Gross discount = 1,100 × 2% = $22; cash = $1,078.","Debit Bank $1,078 and Discount allowed $20.","Debit GST payable $2.","Credit Accounts receivable $1,100."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-discounts-q1"},{"prompt":"Settle a supplier invoice of $2,200 with a 2% discount on the gross amount. Record it.","steps":["Discount = $44; cash paid = $2,156.","Debit Accounts payable $2,200.","Credit Bank $2,156 and Discount received $40.","Credit GST receivable $4."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-discounts-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Post the entire gross discount to Discount allowed or received.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Split a taxable gross settlement discount into net discount and GST adjustment.","The tax component must also be reversed when the taxable price is reduced.","Example evidence: Gross discount = 1,100 × 2% = $22; cash = $1,078."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-discounts-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is all of a GST-inclusive discount posted to the discount account?","o":["Split a taxable gross settlement discount into net discount and GST adjustment.","Post the entire gross discount to Discount allowed or received."],"a":0,"why":"The tax component must also be reversed when the taxable price is reduced."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The tax component must also be reversed when the taxable price is reduced.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Split a taxable gross settlement discount into net discount and GST adjustment."},{"q":"In the model “Customer settlement”, what belongs to “Receivable cleared”?","o":["$1,100","$1,078","$20 + $2"],"a":0,"why":"Receivable cleared: $1,100. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Customer settlement”, what belongs to “Cash received”?","o":["$1,100","$1,078","$20 + $2"],"a":1,"why":"Cash received: $1,078. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Customer settlement","rows":[["Receivable cleared","$1,100"],["Cash received","$1,078"],["Net discount and GST","$20 + $2"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is all of a GST-inclusive discount posted to the discount account?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can distinguish trade and settlement discounts.</li><li>I can adjust gross debt and tax.</li><li>I can record allowed and received discounts.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish trade and settlement discounts","html":"<p>A trade discount reduces the invoice price before recording the transaction. A settlement discount rewards early payment of an already recorded debt. Discount allowed is given to a customer; discount received is obtained from a supplier.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Adjust gross debt and tax","html":"<p>When a settlement discount changes a taxable invoice, adjust the related <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> as well as the net discount. For a gross discount of $22 including <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>, the net discount is $20 and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> adjustment $2. Apply the stated percentage to the stated invoice base.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record allowed and received discounts","html":"<p>The original receivable or payable must be cleared by the full gross amount settled. Bank records the cash actually exchanged. Use the task’s account presentation: the SCSA Year 12 net-sales definition later subtracts discount allowed, and total cost of sales subtracts discount received.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain settlement discounts and gst using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-drawings","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Drawings of cash and inventory","subtitle":"Separate owner transactions from expenses · Record inventory withdrawals · Explain effects on equity","scope":"Separate owner transactions from expenses · Record inventory withdrawals · Explain effects on equity","outcomes":["Separate owner transactions from expenses","Record inventory withdrawals","Explain effects on equity"],"paragraphs":["Drawings are resources withdrawn by the owner for personal use. They reduce owner equity but are not business expenses. Paying a personal bill from Bank is recorded as Drawings; otherwise profit would be understated.","Inventory withdrawn is credited at its cost, not retail price. If the task states that an input tax credit was claimed and a GST adjustment is required, debit Drawings for cost plus that adjustment and credit Inventory and GST payable accordingly.","Keep owner and business evidence separate. Drawings are closed to Capital at period end. They appear in the equity reconciliation rather than as a cost of running the business in the income statement."],"questions":[{"prompt":"The owner withdraws $500 cash and inventory costing $200. Assume the inventory withdrawal requires $20 GST adjustment. Record both.","steps":["Cash: debit Drawings $500; credit Bank $500.","Inventory: debit Drawings $220.","Credit Inventory $200 and GST payable $20.","Total drawings reduce equity by $720, not profit."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-drawings-q1"},{"prompt":"The owner pays a $300 personal bill from business Bank and introduces new capital $1,000. Show both entries and net equity effect.","steps":["Debit Drawings $300; credit Bank $300.","Debit Bank $1,000; credit Capital $1,000.","Net equity increase = 1,000 − 300 = $700.","Neither owner transaction is revenue or expense."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-drawings-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “All business-bank payments belong in operating expenses.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Personal withdrawals are drawings and reduce equity directly.","The entity convention separates owner consumption from business performance.","Example evidence: Cash: debit Drawings $500; credit Bank $500."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-drawings-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should personal spending be a business expense?","o":["Personal withdrawals are drawings and reduce equity directly.","All business-bank payments belong in operating expenses."],"a":0,"why":"The entity convention separates owner consumption from business performance."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The entity convention separates owner consumption from business performance.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Personal withdrawals are drawings and reduce equity directly."},{"q":"In the model “Equity bridge”, what belongs to “Opening capital”?","o":["Owner claim at start","Increase equity","Reduce equity without expense"],"a":0,"why":"Opening capital: Owner claim at start. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Equity bridge”, what belongs to “Add contributions and profit”?","o":["Owner claim at start","Increase equity","Reduce equity without expense"],"a":1,"why":"Add contributions and profit: Increase equity. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Equity bridge","rows":[["Opening capital","Owner claim at start"],["Add contributions and profit","Increase equity"],["Less drawings","Reduce equity without expense"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should personal spending be a business expense?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can separate owner transactions from expenses.</li><li>I can record inventory withdrawals.</li><li>I can explain effects on equity.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate owner transactions from expenses","html":"<p>Drawings are resources withdrawn by the owner for personal use. They reduce owner <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> but are not business expenses. Paying a personal bill from Bank is recorded as Drawings; otherwise <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> would be understated.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record inventory withdrawals","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Inventory</strong> withdrawn is credited at its cost, not retail price. If the task states that an input tax <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> was claimed and a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> adjustment is required, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> Drawings for cost plus that adjustment and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Inventory</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> payable accordingly.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain effects on equity","html":"<p>Keep owner and business evidence separate. Drawings are closed to Capital at period end. They appear in the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> reconciliation rather than as a cost of running the business in the income statement.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain drawings of cash and inventory using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-bad-debts","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Writing off an irrecoverable account","subtitle":"Distinguish bad debt from slow payment · Remove the receivable · Handle stated GST adjustment","scope":"Distinguish bad debt from slow payment · Remove the receivable · Handle stated GST adjustment","outcomes":["Distinguish bad debt from slow payment","Remove the receivable","Handle stated GST adjustment"],"paragraphs":["An irrecoverable receivable no longer represents an amount expected to be collected. A direct write-off reduces Accounts receivable and recognises Bad debts expense. A late customer is not automatically a bad debt; review evidence of irrecoverability.","In a Unit 1 taxable invoice task, a permitted GST bad-debt adjustment reduces GST payable for the tax component. This example explicitly assumes the adjustment conditions are met. Do not assume every overdue invoice immediately creates a GST adjustment.","The write-off has no Bank entry because no cash is received or paid. Controls such as credit checks and collection follow-up reduce risk, but even strong controls cannot eliminate customer failure. Unit 2 introduces an allowance for estimated doubtful debts."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Write off a $550 taxable receivable. Assume the $50 GST bad-debt adjustment is permitted.","steps":["Debit Bad debts expense $500.","Debit GST payable $50.","Credit Accounts receivable $550.","The entry removes the receivable without changing Bank."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-bad-debts-q1"},{"prompt":"A non-GST receivable of $800 is irrecoverable. Record the write-off and explain its profit effect.","steps":["Debit Bad debts expense $800.","Credit Accounts receivable $800.","Profit decreases by $800.","There is no cash flow from the write-off itself."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-bad-debts-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Credit Bank when writing off an unpaid invoice.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["A write-off reduces a receivable without a Bank entry.","No cash has moved; the business is recognising the loss of an existing claim.","Example evidence: Debit Bad debts expense $500."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-bad-debts-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a write-off always reduce Bank?","o":["A write-off reduces a receivable without a Bank entry.","Credit Bank when writing off an unpaid invoice."],"a":0,"why":"No cash has moved; the business is recognising the loss of an existing claim."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["No cash has moved; the business is recognising the loss of an existing claim.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"A write-off reduces a receivable without a Bank entry."},{"q":"In the model “Write-off pattern”, what belongs to “Bad debts expense”?","o":["Debit net loss","Debit permitted tax adjustment","Credit gross debt removed"],"a":0,"why":"Bad debts expense: Debit net loss. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Write-off pattern”, what belongs to “GST payable”?","o":["Debit net loss","Debit permitted tax adjustment","Credit gross debt removed"],"a":1,"why":"GST payable: Debit permitted tax adjustment. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Write-off pattern","rows":[["Bad debts expense","Debit net loss"],["GST payable","Debit permitted tax adjustment"],["Accounts receivable","Credit gross debt removed"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a write-off always reduce Bank?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can distinguish bad debt from slow payment.</li><li>I can remove the receivable.</li><li>I can handle stated GST adjustment.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish bad debt from slow payment","html":"<p>An irrecoverable receivable no longer represents an amount expected to be collected. A direct write-off reduces Accounts receivable and recognises Bad debts expense. A late customer is not automatically a bad debt; review evidence of irrecoverability.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Remove the receivable","html":"<p>In a Unit 1 taxable invoice task, a permitted <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> bad-debt adjustment reduces <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> payable for the tax component. This example explicitly assumes the adjustment conditions are met. Do not assume every overdue invoice immediately creates a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> adjustment.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Handle stated GST adjustment","html":"<p>The write-off has no Bank entry because no cash is received or paid. Controls such as <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> checks and collection follow-up reduce risk, but even strong controls cannot eliminate customer failure. Unit 2 introduces an allowance for estimated doubtful debts.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain writing off an irrecoverable account using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-ledger","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Posting and balancing ledger accounts","subtitle":"Post both sides accurately · Calculate debit or credit balances · Trace balances to evidence","scope":"Post both sides accurately · Calculate debit or credit balances · Trace balances to evidence","outcomes":["Post both sides accurately","Calculate debit or credit balances","Trace balances to evidence"],"paragraphs":["A ledger account collects increases and decreases for one item. In a T-account the left is debit and the right is credit; in a running-balance ledger include date, details and resulting balance. Always identify whether the final balance is debit or credit.","Bank normally has a debit balance. Start with the opening amount, add debit receipts and subtract credit payments. A payable normally has a credit balance; supplier invoices increase it by credit and settlements reduce it by debit.","Cross-reference ledger lines to the journal and source documents. The balance is the net result, not the total of the larger side. A ledger can balance arithmetically while containing a transaction posted to the wrong account."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Bank opens with a $2,000 debit balance. Post capital introduced $3,000, wages paid $700 and equipment purchased $1,100. Find the balance.","steps":["Debit side totals 2,000 + 3,000 = $5,000.","Credit payments total 700 + 1,100 = $1,800.","Closing Bank = $3,200 debit.","Each Bank line has a corresponding entry in another ledger."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-ledger-q1"},{"prompt":"Accounts payable opens with $900 credit. New invoices total $1,600, returns $200 and payments $1,300. Find the closing balance.","steps":["Credit obligations total 900 + 1,600 = $2,500.","Debit reductions total 200 + 1,300 = $1,500.","Closing Accounts payable = $1,000 credit.","The balance is the amount still owed, not $2,500."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-ledger-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “A ledger balance equals its total debit entries.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["A balance is the difference between the two sides, labelled debit or credit.","Both increases and decreases must be included before the net balance is reported.","Example evidence: Debit side totals 2,000 + 3,000 = $5,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-ledger-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is the balance always the total of the debit side?","o":["A balance is the difference between the two sides, labelled debit or credit.","A ledger balance equals its total debit entries."],"a":0,"why":"Both increases and decreases must be included before the net balance is reported."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Both increases and decreases must be included before the net balance is reported.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"A balance is the difference between the two sides, labelled debit or credit."},{"q":"In the model “Bank T-account”, what belongs to “Debit”?","o":["Opening 2,000 + receipts 3,000","Wages 700 + equipment 1,100","3,200 debit"],"a":0,"why":"Debit: Opening 2,000 + receipts 3,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Bank T-account”, what belongs to “Credit”?","o":["Opening 2,000 + receipts 3,000","Wages 700 + equipment 1,100","3,200 debit"],"a":1,"why":"Credit: Wages 700 + equipment 1,100. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Bank T-account","rows":[["Debit","Opening 2,000 + receipts 3,000"],["Credit","Wages 700 + equipment 1,100"],["Balance","3,200 debit"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is the balance always the total of the debit side?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can post both sides accurately.</li><li>I can calculate debit or credit balances.</li><li>I can trace balances to evidence.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Post both sides accurately","html":"<p>A ledger account collects increases and decreases for one item. In a T-account the left is <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> and the right is <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong>; in a running-balance ledger include date, details and resulting balance. Always identify whether the final balance is <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> or <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate debit or credit balances","html":"<p>Bank normally has a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> balance. Start with the opening amount, add <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> receipts and subtract <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> payments. A payable normally has a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> balance; supplier invoices increase it by <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> and settlements reduce it by <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong>.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Trace balances to evidence","html":"<p>Cross-reference ledger lines to the journal and source documents. The balance is the net result, not the total of the larger side. A ledger can balance arithmetically while containing a transaction posted to the wrong account.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain posting and balancing ledger accounts using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-trial-balance","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Trial balance and errors","subtitle":"Prepare a list of balances · Identify errors affecting equality · Recognise errors not detected","scope":"Prepare a list of balances · Identify errors affecting equality · Recognise errors not detected","outcomes":["Prepare a list of balances","Identify errors affecting equality","Recognise errors not detected"],"paragraphs":["The trial balance lists ledger balances in debit and credit columns. Asset, expense and drawings balances normally appear as debits; liabilities, capital and revenue normally appear as credits. Compare totals after posting every account.","An unequal trial balance can reveal one-sided posting, arithmetic errors or placing a balance in the wrong column. If a $400 debit balance is put in the credit column, the difference is $800: one column loses $400 while the other gains $400.","Equal totals cannot reveal a complete omission, equal errors on both sides or recording equipment as Repairs expense with the same credit to Bank. Equality confirms arithmetic consistency only; review classifications and source evidence separately."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Bank debit $3,000, Equipment debit $5,000, Wages debit $800, Capital credit $6,000 and Sales credit $2,800 are listed. Test equality and explain a limitation.","steps":["Debit total = 3,000 + 5,000 + 800 = $8,800.","Credit total = 6,000 + 2,800 = $8,800.","The trial balance agrees.","A wholly omitted transaction or wrong expense/asset classification could still exist."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-trial-balance-q1"},{"prompt":"A $250 debit is posted only to Bank with no matching capital credit. State the imbalance and correction.","steps":["Debits exceed credits by $250.","Post the omitted $250 credit to Capital after checking evidence.","This is a one-sided error disclosed by unequal totals.","Do not invent an unrelated entry merely to force equality."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-trial-balance-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “An agreeing trial balance proves no transactions are missing.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Equal debits and credits do not prove complete or correctly classified records.","A complete omission affects neither column and therefore leaves equality unchanged.","Example evidence: Debit total = 3,000 + 5,000 + 800 = $8,800."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-trial-balance-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does an agreeing trial balance prove all records are correct?","o":["Equal debits and credits do not prove complete or correctly classified records.","An agreeing trial balance proves no transactions are missing."],"a":0,"why":"A complete omission affects neither column and therefore leaves equality unchanged."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A complete omission affects neither column and therefore leaves equality unchanged.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Equal debits and credits do not prove complete or correctly classified records."}],"model":{"title":"What equality can reveal","rows":[["One-sided posting","Often unequal totals"],["Complete omission","May leave totals equal"],["Wrong account with same amount","May leave totals equal"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does an agreeing trial balance prove all records are correct?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can prepare a list of balances.</li><li>I can identify errors affecting equality.</li><li>I can recognise errors not detected.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Prepare a list of balances","html":"<p>The trial balance lists ledger balances in <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> columns. Asset, expense and drawings balances normally appear as debits; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>, capital and revenue normally appear as credits. Compare totals after posting every account.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify errors affecting equality","html":"<p>An unequal trial balance can reveal one-sided posting, arithmetic errors or placing a balance in the wrong column. If a $400 <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> balance is put in the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> column, the difference is $800: one column loses $400 while the other gains $400.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recognise errors not detected","html":"<p>Equal totals cannot reveal a complete omission, equal errors on both sides or recording equipment as Repairs expense with the same <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> to Bank. Equality confirms arithmetic consistency only; review classifications and source evidence separately.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain trial balance and errors using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-correction","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Correcting journal and ledger errors","subtitle":"Compare actual with required entries · Record the difference · Maintain an audit trail","scope":"Compare actual with required entries · Record the difference · Maintain an audit trail","outcomes":["Compare actual with required entries","Record the difference","Maintain an audit trail"],"paragraphs":["To correct an error, compare the entry recorded with the entry that should have been recorded. Reverse the incorrect effect and post the correct effect; when the Bank side is already correct, it must not be recorded again.","An equipment purchase wrongly debited to Repairs expense understates assets and profit. A correction debits Equipment and credits Repairs expense. If both debit and credit were recorded at the wrong amount, correct both by the difference.","Date and narrate corrections with a clear reference. Do not remove evidence just to hide the error. A correction changes reports because account classification matters even when the original trial balance agreed."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Equipment $1,200 excluding GST was wrongly recorded Dr Repairs expense $1,200 / Cr Bank $1,200. Correct it.","steps":["Debit Equipment $1,200.","Credit Repairs expense $1,200.","Bank is already correct and needs no second entry.","Assets and profit increase by $1,200 relative to the erroneous records."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-correction-q1"},{"prompt":"A non-GST cash service sale of $900 was recorded as $90 on both sides. Record the correcting difference.","steps":["Missing amount = 900 − 90 = $810.","Debit Bank $810.","Credit Service revenue $810.","The original trial balance could agree despite the amount error."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-correction-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Always enter the full cash amount again when correcting an error.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Correct only the accounts or amounts that differ from the required entry.","Repeating an already correct Bank entry creates a new error.","Example evidence: Debit Equipment $1,200."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-correction-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should every correction reverse and re-record the Bank entry?","o":["Correct only the accounts or amounts that differ from the required entry.","Always enter the full cash amount again when correcting an error."],"a":0,"why":"Repeating an already correct Bank entry creates a new error."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Repeating an already correct Bank entry creates a new error.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Correct only the accounts or amounts that differ from the required entry."},{"q":"In the model “Correction analysis”, what belongs to “Actual”?","o":["Debit wrong expense; credit Bank correctly","Debit Equipment; credit Bank","Debit Equipment; credit wrong expense"],"a":0,"why":"Actual: Debit wrong expense; credit Bank correctly. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Correction analysis”, what belongs to “Required”?","o":["Debit wrong expense; credit Bank correctly","Debit Equipment; credit Bank","Debit Equipment; credit wrong expense"],"a":1,"why":"Required: Debit Equipment; credit Bank. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Correction analysis","rows":[["Actual","Debit wrong expense; credit Bank correctly"],["Required","Debit Equipment; credit Bank"],["Correction","Debit Equipment; credit wrong expense"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should every correction reverse and re-record the Bank entry?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare actual with required entries.</li><li>I can record the difference.</li><li>I can maintain an audit trail.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare actual with required entries","html":"<p>To correct an error, compare the entry recorded with the entry that should have been recorded. Reverse the incorrect effect and post the correct effect; when the Bank side is already correct, it must not be recorded again.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record the difference","html":"<p>An equipment purchase wrongly debited to Repairs expense understates <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>. A correction debits Equipment and credits Repairs expense. If both <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> were recorded at the wrong amount, correct both by the difference.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Maintain an audit trail","html":"<p>Date and narrate corrections with a clear reference. Do not remove evidence just to hide the error. A correction changes reports because account classification matters even when the original trial balance agreed.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain correcting journal and ledger errors using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-closing","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Closing income, expenses and drawings","subtitle":"Distinguish permanent and temporary accounts · Transfer profit to capital · Prepare for the next period","scope":"Distinguish permanent and temporary accounts · Transfer profit to capital · Prepare for the next period","outcomes":["Distinguish permanent and temporary accounts","Transfer profit to capital","Prepare for the next period"],"paragraphs":["Income, expense and drawings accounts describe one period and are closed. Assets, liabilities and Capital continue with their balances. Closing does not mean emptying Bank or paying every liability.","Transfer income to Profit and loss summary with a debit to income and a credit to the summary. Transfer expenses with a debit to the summary and credits to expenses. A net credit summary represents profit; transfer it to Capital.","Close Drawings by debiting Capital and crediting Drawings. Closing entries are bookkeeping transfers rather than cash transactions. The next period starts income, expense and drawings accounts at zero while permanent accounts carry forward."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Sales $10,000 and expenses $7,000 are closed; Drawings is $1,000. Show summary transfers and capital effect.","steps":["Debit Sales $10,000; credit Profit and loss summary $10,000.","Debit Profit and loss summary $7,000; credit expense accounts $7,000.","Debit Profit and loss summary $3,000; credit Capital $3,000.","Debit Capital $1,000; credit Drawings $1,000. Net capital increase is $2,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-closing-q1"},{"prompt":"Service revenue is $6,000, expenses $7,500 and drawings $500. Explain closing a loss and total capital effect.","steps":["Loss = 6,000 − 7,500 = $1,500.","Close revenue and expenses to the summary.","Debit Capital $1,500; credit Profit and loss summary $1,500.","Close drawings: debit Capital $500; credit Drawings $500. Equity decreases $2,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-closing-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Close Bank and Equipment to Profit and loss summary every period.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Permanent asset and liability accounts carry their balances forward.","Closing resets period performance accounts rather than disposing of resources.","Example evidence: Debit Sales $10,000; credit Profit and loss summary $10,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-closing-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are assets closed to zero each period?","o":["Permanent asset and liability accounts carry their balances forward.","Close Bank and Equipment to Profit and loss summary every period."],"a":0,"why":"Closing resets period performance accounts rather than disposing of resources."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Closing resets period performance accounts rather than disposing of resources.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Permanent asset and liability accounts carry their balances forward."},{"q":"In the model “Closing flow”, what belongs to “Revenue and expenses”?","o":["Transfer to Profit and loss summary","Transfer to Capital","Transfer directly to Capital"],"a":0,"why":"Revenue and expenses: Transfer to Profit and loss summary. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Closing flow”, what belongs to “Profit or loss”?","o":["Transfer to Profit and loss summary","Transfer to Capital","Transfer directly to Capital"],"a":1,"why":"Profit or loss: Transfer to Capital. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Closing flow","rows":[["Revenue and expenses","Transfer to Profit and loss summary"],["Profit or loss","Transfer to Capital"],["Drawings","Transfer directly to Capital"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are assets closed to zero each period?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can distinguish permanent and temporary accounts.</li><li>I can transfer profit to capital.</li><li>I can prepare for the next period.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish permanent and temporary accounts","html":"<p>Income, expense and drawings accounts describe one period and are closed. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Assets</strong>, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> and Capital continue with their balances. Closing does not mean emptying Bank or paying every liability.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Transfer profit to capital","html":"<p>Transfer income to <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Profit</strong> and loss summary with a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> to income and a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> to the summary. Transfer expenses with a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> to the summary and credits to expenses. A net <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> summary represents <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>; transfer it to Capital.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Prepare for the next period","html":"<p>Close Drawings by debiting Capital and crediting Drawings. Closing entries are bookkeeping transfers rather than cash transactions. The next period starts income, expense and drawings accounts at zero while permanent accounts carry forward.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain closing income, expenses and drawings using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-income-statement","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"A classified sole-trader income statement","subtitle":"Calculate net sales and gross profit · Classify operating expenses · Present period performance","scope":"Calculate net sales and gross profit · Classify operating expenses · Present period performance","outcomes":["Calculate net sales and gross profit","Classify operating expenses","Present period performance"],"paragraphs":["An income statement measures performance for a period. Begin with the business name, report title and period. For a trader, net sales subtract returns and relevant discount allowed; gross profit subtracts cost of sales. Other income and operating expenses lead to profit.","Cost of sales is distinct from operating expenses such as wages, rent and advertising. Capital introduced, drawings, borrowing and equipment purchases do not belong as ordinary income or expenses. GST is separated where recoverable or payable.","A classification should help users understand the business. Show subtotals clearly and identify whether the business is trading or service. Unit 1 statements here exclude balance-day adjustments; Unit 2 teaches accrual adjustments before reporting."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Sales $20,000, sales returns $1,000, cost of sales $11,000, wages $3,000 and rent $2,000 are GST-exclusive balances. Prepare the subtotals.","steps":["Net sales = 20,000 − 1,000 = $19,000.","Gross profit = 19,000 − 11,000 = $8,000.","Operating expenses = 3,000 + 2,000 = $5,000.","Profit = $3,000; title the statement for the period."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-income-statement-q1"},{"prompt":"Service revenue $15,000, wages $7,000, rent $3,000 and interest income $500 are given. Capital introduced is $4,000. Find profit and explain capital treatment.","steps":["Total income = 15,000 + 500 = $15,500.","Expenses = 7,000 + 3,000 = $10,000.","Profit = $5,500.","Capital introduced is owner equity, not income."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-income-statement-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Any money received must increase income-statement profit.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Borrowing increases a liability and cash rather than revenue.","The income statement reports earned income and consumed resources, not every cash movement.","Example evidence: Net sales = 20,000 − 1,000 = $19,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-income-statement-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is loan money included in income statement revenue?","o":["Borrowing increases a liability and cash rather than revenue.","Any money received must increase income-statement profit."],"a":0,"why":"The income statement reports earned income and consumed resources, not every cash movement."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The income statement reports earned income and consumed resources, not every cash movement.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Borrowing increases a liability and cash rather than revenue."},{"q":"In the model “Trading statement structure”, what belongs to “Net sales”?","o":["Sales less sales deductions","Net sales less cost of sales","Gross profit + other income − operating expenses"],"a":0,"why":"Net sales: Sales less sales deductions. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Trading statement structure”, what belongs to “Gross profit”?","o":["Sales less sales deductions","Net sales less cost of sales","Gross profit + other income − operating expenses"],"a":1,"why":"Gross profit: Net sales less cost of sales. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Trading statement structure","rows":[["Net sales","Sales less sales deductions"],["Gross profit","Net sales less cost of sales"],["Profit","Gross profit + other income − operating expenses"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is loan money included in income statement revenue?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate net sales and gross profit.</li><li>I can classify operating expenses.</li><li>I can present period performance.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate net sales and gross profit","html":"<p>An income statement measures performance for a period. Begin with the business name, report title and period. For a trader, net sales subtract returns and relevant discount allowed; gross <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> subtracts cost of sales. Other income and operating expenses lead to <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Classify operating expenses","html":"<p>Cost of sales is distinct from operating expenses such as wages, rent and advertising. Capital introduced, drawings, borrowing and equipment purchases do not belong as ordinary income or expenses. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> is separated where recoverable or payable.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Present period performance","html":"<p>A classification should help users understand the business. Show subtotals clearly and identify whether the business is trading or service. Unit 1 statements here exclude balance-day adjustments; Unit 2 teaches <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">accrual</strong> adjustments before reporting.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain a classified sole-trader income statement using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-financial-position","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"A classified statement of financial position","subtitle":"Separate current and non-current items · Calculate closing owner equity · Verify equation and presentation","scope":"Separate current and non-current items · Calculate closing owner equity · Verify equation and presentation","outcomes":["Separate current and non-current items","Calculate closing owner equity","Verify equation and presentation"],"paragraphs":["A statement of financial position is dated at a point in time. Current assets include cash, receivables and inventory expected to be realised in the normal operating cycle; non-current assets include equipment used over several periods.","Current liabilities include suppliers and loan amounts due within the relevant short-term classification. A long loan may have both current and non-current portions. Classification is based on the facts and settlement rights, not whether an account name sounds long term.","Closing capital is opening capital plus new contributions and profit, less drawings. List assets, liabilities and equity with meaningful subtotals and verify assets = liabilities + equity. A balance check does not replace a classification review."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Cash $4,000, receivables $6,000, inventory $10,000 and equipment $30,000; payables $8,000 and long-term loan $12,000. Classify and calculate equity.","steps":["Current assets = 4,000 + 6,000 + 10,000 = $20,000.","Non-current assets = $30,000; total assets $50,000.","Liabilities = 8,000 + 12,000 = $20,000.","Equity = $30,000; 50,000 = 20,000 + 30,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-financial-position-q1"},{"prompt":"Opening capital $18,000, contributions $2,000, profit $5,000 and drawings $3,000 are given. Liabilities are $9,000. Find closing equity and assets.","steps":["Closing equity = 18,000 + 2,000 + 5,000 − 3,000 = $22,000.","Assets = liabilities + equity = 9,000 + 22,000 = $31,000.","Drawings reduce equity without reducing profit.","Use a report date rather than a performance period heading."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-financial-position-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “A long-term loan account can never contain a current portion.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Amounts due soon may be current even within a longer loan.","Use repayment timing and classification facts rather than the loan label alone.","Example evidence: Current assets = 4,000 + 6,000 + 10,000 = $20,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-financial-position-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does all of a five-year loan have to be non-current?","o":["Amounts due soon may be current even within a longer loan.","A long-term loan account can never contain a current portion."],"a":0,"why":"Use repayment timing and classification facts rather than the loan label alone."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Use repayment timing and classification facts rather than the loan label alone.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Amounts due soon may be current even within a longer loan."},{"q":"In the model “Position structure”, what belongs to “Current assets”?","o":["Cash, receivables, inventory","Equipment held for use","Current and non-current obligations","Owner residual claim"],"a":0,"why":"Current assets: Cash, receivables, inventory. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Position structure”, what belongs to “Non-current assets”?","o":["Cash, receivables, inventory","Equipment held for use","Current and non-current obligations","Owner residual claim"],"a":1,"why":"Non-current assets: Equipment held for use. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Position structure","rows":[["Current assets","Cash, receivables, inventory"],["Non-current assets","Equipment held for use"],["Liabilities","Current and non-current obligations"],["Equity","Owner residual claim"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does all of a five-year loan have to be non-current?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can separate current and non-current items.</li><li>I can calculate closing owner equity.</li><li>I can verify equation and presentation.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate current and non-current items","html":"<p>A statement of financial position is dated at a point in time. Current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> include cash, receivables and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> expected to be realised in the normal operating cycle; non-current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> include equipment used over several periods.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate closing owner equity","html":"<p>Current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> include suppliers and loan amounts due within the relevant short-term classification. A long loan may have both current and non-current portions. Classification is based on the facts and settlement rights, not whether an account name sounds long term.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Verify equation and presentation","html":"<p>Closing capital is opening capital plus new contributions and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>, less drawings. List <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> with meaningful subtotals and verify <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> = <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> + <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong>. A balance check does not replace a classification review.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain a classified statement of financial position using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-controls","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Internal control across business assets","subtitle":"Match controls to risks · Separate duties and evidence · Recognise control limitations","scope":"Match controls to risks · Separate duties and evidence · Recognise control limitations","outcomes":["Match controls to risks","Separate duties and evidence","Recognise control limitations"],"paragraphs":["Internal control reduces risk of error, fraud and resource loss. Useful principles include authorisation, separation of custody from recording, documentation, physical security and independent checks. A named control must be linked to the particular risk.","Cash controls include daily banking and independent bank reconciliation. Inventory controls include restricted access and stocktakes. Receivables need credit approval and follow-up; payables need invoice matching and authorised payment. Asset registers and physical checks protect equipment.","Controls have costs and limitations: collusion, management override, human error and small staffing can weaken them. A single owner should use compensating independent review where full separation is impractical. Control reduces risk rather than guaranteeing no loss."],"questions":[{"prompt":"One employee receives cash, records receipts and reconciles Bank. Identify a risk and recommend two linked controls.","steps":["The employee could conceal missing cash by altering records.","Separate cash custody from recording or reconciliation.","Use numbered receipts and reconcile daily cash to banking.","An independent owner review compensates if staffing is limited."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-controls-q1"},{"prompt":"Inventory disappears and duplicate supplier invoices are paid. Propose one specific control for each and a limitation.","steps":["Restrict inventory access and compare physical counts with perpetual records.","Match supplier invoices to orders and receipt records; mark paid invoices.","Independent review can identify discrepancies.","Collusion or override can still defeat otherwise sound controls."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-controls-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Proper internal control guarantees no fraud can occur.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Controls reduce risk but remain subject to collusion, override and error.","A risk reduction system must be reviewed and supplemented with evidence.","Example evidence: The employee could conceal missing cash by altering records."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-controls-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a good control system remove every possibility of fraud?","o":["Controls reduce risk but remain subject to collusion, override and error.","Proper internal control guarantees no fraud can occur."],"a":0,"why":"A risk reduction system must be reviewed and supplemented with evidence."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A risk reduction system must be reviewed and supplemented with evidence.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Controls reduce risk but remain subject to collusion, override and error."},{"q":"In the model “Risk-control links”, what belongs to “Cash theft”?","o":["Independent reconciliation and daily banking","Restricted access and stocktake","Document matching and paid-invoice controls"],"a":0,"why":"Cash theft: Independent reconciliation and daily banking. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Risk-control links”, what belongs to “Inventory loss”?","o":["Independent reconciliation and daily banking","Restricted access and stocktake","Document matching and paid-invoice controls"],"a":1,"why":"Inventory loss: Restricted access and stocktake. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Risk-control links","rows":[["Cash theft","Independent reconciliation and daily banking"],["Inventory loss","Restricted access and stocktake"],["Duplicate payment","Document matching and paid-invoice controls"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a good control system remove every possibility of fraud?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can match controls to risks.</li><li>I can separate duties and evidence.</li><li>I can recognise control limitations.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Match controls to risks","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Internal control</strong> reduces risk of error, fraud and resource loss. Useful principles include authorisation, separation of custody from recording, documentation, physical security and independent checks. A named control must be linked to the particular risk.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate duties and evidence","html":"<p>Cash controls include daily banking and independent bank reconciliation. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Inventory</strong> controls include restricted access and stocktakes. Receivables need <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> approval and follow-up; payables need invoice matching and authorised payment. Asset registers and physical checks protect equipment.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recognise control limitations","html":"<p>Controls have costs and limitations: collusion, management override, human error and small staffing can weaken them. A single owner should use compensating independent review where full separation is impractical. Control reduces risk rather than guaranteeing no loss.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain internal control across business assets using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-conventions-law","year":"11","unit":"Unit 1 – Double entry and small business","title":"Conventions, business law and ethical choices","subtitle":"Apply accounting conventions · Explain small-business legal obligations · Evaluate social and environmental decisions","scope":"Apply accounting conventions · Explain small-business legal obligations · Evaluate social and environmental decisions","outcomes":["Apply accounting conventions","Explain small-business legal obligations","Evaluate social and environmental decisions"],"paragraphs":["Entity separates business and owner; monetary measurement records measurable financial effects; historical cost begins with transaction cost. Period divides business life into reporting intervals. Going concern assumes continued operation unless evidence indicates otherwise; materiality asks whether omission or error could influence users.","Business Names Registration Act 2011 regulates registered business names, not creation of a separate legal company. Partnership Act 1895 applies in WA; GST law governs GST obligations, and Bankruptcy Act 1966 concerns personal bankruptcy. Registering a name does not protect an owner from debts.","Ethical records recognise legitimate transactions without hiding income, overstating costs or disguising personal spending. Resource conservation and sponsorship can have measurable costs and wider community benefits. Explain a decision using financial effects, affected stakeholders and evidence rather than assuming every environmental benefit produces immediate profit."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A sole trader records a personal car as business equipment and omits cash sales to reduce tax. Identify two principles and corrective action.","steps":["Entity requires business records to exclude purely personal resources.","Omitting sales misrepresents income and fails ethical/tax responsibilities.","Correct the records using evidence and disclose the errors appropriately.","A registered business name does not make the trader a separate legal company."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-conventions-law-q1"},{"prompt":"A shop spends $2,000 on energy-efficient lighting expected to save $600 annually and sponsors a local team for $500. Evaluate the decisions without assuming all benefits are cash.","steps":["Lighting offers forecast annual savings but requires initial funding.","Simple savings recovery is 2,000 ÷ 600 = about 3.33 years, ignoring timing and other costs.","Sponsorship has a $500 cost and possible community/reputation benefits.","Evaluate evidence, stakeholder effects and uncertainty rather than claiming guaranteed profit."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-conventions-law-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Business-name registration automatically gives a sole trader limited liability.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["A registered name is not a separate legal company.","Legal form determines owner liability; a trading name alone does not change it.","Example evidence: Entity requires business records to exclude purely personal resources."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-conventions-law-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does registering a business name create limited liability?","o":["A registered name is not a separate legal company.","Business-name registration automatically gives a sole trader limited liability."],"a":0,"why":"Legal form determines owner liability; a trading name alone does not change it."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Legal form determines owner liability; a trading name alone does not change it.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"A registered name is not a separate legal company."},{"q":"In the model “Conventions in decisions”, what belongs to “Entity”?","o":["Whose transaction is this?","Which reporting interval?","Could the error influence users?","Is continued operation supported?"],"a":0,"why":"Entity: Whose transaction is this?. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Conventions in decisions”, what belongs to “Period”?","o":["Whose transaction is this?","Which reporting interval?","Could the error influence users?","Is continued operation supported?"],"a":1,"why":"Period: Which reporting interval?. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Conventions in decisions","rows":[["Entity","Whose transaction is this?"],["Period","Which reporting interval?"],["Materiality","Could the error influence users?"],["Going concern","Is continued operation supported?"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does registering a business name create limited liability?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply accounting conventions.</li><li>I can explain small-business legal obligations.</li><li>I can evaluate social and environmental decisions.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply accounting conventions","html":"<p>Entity separates business and owner; monetary measurement records measurable financial effects; historical cost begins with transaction cost. Period divides business life into reporting intervals. Going concern assumes continued operation unless evidence indicates otherwise; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">materiality</strong> asks whether omission or error could influence users.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain small-business legal obligations","html":"<p>Business Names Registration Act 2011 regulates registered business names, not creation of a separate legal company. Partnership Act 1895 applies in WA; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> law governs <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> obligations, and Bankruptcy Act 1966 concerns personal bankruptcy. Registering a name does not protect an owner from debts.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate social and environmental decisions","html":"<p>Ethical records recognise legitimate transactions without hiding income, overstating costs or disguising personal spending. Resource conservation and sponsorship can have measurable costs and wider community benefits. Explain a decision using financial effects, affected stakeholders and evidence rather than assuming every environmental benefit produces immediate <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain conventions, business law and ethical choices using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-accrual","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Cash and accrual accounting","subtitle":"Separate cash timing from earning · Calculate accrual performance · Evaluate electronic payments","scope":"Separate cash timing from earning · Calculate accrual performance · Evaluate electronic payments","outcomes":["Separate cash timing from earning","Calculate accrual performance","Evaluate electronic payments"],"paragraphs":["Cash accounting follows receipts and payments; accrual accounting recognises income when earned and expenses when incurred, with the relevant asset or liability. A credit sale earns revenue before collection; paying next year’s insurance creates a prepayment rather than this year’s entire expense.","Accrual reports can therefore show profit while cash falls. To explain the difference, trace receivables, payables, prepayments and non-cash depreciation. Cash flow remains essential for paying debts even when accrual profit is positive.","EFTPOS, direct debit, online banking and bill payment can improve speed and record trails, but create fees, cyber-security and authorisation risks. Check electronic payments independently and match them to invoices; an electronic record is evidence of payment, not proof of correct expense timing."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Services earned $9,000; receipts $7,000. Expenses incurred $5,000; cash expenses paid $4,000. Find accrual profit and cash surplus.","steps":["Accrual profit = 9,000 − 5,000 = $4,000.","Cash surplus = 7,000 − 4,000 = $3,000.","$2,000 earned revenue remains receivable.","$1,000 incurred expenses remain payable; payment timing explains the difference."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-accrual-q1"},{"prompt":"Earned revenue $12,000; receipts $10,000; incurred expenses $7,000; cash payments $8,000 including $1,000 for next period. Compare profit and cash surplus.","steps":["Profit = 12,000 − 7,000 = $5,000.","Cash surplus = 10,000 − 8,000 = $2,000.","$2,000 revenue remains receivable.","The $1,000 prepayment is a current asset rather than this period’s expense."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-accrual-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Accrual revenue follows earning, while cash reports follow collection.","Revenue can be earned before or after cash is received.","Example evidence: Accrual profit = 9,000 − 5,000 = $4,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-accrual-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does receipt timing always determine earned revenue?","o":["Accrual revenue follows earning, while cash reports follow collection.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit."],"a":0,"why":"Revenue can be earned before or after cash is received."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Revenue can be earned before or after cash is received.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Accrual revenue follows earning, while cash reports follow collection."},{"q":"In the model “Timing comparison”, what belongs to “Earn service now, collect later”?","o":["Revenue + receivable","Cash decreases + prepayment","Expense + reduction of prepayment"],"a":0,"why":"Earn service now, collect later: Revenue + receivable. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Timing comparison”, what belongs to “Pay insurance for future”?","o":["Revenue + receivable","Cash decreases + prepayment","Expense + reduction of prepayment"],"a":1,"why":"Pay insurance for future: Cash decreases + prepayment. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Timing comparison","rows":[["Earn service now, collect later","Revenue + receivable"],["Pay insurance for future","Cash decreases + prepayment"],["Use insurance this period","Expense + reduction of prepayment"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does receipt timing always determine earned revenue?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can separate cash timing from earning.</li><li>I can calculate accrual performance.</li><li>I can evaluate electronic payments.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate cash timing from earning","html":"<p>Cash accounting follows receipts and payments; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">accrual</strong> accounting recognises income when earned and expenses when incurred, with the relevant asset or liability. A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sale earns revenue before collection; paying next year’s insurance creates a prepayment rather than this year’s entire expense.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate accrual performance","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Accrual</strong> reports can therefore show <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> while cash falls. To explain the difference, trace receivables, payables, prepayments and non-cash <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Cash flow</strong> remains essential for paying debts even when <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">accrual</strong> <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> is positive.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate electronic payments","html":"<p>EFTPOS, direct <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong>, online banking and bill payment can improve speed and record trails, but create fees, cyber-security and authorisation risks. Check electronic payments independently and match them to invoices; an electronic record is evidence of payment, not proof of correct expense timing.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain cash and accrual accounting using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-recognition","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Elements and recognition under the Framework","subtitle":"Apply modern element definitions · Distinguish definition from recognition · Explain relevance and faithful representation","scope":"Apply modern element definitions · Distinguish definition from recognition · Explain relevance and faithful representation","outcomes":["Apply modern element definitions","Distinguish definition from recognition","Explain relevance and faithful representation"],"paragraphs":["An asset is a present economic resource controlled because of past events; the resource is a right with the potential to produce economic benefits. A liability is a present obligation to transfer an economic resource arising from past events. Equity is the residual after liabilities.","Income increases assets or decreases liabilities and increases equity apart from owner contributions. Expenses decrease assets or increase liabilities and reduce equity apart from owner distributions. A loan receipt is therefore not income, and a cash drawing is not an expense.","Meeting an element definition does not automatically justify recognition. Recognition should provide relevant information and a faithful representation, with costs and uncertainty considered. Do not substitute an old blanket rule that every item must pass a fixed probability threshold; specific accounting standards govern particular items."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A customer owes $2,000 for completed work. Explain the asset and income and why the invoice supports recognition.","steps":["The enforceable right to payment is a present resource controlled from completed work.","Recognise Accounts receivable $2,000.","The increase in assets increases equity as income, not owner capital.","Invoice and completed-work evidence support relevant and faithfully represented information."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-recognition-q1"},{"prompt":"A business merely hopes to win a future $10,000 contract. Explain why it should not record a receivable now.","steps":["A hope of future business does not establish a present controlled right.","No completed past event creates a customer obligation in this case.","No receivable or revenue is recognised simply for the hoped-for sale.","Recording it would misrepresent current performance and resources."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-recognition-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Any expected future benefit is automatically a recognised asset.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["A present controlled right from past events is needed, not just a forecast.","A forecast can support planning without being a present resource in financial statements.","Example evidence: The enforceable right to payment is a present resource controlled from completed work."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-recognition-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is a hoped-for future sale already an asset?","o":["A present controlled right from past events is needed, not just a forecast.","Any expected future benefit is automatically a recognised asset."],"a":0,"why":"A forecast can support planning without being a present resource in financial statements."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A forecast can support planning without being a present resource in financial statements.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"A present controlled right from past events is needed, not just a forecast."},{"q":"In the model “Recognition reasoning”, what belongs to “Definition”?","o":["Present resource or obligation from past events","Relevant information and faithful representation","Assess uncertainty and applicable standards"],"a":0,"why":"Definition: Present resource or obligation from past events. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Recognition reasoning”, what belongs to “Usefulness”?","o":["Present resource or obligation from past events","Relevant information and faithful representation","Assess uncertainty and applicable standards"],"a":1,"why":"Usefulness: Relevant information and faithful representation. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Recognition reasoning","rows":[["Definition","Present resource or obligation from past events"],["Usefulness","Relevant information and faithful representation"],["Measurement","Assess uncertainty and applicable standards"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is a hoped-for future sale already an asset?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply modern element definitions.</li><li>I can distinguish definition from recognition.</li><li>I can explain relevance and faithful representation.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply modern element definitions","html":"<p>An asset is a present economic resource controlled because of past events; the resource is a right with the potential to produce economic benefits. A liability is a present obligation to transfer an economic resource arising from past events. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Equity</strong> is the residual after <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish definition from recognition","html":"<p>Income increases <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> or decreases <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> and increases <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> apart from owner contributions. Expenses decrease <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> or increase <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> and reduce <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> apart from owner distributions. A loan receipt is therefore not income, and a cash drawing is not an expense.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain relevance and faithful representation","html":"<p>Meeting an element definition does not automatically justify <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">recognition</strong>. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Recognition</strong> should provide relevant information and a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">faithful representation</strong>, with costs and uncertainty considered. Do not substitute an old blanket rule that every item must pass a fixed probability threshold; specific accounting standards govern particular items.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain elements and recognition under the framework using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-expense-class","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Expense classification by nature and function","subtitle":"Distinguish classification methods · Avoid double counting · Explain presentation choices","scope":"Distinguish classification methods · Avoid double counting · Explain presentation choices","outcomes":["Distinguish classification methods","Avoid double counting","Explain presentation choices"],"paragraphs":["Classifying by nature groups what a resource is: wages, depreciation, rent and materials. Classifying by function groups why it was used: production, selling, administration or distribution. The same wage cost can belong to different functions depending on the employee’s work.","An expense must not be counted once by nature and again by function in the same total. Choose a consistent main classification and disclose supporting detail where useful. Function-based reporting may require reasonable cost allocation when an expense supports more than one function.","Compare like categories when interpreting reports. A fall in administration expense may reflect a reclassification rather than a real saving. Explain the allocation basis and use evidence of actual resource consumption before claiming improved efficiency."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Wages are $20,000: $12,000 production staff, $5,000 sales staff and $3,000 office staff. Show both classifications.","steps":["By nature: Wages expense $20,000.","By function: Production $12,000; Selling $5,000; Administration $3,000.","Both classifications total $20,000.","Do not add the nature total to the function totals."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-expense-class-q1"},{"prompt":"Depreciation $6,000 relates 60% to production and 40% to administration. Allocate it and explain a limitation.","steps":["Production depreciation = 6,000 × 60% = $3,600.","Administration depreciation = 6,000 × 40% = $2,400.","The total remains $6,000.","The allocation basis should reflect use; a convenient percentage may distort functional costs."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-expense-class-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Add both classifications together to find total expenses.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["They are alternative ways to present the same consumed resources.","Changing presentation does not double the underlying cost.","Example evidence: By nature: Wages expense $20,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-expense-class-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Do nature and function classifications create two separate expenses?","o":["They are alternative ways to present the same consumed resources.","Add both classifications together to find total expenses."],"a":0,"why":"Changing presentation does not double the underlying cost."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Changing presentation does not double the underlying cost.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"They are alternative ways to present the same consumed resources."},{"q":"In the model “Classification model”, what belongs to “Nature”?","o":["Wages $20,000","$12,000","$5,000","$3,000"],"a":0,"why":"Nature: Wages $20,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Classification model”, what belongs to “Production function”?","o":["Wages $20,000","$12,000","$5,000","$3,000"],"a":1,"why":"Production function: $12,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Classification model","rows":[["Nature","Wages $20,000"],["Production function","$12,000"],["Selling function","$5,000"],["Administration function","$3,000"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Do nature and function classifications create two separate expenses?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can distinguish classification methods.</li><li>I can avoid double counting.</li><li>I can explain presentation choices.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish classification methods","html":"<p>Classifying by nature groups what a resource is: wages, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>, rent and materials. Classifying by function groups why it was used: production, selling, administration or distribution. The same wage cost can belong to different functions depending on the employee’s work.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Avoid double counting","html":"<p>An expense must not be counted once by nature and again by function in the same total. Choose a consistent main classification and disclose supporting detail where useful. Function-based reporting may require reasonable cost allocation when an expense supports more than one function.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain presentation choices","html":"<p>Compare like categories when interpreting reports. A fall in administration expense may reflect a reclassification rather than a real saving. Explain the allocation basis and use evidence of actual resource consumption before claiming improved efficiency.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain expense classification by nature and function using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-accrued-expense","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Accrued expenses","subtitle":"Identify incurred but unpaid costs · Record the adjusting liability · Explain profit and position effects","scope":"Identify incurred but unpaid costs · Record the adjusting liability · Explain profit and position effects","outcomes":["Identify incurred but unpaid costs","Record the adjusting liability","Explain profit and position effects"],"paragraphs":["An accrued expense has been incurred by balance day but has not yet been recorded or paid. The adjustment debits the relevant expense and credits an accrued-expense liability. No Bank entry is made at balance day because no payment has occurred.","Find the amount relating to the reporting period using days, hours or months supplied. Avoid counting an amount already in the ledger a second time. Subsequent payment settles the liability; only any additional current-period expense belongs in the new period.","The unadjusted records overstate profit and equity and understate liabilities. These Unit 2 adjustments exclude GST and use the asset/liability approach specified in the syllabus. Link the adjusting entry to both performance and position, not merely to a memorised rule."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Weekly wages are $2,000 for five working days. Three unpaid working days belong to the current period. Adjust the accounts.","steps":["Accrued wages = 2,000 ÷ 5 × 3 = $1,200.","Debit Wages expense $1,200.","Credit Accrued wages $1,200.","Profit and equity fall $1,200; current liabilities rise $1,200."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-accrued-expense-q1"},{"prompt":"Interest of $400 has been incurred but not recorded or paid at year end. Record the adjustment and later payment.","steps":["Balance day: debit Interest expense $400; credit Accrued interest $400.","Profit decreases $400 and liabilities increase $400.","When paid: debit Accrued interest $400; credit Bank $400.","The same expense must not be counted twice."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-accrued-expense-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Credit Bank for an unpaid balance-day expense.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Credit an accrued liability because the cost is unpaid.","An accrual records the obligation before the cash movement.","Example evidence: Accrued wages = 2,000 ÷ 5 × 3 = $1,200."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-accrued-expense-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does the balance-day accrual credit Bank?","o":["Credit an accrued liability because the cost is unpaid.","Credit Bank for an unpaid balance-day expense."],"a":0,"why":"An accrual records the obligation before the cash movement."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["An accrual records the obligation before the cash movement.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Credit an accrued liability because the cost is unpaid."},{"q":"In the model “Accrual bridge”, what belongs to “Before adjustment”?","o":["Expense and liability understated","Debit Expense; credit Accrued expense","Debit liability; credit Bank"],"a":0,"why":"Before adjustment: Expense and liability understated. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Accrual bridge”, what belongs to “Entry”?","o":["Expense and liability understated","Debit Expense; credit Accrued expense","Debit liability; credit Bank"],"a":1,"why":"Entry: Debit Expense; credit Accrued expense. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Accrual bridge","rows":[["Before adjustment","Expense and liability understated"],["Entry","Debit Expense; credit Accrued expense"],["Payment later","Debit liability; credit Bank"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does the balance-day accrual credit Bank?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can identify incurred but unpaid costs.</li><li>I can record the adjusting liability.</li><li>I can explain profit and position effects.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify incurred but unpaid costs","html":"<p>An accrued expense has been incurred by balance day but has not yet been recorded or paid. The adjustment debits the relevant expense and credits an accrued-expense liability. No Bank entry is made at balance day because no payment has occurred.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record the adjusting liability","html":"<p>Find the amount relating to the reporting period using days, hours or months supplied. Avoid counting an amount already in the ledger a second time. Subsequent payment settles the liability; only any additional current-period expense belongs in the new period.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain profit and position effects","html":"<p>The unadjusted records overstate <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> and understate <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>. These Unit 2 adjustments exclude <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> and use the asset/liability approach specified in the syllabus. Link the adjusting entry to both performance and position, not merely to a memorised rule.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain accrued expenses using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-prepayment","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Prepaid expenses: asset method","subtitle":"Record advance payment as an asset · Expense the portion consumed · Calculate remaining prepayment","scope":"Record advance payment as an asset · Expense the portion consumed · Calculate remaining prepayment","outcomes":["Record advance payment as an asset","Expense the portion consumed","Calculate remaining prepayment"],"paragraphs":["Under the asset method, an advance payment debits Prepaid expense and credits Bank. At balance day the part consumed is transferred from the asset to the expense. The future portion stays as an asset because it represents an unused service right.","Count the coverage period carefully, including the commencement date. For equal monthly benefits, monthly expense equals total payment divided by months covered. State the elapsed and remaining months so another reader can check the calculation.","Failure to adjust leaves assets and profit overstated because the consumed part is still shown as a resource. Unit 2 asks for the asset method, not an alternative expense-method entry. No GST is included in these adjustment examples."],"questions":[{"prompt":"On 1 April pay $2,400 for 12 months’ insurance and debit Prepaid insurance. Adjust at 30 June.","steps":["Monthly insurance = 2,400 ÷ 12 = $200.","Three months consumed = $600.","Debit Insurance expense $600; credit Prepaid insurance $600.","Remaining prepayment = $1,800."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-prepayment-q1"},{"prompt":"Pay $3,600 on 1 January for 12 months’ rent, initially using the asset method. Adjust at 31 March.","steps":["Monthly rent = $300.","Three months’ expense = $900.","Debit Rent expense $900; credit Prepaid rent $900.","Remaining current asset = $2,700."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-prepayment-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Expense the entire advance payment regardless of its coverage period.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Only the consumed portion is transferred to expense at balance day.","The unconsumed service right remains a prepayment asset.","Example evidence: Monthly insurance = 2,400 ÷ 12 = $200."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-prepayment-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Under the asset method, is all prepaid insurance immediately an expense?","o":["Only the consumed portion is transferred to expense at balance day.","Expense the entire advance payment regardless of its coverage period."],"a":0,"why":"The unconsumed service right remains a prepayment asset."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The unconsumed service right remains a prepayment asset.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Only the consumed portion is transferred to expense at balance day."},{"q":"In the model “Coverage timeline”, what belongs to “April–June”?","o":["3 months consumed: $600","9 months remaining: $1,800","Expense consumed part; retain future asset"],"a":0,"why":"April–June: 3 months consumed: $600. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Coverage timeline”, what belongs to “July–March”?","o":["3 months consumed: $600","9 months remaining: $1,800","Expense consumed part; retain future asset"],"a":1,"why":"July–March: 9 months remaining: $1,800. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Coverage timeline","rows":[["April–June","3 months consumed: $600"],["July–March","9 months remaining: $1,800"],["Adjustment","Expense consumed part; retain future asset"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Under the asset method, is all prepaid insurance immediately an expense?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can record advance payment as an asset.</li><li>I can expense the portion consumed.</li><li>I can calculate remaining prepayment.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record advance payment as an asset","html":"<p>Under the asset method, an advance payment debits Prepaid expense and credits Bank. At balance day the part consumed is transferred from the asset to the expense. The future portion stays as an asset because it represents an unused service right.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Expense the portion consumed","html":"<p>Count the coverage period carefully, including the commencement date. For equal monthly benefits, monthly expense equals total payment divided by months covered. State the elapsed and remaining months so another reader can check the calculation.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate remaining prepayment","html":"<p>Failure to adjust leaves <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> overstated because the consumed part is still shown as a resource. Unit 2 asks for the asset method, not an alternative expense-method entry. No <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> is included in these adjustment examples.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain prepaid expenses: asset method using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-supplies","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Stock of supplies used","subtitle":"Reconcile supplies available and remaining · Record consumption · Distinguish supplies from resale inventory","scope":"Reconcile supplies available and remaining · Record consumption · Distinguish supplies from resale inventory","outcomes":["Reconcile supplies available and remaining","Record consumption","Distinguish supplies from resale inventory"],"paragraphs":["Office or cleaning supplies held for use are assets until consumed under the asset method. Supplies are different from inventory acquired for resale, although both require physical records. A stocktake measures what remains at balance day.","Supplies used equals opening supplies plus purchases less closing supplies. Debit Supplies expense and credit Stock of supplies for the consumed amount. Check whether purchases were already recorded in the asset account before making the adjustment.","Missing or wasted supplies also reduce the resource and may require investigation. A stocktake establishes quantity but does not explain every cause of loss. Compare expected use with actual use and document the adjustment."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Opening supplies $300, purchases debited to supplies $900 and closing stocktake $250. Calculate use and adjust.","steps":["Available supplies = 300 + 900 = $1,200.","Consumed = 1,200 − 250 = $950.","Debit Supplies expense $950; credit Stock of supplies $950.","Closing supplies asset is $250."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-supplies-q1"},{"prompt":"Supplies available are $1,500 and closing supplies $400. Find use and explain effects of omitting the adjustment.","steps":["Supplies used = 1,500 − 400 = $1,100.","Debit Supplies expense $1,100; credit Supplies $1,100.","Omitting the adjustment overstates assets by $1,100.","Profit and equity would also be overstated $1,100."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-supplies-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Used supplies equal opening plus purchases plus closing supplies.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Supplies remaining are subtracted from supplies available.","Closing stock is unconsumed and must be retained as an asset.","Example evidence: Available supplies = 300 + 900 = $1,200."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-supplies-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is closing supplies added when calculating consumption?","o":["Supplies remaining are subtracted from supplies available.","Used supplies equal opening plus purchases plus closing supplies."],"a":0,"why":"Closing stock is unconsumed and must be retained as an asset."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Closing stock is unconsumed and must be retained as an asset.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Supplies remaining are subtracted from supplies available."},{"q":"In the model “Supplies reconciliation”, what belongs to “Available”?","o":["Opening $300 + purchases $900","Stocktake $250","$950 expense"],"a":0,"why":"Available: Opening $300 + purchases $900. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Supplies reconciliation”, what belongs to “Remaining”?","o":["Opening $300 + purchases $900","Stocktake $250","$950 expense"],"a":1,"why":"Remaining: Stocktake $250. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Supplies reconciliation","rows":[["Available","Opening $300 + purchases $900"],["Remaining","Stocktake $250"],["Used","$950 expense"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is closing supplies added when calculating consumption?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can reconcile supplies available and remaining.</li><li>I can record consumption.</li><li>I can distinguish supplies from resale inventory.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile supplies available and remaining","html":"<p>Office or cleaning supplies held for use are <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> until consumed under the asset method. Supplies are different from <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> acquired for resale, although both require physical records. A stocktake measures what remains at balance day.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record consumption","html":"<p>Supplies used equals opening supplies plus purchases less closing supplies. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Debit</strong> Supplies expense and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> Stock of supplies for the consumed amount. Check whether purchases were already recorded in the asset account before making the adjustment.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish supplies from resale inventory","html":"<p>Missing or wasted supplies also reduce the resource and may require investigation. A stocktake establishes quantity but does not explain every cause of loss. Compare expected use with actual use and document the adjustment.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain stock of supplies used using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-accrued-income","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Accrued income","subtitle":"Recognise earned but unreceived income · Create a receivable · Avoid double revenue on collection","scope":"Recognise earned but unreceived income · Create a receivable · Avoid double revenue on collection","outcomes":["Recognise earned but unreceived income","Create a receivable","Avoid double revenue on collection"],"paragraphs":["Accrued income has been earned but not yet recorded or received. The adjustment debits an accrued-income asset and credits the relevant income. Interest earned on an investment may accrue between the last receipt and balance day.","Use the task’s time fraction and rate. Match the earning period to the report rather than assuming every interest receipt belongs entirely to that period. No cash moves when the accrual is recorded.","Later receipt debits Bank and credits the accrued-income asset for the already recognised portion. Any newly earned portion is recorded separately as income. Without the balance-day adjustment, assets, income, profit and equity are understated."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A $12,000 deposit earns 6% simple interest annually. Two months’ interest is earned but unpaid at balance day. Adjust.","steps":["Annual interest = 12,000 × 0.06 = $720.","Two months earned = 720 × 2/12 = $120.","Debit Accrued interest income $120; credit Interest income $120.","Assets and profit both increase $120."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-accrued-income-q1"},{"prompt":"Consulting fees $700 are earned by year end but unrecorded. Record the adjustment and later collection.","steps":["Debit Accrued fees/Accounts receivable $700.","Credit Consulting income $700.","Later debit Bank $700; credit the receivable $700.","Collection does not create another $700 income."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-accrued-income-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Accrued income must be credited to a liability.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["It creates a receivable asset and income.","The business has a right to receive value rather than an obligation to provide it.","Example evidence: Annual interest = 12,000 × 0.06 = $720."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-accrued-income-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does an earned-income accrual increase a liability?","o":["It creates a receivable asset and income.","Accrued income must be credited to a liability."],"a":0,"why":"The business has a right to receive value rather than an obligation to provide it."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The business has a right to receive value rather than an obligation to provide it.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"It creates a receivable asset and income."},{"q":"In the model “Earn then collect”, what belongs to “Balance day”?","o":["Debit accrued-income asset; credit income","Debit Bank; credit accrued-income asset","Income is recognised once"],"a":0,"why":"Balance day: Debit accrued-income asset; credit income. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Earn then collect”, what belongs to “Collection”?","o":["Debit accrued-income asset; credit income","Debit Bank; credit accrued-income asset","Income is recognised once"],"a":1,"why":"Collection: Debit Bank; credit accrued-income asset. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Earn then collect","rows":[["Balance day","Debit accrued-income asset; credit income"],["Collection","Debit Bank; credit accrued-income asset"],["Effect","Income is recognised once"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does an earned-income accrual increase a liability?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can recognise earned but unreceived income.</li><li>I can create a receivable.</li><li>I can avoid double revenue on collection.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recognise earned but unreceived income","html":"<p>Accrued income has been earned but not yet recorded or received. The adjustment debits an accrued-income asset and credits the relevant income. Interest earned on an investment may accrue between the last receipt and balance day.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Create a receivable","html":"<p>Use the task’s time fraction and rate. Match the earning period to the report rather than assuming every interest receipt belongs entirely to that period. No cash moves when the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">accrual</strong> is recorded.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Avoid double revenue on collection","html":"<p>Later receipt debits Bank and credits the accrued-income asset for the already recognised portion. Any newly earned portion is recorded separately as income. Without the balance-day adjustment, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>, income, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> are understated.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain accrued income using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-advance-income","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Income received in advance: liability method","subtitle":"Recognise an unearned liability · Transfer the earned portion · Explain refund and service obligations","scope":"Recognise an unearned liability · Transfer the earned portion · Explain refund and service obligations","outcomes":["Recognise an unearned liability","Transfer the earned portion","Explain refund and service obligations"],"paragraphs":["Receiving cash before earning it creates an obligation to provide goods or services. Under the liability method, debit Bank and credit Income received in advance. Cash receipt alone is not evidence that the whole amount has been earned.","At balance day, debit the advance-income liability and credit income for the portion earned. Use the completed service or elapsed earning period supplied. The balance remaining is a liability rather than a negative asset.","Omitting the adjustment leaves the earned portion out of income and overstates the liability. Recording everything as income at receipt has the opposite problem: it recognises unearned revenue too early. Identify which initial method was actually used before adjusting."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Receive $4,800 on 1 May for 12 months’ evenly earned service, recorded as a liability. Adjust at 30 June.","steps":["Monthly earning = 4,800 ÷ 12 = $400.","Two months earned = $800.","Debit Income received in advance $800; credit Service income $800.","Remaining liability = $4,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-advance-income-q1"},{"prompt":"A $3,000 advance is recorded as a liability. By balance day 40% of the promised service is completed. Adjust.","steps":["Earned income = 3,000 × 40% = $1,200.","Debit Income received in advance $1,200.","Credit Service income $1,200.","Remaining liability = $1,800."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-advance-income-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Recognise all customer advances as income immediately.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["The unearned portion remains a liability for future performance.","The business must still deliver the uncompleted service.","Example evidence: Monthly earning = 4,800 ÷ 12 = $400."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-advance-income-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is an advance from a customer always fully earned income?","o":["The unearned portion remains a liability for future performance.","Recognise all customer advances as income immediately."],"a":0,"why":"The business must still deliver the uncompleted service."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The business must still deliver the uncompleted service.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"The unearned portion remains a liability for future performance."},{"q":"In the model “Advance-income split”, what belongs to “Cash received”?","o":["$4,800","$800 income","$4,000 liability"],"a":0,"why":"Cash received: $4,800. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Advance-income split”, what belongs to “Earned by June”?","o":["$4,800","$800 income","$4,000 liability"],"a":1,"why":"Earned by June: $800 income. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Advance-income split","rows":[["Cash received","$4,800"],["Earned by June","$800 income"],["Future service","$4,000 liability"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is an advance from a customer always fully earned income?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can recognise an unearned liability.</li><li>I can transfer the earned portion.</li><li>I can explain refund and service obligations.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recognise an unearned liability","html":"<p>Receiving cash before earning it creates an obligation to provide goods or services. Under the liability method, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> Bank and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> Income received in advance. Cash receipt alone is not evidence that the whole amount has been earned.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Transfer the earned portion","html":"<p>At balance day, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> the advance-income liability and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> income for the portion earned. Use the completed service or elapsed earning period supplied. The balance remaining is a liability rather than a negative asset.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain refund and service obligations","html":"<p>Omitting the adjustment leaves the earned portion out of income and overstates the liability. Recording everything as income at receipt has the opposite problem: it recognises unearned revenue too early. Identify which initial method was actually used before adjusting.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain income received in advance: liability method using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-allowance","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Doubtful debts and the allowance","subtitle":"Estimate collection losses · Adjust to a required closing allowance · Present net receivables","scope":"Estimate collection losses · Adjust to a required closing allowance · Present net receivables","outcomes":["Estimate collection losses","Adjust to a required closing allowance","Present net receivables"],"paragraphs":["An allowance for doubtful debts estimates part of receivables that may not be collected. It is a contra-asset with a normal credit balance, deducted from Accounts receivable. It is not a liability owed to a customer.","The adjustment is the difference between the required closing allowance and the existing balance, not automatically the full required allowance. Increase it by debiting Doubtful debts expense and crediting the allowance; a reduction reverses that direction.","A specific debt written off against an existing allowance debits the allowance and credits Receivables. Use the method specified in the task; distinguish an estimate from an identified irrecoverable debt. The estimate involves judgement and should be supported by collection evidence."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Receivables are $20,000. A 5% allowance is required; the existing allowance is $300 credit. Adjust and report net receivables.","steps":["Required allowance = 20,000 × 5% = $1,000.","Increase needed = 1,000 − 300 = $700.","Debit Doubtful debts expense $700; credit Allowance $700.","Net receivables = 20,000 − 1,000 = $19,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-allowance-q1"},{"prompt":"Required allowance is $800 and existing allowance $1,100 credit. Record the adjustment and its profit effect.","steps":["Required reduction = 1,100 − 800 = $300.","Debit Allowance for doubtful debts $300.","Credit Doubtful debts expense $300.","The expense reduction increases profit $300."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-allowance-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Expense the entire target allowance without checking the opening balance.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Adjust from the existing allowance to the required closing balance.","Ignoring the existing balance double counts part of the estimate.","Example evidence: Required allowance = 20,000 × 5% = $1,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-allowance-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should the full target allowance always be expensed again?","o":["Adjust from the existing allowance to the required closing balance.","Expense the entire target allowance without checking the opening balance."],"a":0,"why":"Ignoring the existing balance double counts part of the estimate."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Ignoring the existing balance double counts part of the estimate.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Adjust from the existing allowance to the required closing balance."},{"q":"In the model “Allowance bridge”, what belongs to “Existing credit”?","o":["$300","$1,000","$700 credit increase","$20,000 − $1,000"],"a":0,"why":"Existing credit: $300. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Allowance bridge”, what belongs to “Required closing credit”?","o":["$300","$1,000","$700 credit increase","$20,000 − $1,000"],"a":1,"why":"Required closing credit: $1,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Allowance bridge","rows":[["Existing credit","$300"],["Required closing credit","$1,000"],["Adjustment","$700 credit increase"],["Net receivables","$20,000 − $1,000"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should the full target allowance always be expensed again?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can estimate collection losses.</li><li>I can adjust to a required closing allowance.</li><li>I can present net receivables.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Estimate collection losses","html":"<p>An allowance for doubtful debts estimates part of receivables that may not be collected. It is a contra-asset with a normal <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> balance, deducted from Accounts receivable. It is not a liability owed to a customer.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Adjust to a required closing allowance","html":"<p>The adjustment is the difference between the required closing allowance and the existing balance, not automatically the full required allowance. Increase it by debiting Doubtful debts expense and crediting the allowance; a reduction reverses that direction.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Present net receivables","html":"<p>A specific debt written off against an existing allowance debits the allowance and credits Receivables. Use the method specified in the task; distinguish an estimate from an identified irrecoverable debt. The estimate involves judgement and should be supported by collection evidence.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain doubtful debts and the allowance using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-asset-cost","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Cost of a depreciable non-current asset","subtitle":"Identify costs of making an asset ready · Separate later operating expenses · Calculate depreciable amount","scope":"Identify costs of making an asset ready · Separate later operating expenses · Calculate depreciable amount","outcomes":["Identify costs of making an asset ready","Separate later operating expenses","Calculate depreciable amount"],"paragraphs":["Asset cost includes purchase price and costs necessary to bring the asset to the location and condition for intended use, such as delivery and installation. Routine running costs after it is ready are expenses. Decide using purpose rather than the supplier’s label.","The depreciable amount is cost less estimated residual value. It is allocated across the asset’s useful life. Residual value estimates the amount recoverable at the end of use; useful life reflects the business’s expected use, not necessarily maximum physical life.","Depreciation is allocation of cost, not a statement that cash has been saved for replacement. These Unit 2 tasks exclude GST. Capitalising an expense overstates assets and current profit, while expensing a genuine asset cost understates them."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Machine price $20,000, delivery $800, installation $1,200 and ordinary post-installation servicing $500. Residual $2,000. Find cost and depreciable amount.","steps":["Capitalised cost = 20,000 + 800 + 1,200 = $22,000.","The $500 routine service is an expense.","Depreciable amount = 22,000 − 2,000 = $20,000.","Allocate the depreciable amount over useful life."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-asset-cost-q1"},{"prompt":"Equipment price $10,000, delivery $400, installation $600; residual $1,000. Find cost and depreciable amount.","steps":["Cost = 10,000 + 400 + 600 = $11,000.","Depreciable amount = 11,000 − 1,000 = $10,000.","Delivery and installation make the asset ready for use.","Later routine repairs do not automatically add to asset cost."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-asset-cost-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Depreciation automatically puts cash aside to replace equipment.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Depreciation allocates asset cost as an expense without itself moving cash.","A separate cash-saving decision is needed to fund future replacement.","Example evidence: Capitalised cost = 20,000 + 800 + 1,200 = $22,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-asset-cost-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does depreciation create a replacement cash fund?","o":["Depreciation allocates asset cost as an expense without itself moving cash.","Depreciation automatically puts cash aside to replace equipment."],"a":0,"why":"A separate cash-saving decision is needed to fund future replacement."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A separate cash-saving decision is needed to fund future replacement.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Depreciation allocates asset cost as an expense without itself moving cash."},{"q":"In the model “Asset-cost build”, what belongs to “Purchase”?","o":["$20,000","Delivery $800 + installation $1,200","$22,000","$20,000 after residual"],"a":0,"why":"Purchase: $20,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Asset-cost build”, what belongs to “Ready-for-use costs”?","o":["$20,000","Delivery $800 + installation $1,200","$22,000","$20,000 after residual"],"a":1,"why":"Ready-for-use costs: Delivery $800 + installation $1,200. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Asset-cost build","rows":[["Purchase","$20,000"],["Ready-for-use costs","Delivery $800 + installation $1,200"],["Cost","$22,000"],["Depreciable amount","$20,000 after residual"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does depreciation create a replacement cash fund?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can identify costs of making an asset ready.</li><li>I can separate later operating expenses.</li><li>I can calculate depreciable amount.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify costs of making an asset ready","html":"<p>Asset cost includes purchase price and costs necessary to bring the asset to the location and condition for intended use, such as delivery and installation. Routine running costs after it is ready are expenses. Decide using purpose rather than the supplier’s label.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate later operating expenses","html":"<p>The depreciable amount is cost less estimated residual value. It is allocated across the asset’s useful life. Residual value estimates the amount recoverable at the end of use; useful life reflects the business’s expected use, not necessarily maximum physical life.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate depreciable amount","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Depreciation</strong> is allocation of cost, not a statement that cash has been saved for replacement. These Unit 2 tasks exclude <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>. Capitalising an expense overstates <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>, while expensing a genuine asset cost understates them.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain cost of a depreciable non-current asset using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-straight-line","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Straight-line depreciation","subtitle":"Calculate annual and part-year expense · Record accumulated depreciation · Determine carrying amount","scope":"Calculate annual and part-year expense · Record accumulated depreciation · Determine carrying amount","outcomes":["Calculate annual and part-year expense","Record accumulated depreciation","Determine carrying amount"],"paragraphs":["Straight-line depreciation allocates the depreciable amount evenly across useful life. Annual expense = (cost − residual value) ÷ useful life. If the asset is available for only part of the year, apply the stated time fraction.","Debit Depreciation expense and credit Accumulated depreciation. The original cost remains in the asset account; accumulated depreciation is a contra-asset. Carrying amount equals cost less accumulated depreciation.","Do not subtract residual value from carrying amount a second time. Residual is already built into the annual allocation. The method fits relatively even consumption of benefits and is an estimate subject to review when life or residual assumptions change."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Equipment costs $18,000, residual $3,000 and useful life five years. It is used for six months this year. Calculate expense and carrying amount.","steps":["Annual depreciation = (18,000 − 3,000) ÷ 5 = $3,000.","Six months expense = $1,500.","Debit Depreciation expense $1,500; credit Accumulated depreciation $1,500.","Carrying amount = 18,000 − 1,500 = $16,500."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-straight-line-q1"},{"prompt":"Asset cost $24,000, residual $4,000, useful life four years; a full year is used. Calculate and record depreciation.","steps":["Annual expense = (24,000 − 4,000) ÷ 4 = $5,000.","Debit Depreciation expense $5,000.","Credit Accumulated depreciation $5,000.","End-of-first-year carrying amount = $19,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-straight-line-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Straight-line uses a fixed percentage of each year’s reduced carrying amount.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Straight-line uses depreciable original cost divided by useful life.","Applying a percentage to reduced carrying amount describes reducing-balance depreciation instead.","Example evidence: Annual depreciation = (18,000 − 3,000) ÷ 5 = $3,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-straight-line-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is straight-line expense calculated from closing carrying amount each year?","o":["Straight-line uses depreciable original cost divided by useful life.","Straight-line uses a fixed percentage of each year’s reduced carrying amount."],"a":0,"why":"Applying a percentage to reduced carrying amount describes reducing-balance depreciation instead."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Applying a percentage to reduced carrying amount describes reducing-balance depreciation instead.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Straight-line uses depreciable original cost divided by useful life."},{"q":"In the model “Straight-line path”, what belongs to “Cost”?","o":["$18,000","$3,000 constant for full years","$1,500","$16,500 after first half year"],"a":0,"why":"Cost: $18,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Straight-line path”, what belongs to “Annual expense”?","o":["$18,000","$3,000 constant for full years","$1,500","$16,500 after first half year"],"a":1,"why":"Annual expense: $3,000 constant for full years. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Straight-line path","rows":[["Cost","$18,000"],["Annual expense","$3,000 constant for full years"],["Half-year expense","$1,500"],["Carrying amount","$16,500 after first half year"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is straight-line expense calculated from closing carrying amount each year?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate annual and part-year expense.</li><li>I can record accumulated depreciation.</li><li>I can determine carrying amount.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate annual and part-year expense","html":"<p>Straight-line <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> allocates the depreciable amount evenly across useful life. Annual expense = (cost − residual value) ÷ useful life. If the asset is available for only part of the year, apply the stated time fraction.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record accumulated depreciation","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Debit</strong> <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Depreciation</strong> expense and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> Accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>. The original cost remains in the asset account; accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> is a contra-asset. Carrying amount equals cost less accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Determine carrying amount","html":"<p>Do not subtract residual value from carrying amount a second time. Residual is already built into the annual allocation. The method fits relatively even consumption of benefits and is an estimate subject to review when life or residual assumptions change.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain straight-line depreciation using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-reducing-balance","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Reducing-balance depreciation","subtitle":"Apply the rate to opening carrying amount · Calculate successive-year expense · Compare allocation patterns","scope":"Apply the rate to opening carrying amount · Calculate successive-year expense · Compare allocation patterns","outcomes":["Apply the rate to opening carrying amount","Calculate successive-year expense","Compare allocation patterns"],"paragraphs":["Reducing-balance depreciation applies the given rate to carrying amount at the start of the depreciation period. Because the base falls, expense normally falls over successive full years. It can match assets that provide more benefits or lose usefulness rapidly early in their life.","First-year carrying amount begins with cost when there is no accumulated depreciation. Later years use cost less all previous accumulated depreciation. Apply the time fraction for a part year if the task requires it.","Residual value remains a lower limit under the stated assumptions; avoid depreciating below it. Choosing a rate is not the same as choosing a useful life. State the supplied method and rate, and never mix a straight-line formula into the reducing-balance calculation."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Equipment costs $20,000 and uses 20% reducing balance for full years. Find depreciation and carrying amount for the first two years.","steps":["Year 1 expense = 20,000 × 20% = $4,000.","Year 1 carrying amount = $16,000.","Year 2 expense = 16,000 × 20% = $3,200.","Year 2 carrying amount = $12,800; accumulated depreciation $7,200."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-reducing-balance-q1"},{"prompt":"Cost $15,000, existing accumulated depreciation $3,000, rate 25%. Find next full-year expense and carrying amount.","steps":["Opening carrying amount = 15,000 − 3,000 = $12,000.","Expense = 12,000 × 25% = $3,000.","Debit Depreciation expense and credit Accumulated depreciation $3,000.","Closing carrying amount = $9,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-reducing-balance-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Always multiply original cost by the reducing-balance rate.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Apply the given rate to the opening carrying amount each period.","The declining base creates the reducing expense pattern.","Example evidence: Year 1 expense = 20,000 × 20% = $4,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-reducing-balance-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Do we apply the reducing-balance rate to original cost every year?","o":["Apply the given rate to the opening carrying amount each period.","Always multiply original cost by the reducing-balance rate."],"a":0,"why":"The declining base creates the reducing expense pattern."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The declining base creates the reducing expense pattern.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Apply the given rate to the opening carrying amount each period."},{"q":"In the model “Reducing-balance path”, what belongs to “Initial cost”?","o":["$20,000","20% × 20,000 = 4,000","20% × 16,000 = 3,200","$12,800"],"a":0,"why":"Initial cost: $20,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Reducing-balance path”, what belongs to “Year 1”?","o":["$20,000","20% × 20,000 = 4,000","20% × 16,000 = 3,200","$12,800"],"a":1,"why":"Year 1: 20% × 20,000 = 4,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Reducing-balance path","rows":[["Initial cost","$20,000"],["Year 1","20% × 20,000 = 4,000"],["Year 2","20% × 16,000 = 3,200"],["Year 2 carrying amount","$12,800"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Do we apply the reducing-balance rate to original cost every year?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply the rate to opening carrying amount.</li><li>I can calculate successive-year expense.</li><li>I can compare allocation patterns.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply the rate to opening carrying amount","html":"<p>Reducing-balance <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> applies the given rate to carrying amount at the start of the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> period. Because the base falls, expense normally falls over successive full years. It can match <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> that provide more benefits or lose usefulness rapidly early in their life.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate successive-year expense","html":"<p>First-year carrying amount begins with cost when there is no accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>. Later years use cost less all previous accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>. Apply the time fraction for a part year if the task requires it.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare allocation patterns","html":"<p>Residual value remains a lower limit under the stated assumptions; avoid depreciating below it. Choosing a rate is not the same as choosing a useful life. State the supplied method and rate, and never mix a straight-line formula into the reducing-balance calculation.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain reducing-balance depreciation using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-depreciation-choice","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Choosing a method and interpreting disposal results","subtitle":"Match allocation to consumption · Explain over- and under-depreciation · Distinguish estimate from market valuation","scope":"Match allocation to consumption · Explain over- and under-depreciation · Distinguish estimate from market valuation","outcomes":["Match allocation to consumption","Explain over- and under-depreciation","Distinguish estimate from market valuation"],"paragraphs":["Straight-line fits relatively even consumption; reducing balance fits higher early consumption. Choose by expected benefit pattern, not by the method producing the most convenient profit. Estimates of life and residual value should be defensible.","If sale proceeds exceed carrying amount, the disposal produces a gain. In a simple classroom interpretation the asset was over-depreciated relative to its sale value; if proceeds are below carrying amount, a loss suggests under-depreciation relative to that value.","A gain or loss does not prove the original estimate was unreasonable, because market conditions or asset condition may change. Update depreciation to the disposal date before comparing carrying amount and proceeds. Depreciation itself is not a market-price forecast."],"questions":[{"prompt":"An asset has cost $10,000 and accumulated depreciation $7,000 at sale. Proceeds are $4,000. Determine gain and interpret depreciation.","steps":["Carrying amount = 10,000 − 7,000 = $3,000.","Gain = 4,000 − 3,000 = $1,000.","The carrying amount is below sale value: over-depreciation relative to realised value.","Changed market conditions may also explain the gain."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-depreciation-choice-q1"},{"prompt":"A vehicle’s carrying amount at disposal is $8,000 and proceeds $6,500. Calculate the result and suggest a suitable method where benefits are greater early.","steps":["Loss = 8,000 − 6,500 = $1,500.","This indicates under-depreciation relative to realised sale value.","Reducing balance may fit greater early consumption.","Method choice needs a benefit-pattern justification."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-depreciation-choice-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Any gain proves the original depreciation estimate was dishonest.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["A gain compares carrying amount with sale value and may reflect changed conditions.","An estimate can be reasonable when made even if the eventual sale result differs.","Example evidence: Carrying amount = 10,000 − 7,000 = $3,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-depreciation-choice-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a gain necessarily prove an incorrect original estimate?","o":["A gain compares carrying amount with sale value and may reflect changed conditions.","Any gain proves the original depreciation estimate was dishonest."],"a":0,"why":"An estimate can be reasonable when made even if the eventual sale result differs."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["An estimate can be reasonable when made even if the eventual sale result differs.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"A gain compares carrying amount with sale value and may reflect changed conditions."},{"q":"In the model “Disposal interpretation”, what belongs to “Proceeds above carrying amount”?","o":["Gain; relatively over-depreciated","Loss; relatively under-depreciated","Update depreciation to sale date"],"a":0,"why":"Proceeds above carrying amount: Gain; relatively over-depreciated. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Disposal interpretation”, what belongs to “Proceeds below carrying amount”?","o":["Gain; relatively over-depreciated","Loss; relatively under-depreciated","Update depreciation to sale date"],"a":1,"why":"Proceeds below carrying amount: Loss; relatively under-depreciated. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Disposal interpretation","rows":[["Proceeds above carrying amount","Gain; relatively over-depreciated"],["Proceeds below carrying amount","Loss; relatively under-depreciated"],["Before comparing","Update depreciation to sale date"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a gain necessarily prove an incorrect original estimate?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can match allocation to consumption.</li><li>I can explain over- and under-depreciation.</li><li>I can distinguish estimate from market valuation.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Match allocation to consumption","html":"<p>Straight-line fits relatively even consumption; reducing balance fits higher early consumption. Choose by expected benefit pattern, not by the method producing the most convenient <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>. Estimates of life and residual value should be defensible.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain over- and under-depreciation","html":"<p>If sale proceeds exceed carrying amount, the disposal produces a gain. In a simple classroom interpretation the asset was over-depreciated relative to its sale value; if proceeds are below carrying amount, a loss suggests under-<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> relative to that value.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish estimate from market valuation","html":"<p>A gain or loss does not prove the original estimate was unreasonable, because market conditions or asset condition may change. Update <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> to the disposal date before comparing carrying amount and proceeds. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Depreciation</strong> itself is not a market-price forecast.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain choosing a method and interpreting disposal results using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-disposal","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Disposal using the sale-of-asset account","subtitle":"Update depreciation before sale · Transfer cost and accumulated depreciation · Recognise gain or loss","scope":"Update depreciation before sale · Transfer cost and accumulated depreciation · Recognise gain or loss","outcomes":["Update depreciation before sale","Transfer cost and accumulated depreciation","Recognise gain or loss"],"paragraphs":["The sale-of-asset method uses a temporary disposal account to compare the asset’s carrying amount with proceeds. First update depreciation to the date of sale. Transfer original cost by debiting Sale of asset and crediting the asset account.","Transfer accumulated depreciation by debiting it and crediting Sale of asset. Record proceeds by debiting Bank and crediting Sale of asset. The remaining disposal balance represents gain or loss and is transferred to the appropriate income or expense account.","Remove both cost and accumulated depreciation so a sold asset is not still reported. Proceeds are cash received, whereas a gain is only proceeds less carrying amount. Unit 2 single-asset disposal examples here exclude GST."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Cost $12,000 and accumulated depreciation $8,000 are current at sale. Proceeds are $5,000. Use the sale-of-asset method.","steps":["Debit Sale of asset $12,000; credit Equipment $12,000.","Debit Accumulated depreciation $8,000; credit Sale of asset $8,000.","Debit Bank $5,000; credit Sale of asset $5,000.","Disposal has $1,000 credit balance: debit Sale of asset $1,000; credit Gain on disposal $1,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-disposal-q1"},{"prompt":"Cost $20,000, accumulated depreciation $11,000 at sale and proceeds $7,000. Prepare disposal transfers.","steps":["Transfer cost: debit Sale of asset $20,000; credit Equipment.","Transfer accumulated depreciation: debit it $11,000; credit Sale of asset.","Debit Bank $7,000; credit Sale of asset $7,000.","Debit Loss on disposal $2,000; credit Sale of asset $2,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-disposal-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “The entire sale proceeds are a disposal gain.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Gain or loss compares proceeds with carrying amount.","Part of the proceeds recovers the asset’s remaining carrying amount.","Example evidence: Debit Sale of asset $12,000; credit Equipment $12,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-disposal-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are sale proceeds themselves the gain on disposal?","o":["Gain or loss compares proceeds with carrying amount.","The entire sale proceeds are a disposal gain."],"a":0,"why":"Part of the proceeds recovers the asset’s remaining carrying amount."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Part of the proceeds recovers the asset’s remaining carrying amount.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Gain or loss compares proceeds with carrying amount."},{"q":"In the model “Disposal account model”, what belongs to “Debit”?","o":["Original cost $12,000","Accumulated depreciation $8,000 + proceeds $5,000","$1,000 gain"],"a":0,"why":"Debit: Original cost $12,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Disposal account model”, what belongs to “Credit”?","o":["Original cost $12,000","Accumulated depreciation $8,000 + proceeds $5,000","$1,000 gain"],"a":1,"why":"Credit: Accumulated depreciation $8,000 + proceeds $5,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Disposal account model","rows":[["Debit","Original cost $12,000"],["Credit","Accumulated depreciation $8,000 + proceeds $5,000"],["Net","$1,000 gain"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are sale proceeds themselves the gain on disposal?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can update depreciation before sale.</li><li>I can transfer cost and accumulated depreciation.</li><li>I can recognise gain or loss.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Update depreciation before sale","html":"<p>The sale-of-asset method uses a temporary disposal account to compare the asset’s carrying amount with proceeds. First update <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> to the date of sale. Transfer original cost by debiting Sale of asset and crediting the asset account.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Transfer cost and accumulated depreciation","html":"<p>Transfer accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> by debiting it and crediting Sale of asset. Record proceeds by debiting Bank and crediting Sale of asset. The remaining disposal balance represents gain or loss and is transferred to the appropriate income or expense account.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recognise gain or loss","html":"<p>Remove both cost and accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> so a sold asset is not still reported. Proceeds are cash received, whereas a gain is only proceeds less carrying amount. Unit 2 single-asset disposal examples here exclude <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain disposal using the sale-of-asset account using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-inventory-methods","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"FIFO and weighted-average inventory concepts","subtitle":"Compare cost allocation assumptions · Calculate a simple illustration · Explain profit and stock effects","scope":"Compare cost allocation assumptions · Calculate a simple illustration · Explain profit and stock effects","outcomes":["Compare cost allocation assumptions","Calculate a simple illustration","Explain profit and stock effects"],"paragraphs":["FIFO assigns the earliest costs to the earliest goods sold, leaving more recent costs in closing inventory. Weighted average blends the relevant costs and units to obtain an average unit cost. These are cost-flow assumptions; actual physical movement may differ.","For a simple illustration with purchases completed before the sale, divide total available cost by total available units for the weighted average. Under a moving-average perpetual system, recalculate after purchases, not after each sale. Do not mix periodic and moving averages without stating the system.","When prices rise, FIFO often gives lower cost of sales and higher closing inventory than average cost in comparable simple cases. The choice changes reported profit but does not create extra cash. Unit 2 emphasises these concepts and consistent application."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Ten units cost $5 each and another ten cost $7 each; then ten units are sold. Compare FIFO and weighted average.","steps":["Available cost = 10×5 + 10×7 = $120 for 20 units.","FIFO cost of sales = 10×5 = $50; closing inventory $70.","Weighted average unit cost = 120 ÷ 20 = $6.","Average cost of sales $60; closing inventory $60."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-inventory-methods-q1"},{"prompt":"Five units cost $8 each and five cost $12 each; then five units sell. Compare the methods and profit effect.","steps":["Total available cost = $100; average cost = $10 per unit.","FIFO cost of sales $40 and closing inventory $60.","Average cost of sales $50 and closing inventory $50.","For equal sales, FIFO profit is $10 higher; no extra cash is created by the method."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-inventory-methods-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “FIFO proves the oldest physical item was delivered to every customer.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["FIFO is a cost-flow assumption that can differ from physical movement.","Cost allocation and physical stock handling are distinct, even when they often align.","Example evidence: Available cost = 10×5 + 10×7 = $120 for 20 units."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-inventory-methods-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is FIFO necessarily a claim about the actual physical movement of each item?","o":["FIFO is a cost-flow assumption that can differ from physical movement.","FIFO proves the oldest physical item was delivered to every customer."],"a":0,"why":"Cost allocation and physical stock handling are distinct, even when they often align."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Cost allocation and physical stock handling are distinct, even when they often align.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"FIFO is a cost-flow assumption that can differ from physical movement."},{"q":"In the model “Two cost layers”, what belongs to “First layer”?","o":["10 units × $5","10 units × $7","First layer $50","10 × $6 = $60"],"a":0,"why":"First layer: 10 units × $5. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Two cost layers”, what belongs to “Second layer”?","o":["10 units × $5","10 units × $7","First layer $50","10 × $6 = $60"],"a":1,"why":"Second layer: 10 units × $7. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Two cost layers","rows":[["First layer","10 units × $5"],["Second layer","10 units × $7"],["FIFO sold cost","First layer $50"],["Average sold cost","10 × $6 = $60"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is FIFO necessarily a claim about the actual physical movement of each item?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare cost allocation assumptions.</li><li>I can calculate a simple illustration.</li><li>I can explain profit and stock effects.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare cost allocation assumptions","html":"<p>FIFO assigns the earliest costs to the earliest goods sold, leaving more recent costs in closing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong>. Weighted average blends the relevant costs and units to obtain an average unit cost. These are cost-flow assumptions; actual physical movement may differ.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate a simple illustration","html":"<p>For a simple illustration with purchases completed before the sale, divide total available cost by total available units for the weighted average. Under a moving-average perpetual system, recalculate after purchases, not after each sale. Do not mix periodic and moving averages without stating the system.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain profit and stock effects","html":"<p>When prices rise, FIFO often gives lower cost of sales and higher closing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> than average cost in comparable simple cases. The choice changes reported <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> but does not create extra cash. Unit 2 emphasises these concepts and consistent application.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain fifo and weighted-average inventory concepts using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-adjusted-trial","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"An adjusted trial balance","subtitle":"Post all adjustments · Check contra-asset and liability balances · Reconcile adjusted debit and credit totals","scope":"Post all adjustments · Check contra-asset and liability balances · Reconcile adjusted debit and credit totals","outcomes":["Post all adjustments","Check contra-asset and liability balances","Reconcile adjusted debit and credit totals"],"paragraphs":["An adjusted trial balance is prepared after balance-day entries have been posted. It includes new accrued assets and liabilities, expense/income changes, prepayment balances and contra-assets. Each adjustment still has equal debit and credit totals.","Do not simply add every adjustment amount to both trial-balance columns: an entry can reduce an existing balance. For example, crediting Prepaid insurance reduces an asset debit while debiting Insurance expense increases another debit; total debits may stay unchanged.","Use a worksheet with unadjusted balances, adjustments and adjusted balances if helpful. Check each final account’s nature and side. Equal totals do not validate the estimates, coverage periods or recognition judgements used."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Prepaid insurance debit $1,200 is reduced by $300 consumed; wages expense debit $2,000 has a $400 accrual. Find adjusted balances.","steps":["Prepaid insurance = $900 debit.","Insurance expense increases $300 debit.","Wages expense = $2,400 debit.","Accrued wages = $400 credit; adjustments each balance."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-adjusted-trial-q1"},{"prompt":"Receivables $10,000 debit and allowance $200 credit require a closing allowance $500. Find the adjustment and adjusted presentation.","steps":["Increase allowance by $300.","Debit Doubtful debts expense $300; credit Allowance $300.","Adjusted allowance is $500 credit.","Receivables remain $10,000 debit; net statement amount is $9,500."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-adjusted-trial-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every adjustment adds its full amount to the trial balance debit total.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Some adjustments transfer value between debit-balance accounts.","A credit to a debit-balance asset may offset a debit to an expense.","Example evidence: Prepaid insurance = $900 debit."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-adjusted-trial-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Must every adjustment increase total debits?","o":["Some adjustments transfer value between debit-balance accounts.","Every adjustment adds its full amount to the trial balance debit total."],"a":0,"why":"A credit to a debit-balance asset may offset a debit to an expense."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A credit to a debit-balance asset may offset a debit to an expense.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Some adjustments transfer value between debit-balance accounts."},{"q":"In the model “Adjustment worksheet”, what belongs to “Unadjusted prepaid asset”?","o":["$1,200 debit","$300","$900 debit","$300 debit"],"a":0,"why":"Unadjusted prepaid asset: $1,200 debit. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Adjustment worksheet”, what belongs to “Credit consumption”?","o":["$1,200 debit","$300","$900 debit","$300 debit"],"a":1,"why":"Credit consumption: $300. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Adjustment worksheet","rows":[["Unadjusted prepaid asset","$1,200 debit"],["Credit consumption","$300"],["Adjusted prepaid asset","$900 debit"],["Expense created","$300 debit"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Must every adjustment increase total debits?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can post all adjustments.</li><li>I can check contra-asset and liability balances.</li><li>I can reconcile adjusted debit and credit totals.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Post all adjustments","html":"<p>An adjusted trial balance is prepared after balance-day entries have been posted. It includes new accrued <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>, expense/income changes, prepayment balances and contra-<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>. Each adjustment still has equal <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> totals.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check contra-asset and liability balances","html":"<p>Do not simply add every adjustment amount to both trial-balance columns: an entry can reduce an existing balance. For example, crediting Prepaid insurance reduces an asset <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> while debiting Insurance expense increases another <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong>; total debits may stay unchanged.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile adjusted debit and credit totals","html":"<p>Use a worksheet with unadjusted balances, adjustments and adjusted balances if helpful. Check each final account’s nature and side. Equal totals do not validate the estimates, coverage periods or <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">recognition</strong> judgements used.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain an adjusted trial balance using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-adjusted-income","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Accrual income statement: integrated case","subtitle":"Combine adjustments with existing balances · Calculate adjusted profit · Exclude owner and financing transactions","scope":"Combine adjustments with existing balances · Calculate adjusted profit · Exclude owner and financing transactions","outcomes":["Combine adjustments with existing balances","Calculate adjusted profit","Exclude owner and financing transactions"],"paragraphs":["Prepare the income statement from adjusted balances, not by inserting adjustment entries as extra unrelated lines. Accrued expenses increase expense; consumed prepayments become expense; accrued income increases income; earned advances move from liabilities to income.","Depreciation and doubtful debts affect profit despite no current cash payment. Avoid subtracting loan principal or drawings as expenses. The adjusted income statement reports period performance while the statement of financial position reports resources and obligations remaining.","Show the calculation bridge for each adjusted figure and clear subtotals. A well-supported profit answer should explain at least one adjustment effect. Completing the numerical total alone does not establish that cash is available to the owner."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Revenue $30,000 includes no accrued fees of $1,000. Wages $12,000 excludes accrual $500. Rent expense is $4,000; depreciation $2,000 is unrecorded. Calculate adjusted profit.","steps":["Adjusted revenue = 30,000 + 1,000 = $31,000.","Adjusted wages = 12,000 + 500 = $12,500.","Total expenses = 12,500 + 4,000 + 2,000 = $18,500.","Adjusted profit = $12,500."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-adjusted-income-q1"},{"prompt":"Service revenue $20,000 needs $600 earned advance added. Expenses $9,000 need supplies used $700 and depreciation $1,500 added. Find profit.","steps":["Adjusted revenue = 20,000 + 600 = $20,600.","Adjusted expenses = 9,000 + 700 + 1,500 = $11,200.","Profit = $9,400.","Earned advance increases income while reducing its liability; it is not a new cash receipt."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-adjusted-income-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Only current cash payments count as accrual expenses.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Accrual profit includes resource consumption such as depreciation.","Non-cash cost allocation is part of measuring the period’s performance.","Example evidence: Adjusted revenue = 30,000 + 1,000 = $31,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-adjusted-income-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is depreciation excluded because no cash was paid this period?","o":["Accrual profit includes resource consumption such as depreciation.","Only current cash payments count as accrual expenses."],"a":0,"why":"Non-cash cost allocation is part of measuring the period’s performance."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Non-cash cost allocation is part of measuring the period’s performance.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Accrual profit includes resource consumption such as depreciation."},{"q":"In the model “Profit adjustment bridge”, what belongs to “Unadjusted income”?","o":["$30,000","Add $1,000","$18,500","$12,500"],"a":0,"why":"Unadjusted income: $30,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Profit adjustment bridge”, what belongs to “Earned but unrecorded”?","o":["$30,000","Add $1,000","$18,500","$12,500"],"a":1,"why":"Earned but unrecorded: Add $1,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Profit adjustment bridge","rows":[["Unadjusted income","$30,000"],["Earned but unrecorded","Add $1,000"],["Adjusted expenses","$18,500"],["Adjusted profit","$12,500"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is depreciation excluded because no cash was paid this period?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can combine adjustments with existing balances.</li><li>I can calculate adjusted profit.</li><li>I can exclude owner and financing transactions.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Combine adjustments with existing balances","html":"<p>Prepare the income statement from adjusted balances, not by inserting adjustment entries as extra unrelated lines. Accrued expenses increase expense; consumed prepayments become expense; accrued income increases income; earned advances move from <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> to income.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate adjusted profit","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Depreciation</strong> and doubtful debts affect <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> despite no current cash payment. Avoid subtracting loan principal or drawings as expenses. The adjusted income statement reports period performance while the statement of financial position reports resources and obligations remaining.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Exclude owner and financing transactions","html":"<p>Show the calculation bridge for each adjusted figure and clear subtotals. A well-supported <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> answer should explain at least one adjustment effect. Completing the numerical total alone does not establish that cash is available to the owner.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain accrual income statement: integrated case using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-adjusted-position","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Accrual financial position: integrated case","subtitle":"Present assets net of allowances · Classify accrual balances · Reconcile adjusted equity","scope":"Present assets net of allowances · Classify accrual balances · Reconcile adjusted equity","outcomes":["Present assets net of allowances","Classify accrual balances","Reconcile adjusted equity"],"paragraphs":["Adjustments change both performance and position. Prepayments and accrued income are assets; accrued expenses and income in advance are liabilities. Present receivables net of doubtful-debt allowance and equipment net of accumulated depreciation.","Closing equity incorporates adjusted profit, not the unadjusted figure. A change in profit must therefore also flow to the equity section after closing. A balance-day expense entry increases a liability or reduces an asset while reducing equity through profit.","Classify remaining balances consistently. Report equipment cost, accumulated depreciation and net carrying amount transparently. Check the equation and review whether every adjustment has both its statement effects represented."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Bank $5,000, receivables $10,000 less allowance $500, prepaid rent $1,000, equipment $20,000 less accumulated depreciation $6,000. Liabilities $8,000 plus accrued wages $1,500. Find assets and equity.","steps":["Net receivables = $9,500; net equipment = $14,000.","Total assets = 5,000 + 9,500 + 1,000 + 14,000 = $29,500.","Liabilities = 8,000 + 1,500 = $9,500.","Equity = $20,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-adjusted-position-q1"},{"prompt":"Before adjustment assets $40,000, liabilities $12,000 and equity $28,000. Record depreciation $2,000 and accrued wages $500, then reconcile.","steps":["Depreciation reduces net assets and equity by $2,000.","Accrued wages increase liabilities $500 and reduce equity $500.","Adjusted assets $38,000; liabilities $12,500; equity $25,500.","Check: 38,000 = 12,500 + 25,500."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-adjusted-position-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Deduct accrued wages from cash even before payment.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["An unpaid incurred wage is a liability.","The adjustment recognises an obligation; Bank stays unchanged until payment.","Example evidence: Net receivables = $9,500; net equipment = $14,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-adjusted-position-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are accrued wages reported as a negative asset?","o":["An unpaid incurred wage is a liability.","Deduct accrued wages from cash even before payment."],"a":0,"why":"The adjustment recognises an obligation; Bank stays unchanged until payment."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The adjustment recognises an obligation; Bank stays unchanged until payment.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"An unpaid incurred wage is a liability."},{"q":"In the model “Adjustment effects”, what belongs to “Depreciation”?","o":["Net assets down; equity down","Liabilities up; equity down","Assets up; equity up","Liabilities down; equity up"],"a":0,"why":"Depreciation: Net assets down; equity down. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Adjustment effects”, what belongs to “Accrued wages”?","o":["Net assets down; equity down","Liabilities up; equity down","Assets up; equity up","Liabilities down; equity up"],"a":1,"why":"Accrued wages: Liabilities up; equity down. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Adjustment effects","rows":[["Depreciation","Net assets down; equity down"],["Accrued wages","Liabilities up; equity down"],["Accrued income","Assets up; equity up"],["Earned advance","Liabilities down; equity up"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are accrued wages reported as a negative asset?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can present assets net of allowances.</li><li>I can classify accrual balances.</li><li>I can reconcile adjusted equity.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Present assets net of allowances","html":"<p>Adjustments change both performance and position. Prepayments and accrued income are <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>; accrued expenses and income in advance are <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>. Present receivables net of doubtful-debt allowance and equipment net of accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Classify accrual balances","html":"<p>Closing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> incorporates adjusted <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>, not the unadjusted figure. A change in <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> must therefore also flow to the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> section after closing. A balance-day expense entry increases a liability or reduces an asset while reducing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> through <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile adjusted equity","html":"<p>Classify remaining balances consistently. Report equipment cost, accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> and net carrying amount transparently. Check the equation and review whether every adjustment has both its statement effects represented.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain accrual financial position: integrated case using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-adjusted-closing","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Closing adjusted accounts and opening the next period","subtitle":"Use adjusted balances in closing · Retain accrual assets and liabilities · Avoid double counting across periods","scope":"Use adjusted balances in closing · Retain accrual assets and liabilities · Avoid double counting across periods","outcomes":["Use adjusted balances in closing","Retain accrual assets and liabilities","Avoid double counting across periods"],"paragraphs":["Close adjusted income and expense balances to Profit and loss summary, then transfer the profit or loss to Capital. Close Drawings directly to Capital. Accrued income, prepayments, income in advance and accrued expenses are permanent assets/liabilities that carry forward.","A next-period receipt or payment may settle an opening accrual. The portion already recognised must reduce that asset or liability rather than repeat the income or expense. Identify the opening balance before analysing a payment covering multiple periods.","A post-closing trial balance contains permanent accounts only. It supports the opening records but does not reverse every balance-day adjustment automatically. Use the stated method and avoid mixing reversing entries into an asset/liability approach without instruction."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Adjusted revenue $15,000, expenses $11,000 and Drawings $1,000. Accrued wages $600 remains unpaid. Explain closing and the next-period payment.","steps":["Adjusted profit = $4,000, transferred from summary to Capital.","Drawings reduce Capital by $1,000.","Accrued wages $600 carries forward as a liability.","Payment later debits Accrued wages $600 and credits Bank $600."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-adjusted-closing-q1"},{"prompt":"Next period a $1,000 wages payment includes opening accrued wages $400 and new-period wages $600. Record it.","steps":["Debit Accrued wages $400.","Debit Wages expense $600.","Credit Bank $1,000.","Only $600 affects new-period profit because $400 was previously recognised."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-adjusted-closing-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Close every accrued asset and liability to zero at year end.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Permanent accrual balances carry forward until settled or consumed.","Closing applies to income, expenses and drawings rather than all balance-sheet accounts.","Example evidence: Adjusted profit = $4,000, transferred from summary to Capital."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-adjusted-closing-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should all accrual assets and liabilities be closed to profit summary?","o":["Permanent accrual balances carry forward until settled or consumed.","Close every accrued asset and liability to zero at year end."],"a":0,"why":"Closing applies to income, expenses and drawings rather than all balance-sheet accounts."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Closing applies to income, expenses and drawings rather than all balance-sheet accounts.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Permanent accrual balances carry forward until settled or consumed."},{"q":"In the model “Across-period settlement”, what belongs to “Prior expense”?","o":["Already recognised $400","Settled by debit $400","Debit $600","Credit $1,000"],"a":0,"why":"Prior expense: Already recognised $400. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Across-period settlement”, what belongs to “Opening liability”?","o":["Already recognised $400","Settled by debit $400","Debit $600","Credit $1,000"],"a":1,"why":"Opening liability: Settled by debit $400. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Across-period settlement","rows":[["Prior expense","Already recognised $400"],["Opening liability","Settled by debit $400"],["New expense","Debit $600"],["Cash paid","Credit $1,000"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should all accrual assets and liabilities be closed to profit summary?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can use adjusted balances in closing.</li><li>I can retain accrual assets and liabilities.</li><li>I can avoid double counting across periods.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use adjusted balances in closing","html":"<p>Close adjusted income and expense balances to <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Profit</strong> and loss summary, then transfer the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> or loss to Capital. Close Drawings directly to Capital. Accrued income, prepayments, income in advance and accrued expenses are permanent <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>/<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> that carry forward.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Retain accrual assets and liabilities","html":"<p>A next-period receipt or payment may settle an opening <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">accrual</strong>. The portion already recognised must reduce that asset or liability rather than repeat the income or expense. Identify the opening balance before analysing a payment covering multiple periods.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Avoid double counting across periods","html":"<p>A post-closing trial balance contains permanent accounts only. It supports the opening records but does not reverse every balance-day adjustment automatically. Use the stated method and avoid mixing reversing entries into an asset/liability approach without instruction.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain closing adjusted accounts and opening the next period using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-software","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Electronic accounting and the chart of accounts","subtitle":"Use account codes consistently · Trace perpetual transactions in software · Validate reports and electronic controls","scope":"Use account codes consistently · Trace perpetual transactions in software · Validate reports and electronic controls","outcomes":["Use account codes consistently","Trace perpetual transactions in software","Validate reports and electronic controls"],"paragraphs":["A chart of accounts assigns codes and types to accounts so software can process and classify entries. Choosing the wrong account type can misstate reports even when the software balances debits and credits automatically. Software does not decide whether a transaction is genuine.","Use the established chart and perpetual inventory setup. Record source documents, dates, customer/supplier, amount and any applicable tax code, then inspect both ledger effects. These classroom adjustment examples exclude GST; tax codes in real software must match the actual transaction.","Review trial balance, income statement and financial position outputs. Restrict access, preserve audit logs, back up records and independently authorise electronic payments. This lesson simulates processing principles; a real MYOB or QuickBooks interface can differ."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Chart codes: 100 Bank, 120 Inventory, 200 Payables, 400 Sales, 500 Cost of sales. Record a non-GST credit sale $700 of goods costing $400.","steps":["Debit Accounts receivable $700 and credit 400 Sales $700; add the receivable code from the chart.","Debit 500 Cost of sales $400.","Credit 120 Inventory $400.","Check receivable, inventory and profit reports; gross profit is $300."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-software-q1"},{"prompt":"An operator records equipment $2,000 to Advertising expense. Explain why software may accept it and how to detect and correct it.","steps":["Equal debits and credits can allow an incorrectly classified transaction.","Compare the report with the source invoice and chart type.","Debit Equipment $2,000; credit Advertising expense $2,000, assuming Bank was correct.","Review access, audit log and report validation controls."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-software-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “An entry accepted by software is necessarily correct.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Balanced software entries can still use the wrong account classification.","Source review and report checks are needed even with automated posting.","Example evidence: Debit Accounts receivable $700 and credit 400 Sales $700; add the receivable code from the chart."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-software-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does software acceptance prove the right account was chosen?","o":["Balanced software entries can still use the wrong account classification.","An entry accepted by software is necessarily correct."],"a":0,"why":"Source review and report checks are needed even with automated posting."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Source review and report checks are needed even with automated posting.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Balanced software entries can still use the wrong account classification."},{"q":"In the model “Electronic processing checks”, what belongs to “Input”?","o":["Document, date, party, amount and code","Both ledger effects and inventory movement","Trial balance and classified reports","Authorisation, access and backup"],"a":0,"why":"Input: Document, date, party, amount and code. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Electronic processing checks”, what belongs to “Posting”?","o":["Document, date, party, amount and code","Both ledger effects and inventory movement","Trial balance and classified reports","Authorisation, access and backup"],"a":1,"why":"Posting: Both ledger effects and inventory movement. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Electronic processing checks","rows":[["Input","Document, date, party, amount and code"],["Posting","Both ledger effects and inventory movement"],["Output","Trial balance and classified reports"],["Control","Authorisation, access and backup"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does software acceptance prove the right account was chosen?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can use account codes consistently.</li><li>I can trace perpetual transactions in software.</li><li>I can validate reports and electronic controls.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use account codes consistently","html":"<p>A chart of accounts assigns codes and types to accounts so software can process and classify entries. Choosing the wrong account type can misstate reports even when the software balances debits and credits automatically. Software does not decide whether a transaction is genuine.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Trace perpetual transactions in software","html":"<p>Use the established chart and perpetual <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> setup. Record source documents, dates, customer/supplier, amount and any applicable tax code, then inspect both ledger effects. These classroom adjustment examples exclude <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong>; tax codes in real software must match the actual transaction.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Validate reports and electronic controls","html":"<p>Review trial balance, income statement and financial position outputs. Restrict access, preserve audit logs, back up records and independently authorise electronic payments. This lesson simulates processing principles; a real MYOB or QuickBooks interface can differ.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain electronic accounting and the chart of accounts using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-profit-ratios","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Year 11 profitability ratios","subtitle":"Calculate profit, gross profit and expense ratios · Use average assets for return · Interpret changes with evidence","scope":"Calculate profit, gross profit and expense ratios · Use average assets for return · Interpret changes with evidence","outcomes":["Calculate profit, gross profit and expense ratios","Use average assets for return","Interpret changes with evidence"],"paragraphs":["For Year 11, profit ratio = profit ÷ net sales, gross profit ratio = gross profit ÷ net sales, and expense ratio = operating expenses ÷ net sales. Multiply by 100 for percentages. A ratio must name its numerator, denominator and period.","Year 11 return on assets uses profit divided by average total assets; average assets are usually opening plus closing divided by two. This differs from the Year 12 specification’s pre-tax-plus-interest numerator. State the year-level convention so the formula is not silently changed.","A higher gross margin may reflect prices, sales mix or purchase costs. A lower expense ratio may reflect genuine efficiency or merely rising sales. Compare trends and business context; no single percentage proves an overall better business."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Net sales $100,000, gross profit $40,000, operating expenses $25,000, profit $15,000; assets open $80,000 and close $120,000. Calculate four Year 11 ratios.","steps":["Profit ratio = 15,000 ÷ 100,000 × 100 = 15%.","Gross profit ratio = 40%.","Expense ratio = 25%.","Average assets $100,000; Year 11 return = 15,000 ÷ 100,000 × 100 = 15%."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-profit-ratios-q1"},{"prompt":"Net sales $80,000, gross profit $28,000, expenses $20,000, profit $8,000; average assets $50,000. Calculate and explain profit and return.","steps":["Profit ratio = 8,000 ÷ 80,000 × 100 = 10%.","Gross profit ratio 35%; expense ratio 25%.","Year 11 return on assets = 8,000 ÷ 50,000 × 100 = 16%.","Profit per sales dollar and profit per asset dollar answer different questions."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-profit-ratios-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Silently use the Year 12 numerator in every Year 11 ratio task.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Use the specified Year 11 profit/average-assets convention here.","The syllabus and specification distinguish the conventions; identify the applicable one.","Example evidence: Profit ratio = 15,000 ÷ 100,000 × 100 = 15%."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-profit-ratios-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Do Year 11 and Year 12 use identical return-on-assets conventions?","o":["Use the specified Year 11 profit/average-assets convention here.","Silently use the Year 12 numerator in every Year 11 ratio task."],"a":0,"why":"The syllabus and specification distinguish the conventions; identify the applicable one."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The syllabus and specification distinguish the conventions; identify the applicable one.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Use the specified Year 11 profit/average-assets convention here."},{"q":"In the model “Year 11 ratio map”, what belongs to “Profit margin”?","o":["Profit / net sales","Gross profit / net sales","Operating expenses / net sales","Profit / average assets"],"a":0,"why":"Profit margin: Profit / net sales. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Year 11 ratio map”, what belongs to “Gross margin”?","o":["Profit / net sales","Gross profit / net sales","Operating expenses / net sales","Profit / average assets"],"a":1,"why":"Gross margin: Gross profit / net sales. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Year 11 ratio map","rows":[["Profit margin","Profit / net sales"],["Gross margin","Gross profit / net sales"],["Expense ratio","Operating expenses / net sales"],["Return on assets","Profit / average assets"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Do Year 11 and Year 12 use identical return-on-assets conventions?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate profit, gross profit and expense ratios.</li><li>I can use average assets for return.</li><li>I can interpret changes with evidence.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate profit, gross profit and expense ratios","html":"<p>For Year 11, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> ratio = <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> ÷ net sales, gross <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> ratio = gross <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> ÷ net sales, and expense ratio = operating expenses ÷ net sales. Multiply by 100 for percentages. A ratio must name its numerator, denominator and period.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use average assets for return","html":"<p>Year 11 return on <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> uses <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> divided by average total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>; average <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> are usually opening plus closing divided by two. This differs from the Year 12 specification’s pre-tax-plus-interest numerator. State the year-level convention so the formula is not silently changed.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret changes with evidence","html":"<p>A higher gross margin may reflect prices, sales mix or purchase costs. A lower expense ratio may reflect genuine efficiency or merely rising sales. Compare trends and business context; no single percentage proves an overall better business.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain year 11 profitability ratios using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-liquidity-leverage","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Year 11 liquidity and leverage ratios","subtitle":"Calculate current and quick ratios · Apply the specified overdraft exclusion · Interpret debt to equity","scope":"Calculate current and quick ratios · Apply the specified overdraft exclusion · Interpret debt to equity","outcomes":["Calculate current and quick ratios","Apply the specified overdraft exclusion","Interpret debt to equity"],"paragraphs":["Current/working-capital ratio is current assets divided by current liabilities, expressed as x:1. Quick asset ratio excludes inventory and prepayments from current assets and, under the SCSA convention, excludes bank overdraft from current liabilities. Show these adjusted totals before dividing.","Debt to equity compares total liabilities with closing equity and can be shown as a percentage. Higher leverage can increase financial risk and repayment pressure but must be interpreted with earnings stability and financing terms. Liquidity concerns near-term payment capacity rather than profitability.","Ratios are snapshots. A current ratio supported by slow inventory or doubtful receivables may be less reassuring than the number suggests. Consider timing, asset quality and trends; do not claim a universal ideal applies to all businesses."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Current assets $30,000 include inventory $12,000 and prepayments $3,000. Current liabilities $15,000 include overdraft $5,000. Total liabilities $40,000, equity $50,000. Calculate ratios.","steps":["Current ratio = 30,000 ÷ 15,000 = 2.00:1.","Quick assets = 30,000 − 12,000 − 3,000 = $15,000.","Quick liabilities = 15,000 − 5,000 = $10,000; quick ratio 1.50:1.","Debt/equity = 40,000 ÷ 50,000 × 100 = 80%."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-liquidity-leverage-q1"},{"prompt":"Current assets $24,000 include inventory $8,000 and prepayments $2,000. Current liabilities $12,000 include overdraft $2,000. Calculate current and quick ratios.","steps":["Current ratio = 24,000 ÷ 12,000 = 2.00:1.","Quick assets = 24,000 − 8,000 − 2,000 = $14,000.","Quick liabilities = 12,000 − 2,000 = $10,000.","Quick ratio = 1.40:1; interpret asset quality and timing too."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-liquidity-leverage-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Use all current assets and liabilities unchanged in the quick ratio.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Exclude inventory and prepayments, and exclude overdraft from the liability denominator.","The quick ratio uses a narrower liquidity measure with the specified SCSA adjustments.","Example evidence: Current ratio = 30,000 ÷ 15,000 = 2.00:1."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-liquidity-leverage-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does the SCSA quick ratio include inventory in quick assets?","o":["Exclude inventory and prepayments, and exclude overdraft from the liability denominator.","Use all current assets and liabilities unchanged in the quick ratio."],"a":0,"why":"The quick ratio uses a narrower liquidity measure with the specified SCSA adjustments."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The quick ratio uses a narrower liquidity measure with the specified SCSA adjustments.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Exclude inventory and prepayments, and exclude overdraft from the liability denominator."},{"q":"In the model “Quick-ratio bridge”, what belongs to “Current assets”?","o":["$30,000","$15,000 quick assets","$10,000","1.50:1"],"a":0,"why":"Current assets: $30,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Quick-ratio bridge”, what belongs to “Less inventory/prepayments”?","o":["$30,000","$15,000 quick assets","$10,000","1.50:1"],"a":1,"why":"Less inventory/prepayments: $15,000 quick assets. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Quick-ratio bridge","rows":[["Current assets","$30,000"],["Less inventory/prepayments","$15,000 quick assets"],["Current liabilities less overdraft","$10,000"],["Quick ratio","1.50:1"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does the SCSA quick ratio include inventory in quick assets?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate current and quick ratios.</li><li>I can apply the specified overdraft exclusion.</li><li>I can interpret debt to equity.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate current and quick ratios","html":"<p>Current/working-capital ratio is current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> divided by current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>, expressed as x:1. Quick asset ratio excludes <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> and prepayments from current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and, under the SCSA convention, excludes bank overdraft from current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>. Show these adjusted totals before dividing.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply the specified overdraft exclusion","html":"<p>Debt to <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> compares total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> with closing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> and can be shown as a percentage. Higher leverage can increase financial risk and repayment pressure but must be interpreted with earnings stability and financing terms. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Liquidity</strong> concerns near-term payment capacity rather than profitability.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret debt to equity","html":"<p>Ratios are snapshots. A current ratio supported by slow <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> or doubtful receivables may be less reassuring than the number suggests. Consider timing, asset quality and trends; do not claim a universal ideal applies to all businesses.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain year 11 liquidity and leverage ratios using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-analysis","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Comparing performance and recommending action","subtitle":"Link ratio changes to causes · Distinguish liquidity from profit · Write evidence-based recommendations","scope":"Link ratio changes to causes · Distinguish liquidity from profit · Write evidence-based recommendations","outcomes":["Link ratio changes to causes","Distinguish liquidity from profit","Write evidence-based recommendations"],"paragraphs":["A strong analysis states the change, gives a plausible cause linked to case evidence, explains the effect and recommends a practical response. A ratio description alone is not a recommendation. Use more than one measure when assessing profitability and stability.","Increasing sales alongside falling gross margin may mean price discounting or rising purchase costs. Rising profit with deteriorating liquidity may mean slow collection or inventory accumulation. These are hypotheses until supported by financial and non-financial evidence.","Recommendations should address the cause: review prices and suppliers for margin pressure, tighten credit follow-up for slow receipts, or review inventory levels. Explain a risk or trade-off, such as losing customers through overly strict credit limits."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Gross margin falls from 40% to 32%, while current ratio rises from 1.5 to 2.0 because inventory grows. Interpret and recommend.","steps":["Gross profit per sales dollar falls by 8 percentage points.","Higher inventory can inflate current assets without immediate cash availability.","Review purchase costs/prices and slow stock lines.","Reduce excess stock while protecting availability of fast-selling goods."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-analysis-q1"},{"prompt":"Profit ratio improves from 8% to 12%, but overdue receivables double. Give a balanced judgement and action.","steps":["Profitability per sales dollar improved by 4 percentage points.","Overdue debts create collection risk and may weaken cash liquidity.","Use aged receivable follow-up, credit review and allowance assessment.","Do not claim higher profit alone proves debt-paying capacity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-analysis-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “A higher ratio always proves management efficiency without further evidence.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Interpret the ratio using case evidence and alternative explanations.","Changes can arise from prices, mix, timing or classification as well as efficiency.","Example evidence: Gross profit per sales dollar falls by 8 percentage points."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-analysis-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does an improved ratio alone establish the cause of improvement?","o":["Interpret the ratio using case evidence and alternative explanations.","A higher ratio always proves management efficiency without further evidence."],"a":0,"why":"Changes can arise from prices, mix, timing or classification as well as efficiency."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Changes can arise from prices, mix, timing or classification as well as efficiency.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Interpret the ratio using case evidence and alternative explanations."},{"q":"In the model “Analysis chain”, what belongs to “Evidence”?","o":["Ratio change plus supporting case fact","Performance or payment-capacity consequence","Specific response and trade-off"],"a":0,"why":"Evidence: Ratio change plus supporting case fact. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Analysis chain”, what belongs to “Effect”?","o":["Ratio change plus supporting case fact","Performance or payment-capacity consequence","Specific response and trade-off"],"a":1,"why":"Effect: Performance or payment-capacity consequence. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Analysis chain","rows":[["Evidence","Ratio change plus supporting case fact"],["Effect","Performance or payment-capacity consequence"],["Action","Specific response and trade-off"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does an improved ratio alone establish the cause of improvement?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can link ratio changes to causes.</li><li>I can distinguish liquidity from profit.</li><li>I can write evidence-based recommendations.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Link ratio changes to causes","html":"<p>A strong analysis states the change, gives a plausible cause linked to case evidence, explains the effect and recommends a practical response. A ratio description alone is not a recommendation. Use more than one measure when assessing profitability and stability.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish liquidity from profit","html":"<p>Increasing sales alongside falling gross margin may mean price discounting or rising purchase costs. Rising <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> with deteriorating <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liquidity</strong> may mean slow collection or <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> accumulation. These are hypotheses until supported by financial and non-financial evidence.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Write evidence-based recommendations","html":"<p>Recommendations should address the cause: review prices and suppliers for margin pressure, tighten <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> follow-up for slow receipts, or review <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> levels. Explain a risk or trade-off, such as losing customers through overly strict <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> limits.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain comparing performance and recommending action using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-professional","year":"11","unit":"Unit 2 – Accrual accounting and analysis","title":"Professional bodies, codes and ethical judgement","subtitle":"Explain professional association roles · Apply ethical principles · Distinguish syllabus terminology from current organisations","scope":"Explain professional association roles · Apply ethical principles · Distinguish syllabus terminology from current organisations","outcomes":["Explain professional association roles","Apply ethical principles","Distinguish syllabus terminology from current organisations"],"paragraphs":["CPA Australia, Chartered Accountants Australia and New Zealand, and the Institute of Public Accountants support professional education, standards and member development. The Year 11 syllabus also names the Financial Planning Association; explain its historical role and that FPA merged with AFA to form FAAA in 2023.","Professional codes promote integrity, objectivity, competence and due care, confidentiality and professional behaviour. Apply them to a case: accepting a supplier gift may threaten objectivity; sharing customer records may breach confidentiality. Naming a principle is only the first step.","Identify the threat, affected stakeholders and a proportionate response such as disclosure, independent review, refusing the gift or correcting records. Confidentiality is not a licence to conceal unlawful conduct; use authorised reporting channels and professional requirements."],"questions":[{"prompt":"An accountant is offered a gift by a supplier whose invoice dispute they are reviewing. Explain the threat and response.","steps":["The gift creates a self-interest threat to objectivity.","The decision could favour the supplier over the business.","Decline or appropriately disclose the gift and arrange independent review.","Document the response and apply the relevant professional code."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-professional-q1"},{"prompt":"A staff member wants to send client financial data to a friend for informal help. Evaluate the request.","steps":["Client records are confidential and may contain sensitive information.","Do not share them without proper authority and controls.","Seek authorised professional support with appropriate confidentiality arrangements.","Competence and due care require reliable review rather than uncontrolled disclosure."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-professional-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Present FPA as a current independent association without any merger context.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["The syllabus name is explained alongside the 2023 merger into FAAA.","Teaching should preserve syllabus terminology while explaining the current professional organisation.","Example evidence: The gift creates a self-interest threat to objectivity."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-professional-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is FPA still accurately presented as a separate current body in this lesson?","o":["The syllabus name is explained alongside the 2023 merger into FAAA.","Present FPA as a current independent association without any merger context."],"a":0,"why":"Teaching should preserve syllabus terminology while explaining the current professional organisation."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Teaching should preserve syllabus terminology while explaining the current professional organisation.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"The syllabus name is explained alongside the 2023 merger into FAAA."},{"q":"In the model “Ethical response”, what belongs to “Threat”?","o":["Self-interest, bias or confidentiality risk","Integrity, objectivity, competence or confidentiality","Disclose, safeguard, refuse or correct"],"a":0,"why":"Threat: Self-interest, bias or confidentiality risk. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Ethical response”, what belongs to “Principle”?","o":["Self-interest, bias or confidentiality risk","Integrity, objectivity, competence or confidentiality","Disclose, safeguard, refuse or correct"],"a":1,"why":"Principle: Integrity, objectivity, competence or confidentiality. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Ethical response","rows":[["Threat","Self-interest, bias or confidentiality risk"],["Principle","Integrity, objectivity, competence or confidentiality"],["Action","Disclose, safeguard, refuse or correct"]]},"icon":"📒","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is FPA still accurately presented as a separate current body in this lesson?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can explain professional association roles.</li><li>I can apply ethical principles.</li><li>I can distinguish syllabus terminology from current organisations.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain professional association roles","html":"<p>CPA Australia, Chartered Accountants Australia and New Zealand, and the Institute of Public Accountants support professional education, standards and member development. The Year 11 syllabus also names the Financial Planning Association; explain its historical role and that FPA merged with AFA to form FAAA in 2023.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply ethical principles","html":"<p>Professional codes promote integrity, objectivity, competence and due care, confidentiality and professional behaviour. Apply them to a case: accepting a supplier gift may threaten objectivity; sharing customer records may breach confidentiality. Naming a principle is only the first step.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish syllabus terminology from current organisations","html":"<p>Identify the threat, affected stakeholders and a proportionate response such as disclosure, independent review, refusing the gift or correcting records. Confidentiality is not a licence to conceal unlawful conduct; use authorised reporting channels and professional requirements.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain professional bodies, codes and ethical judgement using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-funding-assets","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Financial products and asset management","subtitle":"Compare short- and long-term financial products · Match asset investment with finance · Control working capital","scope":"Compare short- and long-term financial products · Match asset investment with finance · Control working capital","outcomes":["Compare short- and long-term financial products","Match asset investment with finance","Control working capital"],"paragraphs":["Cash management accounts and money-market products can support short-term liquidity; term deposits lock funds for an agreed term. Shares provide ownership returns with market risk; debentures and loans create debt obligations. Secured loans expose pledged assets if repayments fail.","Asset management balances return, risk and liquidity. Excess inventory ties up funds; overly little can lose sales. Receivables need collection policies, cash needs forecasts and non-current assets need adequate utilisation. A high asset value alone does not show productive investment.","Match debt maturity with the asset and cash forecast, while maintaining an appropriate equity buffer. Compare rates, fees, access conditions and risk using supplied case data. Year 12 management examples exclude GST and do not assume that every financial institution offers every product in the same form."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A firm has $20,000 cash needed in three weeks and considers a six-month locked deposit earning $500. Evaluate it and suggest an alternative.","steps":["The maturity does not match the near-term cash need.","The interest return does not justify inability to meet obligations.","A liquid cash-management option may better fit the timing.","Compare access restrictions, risk and fees as well as the quoted return."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-funding-assets-q1"},{"prompt":"A business finances a five-year machine entirely with short-term debt and holds slow inventory. Identify two risks and actions.","steps":["Short debt creates refinancing/payment pressure before the machine generates all benefits.","Match a suitable portion of finance to the asset life and assess equity capital.","Slow inventory ties up cash and risks obsolescence.","Review stock levels while protecting reliable supply."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-funding-assets-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Always choose the product with the highest quoted return.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Evaluate liquidity, maturity, risk and cost alongside return.","A profitable investment can still be unsuitable when funds are needed sooner.","Example evidence: The maturity does not match the near-term cash need."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-funding-assets-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is highest quoted return enough to choose a financial product?","o":["Evaluate liquidity, maturity, risk and cost alongside return.","Always choose the product with the highest quoted return."],"a":0,"why":"A profitable investment can still be unsuitable when funds are needed sooner."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A profitable investment can still be unsuitable when funds are needed sooner.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Evaluate liquidity, maturity, risk and cost alongside return."},{"q":"In the model “Asset-finance matching”, what belongs to “Cash need soon”?","o":["Liquid short-term funds","Finance with suitable maturity","Absorbs risk without fixed debt payments"],"a":0,"why":"Cash need soon: Liquid short-term funds. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Asset-finance matching”, what belongs to “Long-life asset”?","o":["Liquid short-term funds","Finance with suitable maturity","Absorbs risk without fixed debt payments"],"a":1,"why":"Long-life asset: Finance with suitable maturity. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Asset-finance matching","rows":[["Cash need soon","Liquid short-term funds"],["Long-life asset","Finance with suitable maturity"],["Equity buffer","Absorbs risk without fixed debt payments"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is highest quoted return enough to choose a financial product?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare short- and long-term financial products.</li><li>I can match asset investment with finance.</li><li>I can control working capital.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare short- and long-term financial products","html":"<p>Cash management accounts and money-market products can support short-term <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liquidity</strong>; term deposits lock funds for an agreed term. Shares provide ownership returns with market risk; debentures and loans create debt obligations. Secured loans expose pledged <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> if repayments fail.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Match asset investment with finance","html":"<p>Asset management balances return, risk and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liquidity</strong>. Excess <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> ties up funds; overly little can lose sales. Receivables need collection policies, cash needs forecasts and non-current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> need adequate utilisation. A high asset value alone does not show productive investment.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Control working capital","html":"<p>Match debt maturity with the asset and cash forecast, while maintaining an appropriate <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> buffer. Compare rates, fees, access conditions and risk using supplied case data. Year 12 management examples exclude <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> and do not assume that every financial institution offers every product in the same form.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain financial products and asset management using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-management-reporting","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Management accounting, financial accounting and internal audit","subtitle":"Compare users and report purposes · Explain accountant and internal-audit roles · Link planning and control","scope":"Compare users and report purposes · Explain accountant and internal-audit roles · Link planning and control","outcomes":["Compare users and report purposes","Explain accountant and internal-audit roles","Link planning and control"],"paragraphs":["Management accounting serves internal planning, pricing and control with forecasts and flexible reports. Financial accounting communicates general-purpose performance and position to external users under reporting requirements. Both use financial records, but their purposes and time orientation differ.","The accountant translates transactions into decision information, prepares budgets, analyses costs and monitors results. Cost leadership seeks efficient low costs; differentiation offers distinct value. A strategy needs measurable goals and resource plans rather than a slogan.","Internal audit reviews procedures, policies and controls, detects weaknesses and recommends corrections. It differs from external audit of reported financial information. Independence within the organisation and follow-up of findings matter; a review without correction does not improve control."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A manager requests next month’s product cost forecast; an investor requests the annual statements. Classify each and explain regulation differences.","steps":["Product forecast is management accounting for internal decisions.","Annual statements are financial accounting for external users.","Internal forecasts can use flexible formats suited to the decision.","General-purpose external reports follow applicable standards and legal requirements."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-management-reporting-q1"},{"prompt":"An internal audit finds duplicate payments and missing approval records. Explain the accountant’s response.","steps":["Investigate and correct duplicate records/payments with evidence.","Require invoice matching and independent payment authorisation.","Review procedures and train staff on the policy.","Monitor subsequent exceptions so the control change is effective."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-management-reporting-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “All management reports must use the exact external financial-statement format.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Internal reports can be tailored to the decision and user.","Internal decision needs differ from regulated general-purpose reporting.","Example evidence: Product forecast is management accounting for internal decisions."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-management-reporting-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are internal reports required to copy annual-report formats exactly?","o":["Internal reports can be tailored to the decision and user.","All management reports must use the exact external financial-statement format."],"a":0,"why":"Internal decision needs differ from regulated general-purpose reporting."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Internal decision needs differ from regulated general-purpose reporting.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Internal reports can be tailored to the decision and user."},{"q":"In the model “Plan–control cycle”, what belongs to “Plan”?","o":["Goals, strategy and budgets","Record costs and resource use","Performance reports and internal audit","Correct weaknesses and revise plans"],"a":0,"why":"Plan: Goals, strategy and budgets. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Plan–control cycle”, what belongs to “Operate”?","o":["Goals, strategy and budgets","Record costs and resource use","Performance reports and internal audit","Correct weaknesses and revise plans"],"a":1,"why":"Operate: Record costs and resource use. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Plan–control cycle","rows":[["Plan","Goals, strategy and budgets"],["Operate","Record costs and resource use"],["Review","Performance reports and internal audit"],["Respond","Correct weaknesses and revise plans"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are internal reports required to copy annual-report formats exactly?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare users and report purposes.</li><li>I can explain accountant and internal-audit roles.</li><li>I can link planning and control.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare users and report purposes","html":"<p>Management accounting serves internal planning, pricing and control with forecasts and flexible reports. Financial accounting communicates general-purpose performance and position to external users under reporting requirements. Both use financial records, but their purposes and time orientation differ.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain accountant and internal-audit roles","html":"<p>The accountant translates transactions into decision information, prepares budgets, analyses costs and monitors results. Cost leadership seeks efficient low costs; differentiation offers distinct value. A strategy needs measurable goals and resource plans rather than a slogan.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Link planning and control","html":"<p>Internal audit reviews procedures, policies and controls, detects weaknesses and recommends corrections. It differs from external audit of reported financial information. Independence within the organisation and follow-up of findings matter; a review without correction does not improve control.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain management accounting, financial accounting and internal audit using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cost-types","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Direct, indirect, product and period costs","subtitle":"Identify the cost object · Classify traceability and treatment · Distinguish past and future costs","scope":"Identify the cost object · Classify traceability and treatment · Distinguish past and future costs","outcomes":["Identify the cost object","Classify traceability and treatment","Distinguish past and future costs"],"paragraphs":["A cost object is what is being costed, such as a job, product or department. Direct costs can be traced economically to that object; indirect costs require allocation. A supervisor’s salary may be direct to a department but indirect to one individual job.","Product costs include manufacturing direct materials, direct labour and production overhead. Period costs such as selling and general administration are expensed for the period rather than included in manufacturing product cost. Traceability and product/period treatment answer different questions.","Past costs are already incurred; future costs are forecast. For a decision, consider relevant future differences and opportunity costs rather than treating every allocated historical cost as avoidable. State the cost object before assigning labels."],"questions":[{"prompt":"For a custom furniture job, classify timber, craft worker wages, factory rent and office advertising.","steps":["Timber is direct materials and a product cost.","Craft labour is direct labour and a product cost.","Factory rent is indirect production overhead and a product cost.","Office advertising is a period/selling cost rather than manufacturing job cost."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cost-types-q1"},{"prompt":"A supervisor serves two production jobs and a sales clerk serves customers. Classify both costs for an individual production job.","steps":["Supervisor cost is indirect to the individual job.","It is production overhead and a product cost.","Sales-clerk cost is a period selling cost.","Classification depends on both cost object and function."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cost-types-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every indirect cost is automatically a period expense.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Indirect factory overhead can be a product cost.","Traceability and treatment are separate classification dimensions.","Example evidence: Timber is direct materials and a product cost."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cost-types-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does indirect automatically mean period cost?","o":["Indirect factory overhead can be a product cost.","Every indirect cost is automatically a period expense."],"a":0,"why":"Traceability and treatment are separate classification dimensions."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Traceability and treatment are separate classification dimensions.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Indirect factory overhead can be a product cost."},{"q":"In the model “Two dimensions”, what belongs to “Traceability”?","o":["Direct or indirect to the named object","Product or period cost","Past or future cost"],"a":0,"why":"Traceability: Direct or indirect to the named object. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Two dimensions”, what belongs to “Treatment”?","o":["Direct or indirect to the named object","Product or period cost","Past or future cost"],"a":1,"why":"Treatment: Product or period cost. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Two dimensions","rows":[["Traceability","Direct or indirect to the named object"],["Treatment","Product or period cost"],["Timing","Past or future cost"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does indirect automatically mean period cost?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can identify the cost object.</li><li>I can classify traceability and treatment.</li><li>I can distinguish past and future costs.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify the cost object","html":"<p>A cost object is what is being costed, such as a job, product or department. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Direct costs</strong> can be traced economically to that object; indirect costs require allocation. A supervisor’s salary may be direct to a department but indirect to one individual job.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Classify traceability and treatment","html":"<p>Product costs include manufacturing direct materials, direct labour and production <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">overhead</strong>. Period costs such as selling and general administration are expensed for the period rather than included in manufacturing product cost. Traceability and product/period treatment answer different questions.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish past and future costs","html":"<p>Past costs are already incurred; future costs are forecast. For a decision, consider relevant future differences and opportunity costs rather than treating every allocated historical cost as avoidable. State the cost object before assigning labels.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain direct, indirect, product and period costs using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cost-behaviour","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Fixed, variable and mixed cost behaviour","subtitle":"Model total and per-unit costs · Apply the relevant range · Explain mixed cost","scope":"Model total and per-unit costs · Apply the relevant range · Explain mixed cost","outcomes":["Model total and per-unit costs","Apply the relevant range","Explain mixed cost"],"paragraphs":["A fixed total cost stays constant over a stated relevant range, so fixed cost per unit falls as volume grows. Variable total cost changes with activity while its per-unit rate is assumed constant. Confusing total and per-unit behaviour leads to wrong forecasts.","A mixed cost has a fixed component plus a variable component: total = fixed + variable rate × activity. Capacity limits or new shifts can change the fixed component. State the relevant range instead of extrapolating indefinitely.","The cost label describes the model used for a particular time horizon. Rent may be fixed for a short period yet change when premises expand. For a decision, determine which costs actually change with the proposed activity."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Monthly cost is $4,000 fixed plus $6 per unit. Calculate total and unit cost at 1,000 and 2,000 units.","steps":["At 1,000: total = 4,000 + 6,000 = $10,000; unit cost $10.","At 2,000: total = 4,000 + 12,000 = $16,000; unit cost $8.","Variable cost per unit stays $6.","Fixed cost per unit falls from $4 to $2 within the assumed range."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cost-behaviour-q1"},{"prompt":"A utility bill is $500 fixed plus $2 per machine hour. Find cost for 300 hours and explain why it is mixed.","steps":["Variable component = 300 × 2 = $600.","Total cost = 500 + 600 = $1,100.","It contains a fixed amount and an activity-dependent component.","The model assumes the given rate and relevant range remain valid."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cost-behaviour-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Fixed total and fixed per-unit cost both stay constant at all volumes.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Fixed total cost is constant; its per-unit amount changes with volume.","The same total is spread over more or fewer units.","Example evidence: At 1,000: total = 4,000 + 6,000 = $10,000; unit cost $10."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cost-behaviour-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is fixed cost per unit constant as output rises?","o":["Fixed total cost is constant; its per-unit amount changes with volume.","Fixed total and fixed per-unit cost both stay constant at all volumes."],"a":0,"why":"The same total is spread over more or fewer units."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The same total is spread over more or fewer units.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Fixed total cost is constant; its per-unit amount changes with volume."},{"q":"In the model “Cost behaviour at 1,000 → 2,000 units”, what belongs to “Fixed total”?","o":["$4,000 → $4,000","$6,000 → $12,000","$10 → $8"],"a":0,"why":"Fixed total: $4,000 → $4,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Cost behaviour at 1,000 → 2,000 units”, what belongs to “Variable total”?","o":["$4,000 → $4,000","$6,000 → $12,000","$10 → $8"],"a":1,"why":"Variable total: $6,000 → $12,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Cost behaviour at 1,000 → 2,000 units","rows":[["Fixed total","$4,000 → $4,000"],["Variable total","$6,000 → $12,000"],["Total cost per unit","$10 → $8"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is fixed cost per unit constant as output rises?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can model total and per-unit costs.</li><li>I can apply the relevant range.</li><li>I can explain mixed cost.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Model total and per-unit costs","html":"<p>A fixed total cost stays constant over a stated relevant range, so fixed cost per unit falls as volume grows. Variable total cost changes with activity while its per-unit rate is assumed constant. Confusing total and per-unit behaviour leads to wrong forecasts.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply the relevant range","html":"<p>A mixed cost has a fixed component plus a variable component: total = fixed + variable rate × activity. Capacity limits or new shifts can change the fixed component. State the relevant range instead of extrapolating indefinitely.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain mixed cost","html":"<p>The cost label describes the model used for a particular time horizon. Rent may be fixed for a short period yet change when premises expand. For a decision, determine which costs actually change with the proposed activity.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain fixed, variable and mixed cost behaviour using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-overhead-rate","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Predetermined overhead recovery rates","subtitle":"Use estimated normal capacity · Choose the allocation base · Apply the rate consistently","scope":"Use estimated normal capacity · Choose the allocation base · Apply the rate consistently","outcomes":["Use estimated normal capacity","Choose the allocation base","Apply the rate consistently"],"paragraphs":["A predetermined overhead rate is estimated manufacturing overhead divided by the estimated allocation base at normal capacity. The base may be machine hours, labour hours or another stated measure. Normal-capacity estimates reduce distortion from short-term output changes.","Apply the rate to the actual base used by a job, not to the whole company’s base again. Units must match: a dollars-per-machine-hour rate multiplies machine hours. Never divide estimated overhead by a single small job’s hours to create the rate.","Recovered overhead is an allocation, not necessarily actual overhead spent on that job. A poor base can distort product costs even if the arithmetic is correct. Explain why machine hours may suit automated production while labour hours may suit labour-intensive work."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Estimated annual factory overhead $120,000; normal capacity 20,000 machine hours. A job uses 150 hours. Find rate and recovered overhead.","steps":["Rate = 120,000 ÷ 20,000 = $6 per machine hour.","Job overhead = 150 × 6 = $900.","Use normal-capacity company estimates for the rate.","Machine-hour use supplies the job allocation base."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-overhead-rate-q1"},{"prompt":"Estimated overhead $90,000 and normal labour capacity 15,000 hours. A job uses 80 hours. Calculate recovery and a base limitation.","steps":["Rate = $6 per labour hour.","Applied overhead = 80 × 6 = $480.","The base must relate to overhead consumption.","Labour hours may distort costs in highly automated production."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-overhead-rate-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Divide all estimated factory overhead by the hours of one job.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Use the estimated total allocation base at normal capacity.","The rate is designed across normal activity, then applied to each job’s use.","Example evidence: Rate = 120,000 ÷ 20,000 = $6 per machine hour."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-overhead-rate-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should the estimated rate use one job’s hours as denominator?","o":["Use the estimated total allocation base at normal capacity.","Divide all estimated factory overhead by the hours of one job."],"a":0,"why":"The rate is designed across normal activity, then applied to each job’s use."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The rate is designed across normal activity, then applied to each job’s use.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Use the estimated total allocation base at normal capacity."},{"q":"In the model “Rate then apply”, what belongs to “Company estimate”?","o":["$120,000 / 20,000 hours","$6 per machine hour","150 hours","$900"],"a":0,"why":"Company estimate: $120,000 / 20,000 hours. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Rate then apply”, what belongs to “Predetermined rate”?","o":["$120,000 / 20,000 hours","$6 per machine hour","150 hours","$900"],"a":1,"why":"Predetermined rate: $6 per machine hour. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Rate then apply","rows":[["Company estimate","$120,000 / 20,000 hours"],["Predetermined rate","$6 per machine hour"],["Job use","150 hours"],["Recovered overhead","$900"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should the estimated rate use one job’s hours as denominator?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can use estimated normal capacity.</li><li>I can choose the allocation base.</li><li>I can apply the rate consistently.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use estimated normal capacity","html":"<p>A predetermined <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">overhead</strong> rate is estimated manufacturing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">overhead</strong> divided by the estimated allocation base at normal capacity. The base may be machine hours, labour hours or another stated measure. Normal-capacity estimates reduce distortion from short-term output changes.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Choose the allocation base","html":"<p>Apply the rate to the actual base used by a job, not to the whole company’s base again. Units must match: a dollars-per-machine-hour rate multiplies machine hours. Never divide estimated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">overhead</strong> by a single small job’s hours to create the rate.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply the rate consistently","html":"<p>Recovered <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">overhead</strong> is an allocation, not necessarily actual <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">overhead</strong> spent on that job. A poor base can distort product costs even if the arithmetic is correct. Explain why machine hours may suit automated production while labour hours may suit labour-intensive work.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain predetermined overhead recovery rates using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-job-cost","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Job order costing and unit cost","subtitle":"Build a job cost sheet · Separate direct inputs and recovered overhead · Calculate cost per unit","scope":"Build a job cost sheet · Separate direct inputs and recovered overhead · Calculate cost per unit","outcomes":["Build a job cost sheet","Separate direct inputs and recovered overhead","Calculate cost per unit"],"paragraphs":["Job order costing accumulates costs for a distinct job or service. Use a cost sheet for direct materials, direct labour and manufacturing overhead recovered at the predetermined rate. Do not mix selling expenses into production cost unless the task explicitly requests a wider quotation calculation.","Direct labour equals hours × rate. Recovered overhead equals job allocation-base usage × predetermined rate. Add these components to direct materials for total job cost, then divide by the number of completed units for unit cost.","The job total is not its profit and unit cost is not automatically a selling price. Inspect quantities and units; dividing by a normal annual output instead of actual job units would misstate the quote. State any supplied assumptions about completed output."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A 50-unit job uses materials $1,500, labour 40 hours at $25 and machine hours 60 at overhead rate $10. Find total and unit cost.","steps":["Direct labour = 40 × 25 = $1,000.","Overhead recovered = 60 × 10 = $600.","Job cost = 1,500 + 1,000 + 600 = $3,100.","Unit cost = 3,100 ÷ 50 = $62."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-job-cost-q1"},{"prompt":"A 100-unit job uses materials $2,000, labour 50 hours at $30 and 80 machine hours at overhead $5/hour. Calculate cost.","steps":["Labour = $1,500.","Overhead = 80 × 5 = $400.","Total job cost = 2,000 + 1,500 + 400 = $3,900.","Unit cost = $39."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-job-cost-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Direct labour cost automatically includes all factory overhead.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Overhead is a separate allocated component of job cost.","A job cost sheet separately gathers direct materials, labour and recovered overhead.","Example evidence: Direct labour = 40 × 25 = $1,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-job-cost-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is overhead already included just because direct labour is counted?","o":["Overhead is a separate allocated component of job cost.","Direct labour cost automatically includes all factory overhead."],"a":0,"why":"A job cost sheet separately gathers direct materials, labour and recovered overhead."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A job cost sheet separately gathers direct materials, labour and recovered overhead.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Overhead is a separate allocated component of job cost."},{"q":"In the model “Job cost sheet”, what belongs to “Direct materials”?","o":["$1,500","$1,000","$600","$3,100 total; $62 each"],"a":0,"why":"Direct materials: $1,500. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Job cost sheet”, what belongs to “Direct labour”?","o":["$1,500","$1,000","$600","$3,100 total; $62 each"],"a":1,"why":"Direct labour: $1,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Job cost sheet","rows":[["Direct materials","$1,500"],["Direct labour","$1,000"],["Recovered overhead","$600"],["50-unit job","$3,100 total; $62 each"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is overhead already included just because direct labour is counted?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can build a job cost sheet.</li><li>I can separate direct inputs and recovered overhead.</li><li>I can calculate cost per unit.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Build a job cost sheet","html":"<p>Job order costing accumulates costs for a distinct job or service. Use a cost sheet for direct materials, direct labour and manufacturing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">overhead</strong> recovered at the predetermined rate. Do not mix selling expenses into production cost unless the task explicitly requests a wider quotation calculation.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate direct inputs and recovered overhead","html":"<p>Direct labour equals hours × rate. Recovered <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">overhead</strong> equals job allocation-base usage × predetermined rate. Add these components to direct materials for total job cost, then divide by the number of completed units for unit cost.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate cost per unit","html":"<p>The job total is not its <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> and unit cost is not automatically a selling price. Inspect quantities and units; dividing by a normal annual output instead of actual job units would misstate the quote. State any supplied assumptions about completed output.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain job order costing and unit cost using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-markup","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Mark-up, margin and quotation price","subtitle":"Apply mark-up to cost · Distinguish margin on selling price · Explain quote limitations","scope":"Apply mark-up to cost · Distinguish margin on selling price · Explain quote limitations","outcomes":["Apply mark-up to cost","Distinguish margin on selling price","Explain quote limitations"],"paragraphs":["Mark-up is calculated on cost: selling price = cost × (1 + mark-up rate). A 25% mark-up on $80 gives price $100 and profit $20. The margin on selling price is 20%, not 25%, because the denominator differs.","If a target margin is stated, price = cost ÷ (1 − margin rate). Identify which percentage the task uses before calculating. A quotation may need to recover non-production costs too; use the supplied scope rather than assuming job cost includes everything.","A mathematically correct quote can still be commercially unsuitable. Consider competitor pricing, customer demand, capacity and risk. Explain the pricing recommendation using the case rather than treating a fixed mark-up as a guarantee that the whole business will earn profit."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Unit job cost $80; quote using a 25% mark-up on cost. Find price and margin on selling price.","steps":["Mark-up amount = 80 × 25% = $20.","Selling price = $100.","Margin = 20 ÷ 100 × 100 = 20%.","Mark-up and margin use different denominator bases."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-markup-q1"},{"prompt":"A job costs $3,000 and the quote uses a 30% mark-up. Find quote and contrast a 30% margin.","steps":["Mark-up quote = 3,000 × 1.30 = $3,900.","Profit on that quote = $900.","A 30% margin quote = 3,000 ÷ 0.70 = $4,285.71.","The margin quote is higher because its percentage uses selling price."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-markup-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “A given mark-up percentage always equals the same margin percentage.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Mark-up uses cost; margin uses selling price.","Different denominator bases produce different percentages.","Example evidence: Mark-up amount = 80 × 25% = $20."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-markup-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is a 25% mark-up the same as a 25% margin?","o":["Mark-up uses cost; margin uses selling price.","A given mark-up percentage always equals the same margin percentage."],"a":0,"why":"Different denominator bases produce different percentages."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Different denominator bases produce different percentages.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Mark-up uses cost; margin uses selling price."},{"q":"In the model “Price bridge”, what belongs to “Cost”?","o":["$80","$20","$100","20%"],"a":0,"why":"Cost: $80. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Price bridge”, what belongs to “25% mark-up on cost”?","o":["$80","$20","$100","20%"],"a":1,"why":"25% mark-up on cost: $20. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Price bridge","rows":[["Cost","$80"],["25% mark-up on cost","$20"],["Selling price","$100"],["Margin on price","20%"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is a 25% mark-up the same as a 25% margin?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply mark-up to cost.</li><li>I can distinguish margin on selling price.</li><li>I can explain quote limitations.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply mark-up to cost","html":"<p>Mark-up is calculated on cost: selling price = cost × (1 + mark-up rate). A 25% mark-up on $80 gives price $100 and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> $20. The margin on selling price is 20%, not 25%, because the denominator differs.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish margin on selling price","html":"<p>If a target margin is stated, price = cost ÷ (1 − margin rate). Identify which percentage the task uses before calculating. A quotation may need to recover non-production costs too; use the supplied scope rather than assuming job cost includes everything.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain quote limitations","html":"<p>A mathematically correct quote can still be commercially unsuitable. Consider competitor pricing, customer demand, capacity and risk. Explain the pricing recommendation using the case rather than treating a fixed mark-up as a guarantee that the whole business will earn <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain mark-up, margin and quotation price using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-material-price","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Materials price variance","subtitle":"Use quantity purchased in price variance · Label favourable or unfavourable · Separate price from usage","scope":"Use quantity purchased in price variance · Label favourable or unfavourable · Separate price from usage","outcomes":["Use quantity purchased in price variance","Label favourable or unfavourable","Separate price from usage"],"paragraphs":["Materials price variance = (actual price − standard price) × actual quantity purchased. Quantity purchased is the price-variance base in the 2026 specification. It may differ from quantity issued into production, so read both figures carefully.","A positive extra cost under this sign convention is unfavourable; a negative difference is favourable. State the dollar magnitude and F/U label so a sign is not mistaken for meaning. Lower purchase price may indicate savings but may also accompany lower quality.","Standards are benchmarks, not actual cash totals. Price variance alone does not explain total materials performance because usage may be worse. Investigate purchasing terms, supplier changes and quality before rewarding a favourable price variance."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Standard price $5/kg. Actual purchase 1,200 kg at $5.50/kg. Calculate price variance; 1,000 kg was issued.","steps":["Price difference = 5.50 − 5.00 = $0.50/kg.","Use quantity purchased: 1,200 kg.","Variance = 0.50 × 1,200 = $600 unfavourable.","The 1,000 kg issued belongs to usage analysis, not this price calculation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-material-price-q1"},{"prompt":"Purchase 800 kg at $7.50 when standard price is $8. Find variance and give a caution.","steps":["Variance = (7.50 − 8.00) × 800 = −$400.","Report $400 favourable.","The firm paid less per purchased kg than the standard.","Check quality and wastage before concluding overall performance improved."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-material-price-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Always use quantity issued in the materials price variance.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Use actual quantity purchased for the materials price variance.","The price difference concerns purchasing; usage has its own production-quantity base.","Example evidence: Price difference = 5.50 − 5.00 = $0.50/kg."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-material-price-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does materials price variance use quantity issued?","o":["Use actual quantity purchased for the materials price variance.","Always use quantity issued in the materials price variance."],"a":0,"why":"The price difference concerns purchasing; usage has its own production-quantity base."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The price difference concerns purchasing; usage has its own production-quantity base.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Use actual quantity purchased for the materials price variance."},{"q":"In the model “Price variance inputs”, what belongs to “Price difference”?","o":["$0.50/kg adverse","1,200 kg","$600 unfavourable"],"a":0,"why":"Price difference: $0.50/kg adverse. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Price variance inputs”, what belongs to “Quantity purchased”?","o":["$0.50/kg adverse","1,200 kg","$600 unfavourable"],"a":1,"why":"Quantity purchased: 1,200 kg. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Price variance inputs","rows":[["Price difference","$0.50/kg adverse"],["Quantity purchased","1,200 kg"],["Variance","$600 unfavourable"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does materials price variance use quantity issued?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can use quantity purchased in price variance.</li><li>I can label favourable or unfavourable.</li><li>I can separate price from usage.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use quantity purchased in price variance","html":"<p>Materials price <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> = (actual price − standard price) × actual quantity purchased. Quantity purchased is the price-<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> base in the 2026 specification. It may differ from quantity issued into production, so read both figures carefully.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Label favourable or unfavourable","html":"<p>A positive extra cost under this sign convention is unfavourable; a negative difference is favourable. State the dollar magnitude and F/U label so a sign is not mistaken for meaning. Lower purchase price may indicate savings but may also accompany lower quality.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate price from usage","html":"<p>Standards are benchmarks, not actual cash totals. Price <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> alone does not explain total materials performance because usage may be worse. Investigate purchasing terms, supplier changes and quality before rewarding a favourable price <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain materials price variance using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-material-usage","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Materials usage variance","subtitle":"Determine standard quantity allowed for actual output · Use standard price · Investigate waste and quality","scope":"Determine standard quantity allowed for actual output · Use standard price · Investigate waste and quality","outcomes":["Determine standard quantity allowed for actual output","Use standard price","Investigate waste and quality"],"paragraphs":["Standard quantity allowed equals standard input per output unit multiplied by actual output. This adjusts the benchmark for what was actually produced, rather than the planned output. Usage variance = (actual quantity issued − standard quantity allowed) × standard price.","Use quantity issued into production, not all material purchased, because unused purchases may remain in inventory. Using standard price isolates the quantity effect from the purchasing price effect.","Excess use may result from waste, poor quality, design changes or staff training. A favourable usage variance can also hide quality shortcuts. Explain plausible causes with evidence and assess interaction with the price variance."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Standard is 2 kg/unit at $4/kg. Actual output 500 units; actual issued material 1,100 kg. Calculate usage variance.","steps":["Standard quantity allowed = 2 × 500 = 1,000 kg.","Excess use = 1,100 − 1,000 = 100 kg.","Variance = 100 × $4 = $400 unfavourable.","Use actual output and standard price to isolate the usage effect."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-material-usage-q1"},{"prompt":"Standard 3 kg/unit at $6; output 200 units; material issued 570 kg. Calculate and interpret.","steps":["Standard allowed = 3 × 200 = 600 kg.","Variance = (570 − 600) × 6 = −$180.","Report $180 favourable.","Investigate whether efficiency improved without sacrificing product quality."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-material-usage-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Use budgeted output regardless of what was actually produced.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Standard input allowed is calculated for actual output.","The benchmark must reflect the production volume being evaluated.","Example evidence: Standard quantity allowed = 2 × 500 = 1,000 kg."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-material-usage-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is standard allowed based on budgeted output even when actual output differs?","o":["Standard input allowed is calculated for actual output.","Use budgeted output regardless of what was actually produced."],"a":0,"why":"The benchmark must reflect the production volume being evaluated."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The benchmark must reflect the production volume being evaluated.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Standard input allowed is calculated for actual output."},{"q":"In the model “Usage variance bridge”, what belongs to “Actual output”?","o":["500 units","500 × 2 = 1,000 kg","1,100 kg","100 × $4 = $400 U"],"a":0,"why":"Actual output: 500 units. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Usage variance bridge”, what belongs to “Standard input allowed”?","o":["500 units","500 × 2 = 1,000 kg","1,100 kg","100 × $4 = $400 U"],"a":1,"why":"Standard input allowed: 500 × 2 = 1,000 kg. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Usage variance bridge","rows":[["Actual output","500 units"],["Standard input allowed","500 × 2 = 1,000 kg"],["Actual issued","1,100 kg"],["Difference at standard price","100 × $4 = $400 U"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is standard allowed based on budgeted output even when actual output differs?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can determine standard quantity allowed for actual output.</li><li>I can use standard price.</li><li>I can investigate waste and quality.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Determine standard quantity allowed for actual output","html":"<p>Standard quantity allowed equals standard input per output unit multiplied by actual output. This adjusts the benchmark for what was actually produced, rather than the planned output. Usage <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> = (actual quantity issued − standard quantity allowed) × standard price.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use standard price","html":"<p>Use quantity issued into production, not all material purchased, because unused purchases may remain in <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong>. Using standard price isolates the quantity effect from the purchasing price effect.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Investigate waste and quality","html":"<p>Excess use may result from waste, poor quality, design changes or staff training. A favourable usage <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> can also hide quality shortcuts. Explain plausible causes with evidence and assess interaction with the price <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain materials usage variance using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-labour-variances","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Labour rate and efficiency variances","subtitle":"Calculate standard hours for actual output · Separate hourly rate and hours effects · Interpret both variances together","scope":"Calculate standard hours for actual output · Separate hourly rate and hours effects · Interpret both variances together","outcomes":["Calculate standard hours for actual output","Separate hourly rate and hours effects","Interpret both variances together"],"paragraphs":["Labour rate variance = (actual hourly rate − standard rate) × actual direct labour hours. Efficiency variance = (actual hours − standard hours allowed) × standard rate. Standard hours allowed uses actual output multiplied by standard hours per unit.","Rate and efficiency isolate different causes. Skilled workers may cost more per hour while completing work faster. A favourable rate with poor efficiency may not save money overall. Label favourable/unfavourable and show the bases explicitly.","Investigate overtime, skill mix, downtime, material quality and training. Do not assume an unfavourable variance means individual misconduct. The total difference reconciles actual labour cost to standard labour cost allowed for actual output."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Output 400 units; standard 0.5 hours/unit at $20/hour. Actual 220 hours at $22/hour. Calculate both variances.","steps":["Standard hours allowed = 400 × 0.5 = 200.","Rate variance = (22 − 20) × 220 = $440 U.","Efficiency variance = (220 − 200) × 20 = $400 U.","Total $840 U: actual $4,840 less standard $4,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-labour-variances-q1"},{"prompt":"Output 100 units; standard 2 hours/unit at $30/hour. Actual 180 hours at $32/hour. Calculate and reconcile.","steps":["Standard hours allowed = 200.","Rate variance = (32 − 30) × 180 = $360 U.","Efficiency variance = (180 − 200) × 30 = $600 F.","Net $240 F: actual $5,760 versus standard $6,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-labour-variances-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “A higher hourly rate always means total labour cost performance is worse.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Rate and efficiency effects must be interpreted together.","Better efficiency can outweigh an unfavourable hourly-rate variance.","Example evidence: Standard hours allowed = 400 × 0.5 = 200."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-labour-variances-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a higher labour rate necessarily mean total labour performance worsened?","o":["Rate and efficiency effects must be interpreted together.","A higher hourly rate always means total labour cost performance is worse."],"a":0,"why":"Better efficiency can outweigh an unfavourable hourly-rate variance."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Better efficiency can outweigh an unfavourable hourly-rate variance.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Rate and efficiency effects must be interpreted together."},{"q":"In the model “Two labour effects”, what belongs to “Rate”?","o":["Actual hours × hourly-rate difference","Hour difference × standard rate","Actual cost less allowed standard cost"],"a":0,"why":"Rate: Actual hours × hourly-rate difference. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Two labour effects”, what belongs to “Efficiency”?","o":["Actual hours × hourly-rate difference","Hour difference × standard rate","Actual cost less allowed standard cost"],"a":1,"why":"Efficiency: Hour difference × standard rate. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Two labour effects","rows":[["Rate","Actual hours × hourly-rate difference"],["Efficiency","Hour difference × standard rate"],["Reconciliation","Actual cost less allowed standard cost"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a higher labour rate necessarily mean total labour performance worsened?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate standard hours for actual output.</li><li>I can separate hourly rate and hours effects.</li><li>I can interpret both variances together.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate standard hours for actual output","html":"<p>Labour rate <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> = (actual hourly rate − standard rate) × actual direct labour hours. Efficiency <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> = (actual hours − standard hours allowed) × standard rate. Standard hours allowed uses actual output multiplied by standard hours per unit.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate hourly rate and hours effects","html":"<p>Rate and efficiency isolate different causes. Skilled workers may cost more per hour while completing work faster. A favourable rate with poor efficiency may not save money overall. Label favourable/unfavourable and show the bases explicitly.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret both variances together","html":"<p>Investigate overtime, skill mix, downtime, material quality and training. Do not assume an unfavourable <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> means individual misconduct. The total difference reconciles actual labour cost to standard labour cost allowed for actual output.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain labour rate and efficiency variances using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-variance-evaluation","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Variance reports and responsibility","subtitle":"Reconcile cost variances · Link causes across departments · Recommend corrective action","scope":"Reconcile cost variances · Link causes across departments · Recommend corrective action","outcomes":["Reconcile cost variances","Link causes across departments","Recommend corrective action"],"paragraphs":["A variance report communicates amount, direction and likely cause. A cost variance is favourable when actual cost is below the comparable standard; that label does not prove the underlying business decision is beneficial. Assess quality, delivery and customer consequences.","Price, usage, rate and efficiency interact. Cheaper material may create extra waste and labour rework. A purchasing manager should not be rewarded for price savings without considering production consequences and evidence of causation.","Recommend actions such as supplier-quality checks, maintenance or training. Distinguish controllable causes from external price changes and avoid blaming staff for a poorly designed standard. Review standards when conditions change rather than continually accepting an unrealistic benchmark."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Materials price $500 F, usage $800 U, labour efficiency $400 U follow purchase of cheaper low-quality inputs. Evaluate.","steps":["Net listed cost variance = −500 + 800 + 400 = $700 U.","Price savings are outweighed by usage and efficiency losses.","Poor material quality plausibly links the variances.","Review supplier quality and total production cost before repeating the purchase."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-variance-evaluation-q1"},{"prompt":"Labour rate $300 U and efficiency $700 F follow specialist training. Give a balanced judgement.","steps":["Net cost effect is $400 F.","Higher hourly skill cost may be offset by less time used.","Verify quality and completion data before assigning the cause.","Consider retaining training if the gains are supported and repeatable."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-variance-evaluation-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every favourable variance should automatically be rewarded.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Favourable is a cost comparison label; quality and longer-term effects also matter.","A lower immediate cost can cause greater downstream losses.","Example evidence: Net listed cost variance = −500 + 800 + 400 = $700 U."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-variance-evaluation-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does favourable always mean desirable?","o":["Favourable is a cost comparison label; quality and longer-term effects also matter.","Every favourable variance should automatically be rewarded."],"a":0,"why":"A lower immediate cost can cause greater downstream losses."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A lower immediate cost can cause greater downstream losses.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Favourable is a cost comparison label; quality and longer-term effects also matter."},{"q":"In the model “Cross-functional evidence”, what belongs to “Purchasing”?","o":["Price and supplier quality","Usage, rework and hours","Net cost plus quality and delivery outcomes"],"a":0,"why":"Purchasing: Price and supplier quality. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Cross-functional evidence”, what belongs to “Production”?","o":["Price and supplier quality","Usage, rework and hours","Net cost plus quality and delivery outcomes"],"a":1,"why":"Production: Usage, rework and hours. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Cross-functional evidence","rows":[["Purchasing","Price and supplier quality"],["Production","Usage, rework and hours"],["Overall","Net cost plus quality and delivery outcomes"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does favourable always mean desirable?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can reconcile cost variances.</li><li>I can link causes across departments.</li><li>I can recommend corrective action.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile cost variances","html":"<p>A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> report communicates amount, direction and likely cause. A cost <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> is favourable when actual cost is below the comparable standard; that label does not prove the underlying business decision is beneficial. Assess quality, delivery and customer consequences.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Link causes across departments","html":"<p>Price, usage, rate and efficiency interact. Cheaper material may create extra waste and labour rework. A purchasing manager should not be rewarded for price savings without considering production consequences and evidence of causation.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recommend corrective action","html":"<p>Recommend actions such as supplier-quality checks, maintenance or training. Distinguish controllable causes from external price changes and avoid blaming staff for a poorly designed standard. Review standards when conditions change rather than continually accepting an unrealistic benchmark.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain variance reports and responsibility using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-contribution","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Contribution and profit planning","subtitle":"Calculate unit and total contribution · Distinguish contribution from profit · Use contribution ratio","scope":"Calculate unit and total contribution · Distinguish contribution from profit · Use contribution ratio","outcomes":["Calculate unit and total contribution","Distinguish contribution from profit","Use contribution ratio"],"paragraphs":["Contribution is sales less variable costs. It is available first to cover fixed costs and then to provide profit. Unit contribution equals selling price per unit less variable cost per unit; total contribution equals units × unit contribution when the model is stable.","Contribution ratio = contribution divided by sales. Use like units: either unit amounts or totals. Profit = total contribution − fixed costs. Contribution is not net profit because fixed costs remain to be covered.","Cost-volume-profit assumes constant unit selling price and variable cost, fixed costs within the relevant range and, for multiple products, a stated stable mix. Check capacity and these assumptions before extrapolating results."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Price $50, variable cost $30, sales 1,000 units and fixed costs $15,000. Calculate contribution, ratio and profit.","steps":["Unit contribution = 50 − 30 = $20.","Total contribution = 1,000 × 20 = $20,000.","Contribution ratio = 20 ÷ 50 = 40%.","Profit = 20,000 − 15,000 = $5,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-contribution-q1"},{"prompt":"Price $80, variable cost $48, volume 500 and fixed costs $10,000. Calculate contribution and profit.","steps":["Unit contribution = $32.","Total contribution = 500 × 32 = $16,000.","Ratio = 32 ÷ 80 = 40%.","Profit = $6,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-contribution-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “All contribution is automatically profit.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Contribution covers fixed costs before any profit remains.","Fixed costs must still be deducted from total contribution.","Example evidence: Unit contribution = 50 − 30 = $20."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-contribution-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is contribution equal to profit?","o":["Contribution covers fixed costs before any profit remains.","All contribution is automatically profit."],"a":0,"why":"Fixed costs must still be deducted from total contribution."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Fixed costs must still be deducted from total contribution.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Contribution covers fixed costs before any profit remains."},{"q":"In the model “Sales dollar split”, what belongs to “Sales $50”?","o":["Variable costs $30 + contribution $20","Covers fixed costs first","Profit after fixed costs"],"a":0,"why":"Sales $50: Variable costs $30 + contribution $20. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Sales dollar split”, what belongs to “Contribution”?","o":["Variable costs $30 + contribution $20","Covers fixed costs first","Profit after fixed costs"],"a":1,"why":"Contribution: Covers fixed costs first. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Sales dollar split","rows":[["Sales $50","Variable costs $30 + contribution $20"],["Contribution","Covers fixed costs first"],["Remainder","Profit after fixed costs"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is contribution equal to profit?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate unit and total contribution.</li><li>I can distinguish contribution from profit.</li><li>I can use contribution ratio.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate unit and total contribution","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Contribution</strong> is sales less variable costs. It is available first to cover fixed costs and then to provide <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>. Unit <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> equals selling price per unit less variable cost per unit; total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> equals units × unit <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> when the model is stable.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish contribution from profit","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Contribution</strong> ratio = <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> divided by sales. Use like units: either unit amounts or totals. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Profit</strong> = total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> − fixed costs. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Contribution</strong> is not net <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> because fixed costs remain to be covered.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use contribution ratio","html":"<p>Cost-volume-<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> assumes constant unit selling price and variable cost, fixed costs within the relevant range and, for multiple products, a stated stable mix. Check capacity and these assumptions before extrapolating results.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain contribution and profit planning using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-break-even","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Break-even units and sales dollars","subtitle":"Calculate both break-even measures · Round feasible unit sales upward · Explain the break-even graph","scope":"Calculate both break-even measures · Round feasible unit sales upward · Explain the break-even graph","outcomes":["Calculate both break-even measures","Round feasible unit sales upward","Explain the break-even graph"],"paragraphs":["At break-even, contribution equals fixed costs and profit is zero. Units = fixed costs ÷ unit contribution. Sales dollars = fixed costs ÷ contribution ratio. These equations use contribution, not total production cost per unit.","If indivisible units produce a fractional threshold, round up to the first feasible whole-unit sales level that avoids a loss. Keep unrounded figures during calculation and label the final result. Exact mathematical break-even and rounded operational volume can differ.","On a graph, revenue begins at zero while total cost begins at fixed costs. Their intersection is break-even. The model assumes constant price and variable rate within the relevant range; a capacity constraint may make break-even unattainable."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Price $40, variable cost $24 and fixed costs $8,000. Find break-even units and sales dollars.","steps":["Contribution per unit = $16.","Break-even units = 8,000 ÷ 16 = 500.","Contribution ratio = 16 ÷ 40 = 40%.","Break-even sales = 8,000 ÷ 0.40 = $20,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-break-even-q1"},{"prompt":"Price $30, variable cost $19 and fixed costs $5,000. Find mathematical and feasible break-even volumes.","steps":["Contribution = $11/unit.","Mathematical break-even = 5,000 ÷ 11 = 454.55 units.","First whole-unit no-loss volume = 455 units.","At 455, contribution $5,005 exceeds fixed cost by $5."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-break-even-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Always round break-even units down to sell fewer units.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Round up to the first feasible whole-unit no-loss volume.","Rounding down leaves contribution below fixed costs.","Example evidence: Contribution per unit = $16."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-break-even-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should an indivisible-unit break-even result be rounded down?","o":["Round up to the first feasible whole-unit no-loss volume.","Always round break-even units down to sell fewer units."],"a":0,"why":"Rounding down leaves contribution below fixed costs."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Rounding down leaves contribution below fixed costs.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Round up to the first feasible whole-unit no-loss volume."},{"q":"In the model “Break-even example”, what belongs to “Fixed costs”?","o":["$8,000","$16","500 units","Revenue $20,000 = variable $12,000 + fixed $8,000"],"a":0,"why":"Fixed costs: $8,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Break-even example”, what belongs to “Contribution/unit”?","o":["$8,000","$16","500 units","Revenue $20,000 = variable $12,000 + fixed $8,000"],"a":1,"why":"Contribution/unit: $16. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Break-even example","rows":[["Fixed costs","$8,000"],["Contribution/unit","$16"],["Threshold","500 units"],["At threshold","Revenue $20,000 = variable $12,000 + fixed $8,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should an indivisible-unit break-even result be rounded down?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate both break-even measures.</li><li>I can round feasible unit sales upward.</li><li>I can explain the break-even graph.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate both break-even measures","html":"<p>At break-even, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> equals fixed costs and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> is zero. Units = fixed costs ÷ unit <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong>. Sales dollars = fixed costs ÷ <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> ratio. These equations use <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong>, not total production cost per unit.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Round feasible unit sales upward","html":"<p>If indivisible units produce a fractional threshold, round up to the first feasible whole-unit sales level that avoids a loss. Keep unrounded figures during calculation and label the final result. Exact mathematical break-even and rounded operational volume can differ.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the break-even graph","html":"<p>On a graph, revenue begins at zero while total cost begins at fixed costs. Their intersection is break-even. The model assumes constant price and variable rate within the relevant range; a capacity constraint may make break-even unattainable.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain break-even units and sales dollars using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-target-safety","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Target profit and margin of safety","subtitle":"Calculate target volume · Measure sales above break-even · Interpret sensitivity to falls in sales","scope":"Calculate target volume · Measure sales above break-even · Interpret sensitivity to falls in sales","outcomes":["Calculate target volume","Measure sales above break-even","Interpret sensitivity to falls in sales"],"paragraphs":["Target units = (fixed costs + target profit) ÷ contribution per unit. The fixed cost must be covered as well as the desired profit. If a task requests a required selling price, rearrange profit = (price − variable cost) × units − fixed costs.","Margin of safety is actual or budgeted sales less break-even sales. Percentage margin of safety divides that difference by actual/budgeted sales. Use consistent units or dollars; it measures the sales cushion under the model, not a guaranteed cash reserve.","A small margin of safety makes profit sensitive to sales declines. Review pricing, costs and forecast reliability, and state assumptions. A target calculation is a planning threshold rather than proof that market demand exists."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Price $60, variable $36, fixed $12,000 and target profit $6,000. Budget 900 units. Find target volume and margin of safety.","steps":["Contribution = $24/unit.","Target units = (12,000 + 6,000) ÷ 24 = 750.","Break-even = 12,000 ÷ 24 = 500 units.","Margin of safety = 900 − 500 = 400 units; percentage = 44.44%."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-target-safety-q1"},{"prompt":"Price $50, variable $30, fixed $10,000 and actual sales 800 units. Find profit and margin of safety percentage.","steps":["Contribution is $20; profit = 800×20 − 10,000 = $6,000.","Break-even = 500 units.","Safety margin = 300 units.","Safety percentage = 300 ÷ 800 × 100 = 37.50%."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-target-safety-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Margin-of-safety percentage always uses break-even sales as denominator.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Divide the safety margin by actual or budgeted sales for its percentage.","The percentage shows how far current/planned sales can fall relative to that current level.","Example evidence: Contribution = $24/unit."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-target-safety-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is margin of safety divided by break-even sales?","o":["Divide the safety margin by actual or budgeted sales for its percentage.","Margin-of-safety percentage always uses break-even sales as denominator."],"a":0,"why":"The percentage shows how far current/planned sales can fall relative to that current level."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The percentage shows how far current/planned sales can fall relative to that current level.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Divide the safety margin by actual or budgeted sales for its percentage."},{"q":"In the model “Planning thresholds”, what belongs to “Break-even”?","o":["500 units","750 units","900 units","400 units above break-even"],"a":0,"why":"Break-even: 500 units. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Planning thresholds”, what belongs to “Target $6,000 profit”?","o":["500 units","750 units","900 units","400 units above break-even"],"a":1,"why":"Target $6,000 profit: 750 units. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Planning thresholds","rows":[["Break-even","500 units"],["Target $6,000 profit","750 units"],["Budget","900 units"],["Safety cushion","400 units above break-even"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is margin of safety divided by break-even sales?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate target volume.</li><li>I can measure sales above break-even.</li><li>I can interpret sensitivity to falls in sales.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate target volume","html":"<p>Target units = (fixed costs + target <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>) ÷ <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> per unit. The fixed cost must be covered as well as the desired <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>. If a task requests a required selling price, rearrange <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> = (price − variable cost) × units − fixed costs.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Measure sales above break-even","html":"<p>Margin of safety is actual or budgeted sales less break-even sales. Percentage margin of safety divides that difference by actual/budgeted sales. Use consistent units or dollars; it measures the sales cushion under the model, not a guaranteed cash reserve.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret sensitivity to falls in sales","html":"<p>A small margin of safety makes <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> sensitive to sales declines. Review pricing, costs and forecast reliability, and state assumptions. A target calculation is a planning threshold rather than proof that market demand exists.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain target profit and margin of safety using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-sales-mix","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Multi-product CVP and weighted contribution","subtitle":"Use unit-based sales mix · Calculate weighted-average contribution · Retain the assumed mix at break-even","scope":"Use unit-based sales mix · Calculate weighted-average contribution · Retain the assumed mix at break-even","outcomes":["Use unit-based sales mix","Calculate weighted-average contribution","Retain the assumed mix at break-even"],"paragraphs":["For up to three products, calculate each unit contribution and weight it by its share of total units sold. Weighted-average contribution is the sum of these weighted amounts. A revenue-based mix cannot silently replace a unit-based mix.","Total break-even units = fixed costs ÷ weighted-average contribution, then allocate units according to the assumed mix. Check feasible whole mix bundles if exact product proportions must be maintained. Rounding products independently can alter the mix.","The threshold depends on product mix. A shift toward lower-contribution products increases required total volume if other assumptions stay constant. State that mix, price, variable rates and fixed costs are held constant."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A contribution $30, B $10; unit mix A 40%, B 60%; fixed costs $18,000. Calculate break-even and product units.","steps":["Weighted contribution = 30×0.40 + 10×0.60 = $18.","Break-even total units = 18,000 ÷ 18 = 1,000.","A units = 400; B units = 600.","The result depends on maintaining the 40:60 unit mix."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-sales-mix-q1"},{"prompt":"Three products contribute $10, $20 and $30 with unit mix 50%, 30%, 20%. Fixed costs $17,000. Find break-even mix.","steps":["Weighted contribution = 10×.5 + 20×.3 + 30×.2 = $17.","Break-even = 1,000 total units.","Product volumes = 500, 300 and 200.","Changing the mix changes weighted contribution and the threshold."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-sales-mix-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Use the simple average regardless of product sales mix.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Weight each contribution by its share of unit sales.","Unequal sales proportions mean each product contributes unequally to the average.","Example evidence: Weighted contribution = 30×0.40 + 10×0.60 = $18."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-sales-mix-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Can we use unweighted average contribution when sales mix is unequal?","o":["Weight each contribution by its share of unit sales.","Use the simple average regardless of product sales mix."],"a":0,"why":"Unequal sales proportions mean each product contributes unequally to the average."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Unequal sales proportions mean each product contributes unequally to the average.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Weight each contribution by its share of unit sales."},{"q":"In the model “Weighted contribution model”, what belongs to “A”?","o":["$30 × 40% = $12","$10 × 60% = $6","$18","$18,000 / $18 = 1,000"],"a":0,"why":"A: $30 × 40% = $12. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Weighted contribution model”, what belongs to “B”?","o":["$30 × 40% = $12","$10 × 60% = $6","$18","$18,000 / $18 = 1,000"],"a":1,"why":"B: $10 × 60% = $6. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Weighted contribution model","rows":[["A","$30 × 40% = $12"],["B","$10 × 60% = $6"],["Weighted unit","$18"],["Break-even","$18,000 / $18 = 1,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Can we use unweighted average contribution when sales mix is unequal?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can use unit-based sales mix.</li><li>I can calculate weighted-average contribution.</li><li>I can retain the assumed mix at break-even.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use unit-based sales mix","html":"<p>For up to three products, calculate each unit <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> and weight it by its share of total units sold. Weighted-average <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> is the sum of these weighted amounts. A revenue-based mix cannot silently replace a unit-based mix.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate weighted-average contribution","html":"<p>Total break-even units = fixed costs ÷ weighted-average <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong>, then allocate units according to the assumed mix. Check feasible whole mix bundles if exact product proportions must be maintained. Rounding products independently can alter the mix.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Retain the assumed mix at break-even","html":"<p>The threshold depends on product mix. A shift toward lower-<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> products increases required total volume if other assumptions stay constant. State that mix, price, variable rates and fixed costs are held constant.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain multi-product cvp and weighted contribution using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cvp-sensitivity","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"CVP sensitivity and capacity","subtitle":"Test changes in price, cost and mix · Check achievable capacity · Recommend using scenario evidence","scope":"Test changes in price, cost and mix · Check achievable capacity · Recommend using scenario evidence","outcomes":["Test changes in price, cost and mix","Check achievable capacity","Recommend using scenario evidence"],"paragraphs":["Sensitivity analysis changes one or more assumptions and recalculates profit or break-even. A lower price may increase demand but also reduce contribution on every unit sold. Include the forecast volume response rather than comparing prices alone.","Capacity limits can prevent a target from being reached. A second shift may raise fixed costs, invalidating the original relevant-range model. Compare feasible scenarios including any additional fixed cost and changes in variable cost.","For a bottleneck, contribution per scarce resource can inform product priorities when demand and other constraints are given. Do not choose solely by contribution per unit if units consume different scarce hours. State non-financial consequences and uncertainty."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Price $50, variable $30, fixed $10,000; current 700 units. A price cut to $45 predicts 900 units. Compare profit.","steps":["Current profit = 700×20 − 10,000 = $4,000.","New contribution = 45 − 30 = $15.","New profit = 900×15 − 10,000 = $3,500.","The proposed cut reduces forecast profit $500 despite greater volume."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cvp-sensitivity-q1"},{"prompt":"Fixed costs $12,000 and contribution $20 imply 600-unit break-even, but capacity is 500. Evaluate.","steps":["Required break-even is 600 units.","Maximum contribution = 500×20 = $10,000.","At capacity the model gives a $2,000 loss.","Review price/costs or feasible capacity expansion, including any new fixed costs."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cvp-sensitivity-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Any calculated target is automatically achievable.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Compare the required volume with feasible capacity and demand.","A valid arithmetic threshold can exceed production or market constraints.","Example evidence: Current profit = 700×20 − 10,000 = $4,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cvp-sensitivity-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Can a calculated target ignore capacity?","o":["Compare the required volume with feasible capacity and demand.","Any calculated target is automatically achievable."],"a":0,"why":"A valid arithmetic threshold can exceed production or market constraints."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["A valid arithmetic threshold can exceed production or market constraints.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Compare the required volume with feasible capacity and demand."},{"q":"In the model “Scenario comparison”, what belongs to “Current”?","o":["700 units × $20 − $10,000 = $4,000","900 units × $15 − $10,000 = $3,500","Forecast profit falls $500"],"a":0,"why":"Current: 700 units × $20 − $10,000 = $4,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Scenario comparison”, what belongs to “Price cut”?","o":["700 units × $20 − $10,000 = $4,000","900 units × $15 − $10,000 = $3,500","Forecast profit falls $500"],"a":1,"why":"Price cut: 900 units × $15 − $10,000 = $3,500. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Scenario comparison","rows":[["Current","700 units × $20 − $10,000 = $4,000"],["Price cut","900 units × $15 − $10,000 = $3,500"],["Decision","Forecast profit falls $500"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Can a calculated target ignore capacity?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can test changes in price, cost and mix.</li><li>I can check achievable capacity.</li><li>I can recommend using scenario evidence.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Test changes in price, cost and mix","html":"<p>Sensitivity analysis changes one or more assumptions and recalculates <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> or break-even. A lower price may increase demand but also reduce <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> on every unit sold. Include the forecast volume response rather than comparing prices alone.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Check achievable capacity","html":"<p>Capacity limits can prevent a target from being reached. A second shift may raise fixed costs, invalidating the original relevant-range model. Compare feasible scenarios including any additional fixed cost and changes in variable cost.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recommend using scenario evidence","html":"<p>For a bottleneck, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> per scarce resource can inform product priorities when demand and other constraints are given. Do not choose solely by <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> per unit if units consume different scarce hours. State non-financial consequences and uncertainty.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain cvp sensitivity and capacity using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-make-buy","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Make or buy decisions","subtitle":"Identify avoidable costs · Exclude unavoidable allocations · Include opportunity cost and qualitative risks","scope":"Identify avoidable costs · Exclude unavoidable allocations · Include opportunity cost and qualitative risks","outcomes":["Identify avoidable costs","Exclude unavoidable allocations","Include opportunity cost and qualitative risks"],"paragraphs":["Compare future costs that differ between making and buying. Avoidable production costs are relevant; fixed costs that continue under either option are not savings from outsourcing. An allocated cost is not automatically avoidable.","Include opportunity cost if freeing capacity permits another contribution-earning activity. Compare purchase price, avoidable making costs and the benefit of alternative use. Do not include sunk historical costs in a future difference.","Quality, delivery reliability, confidentiality and supplier dependence affect the decision. Give a numerical recommendation under the assumptions, then identify a relevant non-financial condition that could change it."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Make 1,000 components: variable $8 each, avoidable fixed $2,000, unavoidable fixed $3,000. Buy price $11 each; no alternative use. Compare.","steps":["Relevant make cost = 1,000×8 + 2,000 = $10,000.","Buy cost = 1,000×11 = $11,000.","Unavoidable $3,000 continues under both options and is excluded from the difference.","Making saves $1,000, subject to quality and capacity assumptions."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-make-buy-q1"},{"prompt":"Make variable cost $6/unit for 2,000 units plus avoidable fixed $1,000. Buy $7/unit and free capacity earning $3,000 contribution. Compare.","steps":["Relevant make cost = $13,000.","Buy cost = $14,000, less alternative contribution $3,000 = $11,000 effective.","Buying improves profit by $2,000.","Verify the alternative contribution and supplier reliability."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-make-buy-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every allocated fixed cost is saved when production stops.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Only future costs that actually disappear are savings from buying.","Shared or committed fixed costs may continue despite outsourcing.","Example evidence: Relevant make cost = 1,000×8 + 2,000 = $10,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-make-buy-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are all allocated fixed costs avoided by buying?","o":["Only future costs that actually disappear are savings from buying.","Every allocated fixed cost is saved when production stops."],"a":0,"why":"Shared or committed fixed costs may continue despite outsourcing."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Shared or committed fixed costs may continue despite outsourcing.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Only future costs that actually disappear are savings from buying."},{"q":"In the model “Relevant-cost comparison”, what belongs to “Make”?","o":["Variable + avoidable fixed","Purchase cost − alternative-use benefit","Sunk and unavoidable costs common to both"],"a":0,"why":"Make: Variable + avoidable fixed. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Relevant-cost comparison”, what belongs to “Buy”?","o":["Variable + avoidable fixed","Purchase cost − alternative-use benefit","Sunk and unavoidable costs common to both"],"a":1,"why":"Buy: Purchase cost − alternative-use benefit. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Relevant-cost comparison","rows":[["Make","Variable + avoidable fixed"],["Buy","Purchase cost − alternative-use benefit"],["Exclude","Sunk and unavoidable costs common to both"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are all allocated fixed costs avoided by buying?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can identify avoidable costs.</li><li>I can exclude unavoidable allocations.</li><li>I can include opportunity cost and qualitative risks.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify avoidable costs","html":"<p>Compare future costs that differ between making and buying. Avoidable production costs are relevant; fixed costs that continue under either option are not savings from outsourcing. An allocated cost is not automatically avoidable.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Exclude unavoidable allocations","html":"<p>Include <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">opportunity cost</strong> if freeing capacity permits another <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong>-earning activity. Compare purchase price, avoidable making costs and the benefit of alternative use. Do not include sunk historical costs in a future difference.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Include opportunity cost and qualitative risks","html":"<p>Quality, delivery reliability, confidentiality and supplier dependence affect the decision. Give a numerical recommendation under the assumptions, then identify a relevant non-financial condition that could change it.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain make or buy decisions using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-segment","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Closing a department or dropping a product","subtitle":"Compare lost contribution with avoidable costs · Distinguish reported loss from relevant effect · Consider linked sales","scope":"Compare lost contribution with avoidable costs · Distinguish reported loss from relevant effect · Consider linked sales","outcomes":["Compare lost contribution with avoidable costs","Distinguish reported loss from relevant effect","Consider linked sales"],"paragraphs":["A segment may report a loss after allocation of shared fixed overhead yet contribute positively toward costs that remain if it closes. Closing removes its contribution but saves only avoidable fixed costs. Calculate the incremental effect rather than deleting the reported loss.","Change in profit from closing = avoidable fixed savings − lost contribution, with any other relevant changes included. If lost contribution is greater, closure worsens overall profit. Do not treat continuing head-office costs as savings.","Consider linked product sales, employee effects, customer access and alternative use. A product may attract customers who buy other lines. A numerical recommendation needs the relevant assumptions stated and the qualitative consequences explained."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Segment contribution $20,000, avoidable fixed $8,000 and allocated unavoidable fixed $15,000. It reports a $3,000 loss. Should it close?","steps":["Reported result = 20,000 − 8,000 − 15,000 = −$3,000.","Closing saves $8,000 but loses $20,000 contribution.","Overall profit falls $12,000 if it closes.","Keep under these assumptions; the allocated shared cost remains."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-segment-q1"},{"prompt":"A department contributes $9,000 and has $12,000 avoidable fixed costs. No linked sales or alternative-use effects. Evaluate closure.","steps":["Closing saves $12,000.","Contribution lost is $9,000.","Profit improves $3,000.","Confirm costs are genuinely avoidable and consider customer/employee consequences."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-segment-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Close every segment reporting a loss after shared cost allocations.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Compare lost contribution with genuinely avoidable costs.","Shared costs can remain while the segment’s positive contribution disappears.","Example evidence: Reported result = 20,000 − 8,000 − 15,000 = −$3,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-segment-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a reported segment loss automatically justify closure?","o":["Compare lost contribution with genuinely avoidable costs.","Close every segment reporting a loss after shared cost allocations."],"a":0,"why":"Shared costs can remain while the segment’s positive contribution disappears."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Shared costs can remain while the segment’s positive contribution disappears.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Compare lost contribution with genuinely avoidable costs."},{"q":"In the model “Closure effect”, what belongs to “Lost contribution”?","o":["$20,000","$8,000","$12,000 decrease"],"a":0,"why":"Lost contribution: $20,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Closure effect”, what belongs to “Avoidable savings”?","o":["$20,000","$8,000","$12,000 decrease"],"a":1,"why":"Avoidable savings: $8,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Closure effect","rows":[["Lost contribution","$20,000"],["Avoidable savings","$8,000"],["Net profit change","$12,000 decrease"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a reported segment loss automatically justify closure?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare lost contribution with avoidable costs.</li><li>I can distinguish reported loss from relevant effect.</li><li>I can consider linked sales.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare lost contribution with avoidable costs","html":"<p>A segment may report a loss after allocation of shared fixed <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">overhead</strong> yet contribute positively toward costs that remain if it closes. Closing removes its <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> but saves only avoidable fixed costs. Calculate the incremental effect rather than deleting the reported loss.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish reported loss from relevant effect","html":"<p>Change in <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> from closing = avoidable fixed savings − lost <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong>, with any other relevant changes included. If lost <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> is greater, closure worsens overall <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>. Do not treat continuing head-office costs as savings.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Consider linked sales","html":"<p>Consider linked product sales, employee effects, customer access and alternative use. A product may attract customers who buy other lines. A numerical recommendation needs the relevant assumptions stated and the qualitative consequences explained.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain closing a department or dropping a product using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-special-order","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Special orders with spare or constrained capacity","subtitle":"Calculate incremental gain · Include new fixed and opportunity costs · Evaluate market consequences","scope":"Calculate incremental gain · Include new fixed and opportunity costs · Evaluate market consequences","outcomes":["Calculate incremental gain","Include new fixed and opportunity costs","Evaluate market consequences"],"paragraphs":["A special order is evaluated using additional revenue less additional variable cost, new fixed costs and opportunity cost. Existing unavoidable fixed costs do not change because of the order and are excluded from the incremental calculation.","With spare capacity, opportunity cost may be zero if no regular sales are displaced. With limited capacity, lost regular contribution must be deducted. Avoid accepting an order just because its price exceeds variable cost if setup or displacement costs outweigh the margin.","Consider customer expectations, future pricing, regular customer relationships and delivery capacity. State whether the order is isolated and whether it changes standard selling prices. A favourable incremental gain is conditional on these assumptions."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Special order 200 units at $25; variable $18/unit, new setup $300 and spare capacity. Calculate gain.","steps":["Revenue = 200×25 = $5,000.","Variable cost = 200×18 = $3,600.","Gain = 5,000 − 3,600 − 300 = $1,100.","Accept numerically if regular sales are unaffected and qualitative conditions are acceptable."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-special-order-q1"},{"prompt":"A special order earns contribution $2,000 before new setup $400 but displaces 100 regular units contributing $20 each. Evaluate.","steps":["Opportunity cost = 100×20 = $2,000.","Net effect = 2,000 − 400 − 2,000 = −$400.","Reject numerically under these assumptions.","The displaced contribution is relevant despite not being a direct invoice cost."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-special-order-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Opportunity cost is irrelevant because it is not a cash invoice.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Deduct the contribution forgone from the displaced regular sales.","The decision sacrifices an alternative profit contribution.","Example evidence: Revenue = 200×25 = $5,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-special-order-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Can opportunity cost be ignored when regular sales are displaced?","o":["Deduct the contribution forgone from the displaced regular sales.","Opportunity cost is irrelevant because it is not a cash invoice."],"a":0,"why":"The decision sacrifices an alternative profit contribution."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The decision sacrifices an alternative profit contribution.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Deduct the contribution forgone from the displaced regular sales."},{"q":"In the model “Special-order bridge”, what belongs to “Incremental revenue”?","o":["Less variable costs","Deduct new fixed costs","Deduct lost contribution","Gain or loss"],"a":0,"why":"Incremental revenue: Less variable costs. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Special-order bridge”, what belongs to “Additional setup”?","o":["Less variable costs","Deduct new fixed costs","Deduct lost contribution","Gain or loss"],"a":1,"why":"Additional setup: Deduct new fixed costs. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Special-order bridge","rows":[["Incremental revenue","Less variable costs"],["Additional setup","Deduct new fixed costs"],["Displaced regular sales","Deduct lost contribution"],["Remainder","Gain or loss"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Can opportunity cost be ignored when regular sales are displaced?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate incremental gain.</li><li>I can include new fixed and opportunity costs.</li><li>I can evaluate market consequences.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate incremental gain","html":"<p>A special order is evaluated using additional revenue less additional variable cost, new fixed costs and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">opportunity cost</strong>. Existing unavoidable fixed costs do not change because of the order and are excluded from the incremental calculation.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Include new fixed and opportunity costs","html":"<p>With spare capacity, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">opportunity cost</strong> may be zero if no regular sales are displaced. With limited capacity, lost regular <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> must be deducted. Avoid accepting an order just because its price exceeds variable cost if setup or displacement costs outweigh the margin.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate market consequences","html":"<p>Consider customer expectations, future pricing, regular customer relationships and delivery capacity. State whether the order is isolated and whether it changes standard selling prices. A favourable incremental gain is conditional on these assumptions.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain special orders with spare or constrained capacity using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-master-budget","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"The master budget and business planning","subtitle":"Connect operating, capital and financial budgets · Sequence budget preparation · Use planning assumptions","scope":"Connect operating, capital and financial budgets · Sequence budget preparation · Use planning assumptions","outcomes":["Connect operating, capital and financial budgets","Sequence budget preparation","Use planning assumptions"],"paragraphs":["A master budget coordinates operating plans, capital expenditure and financial budgets. Sales assumptions affect production or purchases, staffing and cash. A budgeted income statement measures expected accrual performance; a cash budget predicts liquidity.","Capital expenditure plans identify long-lived asset purchases. Financial budgets show how operations and investment will be funded. An equipment purchase affects cash immediately but its depreciation affects profit over use; keep the two treatments separate.","Budgets should link goals and strategy to realistic resources. Cost leadership and differentiation require different priorities. Document volume, price, collection timing and capacity assumptions so a change can be traced across dependent budgets."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A retailer forecasts sales, purchases new equipment and arranges a loan. Classify these budget components and explain dependencies.","steps":["Sales/purchases belong to operating budgets.","Equipment belongs to capital expenditure planning.","Cash receipts, payments and loan finance belong to financial budgets.","Sales and capital plans feed the cash budget and financing requirement."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-master-budget-q1"},{"prompt":"A machine costs $24,000 cash and annual depreciation is $6,000. Show the different budget effects.","steps":["Cash budget includes the $24,000 payment when due.","Budgeted income statement includes the period’s $6,000 depreciation where a full year applies.","Loan proceeds, if any, are cash finance rather than revenue.","The master budget coordinates both performance and liquidity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-master-budget-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “The master budget needs only a profit forecast because cash is identical.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Cash budgets track receipts/payments; budgeted income tracks earned income and expenses.","Timing differences and capital transactions make liquidity and performance distinct.","Example evidence: Sales/purchases belong to operating budgets."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-master-budget-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is the cash budget interchangeable with budgeted income?","o":["Cash budgets track receipts/payments; budgeted income tracks earned income and expenses.","The master budget needs only a profit forecast because cash is identical."],"a":0,"why":"Timing differences and capital transactions make liquidity and performance distinct."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Timing differences and capital transactions make liquidity and performance distinct.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Cash budgets track receipts/payments; budgeted income tracks earned income and expenses."},{"q":"In the model “Budget dependencies”, what belongs to “Operating”?","o":["Sales, purchases, labour and expenses","Asset purchases and replacements","Cash and financing","Budgeted income and financial position"],"a":0,"why":"Operating: Sales, purchases, labour and expenses. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Budget dependencies”, what belongs to “Capital”?","o":["Sales, purchases, labour and expenses","Asset purchases and replacements","Cash and financing","Budgeted income and financial position"],"a":1,"why":"Capital: Asset purchases and replacements. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Budget dependencies","rows":[["Operating","Sales, purchases, labour and expenses"],["Capital","Asset purchases and replacements"],["Financial","Cash and financing"],["Integration","Budgeted income and financial position"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is the cash budget interchangeable with budgeted income?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can connect operating, capital and financial budgets.</li><li>I can sequence budget preparation.</li><li>I can use planning assumptions.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Connect operating, capital and financial budgets","html":"<p>A master budget coordinates operating plans, capital expenditure and financial budgets. Sales assumptions affect production or purchases, staffing and cash. A budgeted income statement measures expected <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">accrual</strong> performance; a cash budget predicts <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liquidity</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Sequence budget preparation","html":"<p>Capital expenditure plans identify long-lived asset purchases. Financial budgets show how operations and investment will be funded. An equipment purchase affects cash immediately but its <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> affects <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> over use; keep the two treatments separate.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use planning assumptions","html":"<p>Budgets should link goals and strategy to realistic resources. Cost leadership and differentiation require different priorities. Document volume, price, collection timing and capacity assumptions so a change can be traced across dependent budgets.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain the master budget and business planning using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-debtors-schedule","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Debtors’ collection schedules","subtitle":"Apply collection lags to sales cohorts · Include prior-period receivables · Avoid counting sales twice","scope":"Apply collection lags to sales cohorts · Include prior-period receivables · Avoid counting sales twice","outcomes":["Apply collection lags to sales cohorts","Include prior-period receivables","Avoid counting sales twice"],"paragraphs":["A debtor schedule converts credit sales into expected cash receipts by month. Apply each collection percentage to the sales month from which it comes. Current receipts may combine current-month sales and collections from several earlier months.","Use supplied prior-period sales or opening receivable detail. A collection percentage is not a new revenue percentage; the sales were recognised separately. Check whether percentages sum to 100% or whether stated bad debts/uncollectible amounts remain.","Collection assumptions should reflect customer behaviour and credit policy. Slower collection increases receivables and cash funding needs without changing the original sales amount. Exclude GST in Year 12 schedules unless a task explicitly introduces other information outside this scope."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Credit sales December $20,000, January $30,000, February $40,000. Collect 30% in sale month, 70% next month. Find January and February receipts.","steps":["January current collections = 30,000×30% = $9,000.","January prior collections = 20,000×70% = $14,000; total $23,000.","February receipts = 40,000×30% + 30,000×70% = $33,000.","Receipts follow sales cohorts rather than equal that month’s sales."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-debtors-schedule-q1"},{"prompt":"March credit sales $50,000, February $40,000 and January $30,000. Collect 20%, 50%, 30% in sale month/next/second next. Find March receipts.","steps":["March sales collection = $10,000.","February collection = $20,000.","January collection = $9,000.","March total receipts = $39,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-debtors-schedule-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every month’s debtor receipts equal that month’s credit sales.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Receipts can combine current and earlier sales cohorts.","Collection lags shift cash into later periods without shifting earned sales.","Example evidence: January current collections = 30,000×30% = $9,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-debtors-schedule-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Do all February receipts come from February sales?","o":["Receipts can combine current and earlier sales cohorts.","Every month’s debtor receipts equal that month’s credit sales."],"a":0,"why":"Collection lags shift cash into later periods without shifting earned sales."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Collection lags shift cash into later periods without shifting earned sales.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Receipts can combine current and earlier sales cohorts."},{"q":"In the model “January receipt cohorts”, what belongs to “December sales”?","o":["$20,000 × 70% = $14,000","$30,000 × 30% = $9,000","$23,000"],"a":0,"why":"December sales: $20,000 × 70% = $14,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “January receipt cohorts”, what belongs to “January sales”?","o":["$20,000 × 70% = $14,000","$30,000 × 30% = $9,000","$23,000"],"a":1,"why":"January sales: $30,000 × 30% = $9,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"January receipt cohorts","rows":[["December sales","$20,000 × 70% = $14,000"],["January sales","$30,000 × 30% = $9,000"],["Total receipts","$23,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Do all February receipts come from February sales?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply collection lags to sales cohorts.</li><li>I can include prior-period receivables.</li><li>I can avoid counting sales twice.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply collection lags to sales cohorts","html":"<p>A debtor schedule converts <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sales into expected cash receipts by month. Apply each collection percentage to the sales month from which it comes. Current receipts may combine current-month sales and collections from several earlier months.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Include prior-period receivables","html":"<p>Use supplied prior-period sales or opening receivable detail. A collection percentage is not a new revenue percentage; the sales were recognised separately. Check whether percentages sum to 100% or whether stated bad debts/uncollectible amounts remain.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Avoid counting sales twice","html":"<p>Collection assumptions should reflect customer behaviour and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> policy. Slower collection increases receivables and cash funding needs without changing the original sales amount. Exclude <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> in Year 12 schedules unless a task explicitly introduces other information outside this scope.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain debtors’ collection schedules using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-creditors-schedule","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Creditors’ payment schedules","subtitle":"Apply supplier payment lags · Distinguish purchases and payments · Reconcile remaining payables","scope":"Apply supplier payment lags · Distinguish purchases and payments · Reconcile remaining payables","outcomes":["Apply supplier payment lags","Distinguish purchases and payments","Reconcile remaining payables"],"paragraphs":["A creditor schedule forecasts when inventory purchases are paid. Apply the payment percentages to each purchase cohort. Purchases are not necessarily cost of sales, and payment is not a second purchase expense.","Include opening payables or prior purchase details to forecast early-month payments. A payment schedule may use current and prior-month purchases. Check the percentages, dates and whether the task separates cash purchases from credit purchases.","Forecast closing payable equals opening payable plus new credit purchases less payments, assuming no other changes. This reconciliation tests the schedule and explains why cash payments differ from amounts acquired."],"questions":[{"prompt":"December purchases $12,000, January $18,000; pay 40% in purchase month and 60% next month. Calculate January payments and end-January payable from January purchases.","steps":["Pay December cohort = 12,000×60% = $7,200.","Pay January cohort = 18,000×40% = $7,200.","January payments total $14,400.","January unpaid portion = 18,000×60% = $10,800."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-creditors-schedule-q1"},{"prompt":"Opening payable $5,000, new credit purchases $20,000 and supplier payments $17,000. Find closing payable and explain timing.","steps":["Closing payable = 5,000 + 20,000 − 17,000 = $8,000.","Payments settle some opening and new obligations.","Purchases and payments are measured at different stages.","Do not put both full purchases and their cash settlements into the cash budget."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-creditors-schedule-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Purchases always equal cash paid to suppliers in the same month.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Payment timing can leave purchases unpaid and settle earlier purchases.","Credit terms create payable balances and payment lags.","Example evidence: Pay December cohort = 12,000×60% = $7,200."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-creditors-schedule-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are purchases automatically equal to supplier payments?","o":["Payment timing can leave purchases unpaid and settle earlier purchases.","Purchases always equal cash paid to suppliers in the same month."],"a":0,"why":"Credit terms create payable balances and payment lags."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Credit terms create payable balances and payment lags.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Payment timing can leave purchases unpaid and settle earlier purchases."}],"model":{"title":"Payment cohorts","rows":[["December outstanding","$7,200 paid in January"],["January immediate","$7,200 paid in January"],["January carried forward","$10,800"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are purchases automatically equal to supplier payments?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply supplier payment lags.</li><li>I can distinguish purchases and payments.</li><li>I can reconcile remaining payables.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply supplier payment lags","html":"<p>A creditor schedule forecasts when <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> purchases are paid. Apply the payment percentages to each purchase cohort. Purchases are not necessarily cost of sales, and payment is not a second purchase expense.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish purchases and payments","html":"<p>Include opening payables or prior purchase details to forecast early-month payments. A payment schedule may use current and prior-month purchases. Check the percentages, dates and whether the task separates cash purchases from <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> purchases.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile remaining payables","html":"<p>Forecast closing payable equals opening payable plus new <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> purchases less payments, assuming no other changes. This reconciliation tests the schedule and explains why cash payments differ from amounts acquired.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain creditors’ payment schedules using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-budget","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Preparing and interpreting a cash budget","subtitle":"Roll opening cash into closing cash · Separate operating, investment and finance flows · Identify financing needs","scope":"Roll opening cash into closing cash · Separate operating, investment and finance flows · Identify financing needs","outcomes":["Roll opening cash into closing cash","Separate operating, investment and finance flows","Identify financing needs"],"paragraphs":["A cash budget starts with opening cash, adds receipts and subtracts payments. Closing cash becomes next period’s opening cash. Include customer receipts, supplier payments, expenses paid, capital expenditure and financing at the dates cash moves.","Do not include depreciation as a payment or credit sales as immediate receipts unless collected then. Loan proceeds and principal repayments affect cash although they do not create operating profit/expense. Keep the budget’s payment schedules and capital plan consistent.","A forecast deficit signals the need to arrange finance or change timing before it occurs. If a minimum balance is required, financing may exceed the amount needed merely to reach zero. Evaluate feasible responses rather than assuming a bank will approve an overdraft."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Opening cash $4,000; receipts $23,000; suppliers $14,400; wages $7,000; rent $2,000; equipment $6,000. Find closing cash and funding for a $2,000 minimum.","steps":["Total payments = 14,400 + 7,000 + 2,000 + 6,000 = $29,400.","Closing before finance = 4,000 + 23,000 − 29,400 = −$2,400.","Funding to reach $2,000 = $4,400.","Arrange finance or revise feasible timing before the deficit occurs."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-budget-q1"},{"prompt":"Opening cash $3,000, receipts $20,000 and payments $18,000 plus loan principal $4,000. Depreciation $1,000 is also forecast. Find closing cash.","steps":["Cash payments = 18,000 + 4,000 = $22,000.","Depreciation is excluded as a non-cash item.","Closing cash = 3,000 + 20,000 − 22,000 = $1,000.","Loan principal reduces cash without being an ordinary operating expense."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-budget-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Deduct depreciation as an additional cash-budget payment.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Depreciation is non-cash and is excluded from cash payments.","Its profit effect differs from cash acquisition and financing payments.","Example evidence: Total payments = 14,400 + 7,000 + 2,000 + 6,000 = $29,400."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-budget-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does depreciation reduce the cash budget balance directly?","o":["Depreciation is non-cash and is excluded from cash payments.","Deduct depreciation as an additional cash-budget payment."],"a":0,"why":"Its profit effect differs from cash acquisition and financing payments."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Its profit effect differs from cash acquisition and financing payments.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Depreciation is non-cash and is excluded from cash payments."},{"q":"In the model “Cash roll-forward”, what belongs to “Opening”?","o":["$4,000","$23,000","$29,400","−$2,400"],"a":0,"why":"Opening: $4,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Cash roll-forward”, what belongs to “Receipts”?","o":["$4,000","$23,000","$29,400","−$2,400"],"a":1,"why":"Receipts: $23,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Cash roll-forward","rows":[["Opening","$4,000"],["Receipts","$23,000"],["Payments","$29,400"],["Closing before finance","−$2,400"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does depreciation reduce the cash budget balance directly?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can roll opening cash into closing cash.</li><li>I can separate operating, investment and finance flows.</li><li>I can identify financing needs.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Roll opening cash into closing cash","html":"<p>A cash budget starts with opening cash, adds receipts and subtracts payments. Closing cash becomes next period’s opening cash. Include customer receipts, supplier payments, expenses paid, capital expenditure and financing at the dates cash moves.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate operating, investment and finance flows","html":"<p>Do not include <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> as a payment or <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sales as immediate receipts unless collected then. Loan proceeds and principal repayments affect cash although they do not create operating <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>/expense. Keep the budget’s payment schedules and capital plan consistent.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify financing needs","html":"<p>A forecast deficit signals the need to arrange finance or change timing before it occurs. If a minimum balance is required, financing may exceed the amount needed merely to reach zero. Evaluate feasible responses rather than assuming a bank will approve an overdraft.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain preparing and interpreting a cash budget using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-budget-income","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Budgeted income statements","subtitle":"Forecast accrual sales and costs · Calculate inventory-based cost of sales · Reconcile profit with cash planning","scope":"Forecast accrual sales and costs · Calculate inventory-based cost of sales · Reconcile profit with cash planning","outcomes":["Forecast accrual sales and costs","Calculate inventory-based cost of sales","Reconcile profit with cash planning"],"paragraphs":["A budgeted income statement predicts earned income and expenses, not cash collections and payments. Include sales for the period, cost of sales, operating expenses and depreciation. Collection and payment schedules belong to cash forecasting.","For a simple trader, cost of sales = opening inventory + purchases − closing inventory, with any additional specified cost adjustments. Inventory acquired but unsold remains an asset; supplier payment timing does not determine cost of sales.","Compare predicted profit with the cash budget to explain differences such as credit sales, inventory, capital expenditure and loans. A budget is a conditional forecast; review assumptions and sensitivity rather than treating the projected profit as certain."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Sales $50,000; opening inventory $8,000, purchases $30,000, closing inventory $10,000; operating expenses $12,000 including depreciation. Prepare subtotals.","steps":["Cost of sales = 8,000 + 30,000 − 10,000 = $28,000.","Gross profit = 50,000 − 28,000 = $22,000.","Profit = 22,000 − 12,000 = $10,000.","Use earned sales, not just cash receipts."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-budget-income-q1"},{"prompt":"Sales $40,000; opening inventory $5,000, purchases $25,000, closing $7,000; cash operating expenses $10,000 and depreciation $2,000. Find profit.","steps":["Cost of sales = 5,000 + 25,000 − 7,000 = $23,000.","Gross profit = $17,000.","Operating expenses = 10,000 + 2,000 = $12,000.","Budgeted profit = $5,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-budget-income-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “All inventory payments are automatically cost of sales for that period.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Unsold inventory remains an asset; cost of sales reflects goods sold.","Expense recognition follows consumption/sale rather than supplier payment timing.","Example evidence: Cost of sales = 8,000 + 30,000 − 10,000 = $28,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-budget-income-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does paying for inventory make all of it current expense?","o":["Unsold inventory remains an asset; cost of sales reflects goods sold.","All inventory payments are automatically cost of sales for that period."],"a":0,"why":"Expense recognition follows consumption/sale rather than supplier payment timing."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Expense recognition follows consumption/sale rather than supplier payment timing.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Unsold inventory remains an asset; cost of sales reflects goods sold."},{"q":"In the model “Budgeted performance”, what belongs to “Sales”?","o":["$50,000 earned","$28,000","$22,000","$12,000","$10,000"],"a":0,"why":"Sales: $50,000 earned. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Budgeted performance”, what belongs to “Cost of sales”?","o":["$50,000 earned","$28,000","$22,000","$12,000","$10,000"],"a":1,"why":"Cost of sales: $28,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Budgeted performance","rows":[["Sales","$50,000 earned"],["Cost of sales","$28,000"],["Gross profit","$22,000"],["Operating expenses","$12,000"],["Profit","$10,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does paying for inventory make all of it current expense?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can forecast accrual sales and costs.</li><li>I can calculate inventory-based cost of sales.</li><li>I can reconcile profit with cash planning.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Forecast accrual sales and costs","html":"<p>A budgeted income statement predicts earned income and expenses, not cash collections and payments. Include sales for the period, cost of sales, operating expenses and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>. Collection and payment schedules belong to cash forecasting.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate inventory-based cost of sales","html":"<p>For a simple trader, cost of sales = opening <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> + purchases − closing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong>, with any additional specified cost adjustments. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Inventory</strong> acquired but unsold remains an asset; supplier payment timing does not determine cost of sales.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile profit with cash planning","html":"<p>Compare predicted <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> with the cash budget to explain differences such as <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sales, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong>, capital expenditure and loans. A budget is a conditional forecast; review assumptions and sensitivity rather than treating the projected <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> as certain.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain budgeted income statements using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-performance-report","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Cash and income performance reports","subtitle":"Compare actual with budget consistently · Label variances by effect · Recommend actions supported by causes","scope":"Compare actual with budget consistently · Label variances by effect · Recommend actions supported by causes","outcomes":["Compare actual with budget consistently","Label variances by effect","Recommend actions supported by causes"],"paragraphs":["A performance report puts budget, actual and variance beside each other. State whether variance is actual minus budget and label the effect. Higher receipts may improve liquidity; higher expenses usually reduce profit, but their causes require interpretation.","A cash-budget payment variance is not always a profit variance. Early supplier payment changes cash timing, while equipment expenditure creates an asset. For income performance, compare earned revenue and expenses on the same accrual basis.","Explain both amount and cause using evidence. A favourable revenue variance may require extra variable costs; a favourable maintenance expense variance may mean necessary maintenance was postponed. Compare the overall effect and recommend action on controllable causes."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Budget revenue $50,000 and expenses $35,000; actual revenue $54,000 and expenses $39,000. Report variances and profit.","steps":["Revenue variance = $4,000 favourable.","Expense variance = $4,000 unfavourable.","Budget and actual profit both equal $15,000.","Higher sales did not improve profit because expenses rose equally."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-performance-report-q1"},{"prompt":"Budget supplier cash payments $20,000; actual $24,000 because payment was made one month early. Interpret the cash variance.","steps":["Actual payment exceeds budget by $4,000: adverse to current cash availability.","The early payment reduces the payable sooner.","It does not automatically increase cost of sales or reduce accrual profit.","Review whether timing was authorised and whether future budgets need revision."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-performance-report-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every higher cash payment is an equal reduction in current profit.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Cash timing and accrual expense can differ.","Payments can settle old obligations or acquire assets rather than create current expenses.","Example evidence: Revenue variance = $4,000 favourable."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-performance-report-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a payment variance always equal an expense variance?","o":["Cash timing and accrual expense can differ.","Every higher cash payment is an equal reduction in current profit."],"a":0,"why":"Payments can settle old obligations or acquire assets rather than create current expenses."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Payments can settle old obligations or acquire assets rather than create current expenses.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Cash timing and accrual expense can differ."},{"q":"In the model “Performance report”, what belongs to “Revenue”?","o":["Budget 50,000; actual 54,000; 4,000 F","Budget 35,000; actual 39,000; 4,000 U","15,000 in both cases"],"a":0,"why":"Revenue: Budget 50,000; actual 54,000; 4,000 F. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Performance report”, what belongs to “Expenses”?","o":["Budget 50,000; actual 54,000; 4,000 F","Budget 35,000; actual 39,000; 4,000 U","15,000 in both cases"],"a":1,"why":"Expenses: Budget 35,000; actual 39,000; 4,000 U. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Performance report","rows":[["Revenue","Budget 50,000; actual 54,000; 4,000 F"],["Expenses","Budget 35,000; actual 39,000; 4,000 U"],["Profit","15,000 in both cases"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a payment variance always equal an expense variance?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare actual with budget consistently.</li><li>I can label variances by effect.</li><li>I can recommend actions supported by causes.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare actual with budget consistently","html":"<p>A performance report puts budget, actual and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> beside each other. State whether <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> is actual minus budget and label the effect. Higher receipts may improve <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liquidity</strong>; higher expenses usually reduce <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>, but their causes require interpretation.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Label variances by effect","html":"<p>A cash-budget payment <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> is not always a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong>. Early supplier payment changes cash timing, while equipment expenditure creates an asset. For income performance, compare earned revenue and expenses on the same <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">accrual</strong> basis.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recommend actions supported by causes","html":"<p>Explain both amount and cause using evidence. A favourable revenue <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> may require extra variable costs; a favourable maintenance expense <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">variance</strong> may mean necessary maintenance was postponed. Compare the overall effect and recommend action on controllable causes.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain cash and income performance reports using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-capital-cash","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Capital investment and after-tax cash flows","subtitle":"Separate cash investment from depreciation · Calculate tax using stated assumptions · Add back non-cash depreciation","scope":"Separate cash investment from depreciation · Calculate tax using stated assumptions · Add back non-cash depreciation","outcomes":["Separate cash investment from depreciation","Calculate tax using stated assumptions","Add back non-cash depreciation"],"paragraphs":["Capital investment commits funds for future benefits and may be difficult to reverse. Evaluate customer demand, competition, regulation and capacity as well as numerical return. Time value of money means cash now and cash later are not equivalent.","For these tasks, use straight-line depreciation and a supplied tax rate. Taxable operating profit is revenue less cash operating costs and depreciation under the stated classroom assumptions. After-tax operating cash flow is profit after tax plus depreciation.","Equivalently, (revenue − cash operating costs) × (1 − tax rate) + depreciation × tax rate. Initial asset cost is a cash outflow at time zero, not a recurring expense. Include final residual cash and any supplied tax effect only when the task gives relevant assumptions."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Machine costs $40,000, no residual, four-year life. Annual revenue $30,000, cash operating costs $12,000; assume 30% tax and depreciation deductible. Find annual net cash flow.","steps":["Depreciation = 40,000 ÷ 4 = $10,000.","Taxable profit = 30,000 − 12,000 − 10,000 = $8,000.","Tax = $2,400; profit after tax $5,600.","Net cash flow = 5,600 + 10,000 = $15,600."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-capital-cash-q1"},{"prompt":"Cost $30,000, zero residual, three-year life; annual revenue $25,000, cash costs $9,000, tax 25%. Calculate cash flow.","steps":["Depreciation = $10,000/year.","Taxable profit = 25,000 − 9,000 − 10,000 = $6,000.","Tax $1,500 and profit after tax $4,500.","Annual net cash flow = $14,500."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-capital-cash-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Deduct depreciation again from after-tax profit for investment cash flow.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Add back non-cash depreciation to profit after tax.","It already reduced taxable profit without being a current cash payment.","Example evidence: Depreciation = 40,000 ÷ 4 = $10,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-capital-cash-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should depreciation be deducted a second time from after-tax profit to find cash flow?","o":["Add back non-cash depreciation to profit after tax.","Deduct depreciation again from after-tax profit for investment cash flow."],"a":0,"why":"It already reduced taxable profit without being a current cash payment."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["It already reduced taxable profit without being a current cash payment.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Add back non-cash depreciation to profit after tax."},{"q":"In the model “Tax-to-cash bridge”, what belongs to “Operating cash surplus”?","o":["$18,000","$10,000","$8,000","$2,400","$15,600"],"a":0,"why":"Operating cash surplus: $18,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Tax-to-cash bridge”, what belongs to “Depreciation”?","o":["$18,000","$10,000","$8,000","$2,400","$15,600"],"a":1,"why":"Depreciation: $10,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Tax-to-cash bridge","rows":[["Operating cash surplus","$18,000"],["Depreciation","$10,000"],["Taxable profit","$8,000"],["Tax","$2,400"],["Net cash flow","$15,600"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should depreciation be deducted a second time from after-tax profit to find cash flow?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can separate cash investment from depreciation.</li><li>I can calculate tax using stated assumptions.</li><li>I can add back non-cash depreciation.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate cash investment from depreciation","html":"<p>Capital investment commits funds for future benefits and may be difficult to reverse. Evaluate customer demand, competition, regulation and capacity as well as numerical return. Time value of money means cash now and cash later are not equivalent.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate tax using stated assumptions","html":"<p>For these tasks, use straight-line <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> and a supplied tax rate. Taxable operating <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> is revenue less cash operating costs and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> under the stated classroom assumptions. After-tax operating <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">cash flow</strong> is <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> after tax plus <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Add back non-cash depreciation","html":"<p>Equivalently, (revenue − cash operating costs) × (1 − tax rate) + <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> × tax rate. Initial asset cost is a cash outflow at time zero, not a recurring expense. Include final residual cash and any supplied tax effect only when the task gives relevant assumptions.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain capital investment and after-tax cash flows using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-payback","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Payback period and investment risk","subtitle":"Calculate equal and unequal-flow payback · Convert fractional years to months · Explain limitations","scope":"Calculate equal and unequal-flow payback · Convert fractional years to months · Explain limitations","outcomes":["Calculate equal and unequal-flow payback","Convert fractional years to months","Explain limitations"],"paragraphs":["Payback measures how long net cash inflows take to recover the initial investment. Equal annual flows allow cost divided by annual net cash flow. Unequal flows require a cumulative schedule until the cost is recovered.","For a partial year, divide the unrecovered amount at the start of that year by that year’s cash inflow, assuming the flow is earned evenly. Convert the year fraction to months by multiplying by 12; a decimal fraction is not a number of months.","Payback emphasises liquidity and exposure time but ignores the time value of money and flows after recovery. A shorter payback alone does not prove higher overall value. Use it alongside NPV and qualitative risk information."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Initial cost $45,000 and equal annual net cash inflow $18,000. Find payback in years and months.","steps":["Payback = 45,000 ÷ 18,000 = 2.5 years.","Fractional year 0.5 × 12 = 6 months.","Payback = 2 years 6 months.","The method ignores discounting and later cash flows."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-payback-q1"},{"prompt":"Initial cost $50,000; annual net flows $20,000, $15,000, $30,000. Find payback assuming evenly earned flows.","steps":["After two years cumulative receipts are $35,000.","Unrecovered amount = $15,000.","Fraction of year 3 = 15,000 ÷ 30,000 = 0.5.","Payback = 2 years 6 months."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-payback-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Read decimal years directly as years and decimal digits as months.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Convert the fractional year to months by multiplying by 12.","Half a year is six months, not five.","Example evidence: Payback = 45,000 ÷ 18,000 = 2.5 years."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-payback-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does 2.5 years mean two years five months?","o":["Convert the fractional year to months by multiplying by 12.","Read decimal years directly as years and decimal digits as months."],"a":0,"why":"Half a year is six months, not five."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Half a year is six months, not five.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Convert the fractional year to months by multiplying by 12."},{"q":"In the model “Unequal-flow payback”, what belongs to “After year 1”?","o":["$20,000 recovered","$35,000 recovered","$15,000 of year-3 $30,000","2 years 6 months"],"a":0,"why":"After year 1: $20,000 recovered. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Unequal-flow payback”, what belongs to “After year 2”?","o":["$20,000 recovered","$35,000 recovered","$15,000 of year-3 $30,000","2 years 6 months"],"a":1,"why":"After year 2: $35,000 recovered. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Unequal-flow payback","rows":[["After year 1","$20,000 recovered"],["After year 2","$35,000 recovered"],["Remaining","$15,000 of year-3 $30,000"],["Payback","2 years 6 months"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does 2.5 years mean two years five months?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate equal and unequal-flow payback.</li><li>I can convert fractional years to months.</li><li>I can explain limitations.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate equal and unequal-flow payback","html":"<p>Payback measures how long net cash inflows take to recover the initial investment. Equal annual flows allow cost divided by annual net <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">cash flow</strong>. Unequal flows require a cumulative schedule until the cost is recovered.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Convert fractional years to months","html":"<p>For a partial year, divide the unrecovered amount at the start of that year by that year’s cash inflow, assuming the flow is earned evenly. Convert the year fraction to months by multiplying by 12; a decimal fraction is not a number of months.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain limitations","html":"<p>Payback emphasises <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liquidity</strong> and exposure time but ignores the time value of money and flows after recovery. A shorter payback alone does not prove higher overall value. Use it alongside NPV and qualitative risk information.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain payback period and investment risk using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-npv","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Net present value","subtitle":"Discount flows by their timing · Include initial outflow once · Interpret NPV under a required return","scope":"Discount flows by their timing · Include initial outflow once · Interpret NPV under a required return","outcomes":["Discount flows by their timing","Include initial outflow once","Interpret NPV under a required return"],"paragraphs":["Present value of a cash flow in year n is cash flow ÷ (1 + discount rate) raised to n. Discount each dated flow separately, sum present values and subtract initial cost at time zero. A discount factor supplied by the task should be applied with its stated rounding.","Positive NPV indicates expected value above the required return under the assumptions; zero meets that return; negative falls short. For mutually exclusive projects, compare appropriate NPVs alongside constraints and risk rather than summing incompatible choices.","Discount after-tax cash flows, not depreciation or accounting profit. Include terminal cash at its actual date where supplied. NPV depends on forecast accuracy and discount rate, so test sensitivity and explain the conditional recommendation."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Initial cost $10,000; net cash $6,000 at end of years 1 and 2; required return 10%. Calculate NPV.","steps":["PV year 1 = 6,000 ÷ 1.10 = $5,454.55.","PV year 2 = 6,000 ÷ 1.10² = $4,958.68.","NPV = 5,454.55 + 4,958.68 − 10,000 = $413.22 using unrounded values.","Positive NPV supports acceptance under the forecasts and required return."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-npv-q1"},{"prompt":"Cost $12,000, cash flows $7,000 in years 1 and 2, discount 10%. Find NPV and state a limitation.","steps":["PV year 1 = $6,363.64.","PV year 2 = $5,785.12.","NPV using unrounded values = $148.76.","A small positive NPV could reverse if cash forecasts are overstated."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-npv-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Discount every amount including time-zero cost by one year.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Time-zero cost is already at present value and is subtracted once.","Discount periods depend on when each cash movement occurs.","Example evidence: PV year 1 = 6,000 ÷ 1.10 = $5,454.55."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-npv-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Do we discount the initial time-zero cost by one year?","o":["Time-zero cost is already at present value and is subtracted once.","Discount every amount including time-zero cost by one year."],"a":0,"why":"Discount periods depend on when each cash movement occurs."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Discount periods depend on when each cash movement occurs.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Time-zero cost is already at present value and is subtracted once."},{"q":"In the model “NPV timeline”, what belongs to “Time 0”?","o":["−$10,000","$6,000 / 1.10","$6,000 / 1.10²","+$413.22"],"a":0,"why":"Time 0: −$10,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “NPV timeline”, what belongs to “Year 1”?","o":["−$10,000","$6,000 / 1.10","$6,000 / 1.10²","+$413.22"],"a":1,"why":"Year 1: $6,000 / 1.10. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"NPV timeline","rows":[["Time 0","−$10,000"],["Year 1","$6,000 / 1.10"],["Year 2","$6,000 / 1.10²"],["Net present value","+$413.22"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Do we discount the initial time-zero cost by one year?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can discount flows by their timing.</li><li>I can include initial outflow once.</li><li>I can interpret NPV under a required return.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Discount flows by their timing","html":"<p>Present value of a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">cash flow</strong> in year n is <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">cash flow</strong> ÷ (1 + discount rate) raised to n. Discount each dated flow separately, sum present values and subtract initial cost at time zero. A discount factor supplied by the task should be applied with its stated rounding.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Include initial outflow once","html":"<p>Positive NPV indicates expected value above the required return under the assumptions; zero meets that return; negative falls short. For mutually exclusive projects, compare appropriate NPVs alongside constraints and risk rather than summing incompatible choices.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret NPV under a required return","html":"<p>Discount after-tax cash flows, not <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> or accounting <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>. Include terminal cash at its actual date where supplied. NPV depends on forecast accuracy and discount rate, so test sensitivity and explain the conditional recommendation.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain net present value using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-investment-choice","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Interpreting capital investment decisions","subtitle":"Compare NPV with payback · Assess market and regulatory factors · Recommend with assumptions and sensitivity","scope":"Compare NPV with payback · Assess market and regulatory factors · Recommend with assumptions and sensitivity","outcomes":["Compare NPV with payback","Assess market and regulatory factors","Recommend with assumptions and sensitivity"],"paragraphs":["NPV addresses expected value at a required return; payback addresses recovery time without discounting. Projects can rank differently on the two measures. Explain the business objective and funding constraints instead of declaring one ranking automatically wrong.","Consumer preferences, competitor responses and regulation can change sales, costs and project feasibility. Capacity and maintenance needs affect whether forecast benefits can be delivered. A numerical model should incorporate supported estimates rather than optimistic assumptions presented as facts.","A recommendation states the preferred feasible option, the financial evidence and a material risk. Test at least one variable where the NPV is close to zero. A larger NPV may be preferable financially but still require funding or risk safeguards."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Project A NPV $8,000, payback four years; B NPV $5,000, payback two years. Evaluate for a firm with tight cash and high uncertainty.","steps":["A has greater forecast present-value gain.","B recovers cash sooner and may reduce exposure time.","Tight funding and uncertainty make recovery timing relevant.","Recommend conditionally with funding/risk evidence rather than NPV alone."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-investment-choice-q1"},{"prompt":"A project’s NPV is $1,000, but a possible new rule reduces present value of inflows by $1,500. Analyse.","steps":["Revised NPV = 1,000 − 1,500 = −$500.","The regulatory scenario changes the financial conclusion.","Investigate likelihood and cost of compliance.","Delay or revise the investment if the adverse scenario is sufficiently supported."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-investment-choice-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Positive NPV removes all project risk and guarantees cash receipts.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Positive NPV is conditional on the forecast flows and required return.","Forecast error, competition and regulatory change can alter the realised outcome.","Example evidence: A has greater forecast present-value gain."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-investment-choice-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does positive NPV guarantee the forecast cash will arrive?","o":["Positive NPV is conditional on the forecast flows and required return.","Positive NPV removes all project risk and guarantees cash receipts."],"a":0,"why":"Forecast error, competition and regulatory change can alter the realised outcome."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Forecast error, competition and regulatory change can alter the realised outcome.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Positive NPV is conditional on the forecast flows and required return."},{"q":"In the model “Decision evidence”, what belongs to “Value”?","o":["NPV at required return","Payback and cash exposure","Capacity, demand and funding","Sensitivity and qualitative factors"],"a":0,"why":"Value: NPV at required return. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Decision evidence”, what belongs to “Recovery”?","o":["NPV at required return","Payback and cash exposure","Capacity, demand and funding","Sensitivity and qualitative factors"],"a":1,"why":"Recovery: Payback and cash exposure. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Decision evidence","rows":[["Value","NPV at required return"],["Recovery","Payback and cash exposure"],["Feasibility","Capacity, demand and funding"],["Risk","Sensitivity and qualitative factors"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does positive NPV guarantee the forecast cash will arrive?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare NPV with payback.</li><li>I can assess market and regulatory factors.</li><li>I can recommend with assumptions and sensitivity.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare NPV with payback","html":"<p>NPV addresses expected value at a required return; payback addresses recovery time without discounting. Projects can rank differently on the two measures. Explain the business objective and funding constraints instead of declaring one ranking automatically wrong.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Assess market and regulatory factors","html":"<p>Consumer preferences, competitor responses and regulation can change sales, costs and project feasibility. Capacity and maintenance needs affect whether forecast benefits can be delivered. A numerical model should incorporate supported estimates rather than optimistic assumptions presented as facts.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recommend with assumptions and sensitivity","html":"<p>A recommendation states the preferred feasible option, the financial evidence and a material risk. Test at least one variable where the NPV is close to zero. A larger NPV may be preferable financially but still require funding or risk safeguards.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain interpreting capital investment decisions using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-insolvency","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Insolvency, administration, receivership and liquidation","subtitle":"Apply the cash-based solvency test · Distinguish external-administration options · Explain creditor priorities","scope":"Apply the cash-based solvency test · Distinguish external-administration options · Explain creditor priorities","outcomes":["Apply the cash-based solvency test","Distinguish external-administration options","Explain creditor priorities"],"paragraphs":["A company is solvent if it can pay debts as and when they become due and payable; otherwise it is insolvent. A positive asset total or accounting profit does not establish solvency when cash and accessible finance cannot meet due obligations.","Voluntary administration involves an independent assessment and possible restructuring through a deed of company arrangement or liquidation. Liquidation winds up the company. Receivership usually involves a secured creditor appointing a receiver to realise secured assets; the procedures can coexist.","Priority depends on security rights and asset type. Subject to secured-creditor rights, the general unsecured distribution pays liquidation costs, then employee priority classes, then ordinary unsecured creditors proportionately; shareholders receive any residual last. Employee priorities can affect circulating security proceeds. Do not present one universal ladder ignoring these distinctions."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A profitable company has assets $500,000 but cannot pay $40,000 due now or access finance. Explain the concern and two possible processes.","steps":["Profit and asset totals do not prove debts can be paid when due.","The inability to meet due debts indicates possible insolvency.","Voluntary administration may assess restructuring options.","Liquidation realises assets and distributes funds if winding up is required."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-insolvency-q1"},{"prompt":"After secured rights are resolved, an unsecured pool has $30,000. Liquidation costs $5,000, priority wages $10,000, and ordinary claims $30,000. Calculate ordinary payout assuming no other claims.","steps":["After costs and wages, funds are 30,000 − 5,000 − 10,000 = $15,000.","Ordinary creditors receive 15,000 ÷ 30,000 = 50% of claims.","Shareholders receive nothing in this simplified case.","Actual priority depends on security and statutory facts; state the assumptions."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-insolvency-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “A profitable company can never be insolvent.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Solvency concerns paying debts when due, not profit alone.","Cash timing and available finance may prevent payment despite profit.","Example evidence: Profit and asset totals do not prove debts can be paid when due."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-insolvency-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does positive accounting profit prove solvency?","o":["Solvency concerns paying debts when due, not profit alone.","A profitable company can never be insolvent."],"a":0,"why":"Cash timing and available finance may prevent payment despite profit."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Cash timing and available finance may prevent payment despite profit.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Solvency concerns paying debts when due, not profit alone."},{"q":"In the model “External processes”, what belongs to “Administration”?","o":["Assess rescue or creditor outcome","Realise secured assets for appointing creditor","Wind up and distribute under priorities"],"a":0,"why":"Administration: Assess rescue or creditor outcome. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “External processes”, what belongs to “Receivership”?","o":["Assess rescue or creditor outcome","Realise secured assets for appointing creditor","Wind up and distribute under priorities"],"a":1,"why":"Receivership: Realise secured assets for appointing creditor. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"External processes","rows":[["Administration","Assess rescue or creditor outcome"],["Receivership","Realise secured assets for appointing creditor"],["Liquidation","Wind up and distribute under priorities"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does positive accounting profit prove solvency?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply the cash-based solvency test.</li><li>I can distinguish external-administration options.</li><li>I can explain creditor priorities.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply the cash-based solvency test","html":"<p>A company is solvent if it can pay debts as and when they become due and payable; otherwise it is insolvent. A positive asset total or accounting <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> does not establish solvency when cash and accessible finance cannot meet due obligations.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish external-administration options","html":"<p>Voluntary administration involves an independent assessment and possible restructuring through a deed of company arrangement or liquidation. Liquidation winds up the company. Receivership usually involves a secured creditor appointing a receiver to realise secured <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>; the procedures can coexist.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain creditor priorities","html":"<p>Priority depends on security rights and asset type. Subject to secured-creditor rights, the general unsecured distribution pays liquidation costs, then employee priority classes, then ordinary unsecured creditors proportionately; shareholders receive any residual last. Employee priorities can affect circulating security proceeds. Do not present one universal ladder ignoring these distinctions.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain insolvency, administration, receivership and liquidation using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-climate-risk","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Climate risks, opportunities and emissions scopes","subtitle":"Distinguish physical and transition risks · Classify emissions scopes · Connect climate factors to budgets","scope":"Distinguish physical and transition risks · Classify emissions scopes · Connect climate factors to budgets","outcomes":["Distinguish physical and transition risks","Classify emissions scopes","Connect climate factors to budgets"],"paragraphs":["Physical risks arise from climate events or longer-term changes, such as flood damage or heat reducing productivity. Transition risks arise from movement toward a lower-carbon economy, including changing regulation, technology and customer demand. Both can alter asset use, costs and cash forecasts.","Scope 1 covers direct emissions from controlled/owned sources; Scope 2 covers indirect emissions from purchased electricity and other acquired energy; Scope 3 covers other value-chain emissions. The boundary and measurement method matter; do not add figures measured on inconsistent bases.","Opportunities can include energy efficiency, new products and resilient operations. Compare upfront investment, forecast savings and uncertainty. Carbon reduction and financial benefit are related but not identical: evaluate both rather than claiming every emissions initiative has a positive NPV."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A firm burns gas on-site, buys grid electricity and uses third-party freight. Classify emissions and identify a flood risk.","steps":["On-site fuel combustion is Scope 1.","Purchased electricity is Scope 2.","Third-party freight is typically Scope 3 within the value-chain boundary.","Flood damage is a physical risk affecting assets, operations and cash."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-climate-risk-q1"},{"prompt":"New carbon regulation raises fuel costs while customers prefer low-emission products. Classify the change and suggest budget responses.","steps":["The regulation/customer shift creates transition risks.","Forecast changed input costs and sales demand.","Evaluate efficiency investment and new-product opportunities.","State uncertainty and test cash/NPV effects."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-climate-risk-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “All energy-related emissions belong to Scope 1.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Purchased electricity emissions are Scope 2 rather than direct on-site emissions.","The scopes distinguish controlled direct sources from acquired energy and other value-chain sources.","Example evidence: On-site fuel combustion is Scope 1."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-climate-risk-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is purchased electricity normally Scope 1?","o":["Purchased electricity emissions are Scope 2 rather than direct on-site emissions.","All energy-related emissions belong to Scope 1."],"a":0,"why":"The scopes distinguish controlled direct sources from acquired energy and other value-chain sources."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The scopes distinguish controlled direct sources from acquired energy and other value-chain sources.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Purchased electricity emissions are Scope 2 rather than direct on-site emissions."},{"q":"In the model “Climate classification”, what belongs to “Physical”?","o":["Flood, heat or storm effects","Regulation, technology and preference changes","Efficiency and resilient/new products","Direct, purchased energy and other value-chain sources"],"a":0,"why":"Physical: Flood, heat or storm effects. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Climate classification”, what belongs to “Transition”?","o":["Flood, heat or storm effects","Regulation, technology and preference changes","Efficiency and resilient/new products","Direct, purchased energy and other value-chain sources"],"a":1,"why":"Transition: Regulation, technology and preference changes. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Climate classification","rows":[["Physical","Flood, heat or storm effects"],["Transition","Regulation, technology and preference changes"],["Opportunity","Efficiency and resilient/new products"],["Emissions","Direct, purchased energy and other value-chain sources"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is purchased electricity normally Scope 1?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can distinguish physical and transition risks.</li><li>I can classify emissions scopes.</li><li>I can connect climate factors to budgets.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish physical and transition risks","html":"<p>Physical risks arise from climate events or longer-term changes, such as flood damage or heat reducing productivity. Transition risks arise from movement toward a lower-carbon economy, including changing regulation, technology and customer demand. Both can alter asset use, costs and cash forecasts.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Classify emissions scopes","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Scope 1</strong> covers direct emissions from controlled/owned sources; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Scope 2</strong> covers indirect emissions from purchased electricity and other acquired energy; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Scope 3</strong> covers other value-chain emissions. The boundary and measurement method matter; do not add figures measured on inconsistent bases.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Connect climate factors to budgets","html":"<p>Opportunities can include energy efficiency, new products and resilient operations. Compare upfront investment, forecast savings and uncertainty. Carbon reduction and financial benefit are related but not identical: evaluate both rather than claiming every emissions initiative has a positive NPV.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain climate risks, opportunities and emissions scopes using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-business-ethics","year":"12","unit":"Unit 3 – Management accounting and decisions","title":"Ethics in management decisions","subtitle":"Identify unfair compensation and confidentiality issues · Recognise misrepresentation and conflicts · Recommend accountable responses","scope":"Identify unfair compensation and confidentiality issues · Recognise misrepresentation and conflicts · Recommend accountable responses","outcomes":["Identify unfair compensation and confidentiality issues","Recognise misrepresentation and conflicts","Recommend accountable responses"],"paragraphs":["Financial decisions affect employees, clients and investors. Unfair compensation can exploit staff; confidentiality breaches expose sensitive information; misrepresented budgets or accounts can induce decisions on false evidence. Explain the stakeholder harm in the particular case.","A conflict of interest exists when personal benefits may influence a business decision. Disclosure alone may not remove the threat; independent review, recusal or refusing the benefit can be needed. Do not conceal adverse variances merely to meet a bonus target.","A defensible response preserves evidence, corrects information, follows authorised escalation and applies professional requirements. Ethical reasoning should identify the issue, connect a principle to the facts and propose a specific action rather than merely saying the behaviour is bad."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A manager moves expected repairs out of the budget to obtain a performance bonus and awards work to a relative without disclosure. Evaluate.","steps":["Omitting expected costs misrepresents financial information.","The bonus incentive creates a self-interest threat.","Awarding work to a relative creates an undisclosed conflict.","Restore supportable costs and use independent procurement review/disclosure."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-business-ethics-q1"},{"prompt":"A business saves money by delaying agreed staff payments and shares customer data with an unapproved contractor. Recommend responses.","steps":["Unfair delayed compensation harms employees and may breach obligations.","Unauthorised sharing creates a confidentiality problem.","Correct payment practices and assess outstanding obligations.","Restrict data access and use authorised confidentiality controls."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-business-ethics-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Omit real expected costs whenever the profit target is difficult.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Budgets should fairly represent supportable expected costs.","Misrepresentation harms users and defeats planning and accountability.","Example evidence: Omitting expected costs misrepresents financial information."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-business-ethics-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is hiding a cost acceptable when it improves the reported budget?","o":["Budgets should fairly represent supportable expected costs.","Omit real expected costs whenever the profit target is difficult."],"a":0,"why":"Misrepresentation harms users and defeats planning and accountability."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Misrepresentation harms users and defeats planning and accountability.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Budgets should fairly represent supportable expected costs."},{"q":"In the model “Ethics analysis”, what belongs to “Issue”?","o":["Unfairness, confidentiality, misrepresentation or conflict","Who relies on or is harmed by the decision?","Correct, disclose and safeguard"],"a":0,"why":"Issue: Unfairness, confidentiality, misrepresentation or conflict. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Ethics analysis”, what belongs to “Stakeholder”?","o":["Unfairness, confidentiality, misrepresentation or conflict","Who relies on or is harmed by the decision?","Correct, disclose and safeguard"],"a":1,"why":"Stakeholder: Who relies on or is harmed by the decision?. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Ethics analysis","rows":[["Issue","Unfairness, confidentiality, misrepresentation or conflict"],["Stakeholder","Who relies on or is harmed by the decision?"],["Response","Correct, disclose and safeguard"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is hiding a cost acceptable when it improves the reported budget?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can identify unfair compensation and confidentiality issues.</li><li>I can recognise misrepresentation and conflicts.</li><li>I can recommend accountable responses.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Identify unfair compensation and confidentiality issues","html":"<p>Financial decisions affect employees, clients and investors. Unfair compensation can exploit staff; confidentiality breaches expose sensitive information; misrepresented budgets or accounts can induce decisions on false evidence. Explain the stakeholder harm in the particular case.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recognise misrepresentation and conflicts","html":"<p>A conflict of interest exists when personal benefits may influence a business decision. Disclosure alone may not remove the threat; independent review, recusal or refusing the benefit can be needed. Do not conceal adverse variances merely to meet a bonus target.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Recommend accountable responses","html":"<p>A defensible response preserves evidence, corrects information, follows authorised escalation and applies professional requirements. Ethical reasoning should identify the issue, connect a principle to the facts and propose a specific action rather than merely saying the behaviour is bad.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain ethics in management decisions using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-companies","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Public and large proprietary companies","subtitle":"Compare ownership and legal continuity · Explain liability and management separation · Distinguish listed from public","scope":"Compare ownership and legal continuity · Explain liability and management separation · Distinguish listed from public","outcomes":["Compare ownership and legal continuity","Explain liability and management separation","Distinguish listed from public"],"paragraphs":["A company is a separate legal person with continuity beyond changes in shareholders. Owners hold shares while directors manage or oversee management. Shareholders’ limited liability generally concerns amounts unpaid on shares; personal director obligations and guarantees are separate issues.","A proprietary company generally has no more than 50 non-employee members and cannot make ordinary public share offers; particular exceptions such as crowd-sourced funding have separate rules. A public company can have a broad shareholder base, but it is not necessarily listed on the ASX.","An ordinary proprietary company needs at least one Australian-resident director; public companies need at least three directors, at least two resident in Australia, and a resident secretary. Large proprietary status depends on statutory size tests, not simply having many shareholders. Reporting obligations differ by type and circumstances."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Compare a listed public company with a sole trader on legal entity, continuity and owner liability.","steps":["The company is a separate legal entity; the sole trader is not separately incorporated.","The company continues despite share transfers or owner changes.","Shareholders generally have limited liability for unpaid shares; the trader generally has unlimited liability.","Listing supports market transferability but does not remove company or director obligations."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-companies-q1"},{"prompt":"A learner claims all public companies are listed and a company needs only one director. Correct the claims.","steps":["Public companies may be listed or unlisted.","A public company requires at least three directors under the ordinary minimum rules.","At least two directors must ordinarily reside in Australia.","A proprietary company has different minimum requirements and cannot be treated as identical."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-companies-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every public company is automatically listed on the ASX.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Public and listed are different classifications.","Listing involves separate market requirements; an unlisted public company can still exist.","Example evidence: The company is a separate legal entity; the sole trader is not separately incorporated."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-companies-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is every public company listed on the ASX?","o":["Public and listed are different classifications.","Every public company is automatically listed on the ASX."],"a":0,"why":"Listing involves separate market requirements; an unlisted public company can still exist."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Listing involves separate market requirements; an unlisted public company can still exist.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Public and listed are different classifications."},{"q":"In the model “Company comparison”, what belongs to “Proprietary”?","o":["Restricted ordinary public fundraising; ownership limits","Broader fundraising/ownership; may be unlisted","Admitted to an exchange under listing rules"],"a":0,"why":"Proprietary: Restricted ordinary public fundraising; ownership limits. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Company comparison”, what belongs to “Public”?","o":["Restricted ordinary public fundraising; ownership limits","Broader fundraising/ownership; may be unlisted","Admitted to an exchange under listing rules"],"a":1,"why":"Public: Broader fundraising/ownership; may be unlisted. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Company comparison","rows":[["Proprietary","Restricted ordinary public fundraising; ownership limits"],["Public","Broader fundraising/ownership; may be unlisted"],["Listed","Admitted to an exchange under listing rules"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is every public company listed on the ASX?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can compare ownership and legal continuity.</li><li>I can explain liability and management separation.</li><li>I can distinguish listed from public.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Compare ownership and legal continuity","html":"<p>A company is a separate legal person with continuity beyond changes in shareholders. Owners hold shares while directors manage or oversee management. Shareholders’ limited liability generally concerns amounts unpaid on shares; personal director obligations and guarantees are separate issues.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain liability and management separation","html":"<p>A proprietary company generally has no more than 50 non-employee members and cannot make ordinary public share offers; particular exceptions such as crowd-sourced funding have separate rules. A public company can have a broad shareholder base, but it is not necessarily listed on the ASX.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish listed from public","html":"<p>An ordinary proprietary company needs at least one Australian-resident director; public companies need at least three directors, at least two resident in Australia, and a resident secretary. Large proprietary status depends on statutory size tests, not simply having many shareholders. Reporting obligations differ by type and circumstances.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain public and large proprietary companies using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-regulators","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"ASIC, AASB, IASB, ISSB and ASX","subtitle":"Distinguish regulation from standard setting · Explain Australian reporting standards · Connect standards to investor confidence","scope":"Distinguish regulation from standard setting · Explain Australian reporting standards · Connect standards to investor confidence","outcomes":["Distinguish regulation from standard setting","Explain Australian reporting standards","Connect standards to investor confidence"],"paragraphs":["ASIC administers and enforces corporate and financial-services requirements; the AASB sets Australian accounting and sustainability reporting standards. The IASB develops international financial reporting standards, while the ISSB develops international sustainability disclosure standards.","The ASX operates a securities market and its listing framework. AASB standards support comparable reporting by Australian entities and help directors discharge reporting obligations. They protect users by requiring transparent recognition, measurement and disclosure rather than guaranteeing an investment return.","Do not interchange these bodies in an exam answer. Name the body, its function and how that function affects the particular case. International standards influence Australian requirements but the applicable Australian standards and law govern the entity’s reporting."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A company asks who sets Australian accounting standards, who regulates corporate compliance and who supplies international sustainability standards. Answer.","steps":["AASB sets Australian accounting/reporting standards.","ASIC administers and enforces relevant corporate requirements.","ISSB develops international sustainability disclosure standards.","Distinguish these roles from ASX market/listing requirements."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-regulators-q1"},{"prompt":"Explain how standards protect an investor comparing two Australian companies.","steps":["Standards promote consistent recognition and presentation.","Disclosure helps users understand assumptions and material risks.","Comparability and reporting confidence support capital-market decisions.","Standards do not guarantee company success or eliminate judgement."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-regulators-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “ASIC, AASB and ASX perform exactly the same function.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["AASB sets standards; ASIC has regulatory and enforcement functions.","Different institutions address standards, enforcement and market requirements.","Example evidence: AASB sets Australian accounting/reporting standards."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-regulators-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does ASIC set all Australian accounting standards?","o":["AASB sets standards; ASIC has regulatory and enforcement functions.","ASIC, AASB and ASX perform exactly the same function."],"a":0,"why":"Different institutions address standards, enforcement and market requirements."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Different institutions address standards, enforcement and market requirements.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"AASB sets standards; ASIC has regulatory and enforcement functions."},{"q":"In the model “Institution map”, what belongs to “AASB”?","o":["Australian reporting standards","International financial reporting standards","International sustainability standards","Corporate regulation/enforcement","Market and listing framework"],"a":0,"why":"AASB: Australian reporting standards. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Institution map”, what belongs to “IASB”?","o":["Australian reporting standards","International financial reporting standards","International sustainability standards","Corporate regulation/enforcement","Market and listing framework"],"a":1,"why":"IASB: International financial reporting standards. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Institution map","rows":[["AASB","Australian reporting standards"],["IASB","International financial reporting standards"],["ISSB","International sustainability standards"],["ASIC","Corporate regulation/enforcement"],["ASX","Market and listing framework"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does ASIC set all Australian accounting standards?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can distinguish regulation from standard setting.</li><li>I can explain Australian reporting standards.</li><li>I can connect standards to investor confidence.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish regulation from standard setting","html":"<p>ASIC administers and enforces corporate and financial-services requirements; the AASB sets Australian accounting and sustainability reporting standards. The IASB develops international financial reporting standards, while the ISSB develops international sustainability disclosure standards.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain Australian reporting standards","html":"<p>The ASX operates a securities market and its listing framework. AASB standards support comparable reporting by Australian entities and help directors discharge reporting obligations. They protect users by requiring transparent <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">recognition</strong>, measurement and disclosure rather than guaranteeing an investment return.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Connect standards to investor confidence","html":"<p>Do not interchange these bodies in an exam answer. Name the body, its function and how that function affects the particular case. International standards influence Australian requirements but the applicable Australian standards and law govern the entity’s reporting.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain asic, aasb, iasb, issb and asx using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-governance","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Directors, constitutions and replaceable rules","subtitle":"Explain company governance arrangements · Apply directors’ responsibilities · Distinguish a constitution from reporting standards","scope":"Explain company governance arrangements · Apply directors’ responsibilities · Distinguish a constitution from reporting standards","outcomes":["Explain company governance arrangements","Apply directors’ responsibilities","Distinguish a constitution from reporting standards"],"paragraphs":["A written constitution sets agreed rules for company governance. Replaceable rules in the Corporations Act can apply where not displaced, subject to applicable rules. They concern matters such as decision procedures and share/member rights; they are not an income-statement format.","Directors have responsibilities including care and diligence, acting in good faith for proper purposes and avoiding improper use of position or information. Maintaining financial records and monitoring solvency support these responsibilities. Delegating bookkeeping does not remove oversight obligations.","The Corporations Act 2001 governs formation and operation. Apply duties to evidence: a director ignoring repeated overdue debts and unreliable records should investigate rather than relying on an optimistic profit estimate. Explain a safeguard or action, not just the name of the law."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A director approves borrowing without reviewing cash forecasts despite overdue suppliers. Identify duties and action.","steps":["Care and diligence require informed consideration of financial evidence.","The director should assess due debts, finance and solvency rather than profit alone.","Obtain reliable records and investigate warnings.","Use independent advice and appropriate action if insolvency risk is established."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-governance-q1"},{"prompt":"A student calls a constitution the mandatory accounting-standard book. Correct and explain.","steps":["A constitution concerns company governance arrangements.","Replaceable rules are statutory governance rules that may apply.","Accounting standards govern financial reporting requirements.","The Corporations Act interacts with both but the concepts are distinct."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-governance-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Delegating bookkeeping transfers all director responsibilities to the accountant.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Directors retain governance responsibilities when work is delegated.","Oversight requires reliable information and informed decisions despite delegation.","Example evidence: Care and diligence require informed consideration of financial evidence."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-governance-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does hiring an accountant remove directors’ oversight duties?","o":["Directors retain governance responsibilities when work is delegated.","Delegating bookkeeping transfers all director responsibilities to the accountant."],"a":0,"why":"Oversight requires reliable information and informed decisions despite delegation."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Oversight requires reliable information and informed decisions despite delegation.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Directors retain governance responsibilities when work is delegated."},{"q":"In the model “Governance layers”, what belongs to “Constitution / replaceable rules”?","o":["Internal decision arrangements","Conduct and oversight","Financial reporting requirements"],"a":0,"why":"Constitution / replaceable rules: Internal decision arrangements. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Governance layers”, what belongs to “Directors’ duties”?","o":["Internal decision arrangements","Conduct and oversight","Financial reporting requirements"],"a":1,"why":"Directors’ duties: Conduct and oversight. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Governance layers","rows":[["Constitution / replaceable rules","Internal decision arrangements"],["Directors’ duties","Conduct and oversight"],["Accounting standards","Financial reporting requirements"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does hiring an accountant remove directors’ oversight duties?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can explain company governance arrangements.</li><li>I can apply directors’ responsibilities.</li><li>I can distinguish a constitution from reporting standards.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain company governance arrangements","html":"<p>A written constitution sets agreed rules for company governance. Replaceable rules in the Corporations Act can apply where not displaced, subject to applicable rules. They concern matters such as decision procedures and share/member rights; they are not an income-statement format.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply directors’ responsibilities","html":"<p>Directors have responsibilities including care and diligence, acting in good faith for proper purposes and avoiding improper use of position or information. Maintaining financial records and monitoring solvency support these responsibilities. Delegating bookkeeping does not remove oversight obligations.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish a constitution from reporting standards","html":"<p>The Corporations Act 2001 governs formation and operation. Apply duties to evidence: a director ignoring repeated overdue debts and unreliable records should investigate rather than relying on an optimistic <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> estimate. Explain a safeguard or action, not just the name of the law.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain directors, constitutions and replaceable rules using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-shareholder-rights","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Shareholder rights and prospectus information","subtitle":"Explain ordinary shareholder interests · Distinguish ownership from guaranteed income · Evaluate fundraising disclosure","scope":"Explain ordinary shareholder interests · Distinguish ownership from guaranteed income · Evaluate fundraising disclosure","outcomes":["Explain ordinary shareholder interests","Distinguish ownership from guaranteed income","Evaluate fundraising disclosure"],"paragraphs":["Ordinary shareholders may exercise voting and information rights and receive dividends when validly determined, subject to share terms and law. They hold a residual claim rather than a guaranteed repayment like a creditor. Selling a share on the secondary market transfers ownership without paying cash to the company.","A prospectus communicates material information for an applicable public fundraising offer, including business, financial and risk information. It supports informed decisions; it does not guarantee the forecast or value of the shares. Some offers have exemptions or other disclosure forms.","Evaluate information quality and conflicts. An investor should compare risk, funding purpose and reported performance rather than using an advertised dividend alone. Distinguish initial share issue proceeds received by the company from later investor-to-investor transactions."],"questions":[{"prompt":"An investor buys newly issued shares for $5,000, then later sells them to another investor. Explain cash flows and rights.","steps":["The initial issue supplies $5,000 to the company for equity.","The later secondary-market sale normally transfers cash between investors.","Ordinary shareholder rights include applicable voting/information rights.","Dividends and future share value are not guaranteed."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-shareholder-rights-q1"},{"prompt":"A prospectus highlights growth forecasts but omits a material debt-refinancing risk. Evaluate.","steps":["The omitted risk may influence an investment decision.","Fundraising disclosure should communicate material risks alongside forecasts.","A forecast is conditional and not a guaranteed return.","Correct disclosure and reliable supporting information are needed."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-shareholder-rights-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every share sale on the exchange pays its full amount to the company.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["It normally transfers ownership between investors.","The company raises funds from its own issue; later trading is between investors.","Example evidence: The initial issue supplies $5,000 to the company for equity."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-shareholder-rights-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a secondary-market share sale normally raise new company cash?","o":["It normally transfers ownership between investors.","Every share sale on the exchange pays its full amount to the company."],"a":0,"why":"The company raises funds from its own issue; later trading is between investors."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The company raises funds from its own issue; later trading is between investors.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"It normally transfers ownership between investors."},{"q":"In the model “Two share transactions”, what belongs to “New issue”?","o":["Investor pays company; equity raised","Investor pays another investor","Rights and residual risk move with shares"],"a":0,"why":"New issue: Investor pays company; equity raised. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Two share transactions”, what belongs to “Secondary trade”?","o":["Investor pays company; equity raised","Investor pays another investor","Rights and residual risk move with shares"],"a":1,"why":"Secondary trade: Investor pays another investor. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Two share transactions","rows":[["New issue","Investor pays company; equity raised"],["Secondary trade","Investor pays another investor"],["Ownership","Rights and residual risk move with shares"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a secondary-market share sale normally raise new company cash?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can explain ordinary shareholder interests.</li><li>I can distinguish ownership from guaranteed income.</li><li>I can evaluate fundraising disclosure.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain ordinary shareholder interests","html":"<p>Ordinary shareholders may exercise voting and information rights and receive dividends when validly determined, subject to share terms and law. They hold a residual claim rather than a guaranteed repayment like a creditor. Selling a share on the secondary market transfers ownership without paying cash to the company.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish ownership from guaranteed income","html":"<p>A prospectus communicates material information for an applicable public fundraising offer, including business, financial and risk information. It supports informed decisions; it does not guarantee the forecast or value of the shares. Some offers have exemptions or other disclosure forms.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate fundraising disclosure","html":"<p>Evaluate information quality and conflicts. An investor should compare risk, funding purpose and reported performance rather than using an advertised dividend alone. Distinguish initial share issue proceeds received by the company from later investor-to-investor transactions.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain shareholder rights and prospectus information using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-quality","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Reporting entities and useful financial information","subtitle":"Explain general-purpose reporting users · Apply fundamental characteristics · Distinguish enhancing characteristics and materiality","scope":"Explain general-purpose reporting users · Apply fundamental characteristics · Distinguish enhancing characteristics and materiality","outcomes":["Explain general-purpose reporting users","Apply fundamental characteristics","Distinguish enhancing characteristics and materiality"],"paragraphs":["General-purpose financial reports help existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors make resource decisions and assess stewardship. A reporting entity need not always correspond to one legal company; consolidated groups can form a reporting boundary.","Relevance means information can affect decisions, including predictive or confirmatory value. Faithful representation requires a complete, neutral and appropriately error-free depiction of the economic phenomenon. Uncertain estimates can still be useful when methods and uncertainty are transparently represented.","Comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability enhance usefulness. Materiality is entity-specific: size, nature and context matter. A small transaction with a related party may matter despite its amount; do not apply an invented universal dollar cutoff."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A company delays reliable reports for nine months and omits a small related-party transaction. Apply characteristics.","steps":["Delay weakens timeliness and decision usefulness.","Reliable measurement alone does not compensate for late reporting.","Related-party nature may make a small transaction material.","Explain relevant disclosure rather than applying a size-only threshold."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-quality-q1"},{"prompt":"Two companies use different asset-life estimates. Explain comparability and faithful representation without insisting on identical assumptions.","steps":["Comparable reports explain policies and estimates so differences can be understood.","Different genuine consumption patterns may justify different lives.","Faithful representation requires supportable estimates and disclosure of uncertainty.","Identical numbers are not required when underlying facts differ."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-quality-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Anything below a fixed dollar amount is always immaterial.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Nature and context can make information material even at a small amount.","Users may be influenced by the kind of transaction as well as its size.","Example evidence: Delay weakens timeliness and decision usefulness."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-quality-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does materiality depend only on amount?","o":["Nature and context can make information material even at a small amount.","Anything below a fixed dollar amount is always immaterial."],"a":0,"why":"Users may be influenced by the kind of transaction as well as its size."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Users may be influenced by the kind of transaction as well as its size.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Nature and context can make information material even at a small amount."},{"q":"In the model “Information quality”, what belongs to “Fundamental”?","o":["Relevance; faithful representation","Comparability; verifiability; timeliness; understandability","Entity-specific amount, nature and context"],"a":0,"why":"Fundamental: Relevance; faithful representation. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Information quality”, what belongs to “Enhancing”?","o":["Relevance; faithful representation","Comparability; verifiability; timeliness; understandability","Entity-specific amount, nature and context"],"a":1,"why":"Enhancing: Comparability; verifiability; timeliness; understandability. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Information quality","rows":[["Fundamental","Relevance; faithful representation"],["Enhancing","Comparability; verifiability; timeliness; understandability"],["Materiality","Entity-specific amount, nature and context"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does materiality depend only on amount?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can explain general-purpose reporting users.</li><li>I can apply fundamental characteristics.</li><li>I can distinguish enhancing characteristics and materiality.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain general-purpose reporting users","html":"<p>General-purpose financial reports help existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors make resource decisions and assess stewardship. A reporting entity need not always correspond to one legal company; consolidated groups can form a reporting boundary.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply fundamental characteristics","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Relevance</strong> means information can affect decisions, including predictive or confirmatory value. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Faithful representation</strong> requires a complete, neutral and appropriately error-free depiction of the economic phenomenon. Uncertain estimates can still be useful when methods and uncertainty are transparently represented.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish enhancing characteristics and materiality","html":"<p>Comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability enhance usefulness. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Materiality</strong> is entity-specific: size, nature and context matter. A small transaction with a related party may matter despite its amount; do not apply an invented universal dollar cutoff.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain reporting entities and useful financial information using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-corporate-recognition","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Recognition judgements in corporate reports","subtitle":"Apply asset and liability definitions · Evaluate recognition usefulness · Distinguish disclosure and recognition","scope":"Apply asset and liability definitions · Evaluate recognition usefulness · Distinguish disclosure and recognition","outcomes":["Apply asset and liability definitions","Evaluate recognition usefulness","Distinguish disclosure and recognition"],"paragraphs":["The Conceptual Framework defines assets as present controlled economic resources from past events and liabilities as present obligations to transfer resources from past events. Recognition captures a defined item in statements with a monetary amount. The definition and recognition assessment are separate steps.","Recognition should provide relevant information and faithful representation, considering uncertainty and cost. A fixed blanket probability threshold is not a complete statement of the modern Framework. Particular standards may specify additional rules for particular transactions.","Disclosure can explain uncertainties and risks without creating an asset for every hoped-for benefit. Evaluate the case evidence, the applicable standard and the effect on users. Owner contributions are equity transactions rather than income even when they increase assets."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A signed completed-sale invoice gives a company an enforceable $10,000 claim; a proposed expansion may produce future sales. Compare.","steps":["The invoice supports a present receivable resource from completed work.","Recognise revenue and receivable where applicable requirements are met.","Expected expansion sales alone are not present customer receivables.","Forecast information may be disclosed or used in plans without recognition as earned income."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-corporate-recognition-q1"},{"prompt":"A company receives $50,000 from issuing ordinary shares. Explain why it is not revenue.","steps":["Cash assets increase $50,000.","Share capital/equity increases $50,000.","The increase comes from owners acting as owners.","The Framework distinguishes owner contributions from income."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-corporate-recognition-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every equity increase must be revenue in the income statement.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Owner contributions increase equity without being income.","The source and nature of the equity change determine its reporting treatment.","Example evidence: The invoice supports a present receivable resource from completed work."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-corporate-recognition-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does every increase in equity constitute income?","o":["Owner contributions increase equity without being income.","Every equity increase must be revenue in the income statement."],"a":0,"why":"The source and nature of the equity change determine its reporting treatment."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The source and nature of the equity change determine its reporting treatment.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Owner contributions increase equity without being income."},{"q":"In the model “Recognition decision”, what belongs to “Present element?”?","o":["Controlled resource or obligation from past events","Relevance and faithful representation","Apply the relevant standard"],"a":0,"why":"Present element?: Controlled resource or obligation from past events. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Recognition decision”, what belongs to “Useful depiction?”?","o":["Controlled resource or obligation from past events","Relevance and faithful representation","Apply the relevant standard"],"a":1,"why":"Useful depiction?: Relevance and faithful representation. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Recognition decision","rows":[["Present element?","Controlled resource or obligation from past events"],["Useful depiction?","Relevance and faithful representation"],["Specific requirements?","Apply the relevant standard"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does every increase in equity constitute income?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply asset and liability definitions.</li><li>I can evaluate recognition usefulness.</li><li>I can distinguish disclosure and recognition.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply asset and liability definitions","html":"<p>The Conceptual Framework defines <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> as present controlled economic resources from past events and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> as present obligations to transfer resources from past events. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Recognition</strong> captures a defined item in statements with a monetary amount. The definition and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">recognition</strong> assessment are separate steps.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate recognition usefulness","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Recognition</strong> should provide relevant information and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">faithful representation</strong>, considering uncertainty and cost. A fixed blanket probability threshold is not a complete statement of the modern Framework. Particular standards may specify additional rules for particular transactions.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish disclosure and recognition","html":"<p>Disclosure can explain uncertainties and risks without creating an asset for every hoped-for benefit. Evaluate the case evidence, the applicable standard and the effect on users. Owner contributions are <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> transactions rather than income even when they increase <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain recognition judgements in corporate reports using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-share-issue","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Ordinary shares payable in full on application","subtitle":"Record receipt into application account · Transfer to share capital on issue · Explain equity and cash effects","scope":"Record receipt into application account · Transfer to share capital on issue · Explain equity and cash effects","outcomes":["Record receipt into application account","Transfer to share capital on issue","Explain equity and cash effects"],"paragraphs":["For shares payable in full on application, the application account temporarily gathers funds awaiting issue. Receipt debits Bank and credits Share application. When shares are issued/allotted, debit Share application and credit Share capital for the accepted amount.","This syllabus scope does not require instalment calls or partly paid application/allotment schedules. Read the quantity and issue price: total funds are number of shares multiplied by price per share. Share capital is an equity balance rather than income.","After a fully accepted issue, the application account clears and equity increases by the gross issue amount before direct share issue costs. If a task provides refunds or rejected applications, account for those explicitly rather than assuming all funds become capital."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Issue 10,000 ordinary shares at $2, payable in full on application; all accepted. Record receipt and allotment.","steps":["Application funds = 10,000×2 = $20,000.","Debit Bank $20,000; credit Share application $20,000.","Debit Share application $20,000; credit Share capital $20,000.","The application account clears; equity increases $20,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-share-issue-q1"},{"prompt":"A company accepts 5,000 shares at $3 paid fully on application. Record the two stages and profit effect.","steps":["Funds received = $15,000.","Debit Bank and credit Share application $15,000.","On issue debit Share application and credit Share capital $15,000.","Profit is unchanged because this is owner financing."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-share-issue-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Credit Sales revenue for new ordinary share proceeds.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["A share issue is an equity financing transaction.","Owners contribute capital; the company has not earned revenue from selling its own equity.","Example evidence: Application funds = 10,000×2 = $20,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-share-issue-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are ordinary share proceeds Sales revenue?","o":["A share issue is an equity financing transaction.","Credit Sales revenue for new ordinary share proceeds."],"a":0,"why":"Owners contribute capital; the company has not earned revenue from selling its own equity."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Owners contribute capital; the company has not earned revenue from selling its own equity.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"A share issue is an equity financing transaction."},{"q":"In the model “Share issue flow”, what belongs to “Application”?","o":["Bank debit; application credit","Application debit; share capital credit","Cash and equity increase"],"a":0,"why":"Application: Bank debit; application credit. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Share issue flow”, what belongs to “Allotment”?","o":["Bank debit; application credit","Application debit; share capital credit","Cash and equity increase"],"a":1,"why":"Allotment: Application debit; share capital credit. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Share issue flow","rows":[["Application","Bank debit; application credit"],["Allotment","Application debit; share capital credit"],["Completed issue","Cash and equity increase"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are ordinary share proceeds Sales revenue?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can record receipt into application account.</li><li>I can transfer to share capital on issue.</li><li>I can explain equity and cash effects.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record receipt into application account","html":"<p>For shares payable in full on application, the application account temporarily gathers funds awaiting issue. Receipt debits Bank and credits Share application. When shares are issued/allotted, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> Share application and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> Share capital for the accepted amount.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Transfer to share capital on issue","html":"<p>This syllabus scope does not require instalment calls or partly paid application/allotment schedules. Read the quantity and issue price: total funds are number of shares multiplied by price per share. Share capital is an <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> balance rather than income.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain equity and cash effects","html":"<p>After a fully accepted issue, the application account clears and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> increases by the gross issue amount before direct share issue costs. If a task provides refunds or rejected applications, account for those explicitly rather than assuming all funds become capital.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain ordinary shares payable in full on application using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-share-costs","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Share issue costs and preliminary expenses","subtitle":"Deduct direct issue costs from equity · Distinguish incorporation expenses · Explain net funds raised","scope":"Deduct direct issue costs from equity · Distinguish incorporation expenses · Explain net funds raised","outcomes":["Deduct direct issue costs from equity","Distinguish incorporation expenses","Explain net funds raised"],"paragraphs":["Costs directly attributable to an equity issue are deducted from equity under the applicable reporting treatment, subject to any stated tax effect. They do not become an ordinary operating expense simply because paid in cash. Classroom examples here state that no tax effect is required.","Preliminary/incorporation expenses are different from costs directly attributable to a share issue. Do not automatically create a permanent asset from establishment spending; record the specified preliminary expense treatment and distinguish any cost governed by another standard.","Calculate gross proceeds, direct issue costs and net funds separately. Cash and share capital/equity should reconcile. The share count does not decrease merely because a broker or legal adviser was paid."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Gross share proceeds $100,000; direct issue costs $4,000 cash. Ignore tax effects. Record costs and find net equity raised.","steps":["Debit Share capital/equity $4,000.","Credit Bank $4,000.","Net equity raised = 100,000 − 4,000 = $96,000.","The directly attributable issue cost is not an ordinary operating expense."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-share-costs-q1"},{"prompt":"Pay direct share issue cost $2,000 and unrelated incorporation preliminary expense $1,000. Ignore tax effects. Record.","steps":["Debit Share capital $2,000; credit Bank $2,000.","Debit Preliminary expenses $1,000; credit Bank $1,000.","Total cash outflow is $3,000.","Only the preliminary expense reduces current profit under the stated treatment."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-share-costs-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every company-startup cost must reduce share capital.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Distinguish direct equity-issue costs from preliminary expenses.","Costs have different reporting treatment according to their nature and applicable requirements.","Example evidence: Debit Share capital/equity $4,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-share-costs-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are all establishment payments deducted from share capital?","o":["Distinguish direct equity-issue costs from preliminary expenses.","Every company-startup cost must reduce share capital."],"a":0,"why":"Costs have different reporting treatment according to their nature and applicable requirements."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Costs have different reporting treatment according to their nature and applicable requirements.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Distinguish direct equity-issue costs from preliminary expenses."},{"q":"In the model “Issue proceeds bridge”, what belongs to “Gross proceeds”?","o":["$100,000","$4,000 equity deduction","$96,000"],"a":0,"why":"Gross proceeds: $100,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Issue proceeds bridge”, what belongs to “Direct issue costs”?","o":["$100,000","$4,000 equity deduction","$96,000"],"a":1,"why":"Direct issue costs: $4,000 equity deduction. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Issue proceeds bridge","rows":[["Gross proceeds","$100,000"],["Direct issue costs","$4,000 equity deduction"],["Net funds/equity","$96,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are all establishment payments deducted from share capital?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can deduct direct issue costs from equity.</li><li>I can distinguish incorporation expenses.</li><li>I can explain net funds raised.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Deduct direct issue costs from equity","html":"<p>Costs directly attributable to an <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> issue are deducted from <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> under the applicable reporting treatment, subject to any stated tax effect. They do not become an ordinary operating expense simply because paid in cash. Classroom examples here state that no tax effect is required.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish incorporation expenses","html":"<p>Preliminary/incorporation expenses are different from costs directly attributable to a share issue. Do not automatically create a permanent asset from establishment spending; record the specified preliminary expense treatment and distinguish any cost governed by another standard.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain net funds raised","html":"<p>Calculate gross proceeds, direct issue costs and net funds separately. Cash and share capital/<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> should reconcile. The share count does not decrease merely because a broker or legal adviser was paid.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain share issue costs and preliminary expenses using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-bonus-shares","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Bonus share issues","subtitle":"Record transfer within equity · Explain share-count and cash effects · Avoid treating bonus shares as profit","scope":"Record transfer within equity · Explain share-count and cash effects · Avoid treating bonus shares as profit","outcomes":["Record transfer within equity","Explain share-count and cash effects","Avoid treating bonus shares as profit"],"paragraphs":["A bonus issue capitalises a stated reserve or retained earnings into share capital. Debit the specified source equity account and credit Share capital. It increases the number of shares and changes equity composition without adding total equity or cash.","Use the number of bonus shares and amount per share stated by the task. A bonus issue is not a new cash contribution and is not an expense. The source must have the relevant balance and approval conditions assumed in the question.","Per-share measures can be affected because the denominator changes. Use supplied weighted-share information in ratio tasks and apply any required bonus adjustment rather than treating every share increase as a cash issue. This lesson focuses on the journal and equity transfer."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A company issues 10,000 bonus shares at a stated $1 amount from General reserve. Record and explain.","steps":["Transfer amount = 10,000×1 = $10,000.","Debit General reserve $10,000.","Credit Share capital $10,000.","Total equity and Bank are unchanged; share count rises 10,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-bonus-shares-q1"},{"prompt":"A bonus issue capitalises $15,000 of retained earnings. Share capital was $80,000 and retained earnings $40,000. Find new balances.","steps":["Debit Retained earnings $15,000; credit Share capital $15,000.","Share capital becomes $95,000.","Retained earnings becomes $25,000.","Combined equity remains $120,000 and no cash is received."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-bonus-shares-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every bonus share gives the company new cash proceeds.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["A bonus issue transfers amounts within equity without new cash.","Existing equity is reclassified into share capital.","Example evidence: Transfer amount = 10,000×1 = $10,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-bonus-shares-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a bonus issue automatically bring cash into the company?","o":["A bonus issue transfers amounts within equity without new cash.","Every bonus share gives the company new cash proceeds."],"a":0,"why":"Existing equity is reclassified into share capital."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Existing equity is reclassified into share capital.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"A bonus issue transfers amounts within equity without new cash."}],"model":{"title":"Bonus equity bridge","rows":[["Reserve/retained earnings","Decrease $10,000"],["Share capital","Increase $10,000"],["Total equity","Unchanged"],["Cash","Unchanged"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a bonus issue automatically bring cash into the company?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can record transfer within equity.</li><li>I can explain share-count and cash effects.</li><li>I can avoid treating bonus shares as profit.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record transfer within equity","html":"<p>A bonus issue capitalises a stated reserve or <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong> into share capital. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Debit</strong> the specified source <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> account and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> Share capital. It increases the number of shares and changes <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> composition without adding total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> or cash.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain share-count and cash effects","html":"<p>Use the number of bonus shares and amount per share stated by the task. A bonus issue is not a new cash <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">contribution</strong> and is not an expense. The source must have the relevant balance and approval conditions assumed in the question.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Avoid treating bonus shares as profit","html":"<p>Per-share measures can be affected because the denominator changes. Use supplied weighted-share information in ratio tasks and apply any required bonus adjustment rather than treating every share increase as a cash issue. This lesson focuses on the journal and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> transfer.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain bonus share issues using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-dividends","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Interim and final cash dividends","subtitle":"Distinguish declaration from payment · Record dividend payable · Explain retained earnings and cash effects","scope":"Distinguish declaration from payment · Record dividend payable · Explain retained earnings and cash effects","outcomes":["Distinguish declaration from payment","Record dividend payable","Explain retained earnings and cash effects"],"paragraphs":["A dividend distributes equity to shareholders rather than representing an operating expense. When a dividend creates a present obligation under the stated facts, debit Retained earnings or the temporary Dividend account and credit Dividend payable. Payment later clears the liability and reduces Bank.","An interim dividend may be paid during the year; a final dividend may be declared or paid at another date. Read the wording and reporting date carefully. A proposed dividend not creating a present obligation at balance day is not automatically a liability.","The examples explicitly state a valid declared obligation. Temporary dividend accounts close to Retained earnings. Dividends reduce retained earnings but not the current profit figure; payment timing changes cash and liabilities."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A company declares a valid $8,000 cash dividend before year end, payable later. Record declaration and payment.","steps":["Declaration: debit Retained earnings/Dividend $8,000.","Credit Dividend payable $8,000.","Payment: debit Dividend payable $8,000; credit Bank $8,000.","Profit is unchanged; retained earnings decrease."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-dividends-q1"},{"prompt":"Pay interim dividend $3,000 immediately and declare valid final dividend $5,000 unpaid at year end. Explain balances using direct retained-earnings entries.","steps":["Debit Retained earnings $3,000; credit Bank $3,000.","Debit Retained earnings $5,000; credit Dividend payable $5,000.","Retained earnings falls $8,000.","Year-end liability is $5,000; cash has fallen only $3,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-dividends-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Deduct dividends as operating expenses when calculating profit.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Dividends are distributions from equity rather than operating expenses.","Shareholder distributions occur after measuring earned profit.","Example evidence: Declaration: debit Retained earnings/Dividend $8,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-dividends-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is a declared dividend an operating expense?","o":["Dividends are distributions from equity rather than operating expenses.","Deduct dividends as operating expenses when calculating profit."],"a":0,"why":"Shareholder distributions occur after measuring earned profit."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Shareholder distributions occur after measuring earned profit.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Dividends are distributions from equity rather than operating expenses."},{"q":"In the model “Dividend timing”, what belongs to “Declaration with obligation”?","o":["Equity down; liability up","Liability down; Bank down","No dividend expense"],"a":0,"why":"Declaration with obligation: Equity down; liability up. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Dividend timing”, what belongs to “Payment”?","o":["Equity down; liability up","Liability down; Bank down","No dividend expense"],"a":1,"why":"Payment: Liability down; Bank down. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Dividend timing","rows":[["Declaration with obligation","Equity down; liability up"],["Payment","Liability down; Bank down"],["Profit","No dividend expense"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is a declared dividend an operating expense?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can distinguish declaration from payment.</li><li>I can record dividend payable.</li><li>I can explain retained earnings and cash effects.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish declaration from payment","html":"<p>A dividend distributes <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> to shareholders rather than representing an operating expense. When a dividend creates a present obligation under the stated facts, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Retained earnings</strong> or the temporary Dividend account and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> Dividend payable. Payment later clears the liability and reduces Bank.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record dividend payable","html":"<p>An interim dividend may be paid during the year; a final dividend may be declared or paid at another date. Read the wording and reporting date carefully. A proposed dividend not creating a present obligation at balance day is not automatically a liability.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain retained earnings and cash effects","html":"<p>The examples explicitly state a valid declared obligation. Temporary dividend accounts close to <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Retained earnings</strong>. Dividends reduce <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong> but not the current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> figure; payment timing changes cash and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain interim and final cash dividends using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-retained-earnings","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Retained earnings ledger","subtitle":"Reconcile profit, dividends and transfers · Handle debit opening balances · Separate retained earnings from cash","scope":"Reconcile profit, dividends and transfers · Handle debit opening balances · Separate retained earnings from cash","outcomes":["Reconcile profit, dividends and transfers","Handle debit opening balances","Separate retained earnings from cash"],"paragraphs":["Retained earnings accumulates profits less losses and distributions, adjusted for specified transfers. A credit balance represents accumulated retained profit; a debit opening balance represents accumulated losses. Treat the debit opening amount as negative in a numerical reconciliation.","Closing retained earnings = opening retained earnings + profit − loss − dividends − transfers to reserve + transfers from reserve, with no double counting of profit/loss. A transfer to General reserve changes equity composition but not total equity.","Retained earnings is not a cash fund. The company may have invested accumulated profits in inventory or equipment. Show a ledger or reconciliation with clear directions so a debit opening balance cannot accidentally be added as a positive profit."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Opening retained earnings $10,000 debit; current profit $30,000, dividends $8,000 and transfer to reserve $5,000. Find closing balance.","steps":["Opening accumulated loss = −$10,000.","Closing = −10,000 + 30,000 − 8,000 − 5,000.","Closing retained earnings = $7,000 credit.","The transfer changes equity composition, not total equity."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-retained-earnings-q1"},{"prompt":"Opening retained earnings $40,000 credit; loss $6,000, dividends $4,000 and transfer from reserve $3,000. Calculate closing.","steps":["Start with $40,000.","Subtract loss $6,000 and dividends $4,000.","Add reserve transfer $3,000.","Closing retained earnings = $33,000 credit."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-retained-earnings-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “All retained earnings must be held as cash in Bank.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Retained earnings is an equity balance, not a separate cash fund.","Accumulated profits can be invested in many assets or used to reduce liabilities.","Example evidence: Opening accumulated loss = −$10,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-retained-earnings-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does retained earnings equal money held in Bank?","o":["Retained earnings is an equity balance, not a separate cash fund.","All retained earnings must be held as cash in Bank."],"a":0,"why":"Accumulated profits can be invested in many assets or used to reduce liabilities."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Accumulated profits can be invested in many assets or used to reduce liabilities.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Retained earnings is an equity balance, not a separate cash fund."},{"q":"In the model “Retained-earnings reconciliation”, what belongs to “Opening debit”?","o":["−$10,000","+$30,000","−$13,000","$7,000"],"a":0,"why":"Opening debit: −$10,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Retained-earnings reconciliation”, what belongs to “Profit”?","o":["−$10,000","+$30,000","−$13,000","$7,000"],"a":1,"why":"Profit: +$30,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Retained-earnings reconciliation","rows":[["Opening debit","−$10,000"],["Profit","+$30,000"],["Dividends and reserve","−$13,000"],["Closing credit","$7,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does retained earnings equal money held in Bank?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can reconcile profit, dividends and transfers.</li><li>I can handle debit opening balances.</li><li>I can separate retained earnings from cash.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile profit, dividends and transfers","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Retained earnings</strong> accumulates profits less losses and distributions, adjusted for specified transfers. A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> balance represents accumulated retained <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>; a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> opening balance represents accumulated losses. Treat the <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> opening amount as negative in a numerical reconciliation.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Handle debit opening balances","html":"<p>Closing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong> = opening <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong> + <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> − loss − dividends − transfers to reserve + transfers from reserve, with no double counting of <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>/loss. A transfer to General reserve changes <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> composition but not total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong>.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate retained earnings from cash","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Retained earnings</strong> is not a cash fund. The company may have invested accumulated profits in <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> or equipment. Show a ledger or reconciliation with clear directions so a <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> opening balance cannot accidentally be added as a positive <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain retained earnings ledger using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-reserves","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"General reserves and equity transfers","subtitle":"Record transfers to and from reserves · Distinguish restrictions and composition · Explain total-equity invariance","scope":"Record transfers to and from reserves · Distinguish restrictions and composition · Explain total-equity invariance","outcomes":["Record transfers to and from reserves","Distinguish restrictions and composition","Explain total-equity invariance"],"paragraphs":["A General reserve identifies an allocation within equity. Transferring retained earnings to it debits Retained earnings and credits General reserve. A transfer back reverses the entry. No Bank movement or new profit occurs merely from the transfer.","Reserves can have different origins and purposes. A revaluation surplus is different from a General reserve created from retained earnings. Describe the actual source and any stated restrictions; do not assume every reserve is freely distributable.","In the statement of changes in equity, internal transfers appear as decreases and increases in the respective columns with zero total change. The word reserve does not mean cash has been put aside. Explain funding separately if the company wants to earmark cash."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Transfer $6,000 from retained earnings $25,000 to General reserve $10,000. Show new balances and total.","steps":["Debit Retained earnings $6,000; credit General reserve $6,000.","Retained earnings becomes $19,000.","General reserve becomes $16,000.","Combined total remains $35,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-reserves-q1"},{"prompt":"Transfer $4,000 back from General reserve to retained earnings. Record and explain Bank effect.","steps":["Debit General reserve $4,000.","Credit Retained earnings $4,000.","No Bank entry is required.","Total equity is unchanged; composition changes."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-reserves-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Credit income whenever retained earnings move into reserve.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["It reclassifies existing equity without generating income.","No new resource has been earned from an external transaction.","Example evidence: Debit Retained earnings $6,000; credit General reserve $6,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-reserves-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is a reserve transfer new income?","o":["It reclassifies existing equity without generating income.","Credit income whenever retained earnings move into reserve."],"a":0,"why":"No new resource has been earned from an external transaction."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["No new resource has been earned from an external transaction.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"It reclassifies existing equity without generating income."},{"q":"In the model “Equity transfer”, what belongs to “Retained earnings”?","o":["−$6,000","+$6,000","No change"],"a":0,"why":"Retained earnings: −$6,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Equity transfer”, what belongs to “General reserve”?","o":["−$6,000","+$6,000","No change"],"a":1,"why":"General reserve: +$6,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Equity transfer","rows":[["Retained earnings","−$6,000"],["General reserve","+$6,000"],["Total equity and cash","No change"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is a reserve transfer new income?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can record transfers to and from reserves.</li><li>I can distinguish restrictions and composition.</li><li>I can explain total-equity invariance.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record transfers to and from reserves","html":"<p>A General reserve identifies an allocation within <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong>. Transferring <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong> to it debits <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Retained earnings</strong> and credits General reserve. A transfer back reverses the entry. No Bank movement or new <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> occurs merely from the transfer.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish restrictions and composition","html":"<p>Reserves can have different origins and purposes. A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> surplus is different from a General reserve created from <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong>. Describe the actual source and any stated restrictions; do not assume every reserve is freely distributable.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain total-equity invariance","html":"<p>In the statement of changes in <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong>, internal transfers appear as decreases and increases in the respective columns with zero total change. The word reserve does not mean cash has been put aside. Explain funding separately if the company wants to earmark cash.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain general reserves and equity transfers using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-land-revaluation","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Revaluation of non-depreciable property","subtitle":"Record an upward land revaluation · Distinguish OCI from profit · Explain non-cash equity changes","scope":"Record an upward land revaluation · Distinguish OCI from profit · Explain non-cash equity changes","outcomes":["Record an upward land revaluation","Distinguish OCI from profit","Explain non-cash equity changes"],"paragraphs":["In this course’s restricted corporate-statement tasks, revaluation concerns non-depreciable assets such as land. For a straightforward upward revaluation with no prior reversing loss and no tax effect required, debit Land and credit Revaluation surplus through other comprehensive income.","An upward revaluation is not a sale and generates no cash receipt. It increases carrying amount and total equity. Distinguish this surplus from ordinary sales profit and from transfers out of retained earnings.","A prior revaluation decrease, tax effect or detailed standard issue could change the treatment; use facts and scope stated in the task. Do not introduce depreciation on non-depreciable land or extend this lesson’s simple entry to every property case."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Land carrying amount $100,000 is revalued to $130,000. No prior revaluation loss or tax effect applies. Record and explain.","steps":["Increase = $30,000.","Debit Land $30,000.","Credit Revaluation surplus/OCI $30,000.","Assets and total equity increase $30,000; cash and ordinary profit do not."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-land-revaluation-q1"},{"prompt":"Land $80,000 rises to $92,000 under the same assumptions. Find OCI and comprehensive-income effect if profit is $20,000.","steps":["Revaluation increase = $12,000.","Record debit Land, credit Revaluation surplus $12,000.","Other comprehensive income is $12,000.","Total comprehensive income = 20,000 + 12,000 = $32,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-land-revaluation-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Debit Bank for the amount of an upward land revaluation.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["It changes a carrying amount without a cash transaction.","The asset has not been sold and no cash was received.","Example evidence: Increase = $30,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-land-revaluation-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a land revaluation bring cash into Bank?","o":["It changes a carrying amount without a cash transaction.","Debit Bank for the amount of an upward land revaluation."],"a":0,"why":"The asset has not been sold and no cash was received."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The asset has not been sold and no cash was received.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"It changes a carrying amount without a cash transaction."},{"q":"In the model “Revaluation effects”, what belongs to “Land”?","o":["+$30,000 carrying amount","+$30,000","No change","No change in this case"],"a":0,"why":"Land: +$30,000 carrying amount. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Revaluation effects”, what belongs to “OCI/revaluation surplus”?","o":["+$30,000 carrying amount","+$30,000","No change","No change in this case"],"a":1,"why":"OCI/revaluation surplus: +$30,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Revaluation effects","rows":[["Land","+$30,000 carrying amount"],["OCI/revaluation surplus","+$30,000"],["Cash","No change"],["Ordinary profit","No change in this case"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a land revaluation bring cash into Bank?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can record an upward land revaluation.</li><li>I can distinguish OCI from profit.</li><li>I can explain non-cash equity changes.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Record an upward land revaluation","html":"<p>In this course’s restricted corporate-statement tasks, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> concerns non-depreciable <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> such as land. For a straightforward upward <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> with no prior reversing loss and no tax effect required, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> Land and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Revaluation</strong> surplus through other comprehensive income.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish OCI from profit","html":"<p>An upward <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> is not a sale and generates no cash receipt. It increases carrying amount and total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong>. Distinguish this surplus from ordinary sales <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> and from transfers out of <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong>.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain non-cash equity changes","html":"<p>A prior <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> decrease, tax effect or detailed standard issue could change the treatment; use facts and scope stated in the task. Do not introduce <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> on non-depreciable land or extend this lesson’s simple entry to every property case.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain revaluation of non-depreciable property using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-company-tax","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Income tax expense in company statements","subtitle":"Use the specified company tax rate · Distinguish tax expense from payment · Calculate profit after tax","scope":"Use the specified company tax rate · Distinguish tax expense from payment · Calculate profit after tax","outcomes":["Use the specified company tax rate","Distinguish tax expense from payment","Calculate profit after tax"],"paragraphs":["For these syllabus calculation tasks, profit before tax is deemed equal to taxable income. Multiply by the company rate explicitly specified in the question. Do not assume one actual tax rate applies to every Australian company or import tax rules not required by the task.","Income tax expense reduces profit; unpaid tax creates a liability. Paying tax later reduces that liability and Bank. If a tax payable account has opening and closing balances, payments differ from expense by the movement in the liability.","Statement preparation should show profit before tax, tax expense and profit after tax separately. The examples use fictional supplied rates and exclude deferred-tax complexities. Keep the same rate and assumptions throughout the worked statement and ratio calculations."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Profit before tax $40,000; specified tax rate 30%; no opening tax liability and no payment yet. Calculate and record.","steps":["Income tax expense = 40,000×30% = $12,000.","Profit after tax = $28,000.","Debit Income tax expense $12,000; credit Income tax payable $12,000.","Bank is unchanged until tax is paid."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-company-tax-q1"},{"prompt":"Tax expense $15,000, opening payable $4,000 and closing payable $6,000. Find tax paid.","steps":["Tax paid = opening payable + expense − closing payable.","Tax paid = 4,000 + 15,000 − 6,000 = $13,000.","Payment debits tax payable and credits Bank.","Expense and cash payment differ because the liability increased."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-company-tax-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Always replace the stated rate with a remembered real-world rate.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Use the rate and assumptions given in the question.","Syllabus calculations explicitly use a specified rate and simplified taxable-profit assumption.","Example evidence: Income tax expense = 40,000×30% = $12,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-company-tax-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Should a tax rate be invented when the task specifies one?","o":["Use the rate and assumptions given in the question.","Always replace the stated rate with a remembered real-world rate."],"a":0,"why":"Syllabus calculations explicitly use a specified rate and simplified taxable-profit assumption."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Syllabus calculations explicitly use a specified rate and simplified taxable-profit assumption.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Use the rate and assumptions given in the question."},{"q":"In the model “Tax bridge”, what belongs to “Profit before tax”?","o":["$40,000","$12,000","$28,000"],"a":0,"why":"Profit before tax: $40,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Tax bridge”, what belongs to “Tax at stated 30%”?","o":["$40,000","$12,000","$28,000"],"a":1,"why":"Tax at stated 30%: $12,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Tax bridge","rows":[["Profit before tax","$40,000"],["Tax at stated 30%","$12,000"],["Profit after tax","$28,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Should a tax rate be invented when the task specifies one?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can use the specified company tax rate.</li><li>I can distinguish tax expense from payment.</li><li>I can calculate profit after tax.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use the specified company tax rate","html":"<p>For these syllabus calculation tasks, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> before tax is deemed equal to taxable income. Multiply by the company rate explicitly specified in the question. Do not assume one actual tax rate applies to every Australian company or import tax rules not required by the task.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish tax expense from payment","html":"<p>Income tax expense reduces <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>; unpaid tax creates a liability. Paying tax later reduces that liability and Bank. If a tax payable account has opening and closing balances, payments differ from expense by the movement in the liability.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate profit after tax","html":"<p>Statement preparation should show <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> before tax, tax expense and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> after tax separately. The examples use fictional supplied rates and exclude deferred-tax complexities. Keep the same rate and assumptions throughout the worked statement and ratio calculations.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain income tax expense in company statements using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-comprehensive-income","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Statement of comprehensive income: one statement","subtitle":"Prepare net sales and total cost of sales · Separate tax and other comprehensive income · Show total comprehensive income","scope":"Prepare net sales and total cost of sales · Separate tax and other comprehensive income · Show total comprehensive income","outcomes":["Prepare net sales and total cost of sales","Separate tax and other comprehensive income","Show total comprehensive income"],"paragraphs":["The one-statement version presents profit and other comprehensive income together. Calculate net sales using sales less returns/allowances and discount allowed where applicable. Under the specification, total cost of sales includes the cost-of-sales account plus import duties and freight inwards less discount received.","Classify operating expenses and other income, then determine profit before tax and subtract tax expense at the supplied rate. Other comprehensive income such as the straightforward land revaluation in this course is shown separately from profit after tax.","Total comprehensive income = profit after tax + other comprehensive income. Dividends are owner distributions, not expenses or OCI. Include business name, report title and period; ensure reserve movements are not incorrectly treated as earned income."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Sales $100,000, returns $2,000, discount allowed $1,000. Cost-of-sales account $50,000, freight $2,000, duties $1,000, discount received $500. Expenses $20,000; tax 30%, OCI $10,000. Calculate.","steps":["Net sales = 100,000 − 2,000 − 1,000 = $97,000.","Total cost of sales = 50,000 + 2,000 + 1,000 − 500 = $52,500.","Profit before tax = 97,000 − 52,500 − 20,000 = $24,500.","Tax $7,350; profit after tax $17,150.","Total comprehensive income = 17,150 + 10,000 = $27,150."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1],"marks":5,"practice":false,"rows":15,"id":"acf-comprehensive-income-q1"},{"prompt":"Net sales $80,000, total cost of sales $45,000, expenses $15,000, tax 25%, OCI land gain $8,000. Find profit and total comprehensive income.","steps":["Profit before tax = 80,000 − 45,000 − 15,000 = $20,000.","Tax = $5,000.","Profit after tax = $15,000.","Total comprehensive income = $23,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-comprehensive-income-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Subtract dividends as an expense before calculating profit after tax.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Dividends reduce equity as owner distributions after profit is measured.","Company performance and distributions to owners are distinct.","Example evidence: Net sales = 100,000 − 2,000 − 1,000 = $97,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-comprehensive-income-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Are dividends subtracted to calculate profit after tax?","o":["Dividends reduce equity as owner distributions after profit is measured.","Subtract dividends as an expense before calculating profit after tax."],"a":0,"why":"Company performance and distributions to owners are distinct."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Company performance and distributions to owners are distinct.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Dividends reduce equity as owner distributions after profit is measured."},{"q":"In the model “Comprehensive-income layers”, what belongs to “Profit before tax”?","o":["Income less expenses","Deduct tax expense","Separate revaluation item","Profit after tax + OCI"],"a":0,"why":"Profit before tax: Income less expenses. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Comprehensive-income layers”, what belongs to “Profit after tax”?","o":["Income less expenses","Deduct tax expense","Separate revaluation item","Profit after tax + OCI"],"a":1,"why":"Profit after tax: Deduct tax expense. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Comprehensive-income layers","rows":[["Profit before tax","Income less expenses"],["Profit after tax","Deduct tax expense"],["Other comprehensive income","Separate revaluation item"],["Total comprehensive income","Profit after tax + OCI"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Are dividends subtracted to calculate profit after tax?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can prepare net sales and total cost of sales.</li><li>I can separate tax and other comprehensive income.</li><li>I can show total comprehensive income.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Prepare net sales and total cost of sales","html":"<p>The one-statement version presents <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> and other comprehensive income together. Calculate net sales using sales less returns/allowances and discount allowed where applicable. Under the specification, total cost of sales includes the cost-of-sales account plus import duties and freight inwards less discount received.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate tax and other comprehensive income","html":"<p>Classify operating expenses and other income, then determine <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> before tax and subtract tax expense at the supplied rate. Other comprehensive income such as the straightforward land <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> in this course is shown separately from <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> after tax.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Show total comprehensive income","html":"<p>Total comprehensive income = <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> after tax + other comprehensive income. Dividends are owner distributions, not expenses or OCI. Include business name, report title and period; ensure reserve movements are not incorrectly treated as earned income.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain statement of comprehensive income: one statement using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-company-position","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Company statement of financial position","subtitle":"Present classified assets and liabilities · Show separate equity components · Validate total equity","scope":"Present classified assets and liabilities · Show separate equity components · Validate total equity","outcomes":["Present classified assets and liabilities","Show separate equity components","Validate total equity"],"paragraphs":["Corporate position statements separate current and non-current assets and liabilities and identify equity components. Share capital, reserves and retained earnings should not be collapsed into a sole-trader Capital account. Present equipment net of accumulated depreciation and receivables net of allowances.","Income in advance and accrued expenses are liabilities; prepayments and accrued income are assets. A dividend validly payable at balance day is a liability. Revalued non-depreciable land is shown at its resulting carrying amount under the task assumptions.","Total equity must reconcile with the statement of changes in equity. Check assets less liabilities against capital plus reserves plus retained earnings. Classification and notes provide users with information that the equation alone does not show."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Current assets $60,000, non-current assets $140,000, current liabilities $30,000, non-current liabilities $50,000; capital $80,000 and reserves $10,000. Find retained earnings.","steps":["Total assets = $200,000.","Total liabilities = $80,000.","Total equity = $120,000.","Retained earnings = 120,000 − 80,000 − 10,000 = $30,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-company-position-q1"},{"prompt":"Assets $180,000, liabilities $70,000, share capital $75,000, general reserve $15,000 and revaluation surplus $8,000. Find retained earnings.","steps":["Equity = 180,000 − 70,000 = $110,000.","Other equity components total $98,000.","Retained earnings = $12,000.","Use separate equity lines and verify the equation."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-company-position-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Share capital alone always equals total company equity.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Total equity includes share capital plus reserves and retained earnings.","Other accumulated equity components must also be included.","Example evidence: Total assets = $200,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-company-position-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is Share capital the same as total equity?","o":["Total equity includes share capital plus reserves and retained earnings.","Share capital alone always equals total company equity."],"a":0,"why":"Other accumulated equity components must also be included."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Other accumulated equity components must also be included.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Total equity includes share capital plus reserves and retained earnings."},{"q":"In the model “Corporate equity structure”, what belongs to “Share capital”?","o":["Owner issue contributions after direct costs","General reserve and revaluation surplus","Accumulated undistributed results"],"a":0,"why":"Share capital: Owner issue contributions after direct costs. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Corporate equity structure”, what belongs to “Reserves”?","o":["Owner issue contributions after direct costs","General reserve and revaluation surplus","Accumulated undistributed results"],"a":1,"why":"Reserves: General reserve and revaluation surplus. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Corporate equity structure","rows":[["Share capital","Owner issue contributions after direct costs"],["Reserves","General reserve and revaluation surplus"],["Retained earnings","Accumulated undistributed results"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is Share capital the same as total equity?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can present classified assets and liabilities.</li><li>I can show separate equity components.</li><li>I can validate total equity.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Present classified assets and liabilities","html":"<p>Corporate position statements separate current and non-current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> and identify <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> components. Share capital, reserves and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong> should not be collapsed into a sole-trader Capital account. Present equipment net of accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> and receivables net of allowances.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Show separate equity components","html":"<p>Income in advance and accrued expenses are <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>; prepayments and accrued income are <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong>. A dividend validly payable at balance day is a liability. Revalued non-depreciable land is shown at its resulting carrying amount under the task assumptions.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Validate total equity","html":"<p>Total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> must reconcile with the statement of changes in <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong>. Check <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> less <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> against capital plus reserves plus <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong>. Classification and notes provide users with information that the equation alone does not show.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain company statement of financial position using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-equity-statement","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Statement of changes in equity","subtitle":"Track each equity column · Separate comprehensive income and owner transactions · Reconcile opening to closing","scope":"Track each equity column · Separate comprehensive income and owner transactions · Reconcile opening to closing","outcomes":["Track each equity column","Separate comprehensive income and owner transactions","Reconcile opening to closing"],"paragraphs":["The statement of changes in equity bridges opening and closing share capital, reserves and retained earnings. Profit increases retained earnings; OCI revaluation increases the relevant surplus; cash share issues increase capital net of direct costs; dividends decrease retained earnings.","Transfers to reserves and bonus issues move amounts between columns without changing the total. Show decreases and increases in the same row so total-equity invariance is visible. A debit retained-earnings opening balance should be shown as negative.","Reconcile every closing column with the position statement. Total equity changes from comprehensive income and external owner transactions, not from internal relabelling. Do not add a reserve transfer as another source of profit."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Opening capital $80,000, reserve $10,000, retained earnings $20,000. Profit $15,000, dividends $5,000, transfer to reserve $3,000, net cash issue $12,000. Find closing columns.","steps":["Capital closes at $92,000.","Reserve closes at $13,000.","Retained earnings = 20,000 + 15,000 − 5,000 − 3,000 = $27,000.","Total closing equity = $132,000.","Total change $22,000 = profit $15,000 − dividends $5,000 + issue $12,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1],"marks":5,"practice":false,"rows":15,"id":"acf-equity-statement-q1"},{"prompt":"Opening equity $100,000; profit $20,000, OCI $8,000, dividends $6,000 and bonus transfer $5,000. Find closing total equity.","steps":["Comprehensive income = $28,000.","Dividends reduce total equity $6,000.","Bonus transfer has zero total effect.","Closing total = 100,000 + 28,000 − 6,000 = $122,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-equity-statement-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Add bonus transfers again to total equity as new income.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Internal equity transfers have zero total-equity effect.","One equity component falls by the same amount another rises.","Example evidence: Capital closes at $92,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-equity-statement-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a bonus transfer increase the total column?","o":["Internal equity transfers have zero total-equity effect.","Add bonus transfers again to total equity as new income."],"a":0,"why":"One equity component falls by the same amount another rises."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["One equity component falls by the same amount another rises.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Internal equity transfers have zero total-equity effect."},{"q":"In the model “Equity movements”, what belongs to “External share issue”?","o":["Total equity up","Total equity up/down","Total equity down","Composition changes; total unchanged"],"a":0,"why":"External share issue: Total equity up. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Equity movements”, what belongs to “Profit and OCI”?","o":["Total equity up","Total equity up/down","Total equity down","Composition changes; total unchanged"],"a":1,"why":"Profit and OCI: Total equity up/down. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Equity movements","rows":[["External share issue","Total equity up"],["Profit and OCI","Total equity up/down"],["Dividends","Total equity down"],["Reserve/bonus transfer","Composition changes; total unchanged"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a bonus transfer increase the total column?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can track each equity column.</li><li>I can separate comprehensive income and owner transactions.</li><li>I can reconcile opening to closing.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Track each equity column","html":"<p>The statement of changes in <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> bridges opening and closing share capital, reserves and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong>. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Profit</strong> increases <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong>; OCI <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> increases the relevant surplus; cash share issues increase capital net of <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">direct costs</strong>; dividends decrease <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">retained earnings</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate comprehensive income and owner transactions","html":"<p>Transfers to reserves and bonus issues move amounts between columns without changing the total. Show decreases and increases in the same row so total-<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> invariance is visible. A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">debit</strong> retained-earnings opening balance should be shown as negative.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile opening to closing","html":"<p>Reconcile every closing column with the position statement. Total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> changes from comprehensive income and external owner transactions, not from internal relabelling. Do not add a reserve transfer as another source of <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain statement of changes in equity using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-notes","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Notes: share capital, reserves, PPE and dividends","subtitle":"Explain the four required note areas · Reconcile note figures to statements · Distinguish disclosure from a journal entry","scope":"Explain the four required note areas · Reconcile note figures to statements · Distinguish disclosure from a journal entry","outcomes":["Explain the four required note areas","Reconcile note figures to statements","Distinguish disclosure from a journal entry"],"paragraphs":["Notes add detail without replacing the primary statements. For this course, focus on share capital, reserves, property/plant/equipment and dividends. Share-capital notes can show shares, issue movements and balances; reserve notes explain type and movements.","PPE notes show relevant classes, cost or revalued amount, accumulated depreciation and carrying values with movements supplied. Dividend notes distinguish recognised distributions/payables from proposals or events requiring disclosure under the stated facts.","A note is not itself a second journal entry. Reconcile the note amount to the relevant statement line and identify policy or uncertainty where helpful. Clear note labels let a user trace totals rather than guess how an aggregate was built."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Equipment cost $50,000, accumulated depreciation $18,000. Share capital is 20,000 shares with ledger balance $40,000. Outline relevant notes.","steps":["PPE note shows cost $50,000 and accumulated depreciation $18,000.","Carrying amount is $32,000.","Share-capital note identifies 20,000 shares and $40,000 recognised capital.","Note totals reconcile with statement lines."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-notes-q1"},{"prompt":"A general reserve begins $10,000, receives $3,000 from retained earnings and funds a $2,000 bonus issue. Explain its note and closing figure.","steps":["Reserve note identifies its nature and opening $10,000.","Show transfer in $3,000 and bonus transfer out $2,000.","Closing reserve = $11,000.","The note explains reclassifications; it does not create new cash or profit."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-notes-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every note amount must be recorded again as a new transaction.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Notes explain recognised figures and relevant information without duplicating entries.","Double recording disclosure figures would overstate the accounts.","Example evidence: PPE note shows cost $50,000 and accumulated depreciation $18,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-notes-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a disclosure note automatically create another accounting entry?","o":["Notes explain recognised figures and relevant information without duplicating entries.","Every note amount must be recorded again as a new transaction."],"a":0,"why":"Double recording disclosure figures would overstate the accounts."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Double recording disclosure figures would overstate the accounts.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Notes explain recognised figures and relevant information without duplicating entries."},{"q":"In the model “Required note areas”, what belongs to “Share capital”?","o":["Shares, issues and balances","Nature and movements","Cost/revaluation, depreciation and carrying amount","Recognised amounts and relevant disclosure"],"a":0,"why":"Share capital: Shares, issues and balances. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Required note areas”, what belongs to “Reserves”?","o":["Shares, issues and balances","Nature and movements","Cost/revaluation, depreciation and carrying amount","Recognised amounts and relevant disclosure"],"a":1,"why":"Reserves: Nature and movements. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Required note areas","rows":[["Share capital","Shares, issues and balances"],["Reserves","Nature and movements"],["PPE","Cost/revaluation, depreciation and carrying amount"],["Dividends","Recognised amounts and relevant disclosure"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a disclosure note automatically create another accounting entry?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can explain the four required note areas.</li><li>I can reconcile note figures to statements.</li><li>I can distinguish disclosure from a journal entry.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain the four required note areas","html":"<p>Notes add detail without replacing the primary statements. For this course, focus on share capital, reserves, property/plant/equipment and dividends. Share-capital notes can show shares, issue movements and balances; reserve notes explain type and movements.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile note figures to statements","html":"<p>PPE notes show relevant classes, cost or revalued amount, accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> and carrying values with movements supplied. Dividend notes distinguish recognised distributions/payables from proposals or events requiring disclosure under the stated facts.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish disclosure from a journal entry","html":"<p>A note is not itself a second journal entry. Reconcile the note amount to the relevant statement line and identify policy or uncertainty where helpful. Clear note labels let a user trace totals rather than guess how an aggregate was built.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain notes: share capital, reserves, ppe and dividends using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-equivalents","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Cash, cash equivalents and cash-flow classifications","subtitle":"Apply AASB 107 cash-equivalent concepts · Classify operating, investing and financing · Exclude non-cash movements","scope":"Apply AASB 107 cash-equivalent concepts · Classify operating, investing and financing · Exclude non-cash movements","outcomes":["Apply AASB 107 cash-equivalent concepts","Classify operating, investing and financing","Exclude non-cash movements"],"paragraphs":["Cash includes cash on hand and demand deposits. Cash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid investments readily convertible to known cash with insignificant value-change risk, normally with short maturity of around three months or less from acquisition. Not every short-term investment qualifies.","Operating flows arise from main revenue-producing activity; investing flows acquire or dispose of long-term assets and qualifying investments; financing changes equity and borrowings. AASB 107 has classification policy choices for some interest/dividend flows; apply a stated consistent policy.","Non-cash depreciation, bonus shares and revaluation are excluded from cash-flow totals. A demand overdraft integral to cash management can qualify in cash/equivalents under specific circumstances, rather than every bank loan being included automatically."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Classify customer cash receipts, equipment purchase, new loan proceeds and a bonus issue. State why shares are not normally cash equivalents.","steps":["Customer receipts: operating.","Equipment purchase: investing.","Loan proceeds: financing.","Bonus issue: non-cash, excluded from totals.","Ordinary shares carry value-change risk and are not normally cash equivalents."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1],"marks":5,"practice":false,"rows":15,"id":"acf-cash-equivalents-q1"},{"prompt":"A two-month highly liquid deposit converts to known cash with insignificant risk. A six-month volatile investment is also held. Evaluate cash-equivalent status.","steps":["The two-month deposit can qualify if all criteria are met.","The six-month volatile investment does not meet the given criteria.","Assess maturity from acquisition and value-change risk.","A short-term label alone is insufficient."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-equivalents-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Every current investment is automatically a cash equivalent.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Cash-equivalent criteria include liquidity, known value and insignificant risk.","Current classification alone does not satisfy AASB 107 cash-equivalent conditions.","Example evidence: Customer receipts: operating."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-equivalents-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is every investment labelled current a cash equivalent?","o":["Cash-equivalent criteria include liquidity, known value and insignificant risk.","Every current investment is automatically a cash equivalent."],"a":0,"why":"Current classification alone does not satisfy AASB 107 cash-equivalent conditions."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Current classification alone does not satisfy AASB 107 cash-equivalent conditions.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Cash-equivalent criteria include liquidity, known value and insignificant risk."},{"q":"In the model “Cash-flow categories”, what belongs to “Operating”?","o":["Customer receipts and operating payments","Long-term asset acquisitions/disposals","Equity issues and borrowings","Disclose where relevant; exclude from cash totals"],"a":0,"why":"Operating: Customer receipts and operating payments. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Cash-flow categories”, what belongs to “Investing”?","o":["Customer receipts and operating payments","Long-term asset acquisitions/disposals","Equity issues and borrowings","Disclose where relevant; exclude from cash totals"],"a":1,"why":"Investing: Long-term asset acquisitions/disposals. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Cash-flow categories","rows":[["Operating","Customer receipts and operating payments"],["Investing","Long-term asset acquisitions/disposals"],["Financing","Equity issues and borrowings"],["Non-cash","Disclose where relevant; exclude from cash totals"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is every investment labelled current a cash equivalent?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can apply AASB 107 cash-equivalent concepts.</li><li>I can classify operating, investing and financing.</li><li>I can exclude non-cash movements.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply AASB 107 cash-equivalent concepts","html":"<p>Cash includes cash on hand and demand deposits. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Cash equivalents</strong> are short-term, highly liquid investments readily convertible to known cash with insignificant value-change risk, normally with short maturity of around three months or less from acquisition. Not every short-term investment qualifies.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Classify operating, investing and financing","html":"<p>Operating flows arise from main revenue-producing activity; investing flows acquire or dispose of long-term <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and qualifying investments; financing changes <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> and borrowings. AASB 107 has classification policy choices for some interest/dividend flows; apply a stated consistent policy.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Exclude non-cash movements","html":"<p>Non-cash <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong>, bonus shares and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> are excluded from cash-flow totals. A demand overdraft integral to cash management can qualify in cash/equivalents under specific circumstances, rather than every bank loan being included automatically.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain cash, cash equivalents and cash-flow classifications using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-customers","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Direct-method receipts from customers","subtitle":"Reconstruct collections from receivable changes · Use net credit sales consistently · Adjust specified bad-debt effects","scope":"Reconstruct collections from receivable changes · Use net credit sales consistently · Adjust specified bad-debt effects","outcomes":["Reconstruct collections from receivable changes","Use net credit sales consistently","Adjust specified bad-debt effects"],"paragraphs":["The direct method reports gross classes of cash receipts and payments. In a simple case, customer collections = opening receivables + credit sales − closing receivables, then add cash sales separately if given. Use net credit sales after specified deductions.","If a receivable is written off, subtract the non-cash write-off in the receivable reconciliation because it reduced the balance without collection. Other changes must be identified similarly. Do not use total sales and add cash sales again.","Prepare a receivable control reconstruction when facts are complex. GST is excluded in these Year 12 cash-flow tasks. Explain the direction: a receivable increase usually means some sales were not collected, reducing receipts relative to sales."],"questions":[{"prompt":"All sales are credit: net sales $100,000, receivables open $20,000 and close $25,000; no other changes. Find collections.","steps":["Collections = 20,000 + 100,000 − 25,000.","Cash received from customers = $95,000.","Receivables increased $5,000.","This makes collections $5,000 less than net credit sales."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-customers-q1"},{"prompt":"Credit sales $80,000, cash sales $10,000; receivables open $12,000, close $15,000; write-offs $2,000. Find receipts.","steps":["Credit collections = 12,000 + 80,000 − 15,000 − 2,000 = $75,000.","Add cash sales $10,000.","Total customer receipts = $85,000.","The write-off is not a cash receipt."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-customers-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Add every receivable increase to sales to calculate collections.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["An increase usually means sales exceed collections, reducing cash received relative to sales.","Uncollected sales remain in the closing asset balance.","Example evidence: Collections = 20,000 + 100,000 − 25,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-customers-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does an increase in receivables increase collections relative to sales?","o":["An increase usually means sales exceed collections, reducing cash received relative to sales.","Add every receivable increase to sales to calculate collections."],"a":0,"why":"Uncollected sales remain in the closing asset balance."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Uncollected sales remain in the closing asset balance.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"An increase usually means sales exceed collections, reducing cash received relative to sales."},{"q":"In the model “Receivable control reconstruction”, what belongs to “Opening + credit sales”?","o":["$120,000 available claim","$25,000 uncollected","$95,000"],"a":0,"why":"Opening + credit sales: $120,000 available claim. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Receivable control reconstruction”, what belongs to “Closing receivables”?","o":["$120,000 available claim","$25,000 uncollected","$95,000"],"a":1,"why":"Closing receivables: $25,000 uncollected. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Receivable control reconstruction","rows":[["Opening + credit sales","$120,000 available claim"],["Closing receivables","$25,000 uncollected"],["Collections","$95,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does an increase in receivables increase collections relative to sales?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can reconstruct collections from receivable changes.</li><li>I can use net credit sales consistently.</li><li>I can adjust specified bad-debt effects.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconstruct collections from receivable changes","html":"<p>The direct method reports gross classes of cash receipts and payments. In a simple case, customer collections = opening receivables + <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sales − closing receivables, then add cash sales separately if given. Use net <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sales after specified deductions.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use net credit sales consistently","html":"<p>If a receivable is written off, subtract the non-cash write-off in the receivable reconciliation because it reduced the balance without collection. Other changes must be identified similarly. Do not use total sales and add cash sales again.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Adjust specified bad-debt effects","html":"<p>Prepare a receivable control reconstruction when facts are complex. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> is excluded in these Year 12 cash-flow tasks. Explain the direction: a receivable increase usually means some sales were not collected, reducing receipts relative to sales.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain direct-method receipts from customers using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-suppliers","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Direct-method payments to inventory suppliers","subtitle":"Reconstruct purchases from inventory · Use total cost of sales consistently · Adjust payable movements","scope":"Reconstruct purchases from inventory · Use total cost of sales consistently · Adjust payable movements","outcomes":["Reconstruct purchases from inventory","Use total cost of sales consistently","Adjust payable movements"],"paragraphs":["First reconstruct inventory purchases: cost of sales + closing inventory − opening inventory in a simple case with no other stock movements. Then payments to suppliers = opening payables + credit purchases − closing payables. If cash purchases are separately given, avoid double counting them.","Check which cost-of-sales measure and extra inventory movements the task supplies. Freight, duties, discounts and returns require consistent treatment; do not add the same adjustment twice. A control-account schedule makes assumptions visible.","A payable increase means some purchases are unpaid, so payments are below purchases. An inventory increase means purchases exceed goods expensed. These two working-capital changes explain why cost of sales differs from supplier cash payments."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Cost of sales $60,000, inventory opens $10,000 and closes $14,000. All purchases are credit; payables open $8,000 and close $11,000. Find payments.","steps":["Purchases = 60,000 + 14,000 − 10,000 = $64,000.","Payments = 8,000 + 64,000 − 11,000.","Supplier cash payments = $61,000.","Inventory and payable changes both affect the reconstruction."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-suppliers-q1"},{"prompt":"Cost of sales $45,000, inventory open $9,000 close $7,000, payables open $6,000 close $4,000; all purchases credit, no other movements. Find payments.","steps":["Purchases = 45,000 + 7,000 − 9,000 = $43,000.","Payments = 6,000 + 43,000 − 4,000.","Payments = $45,000.","A payable decrease adds to cash payments relative to purchases."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-suppliers-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Use cost of sales unchanged as supplier cash payments in every case.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Inventory and payable movements can make cash payments differ from cost of sales.","Cost recognition, acquisition and payment occur at different stages.","Example evidence: Purchases = 60,000 + 14,000 − 10,000 = $64,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-suppliers-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is cost of sales always the supplier cash payment?","o":["Inventory and payable movements can make cash payments differ from cost of sales.","Use cost of sales unchanged as supplier cash payments in every case."],"a":0,"why":"Cost recognition, acquisition and payment occur at different stages."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Cost recognition, acquisition and payment occur at different stages.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Inventory and payable movements can make cash payments differ from cost of sales."},{"q":"In the model “Two-stage reconstruction”, what belongs to “Cost of sales”?","o":["$60,000","Purchases $64,000","Payments $61,000"],"a":0,"why":"Cost of sales: $60,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Two-stage reconstruction”, what belongs to “Add inventory growth”?","o":["$60,000","Purchases $64,000","Payments $61,000"],"a":1,"why":"Add inventory growth: Purchases $64,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Two-stage reconstruction","rows":[["Cost of sales","$60,000"],["Add inventory growth","Purchases $64,000"],["Less payable growth","Payments $61,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is cost of sales always the supplier cash payment?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can reconstruct purchases from inventory.</li><li>I can use total cost of sales consistently.</li><li>I can adjust payable movements.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconstruct purchases from inventory","html":"<p>First reconstruct <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> purchases: cost of sales + closing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> − opening <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> in a simple case with no other stock movements. Then payments to suppliers = opening payables + <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> purchases − closing payables. If cash purchases are separately given, avoid double counting them.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use total cost of sales consistently","html":"<p>Check which cost-of-sales measure and extra <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> movements the task supplies. Freight, duties, discounts and returns require consistent treatment; do not add the same adjustment twice. A control-account schedule makes assumptions visible.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Adjust payable movements","html":"<p>A payable increase means some purchases are unpaid, so payments are below purchases. An <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> increase means purchases exceed goods expensed. These two working-capital changes explain why cost of sales differs from supplier cash payments.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain direct-method payments to inventory suppliers using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-expenses","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Direct-method expense and tax payments","subtitle":"Adjust accrual expenses to cash · Remove non-cash charges · Explain prepayment and accrual directions","scope":"Adjust accrual expenses to cash · Remove non-cash charges · Explain prepayment and accrual directions","outcomes":["Adjust accrual expenses to cash","Remove non-cash charges","Explain prepayment and accrual directions"],"paragraphs":["For an ordinary cash-related expense, payment = expense + opening accrued liability − closing accrued liability + closing prepayment − opening prepayment, assuming no other movements. Increasing the accrued liability reduces cash paid; increasing a prepayment increases cash paid.","Remove non-cash depreciation or doubtful-debt expense when reconstructing cash payments from an expense total. A control account can avoid memorising signs. Income tax paid similarly equals tax expense plus opening tax payable less closing tax payable in a simple case.","State a consistent interest/dividend classification policy as required by the task. These examples classify interest paid as operating. The cash-flow statement uses payments rather than the income-statement amount merely copied into a new heading."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Wages expense $30,000, accrued wages open $2,000 and close $3,000. Insurance expense $4,000, prepaid insurance open $500 and close $800. Find cash payments.","steps":["Wages paid = 30,000 + 2,000 − 3,000 = $29,000.","Insurance paid = 4,000 + 800 − 500 = $4,300.","Increasing accrued wages lowers current cash paid.","Increasing prepayment raises cash paid above insurance expense."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-expenses-q1"},{"prompt":"Expense total $20,000 includes depreciation $3,000. Accrued operating expenses open $1,000, close $1,500; no prepayments. Find cash operating payments.","steps":["Cash-related expense = 20,000 − 3,000 = $17,000.","Cash paid = 17,000 + 1,000 − 1,500.","Cash paid = $16,500.","Depreciation is excluded and the increased accrual explains the $500 reduction."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-expenses-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Add the closing accrued-expense increase to find cash paid.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["An increase in accrued expenses means more cost remains unpaid.","The growing liability accounts for expense recognised without current payment.","Example evidence: Wages paid = 30,000 + 2,000 − 3,000 = $29,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-expenses-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does an increase in accrued expenses increase cash paid?","o":["An increase in accrued expenses means more cost remains unpaid.","Add the closing accrued-expense increase to find cash paid."],"a":0,"why":"The growing liability accounts for expense recognised without current payment."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The growing liability accounts for expense recognised without current payment.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"An increase in accrued expenses means more cost remains unpaid."},{"q":"In the model “Expense-to-cash bridge”, what belongs to “Wages expense”?","o":["$30,000","Add $2,000","Subtract $3,000","$29,000"],"a":0,"why":"Wages expense: $30,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Expense-to-cash bridge”, what belongs to “Opening accrual”?","o":["$30,000","Add $2,000","Subtract $3,000","$29,000"],"a":1,"why":"Opening accrual: Add $2,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Expense-to-cash bridge","rows":[["Wages expense","$30,000"],["Opening accrual","Add $2,000"],["Closing accrual","Subtract $3,000"],["Cash paid","$29,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does an increase in accrued expenses increase cash paid?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can adjust accrual expenses to cash.</li><li>I can remove non-cash charges.</li><li>I can explain prepayment and accrual directions.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Adjust accrual expenses to cash","html":"<p>For an ordinary cash-related expense, payment = expense + opening accrued liability − closing accrued liability + closing prepayment − opening prepayment, assuming no other movements. Increasing the accrued liability reduces cash paid; increasing a prepayment increases cash paid.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Remove non-cash charges","html":"<p>Remove non-cash <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> or doubtful-debt expense when reconstructing cash payments from an expense total. A control account can avoid memorising signs. Income tax paid similarly equals tax expense plus opening tax payable less closing tax payable in a simple case.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain prepayment and accrual directions","html":"<p>State a consistent interest/dividend classification policy as required by the task. These examples classify interest paid as operating. The cash-flow statement uses payments rather than the income-statement amount merely copied into a new heading.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain direct-method expense and tax payments using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-investing","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Asset purchases and disposal cash flows","subtitle":"Separate cash from non-cash asset movements · Reconstruct acquisition cost · Use sale proceeds rather than gain","scope":"Separate cash from non-cash asset movements · Reconstruct acquisition cost · Use sale proceeds rather than gain","outcomes":["Separate cash from non-cash asset movements","Reconstruct acquisition cost","Use sale proceeds rather than gain"],"paragraphs":["Investing cash flows include cash paid for non-current assets and cash received on disposal. Revaluation and depreciation change carrying amounts without cash. When using comparative asset balances, separate cost, accumulated depreciation and revaluation rather than treating a net-asset increase as the cash purchase.","An asset-cost reconciliation can give purchases: closing cost − opening cost + cost disposed, adjusted for other non-cash transfers or revaluation included in the chosen account. Use the information basis supplied; do not combine a cost account with a net carrying amount.","Disposal proceeds differ from gain/loss: proceeds = carrying amount + gain, or carrying amount − loss. Remove the disposed cost and accumulated depreciation in the control-account reconstruction. State that inferred acquisitions are cash purchases only if the task says so."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Equipment cost opens $50,000 and closes $65,000; disposed original cost $10,000. All acquisitions are cash; no other cost movements. Disposal carrying amount $4,000, gain $1,000. Find investing flows.","steps":["Purchases = 65,000 − 50,000 + 10,000 = $25,000.","Cash paid for equipment = $25,000.","Disposal proceeds = 4,000 + 1,000 = $5,000.","Net investing outflow = $20,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-investing-q1"},{"prompt":"Disposed asset cost $12,000 and accumulated depreciation $9,000; loss $500. Find cash proceeds and explain revaluation exclusion.","steps":["Carrying amount = $3,000.","Proceeds = 3,000 − 500 = $2,500.","Report $2,500 investing receipt rather than the loss.","A revaluation is non-cash and excluded from receipt/payment totals."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-investing-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Use only the gain as the investing cash receipt.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Cash proceeds include recovery of carrying amount plus any gain.","Gain is the difference between proceeds and carrying amount.","Example evidence: Purchases = 65,000 − 50,000 + 10,000 = $25,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-investing-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is the gain on disposal the cash received?","o":["Cash proceeds include recovery of carrying amount plus any gain.","Use only the gain as the investing cash receipt."],"a":0,"why":"Gain is the difference between proceeds and carrying amount."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Gain is the difference between proceeds and carrying amount.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Cash proceeds include recovery of carrying amount plus any gain."},{"q":"In the model “Investing reconstruction”, what belongs to “Cost acquisitions”?","o":["Closing − opening + disposed cost","Carrying amount ± gain/loss","Remove revaluation and other transfers"],"a":0,"why":"Cost acquisitions: Closing − opening + disposed cost. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Investing reconstruction”, what belongs to “Disposal proceeds”?","o":["Closing − opening + disposed cost","Carrying amount ± gain/loss","Remove revaluation and other transfers"],"a":1,"why":"Disposal proceeds: Carrying amount ± gain/loss. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Investing reconstruction","rows":[["Cost acquisitions","Closing − opening + disposed cost"],["Disposal proceeds","Carrying amount ± gain/loss"],["Non-cash changes","Remove revaluation and other transfers"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is the gain on disposal the cash received?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can separate cash from non-cash asset movements.</li><li>I can reconstruct acquisition cost.</li><li>I can use sale proceeds rather than gain.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Separate cash from non-cash asset movements","html":"<p>Investing cash flows include cash paid for non-current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and cash received on disposal. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Revaluation</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> change carrying amounts without cash. When using comparative asset balances, separate cost, accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> and <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> rather than treating a net-asset increase as the cash purchase.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconstruct acquisition cost","html":"<p>An asset-cost reconciliation can give purchases: closing cost − opening cost + cost disposed, adjusted for other non-cash transfers or <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> included in the chosen account. Use the information basis supplied; do not combine a cost account with a net carrying amount.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use sale proceeds rather than gain","html":"<p>Disposal proceeds differ from gain/loss: proceeds = carrying amount + gain, or carrying amount − loss. Remove the disposed cost and accumulated <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">depreciation</strong> in the control-account reconstruction. State that inferred acquisitions are cash purchases only if the task says so.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain asset purchases and disposal cash flows using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-financing","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Share, loan and dividend financing cash flows","subtitle":"Remove bonus transfers from share movements · Reconcile debt changes · Calculate dividend cash payments","scope":"Remove bonus transfers from share movements · Reconcile debt changes · Calculate dividend cash payments","outcomes":["Remove bonus transfers from share movements","Reconcile debt changes","Calculate dividend cash payments"],"paragraphs":["Financing receipts include cash share issues and new borrowings; payments include relevant issue costs, loan principal and dividends under the stated policy. Share-capital increases can include non-cash bonus transfers, so the increase alone is not cash raised.","A loan balance movement can show net borrowing or repayment only when there are no other movements; separate gross flows if supplied. Dividend paid = opening dividend payable + dividends declared − closing payable for a simple case. Dividends declared and paid are different dates.","Apply a consistent AASB 107 policy; these lessons treat dividends paid as financing and interest paid as operating. Check cash/equivalents treatment of an eligible overdraft separately. No GST applies to these Year 12 reconstructions."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Share capital rises $30,000 including $10,000 bonus transfer; cash issue costs $2,000 were charged separately. Dividend payable opens $3,000, declarations $8,000 and closes $5,000. Find cash flows.","steps":["Gross cash share issue = 30,000 − 10,000 = $20,000.","Issue-cost payment = $2,000; net issue financing $18,000.","Dividends paid = 3,000 + 8,000 − 5,000 = $6,000.","Bonus shares are excluded as non-cash."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-financing-q1"},{"prompt":"Loan opens $40,000, new borrowing $15,000 and closes $45,000. No other movements. Find principal repaid and classification.","steps":["Repayment = 40,000 + 15,000 − 45,000 = $10,000.","Report loan proceeds $15,000 as financing receipt.","Report principal payment $10,000 as financing outflow.","Principal repayment is not an operating expense."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-financing-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Copy the entire share-capital increase as cash proceeds in every case.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Remove non-cash bonus transfers and account for direct issue costs.","Equity transfers can increase capital without any cash receipt.","Example evidence: Gross cash share issue = 30,000 − 10,000 = $20,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-financing-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does all share-capital growth represent a cash issue?","o":["Remove non-cash bonus transfers and account for direct issue costs.","Copy the entire share-capital increase as cash proceeds in every case."],"a":0,"why":"Equity transfers can increase capital without any cash receipt."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Equity transfers can increase capital without any cash receipt.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Remove non-cash bonus transfers and account for direct issue costs."},{"q":"In the model “Financing bridge”, what belongs to “Share increase”?","o":["Separate cash issue from bonus transfer","Opening + borrowings − repayments = closing","Opening payable + declared − closing = paid"],"a":0,"why":"Share increase: Separate cash issue from bonus transfer. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Financing bridge”, what belongs to “Debt”?","o":["Separate cash issue from bonus transfer","Opening + borrowings − repayments = closing","Opening payable + declared − closing = paid"],"a":1,"why":"Debt: Opening + borrowings − repayments = closing. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Financing bridge","rows":[["Share increase","Separate cash issue from bonus transfer"],["Debt","Opening + borrowings − repayments = closing"],["Dividend","Opening payable + declared − closing = paid"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does all share-capital growth represent a cash issue?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can remove bonus transfers from share movements.</li><li>I can reconcile debt changes.</li><li>I can calculate dividend cash payments.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Remove bonus transfers from share movements","html":"<p>Financing receipts include cash share issues and new borrowings; payments include relevant issue costs, loan principal and dividends under the stated policy. Share-capital increases can include non-cash bonus transfers, so the increase alone is not cash raised.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile debt changes","html":"<p>A loan balance movement can show net borrowing or repayment only when there are no other movements; separate gross flows if supplied. Dividend paid = opening dividend payable + dividends declared − closing payable for a simple case. Dividends declared and paid are different dates.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate dividend cash payments","html":"<p>Apply a consistent AASB 107 policy; these lessons treat dividends paid as financing and interest paid as operating. Check cash/equivalents treatment of an eligible overdraft separately. No <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">GST</strong> applies to these Year 12 reconstructions.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain share, loan and dividend financing cash flows using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-cash-statement","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Preparing the direct-method cash-flow statement","subtitle":"Present three activity sections · Reconcile opening and closing cash · Interpret movements rather than profit","scope":"Present three activity sections · Reconcile opening and closing cash · Interpret movements rather than profit","outcomes":["Present three activity sections","Reconcile opening and closing cash","Interpret movements rather than profit"],"paragraphs":["Present operating cash receipts and payments directly, then investing and financing sections with subtotals. Sum net activity flows to obtain the change in cash/equivalents and reconcile opening to closing. Use clear period headings and the stated classification policy.","Operating cash indicates how core activity supplies or consumes funds; investing often shows replacement/expansion; financing shows borrowing, equity and owner distributions. A negative investing total is not automatically bad because it may reflect productive investment.","Check the full reconciliation and exclusions for non-cash changes. Profit need not equal operating cash because of working capital and non-cash expenses. Explain large movements using evidence rather than using a positive closing Bank balance as proof of strong operations."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Opening cash $10,000; customer receipts $90,000; suppliers $50,000, wages $20,000, tax $5,000. Asset purchases $25,000; loan proceeds $15,000 and dividends $4,000. Prepare subtotals.","steps":["Operating net = 90,000 − 50,000 − 20,000 − 5,000 = $15,000.","Investing net = −$25,000.","Financing net = 15,000 − 4,000 = $11,000.","Net increase = $1,000; closing cash $11,000.","The loan supports investment; operating cash is not equal to total closing cash."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1,1],"marks":5,"practice":false,"rows":15,"id":"acf-cash-statement-q1"},{"prompt":"Operating inflow $12,000, investing outflow $20,000 and financing inflow $5,000; opening cash $8,000. Find closing and interpret.","steps":["Net change = 12,000 − 20,000 + 5,000 = −$3,000.","Closing cash = $5,000.","Positive operating cash did not fully cover investing outflows.","Assess funding sufficiency and the investment purpose."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-cash-statement-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Any investing outflow proves the company is failing.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["It can reflect asset investment and must be interpreted with purpose and funding.","Purchasing productive assets uses cash now for expected future benefits.","Example evidence: Operating net = 90,000 − 50,000 − 20,000 − 5,000 = $15,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-cash-statement-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a negative investing cash flow always indicate poor performance?","o":["It can reflect asset investment and must be interpreted with purpose and funding.","Any investing outflow proves the company is failing."],"a":0,"why":"Purchasing productive assets uses cash now for expected future benefits."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Purchasing productive assets uses cash now for expected future benefits.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"It can reflect asset investment and must be interpreted with purpose and funding."},{"q":"In the model “Cash-flow reconciliation”, what belongs to “Operating”?","o":["+$15,000","−$25,000","+$11,000","+$1,000","$10,000 → $11,000"],"a":0,"why":"Operating: +$15,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Cash-flow reconciliation”, what belongs to “Investing”?","o":["+$15,000","−$25,000","+$11,000","+$1,000","$10,000 → $11,000"],"a":1,"why":"Investing: −$25,000. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Cash-flow reconciliation","rows":[["Operating","+$15,000"],["Investing","−$25,000"],["Financing","+$11,000"],["Net change","+$1,000"],["Opening to closing","$10,000 → $11,000"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a negative investing cash flow always indicate poor performance?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can present three activity sections.</li><li>I can reconcile opening and closing cash.</li><li>I can interpret movements rather than profit.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Present three activity sections","html":"<p>Present operating cash receipts and payments directly, then investing and financing sections with subtotals. Sum net activity flows to obtain the change in cash/equivalents and reconcile opening to closing. Use clear period headings and the stated classification policy.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Reconcile opening and closing cash","html":"<p>Operating cash indicates how core activity supplies or consumes funds; investing often shows replacement/expansion; financing shows borrowing, <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> and owner distributions. A negative investing total is not automatically bad because it may reflect productive investment.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret movements rather than profit","html":"<p>Check the full reconciliation and exclusions for non-cash changes. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Profit</strong> need not equal operating cash because of working capital and non-cash expenses. Explain large movements using evidence rather than using a positive closing Bank balance as proof of strong operations.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain preparing the direct-method cash-flow statement using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-liquidity-ratios","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Corporate current and quick ratios","subtitle":"Use SCSA quick-asset exclusions · Calculate two-decimal ratios · Evaluate short-term payment risk","scope":"Use SCSA quick-asset exclusions · Calculate two-decimal ratios · Evaluate short-term payment risk","outcomes":["Use SCSA quick-asset exclusions","Calculate two-decimal ratios","Evaluate short-term payment risk"],"paragraphs":["Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities. Quick asset ratio excludes inventory and prepayments from the numerator and bank overdraft from the denominator under the SCSA specification. Prepare each adjusted total before division and report two decimal places.","A high current ratio can be supported by slow stock or doubtful receivables, so inspect asset quality. The quick ratio is narrower but also does not prove every short-term debt can be paid on time. Due dates and cash forecasts remain relevant.","Compare with previous periods and similar businesses using consistent classifications. A snapshot at year end can be influenced by timing or temporary actions. Give a specific action linked to the evidence, such as collection follow-up or reducing excess stock."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Current assets $100,000 include inventory $40,000 and prepayments $10,000. Current liabilities $50,000 include overdraft $10,000. Find ratios.","steps":["Current ratio = 100,000 ÷ 50,000 = 2.00:1.","Quick assets = $50,000.","Quick liabilities = $40,000.","Quick ratio = 1.25:1; asset quality and timing still require review."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-liquidity-ratios-q1"},{"prompt":"Current assets $90,000 include inventory $30,000 and prepayments $6,000. Current liabilities $45,000 include overdraft $9,000. Calculate.","steps":["Current ratio = 2.00:1.","Quick assets = 90,000 − 30,000 − 6,000 = $54,000.","Quick liabilities = 45,000 − 9,000 = $36,000.","Quick ratio = 1.50:1."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-liquidity-ratios-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Use the entire current-liability total unchanged in the quick ratio.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Exclude bank overdraft from the quick-ratio denominator.","The specification gives this explicit denominator adjustment.","Example evidence: Current ratio = 100,000 ÷ 50,000 = 2.00:1."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-liquidity-ratios-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does SCSA include bank overdraft in quick-ratio liabilities?","o":["Exclude bank overdraft from the quick-ratio denominator.","Use the entire current-liability total unchanged in the quick ratio."],"a":0,"why":"The specification gives this explicit denominator adjustment."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The specification gives this explicit denominator adjustment.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Exclude bank overdraft from the quick-ratio denominator."},{"q":"In the model “Corporate quick ratio”, what belongs to “Numerator”?","o":["Current assets − inventory − prepayments","Current liabilities − bank overdraft","Two-decimal x:1 ratio"],"a":0,"why":"Numerator: Current assets − inventory − prepayments. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Corporate quick ratio”, what belongs to “Denominator”?","o":["Current assets − inventory − prepayments","Current liabilities − bank overdraft","Two-decimal x:1 ratio"],"a":1,"why":"Denominator: Current liabilities − bank overdraft. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Corporate quick ratio","rows":[["Numerator","Current assets − inventory − prepayments"],["Denominator","Current liabilities − bank overdraft"],["Presentation","Two-decimal x:1 ratio"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does SCSA include bank overdraft in quick-ratio liabilities?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can use SCSA quick-asset exclusions.</li><li>I can calculate two-decimal ratios.</li><li>I can evaluate short-term payment risk.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use SCSA quick-asset exclusions","html":"<p>Current ratio = current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> ÷ current <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong>. Quick asset ratio excludes <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong> and prepayments from the numerator and bank overdraft from the denominator under the SCSA specification. Prepare each adjusted total before division and report two decimal places.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate two-decimal ratios","html":"<p>A high current ratio can be supported by slow stock or doubtful receivables, so inspect asset quality. The quick ratio is narrower but also does not prove every short-term debt can be paid on time. Due dates and cash forecasts remain relevant.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate short-term payment risk","html":"<p>Compare with previous periods and similar businesses using consistent classifications. A snapshot at year end can be influenced by timing or temporary actions. Give a specific action linked to the evidence, such as collection follow-up or reducing excess stock.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain corporate current and quick ratios using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-efficiency-ratios","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Debtor collection and inventory turnover","subtitle":"Use average balances · Distinguish days from times · Apply net sales and total cost-of-sales definitions","scope":"Use average balances · Distinguish days from times · Apply net sales and total cost-of-sales definitions","outcomes":["Use average balances","Distinguish days from times","Apply net sales and total cost-of-sales definitions"],"paragraphs":["Debtor collection days = average debtors ÷ net credit sales × 365. Average uses opening and closing balances where supplied. Net credit sales subtract relevant returns/allowances and discount allowed. Use credit sales, not total sales when cash sales are separately identified.","Inventory turnover = total cost of sales ÷ average inventory, expressed in times. The specification includes import duties and freight inwards and subtracts discount received from the cost-of-sales account where these are given separately. A larger turnover is not automatically better if shortages occur.","Slower collection ties up cash and increases default risk. Faster stock turnover may indicate efficient stock management or under-stocking. Link conclusions to credit terms, industry, stock type and service levels rather than inventing universal targets."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Debtors open $20,000 and close $30,000; net credit sales $200,000. Inventory opens $15,000 and closes $25,000; total cost of sales $120,000. Calculate.","steps":["Average debtors = $25,000.","Collection = 25,000 ÷ 200,000 × 365 = 45.63 days.","Average inventory = $20,000.","Turnover = 120,000 ÷ 20,000 = 6.00 times."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-efficiency-ratios-q1"},{"prompt":"Average debtors $12,000, net credit sales $100,000. Cost-of-sales account $50,000, duties $1,000, freight $2,000, discount received $500, average stock $10,000. Calculate.","steps":["Collection = 12,000 ÷ 100,000 × 365 = 43.80 days.","Total cost of sales = 50,000 + 1,000 + 2,000 − 500 = $52,500.","Inventory turnover = 52,500 ÷ 10,000 = 5.25 times.","Interpret days and times with appropriate units and business context."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-efficiency-ratios-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Use all income indiscriminately as debtor collection sales.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Use net credit sales in the debtor collection denominator.","The receivable balance arises from credit sales, not cash sales or unrelated income.","Example evidence: Average debtors = $25,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-efficiency-ratios-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does debtor collection use all revenue including cash sales?","o":["Use net credit sales in the debtor collection denominator.","Use all income indiscriminately as debtor collection sales."],"a":0,"why":"The receivable balance arises from credit sales, not cash sales or unrelated income."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The receivable balance arises from credit sales, not cash sales or unrelated income.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Use net credit sales in the debtor collection denominator."},{"q":"In the model “Efficiency measures”, what belongs to “Collection”?","o":["Average debtors / net credit sales × 365 days","Total cost of sales / average inventory times","Credit terms, stock needs and trends"],"a":0,"why":"Collection: Average debtors / net credit sales × 365 days. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Efficiency measures”, what belongs to “Inventory”?","o":["Average debtors / net credit sales × 365 days","Total cost of sales / average inventory times","Credit terms, stock needs and trends"],"a":1,"why":"Inventory: Total cost of sales / average inventory times. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Efficiency measures","rows":[["Collection","Average debtors / net credit sales × 365 days"],["Inventory","Total cost of sales / average inventory times"],["Interpretation","Credit terms, stock needs and trends"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does debtor collection use all revenue including cash sales?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can use average balances.</li><li>I can distinguish days from times.</li><li>I can apply net sales and total cost-of-sales definitions.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use average balances","html":"<p>Debtor collection days = average debtors ÷ net <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sales × 365. Average uses opening and closing balances where supplied. Net <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sales subtract relevant returns/allowances and discount allowed. Use <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> sales, not total sales when cash sales are separately identified.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish days from times","html":"<p><strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Inventory</strong> turnover = total cost of sales ÷ average <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">inventory</strong>, expressed in times. The specification includes import duties and freight inwards and subtracts discount received from the cost-of-sales account where these are given separately. A larger turnover is not automatically better if shortages occur.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Apply net sales and total cost-of-sales definitions","html":"<p>Slower collection ties up cash and increases default risk. Faster stock turnover may indicate efficient stock management or under-stocking. Link conclusions to <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">credit</strong> terms, industry, stock type and service levels rather than inventing universal targets.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain debtor collection and inventory turnover using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-profitability-ratios","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Year 12 profit ratio and return on assets","subtitle":"Use after-tax profit and total revenue · Use pre-tax profit plus interest for asset return · Distinguish Year 11 conventions","scope":"Use after-tax profit and total revenue · Use pre-tax profit plus interest for asset return · Distinguish Year 11 conventions","outcomes":["Use after-tax profit and total revenue","Use pre-tax profit plus interest for asset return","Distinguish Year 11 conventions"],"paragraphs":["Year 12 profit ratio = profit after income tax ÷ total revenue × 100, following the specification. It measures after-tax profit per revenue dollar. Do not silently substitute net sales or profit before tax if the task provides a different total-revenue measure.","Year 12 return on assets = (profit before tax + interest expense) ÷ average total assets × 100. Adding interest separates the operating return from financing choice. This is different from the Year 11 profit/average-assets convention taught earlier.","Show average assets and the numerator explicitly. Compare trends and similar entities cautiously; revaluation, asset age, business mix and accounting estimates affect results. A ratio is evidence for analysis, not a standalone investment recommendation."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Profit before tax $40,000, interest $10,000, tax $12,000, total revenue $200,000; assets open $180,000 close $220,000. Calculate.","steps":["Profit after tax = $28,000.","Profit ratio = 28,000 ÷ 200,000 × 100 = 14.00%.","Average assets = $200,000.","Return on assets = (40,000 + 10,000) ÷ 200,000 × 100 = 25.00%."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-profitability-ratios-q1"},{"prompt":"After-tax profit $18,000, revenue $150,000, pre-tax profit $24,000, interest $6,000 and average assets $120,000. Calculate.","steps":["Profit ratio = 18,000 ÷ 150,000 × 100 = 12.00%.","Asset-return numerator = 24,000 + 6,000 = $30,000.","Return on assets = 30,000 ÷ 120,000 × 100 = 25.00%.","Use Year 12 conventions rather than the Year 11 simplified numerator."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-profitability-ratios-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Use after-tax profit alone as the Year 12 asset-return numerator.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Use profit before tax plus interest expense over average assets.","The specification adjusts for tax and financing effects in this numerator.","Example evidence: Profit after tax = $28,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-profitability-ratios-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does Year 12 return on assets use only after-tax profit?","o":["Use profit before tax plus interest expense over average assets.","Use after-tax profit alone as the Year 12 asset-return numerator."],"a":0,"why":"The specification adjusts for tax and financing effects in this numerator."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["The specification adjusts for tax and financing effects in this numerator.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Use profit before tax plus interest expense over average assets."},{"q":"In the model “Year 12 numerator map”, what belongs to “Profit ratio”?","o":["After-tax profit","Pre-tax profit + interest","(Opening + closing) / 2"],"a":0,"why":"Profit ratio: After-tax profit. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Year 12 numerator map”, what belongs to “Return on assets”?","o":["After-tax profit","Pre-tax profit + interest","(Opening + closing) / 2"],"a":1,"why":"Return on assets: Pre-tax profit + interest. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Year 12 numerator map","rows":[["Profit ratio","After-tax profit"],["Return on assets","Pre-tax profit + interest"],["Average assets","(Opening + closing) / 2"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does Year 12 return on assets use only after-tax profit?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can use after-tax profit and total revenue.</li><li>I can use pre-tax profit plus interest for asset return.</li><li>I can distinguish Year 11 conventions.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use after-tax profit and total revenue","html":"<p>Year 12 <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> ratio = <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> after income tax ÷ total revenue × 100, following the specification. It measures after-tax <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> per revenue dollar. Do not silently substitute net sales or <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> before tax if the task provides a different total-revenue measure.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use pre-tax profit plus interest for asset return","html":"<p>Year 12 return on <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> = (<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> before tax + interest expense) ÷ average total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> × 100. Adding interest separates the operating return from financing choice. This is different from the Year 11 <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong>/average-<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> convention taught earlier.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish Year 11 conventions","html":"<p>Show average <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">assets</strong> and the numerator explicitly. Compare trends and similar entities cautiously; <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong>, asset age, business mix and accounting estimates affect results. A ratio is evidence for analysis, not a standalone investment recommendation.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain year 12 profit ratio and return on assets using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-leverage-interest","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Debt to equity and times interest earned","subtitle":"Calculate leverage and interest cover · Use closing equity · Evaluate risk with earnings stability","scope":"Calculate leverage and interest cover · Use closing equity · Evaluate risk with earnings stability","outcomes":["Calculate leverage and interest cover","Use closing equity","Evaluate risk with earnings stability"],"paragraphs":["Debt to equity = total liabilities ÷ closing equity, commonly reported as a percentage. It compares creditor funding with owners’ funding. Higher leverage can amplify risk, but security, maturity and reliable cash/earnings also matter.","Times interest earned = (profit before tax + interest expense) ÷ interest expense. This shows the earnings buffer above interest, expressed in times. It does not include loan principal repayments and therefore does not replace a cash debt-service forecast.","If interest expense is zero, the ratio is not calculated by dividing by zero; explain the absence of an interest charge. A revaluation can improve debt/equity by increasing equity without supplying cash. Link the interpretation to the actual financing and cash situation."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Liabilities $150,000, closing equity $100,000; pre-tax profit $30,000, interest $10,000. Calculate and interpret.","steps":["Debt to equity = 150,000 ÷ 100,000 × 100 = 150.00%.","Interest cover = (30,000 + 10,000) ÷ 10,000 = 4.00 times.","Liabilities exceed equity funding.","Cover excludes principal and cash timing, which need separate review."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-leverage-interest-q1"},{"prompt":"Liabilities $80,000, equity $160,000, pre-tax profit $18,000 and interest $6,000. Calculate.","steps":["Debt to equity = 50.00%.","Interest-cover numerator = $24,000.","Times interest earned = 4.00.","Compare debt maturities and earnings stability before concluding risk is low."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-leverage-interest-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Four-times interest cover guarantees every principal payment can be met.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["It measures earnings relative to interest, not all cash debt-service obligations.","Principal repayment and cash availability require additional analysis.","Example evidence: Debt to equity = 150,000 ÷ 100,000 × 100 = 150.00%."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-leverage-interest-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does interest cover measure ability to pay every principal repayment?","o":["It measures earnings relative to interest, not all cash debt-service obligations.","Four-times interest cover guarantees every principal payment can be met."],"a":0,"why":"Principal repayment and cash availability require additional analysis."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Principal repayment and cash availability require additional analysis.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"It measures earnings relative to interest, not all cash debt-service obligations."},{"q":"In the model “Debt risk measures”, what belongs to “Debt/equity”?","o":["Total liabilities / closing equity","Pre-tax profit plus interest / interest","Maturity, security, cash and stability"],"a":0,"why":"Debt/equity: Total liabilities / closing equity. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Debt risk measures”, what belongs to “Interest cover”?","o":["Total liabilities / closing equity","Pre-tax profit plus interest / interest","Maturity, security, cash and stability"],"a":1,"why":"Interest cover: Pre-tax profit plus interest / interest. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Debt risk measures","rows":[["Debt/equity","Total liabilities / closing equity"],["Interest cover","Pre-tax profit plus interest / interest"],["Further evidence","Maturity, security, cash and stability"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does interest cover measure ability to pay every principal repayment?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate leverage and interest cover.</li><li>I can use closing equity.</li><li>I can evaluate risk with earnings stability.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate leverage and interest cover","html":"<p>Debt to <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> = total <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">liabilities</strong> ÷ closing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong>, commonly reported as a percentage. It compares creditor funding with owners’ funding. Higher leverage can amplify risk, but security, maturity and reliable cash/earnings also matter.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use closing equity","html":"<p>Times interest earned = (<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> before tax + interest expense) ÷ interest expense. This shows the earnings buffer above interest, expressed in times. It does not include loan principal repayments and therefore does not replace a cash debt-service forecast.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate risk with earnings stability","html":"<p>If interest expense is zero, the ratio is not calculated by dividing by zero; explain the absence of an interest charge. A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> can improve debt/<strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> by increasing <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">equity</strong> without supplying cash. Link the interpretation to the actual financing and cash situation.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain debt to equity and times interest earned using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-eps","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Earnings per share and weighted ordinary shares","subtitle":"Calculate a time-weighted share count · Use profit after tax · Explain per-share comparison limits","scope":"Calculate a time-weighted share count · Use profit after tax · Explain per-share comparison limits","outcomes":["Calculate a time-weighted share count","Use profit after tax","Explain per-share comparison limits"],"paragraphs":["Earnings per share = profit after tax ÷ weighted-average ordinary shares issued for this course’s ordinary-share scope. A share count changed during the year should be weighted by the fraction of the year it was outstanding, unless the task supplies the weighted amount.","A cash issue partway through the year is not outstanding for the whole year. Bonus issues require appropriate retrospective denominator treatment under applicable EPS requirements; use the supplied adjusted count when provided and do not apply an ordinary cash-issue weighting blindly.","EPS is earnings per share, not a dividend promise. Comparisons can be affected by changes in capital structure or share counts. Use consistent currency units for EPS and later price/earnings calculations."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Profit after tax $30,000; 20,000 shares exist for six months, then a cash issue makes 40,000 for six months. Calculate weighted shares and EPS.","steps":["First half weighted shares = 20,000×6/12 = 10,000.","Second half = 40,000×6/12 = 20,000.","Weighted average = 30,000 shares.","EPS = 30,000 ÷ 30,000 = $1.00."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-eps-q1"},{"prompt":"Profit after tax $24,000 and supplied weighted-average ordinary shares 60,000. Calculate EPS and explain dividend distinction.","steps":["EPS = 24,000 ÷ 60,000 = $0.40.","Express as 40 cents per share consistently.","EPS measures earnings allocated per share, not cash paid.","The company may retain some or all profit rather than distribute it."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-eps-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “EPS is always the cash dividend paid per share.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["EPS measures earnings; dividends depend on the distribution decision.","Retained profit can make earnings and dividends differ.","Example evidence: First half weighted shares = 20,000×6/12 = 10,000."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-eps-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Is EPS necessarily the dividend received per share?","o":["EPS measures earnings; dividends depend on the distribution decision.","EPS is always the cash dividend paid per share."],"a":0,"why":"Retained profit can make earnings and dividends differ."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Retained profit can make earnings and dividends differ.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"EPS measures earnings; dividends depend on the distribution decision."},{"q":"In the model “Weighted-share timeline”, what belongs to “First six months”?","o":["20,000 × 0.5","40,000 × 0.5","30,000","$30,000 → EPS $1.00"],"a":0,"why":"First six months: 20,000 × 0.5. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Weighted-share timeline”, what belongs to “Second six months”?","o":["20,000 × 0.5","40,000 × 0.5","30,000","$30,000 → EPS $1.00"],"a":1,"why":"Second six months: 40,000 × 0.5. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Weighted-share timeline","rows":[["First six months","20,000 × 0.5"],["Second six months","40,000 × 0.5"],["Weighted denominator","30,000"],["After-tax profit","$30,000 → EPS $1.00"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Is EPS necessarily the dividend received per share?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate a time-weighted share count.</li><li>I can use profit after tax.</li><li>I can explain per-share comparison limits.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate a time-weighted share count","html":"<p>Earnings per share = <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> after tax ÷ weighted-average ordinary shares issued for this course’s ordinary-share scope. A share count changed during the year should be weighted by the fraction of the year it was outstanding, unless the task supplies the weighted amount.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use profit after tax","html":"<p>A cash issue partway through the year is not outstanding for the whole year. Bonus issues require appropriate retrospective denominator treatment under applicable EPS requirements; use the supplied adjusted count when provided and do not apply an ordinary cash-issue weighting blindly.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain per-share comparison limits","html":"<p>EPS is earnings per share, not a dividend promise. Comparisons can be affected by changes in capital structure or share counts. Use consistent currency units for EPS and later price/earnings calculations.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain earnings per share and weighted ordinary shares using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-market-ratios","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Price/earnings and dividend yield","subtitle":"Calculate market ratios consistently · Use annual dividend per share · Interpret expectations and price effects","scope":"Calculate market ratios consistently · Use annual dividend per share · Interpret expectations and price effects","outcomes":["Calculate market ratios consistently","Use annual dividend per share","Interpret expectations and price effects"],"paragraphs":["Price/earnings = market price per ordinary share ÷ earnings per share, expressed in times. Keep both amounts in dollars or both in cents. A high P/E can reflect growth expectations or a high market price relative to current earnings; it is not automatic evidence of a bargain.","Dividend yield = annual dividend per share ÷ market price per share × 100. Sum applicable annual dividends rather than using only one interim payment if the full-year measure is required. Yield excludes share-price gain/loss.","A high yield may result from a falling share price or an unsustainable payout. Analyse business performance, payout policy and risk using evidence. If EPS is zero or negative, a conventional positive P/E interpretation is not meaningful."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Market price $6, EPS $0.50 and annual dividend $0.24 per share. Calculate P/E and yield.","steps":["P/E = 6 ÷ 0.50 = 12.00 times.","Dividend yield = 0.24 ÷ 6 × 100 = 4.00%.","Use matching currency units.","Yield excludes market-price changes."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-market-ratios-q1"},{"prompt":"Market price $4, EPS 20 cents; interim dividend 5 cents and final dividend 7 cents. Find ratios.","steps":["EPS = $0.20; P/E = 4 ÷ .20 = 20.00 times.","Annual dividend = $0.12.","Yield = .12 ÷ 4 × 100 = 3.00%.","A high P/E needs interpretation of expectations and risk."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-market-ratios-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “Dividend yield automatically includes share-price gains and losses.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Dividend yield measures annual dividends relative to current price.","Total investment return can include price changes but the yield formula does not.","Example evidence: P/E = 6 ÷ 0.50 = 12.00 times."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-market-ratios-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does dividend yield include capital gains?","o":["Dividend yield measures annual dividends relative to current price.","Dividend yield automatically includes share-price gains and losses."],"a":0,"why":"Total investment return can include price changes but the yield formula does not."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Total investment return can include price changes but the yield formula does not.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Dividend yield measures annual dividends relative to current price."},{"q":"In the model “Market ratio units”, what belongs to “Price”?","o":["$6 per share","$0.50 → P/E 12.00 times","$0.24 → yield 4.00%"],"a":0,"why":"Price: $6 per share. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Market ratio units”, what belongs to “EPS”?","o":["$6 per share","$0.50 → P/E 12.00 times","$0.24 → yield 4.00%"],"a":1,"why":"EPS: $0.50 → P/E 12.00 times. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Market ratio units","rows":[["Price","$6 per share"],["EPS","$0.50 → P/E 12.00 times"],["Annual dividend","$0.24 → yield 4.00%"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does dividend yield include capital gains?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can calculate market ratios consistently.</li><li>I can use annual dividend per share.</li><li>I can interpret expectations and price effects.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Calculate market ratios consistently","html":"<p>Price/earnings = market price per ordinary share ÷ earnings per share, expressed in times. Keep both amounts in dollars or both in cents. A high P/E can reflect growth expectations or a high market price relative to current earnings; it is not automatic evidence of a bargain.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Use annual dividend per share","html":"<p>Dividend yield = annual dividend per share ÷ market price per share × 100. Sum applicable annual dividends rather than using only one interim payment if the full-year measure is required. Yield excludes share-price gain/loss.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Interpret expectations and price effects","html":"<p>A high yield may result from a falling share price or an unsustainable payout. Analyse business performance, payout policy and risk using evidence. If EPS is zero or negative, a conventional positive P/E interpretation is not meaningful.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain price/earnings and dividend yield using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-annual-analysis","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Annual report analysis and limitations","subtitle":"Integrate ratios, cash flows and notes · Explain historical cost and comparability limits · Write a supported evaluation","scope":"Integrate ratios, cash flows and notes · Explain historical cost and comparability limits · Write a supported evaluation","outcomes":["Integrate ratios, cash flows and notes","Explain historical cost and comparability limits","Write a supported evaluation"],"paragraphs":["Assess position and performance using statements, notes, ratios and cash movements together. A rise in profit may coincide with receivable growth and weak operating cash. A debt reduction may come from a share issue rather than operating success; identify the funding source.","Historical-cost asset amounts, different asset ages or estimates, business mix and disclosure limitations affect comparability. A revaluation can change ratios without changing cash or operations. No single measure captures reputation, employee skills or every environmental risk.","A supported evaluation states numerical evidence, a case-linked explanation, a limitation and a conditional recommendation. Distinguish a fictional practice entity from a researched actual company. For a real-company extension, use its current published report and cite the exact page and year rather than inventing facts."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Profit grows 20%, but debtor days rise from 30 to 60 and operating cash falls. Evaluate the apparent improvement.","steps":["The profit trend suggests stronger accrual performance.","Slower collection ties up funds and increases collection risk.","Falling operating cash weakens evidence that profits are converting to cash.","Review receivable quality/terms and notes before endorsing the performance."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-annual-analysis-q1"},{"prompt":"A land revaluation improves debt/equity from 120% to 90%, while liabilities and cash are unchanged. Interpret.","steps":["Higher equity from revaluation reduces the ratio mechanically.","The company has not repaid debt or received revaluation cash.","Liquidity and repayment capacity may be unchanged.","Use cash forecasts and financing terms alongside the leverage ratio."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-annual-analysis-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “One favourable ratio proves the company is a good investment.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Financial ratios have measurement and disclosure limitations.","Multiple financial and non-financial sources are needed for a supported evaluation.","Example evidence: The profit trend suggests stronger accrual performance."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-annual-analysis-q3"},{"id":"acf-annual-analysis-q4","prompt":"Wesfarmers FY2025 results, page 4: revenue ($m) 45,700 in 2025 and 44,189 in 2024; net profit after tax ($m) 2,926 and 2,557. Calculate the two profit ratios and explain why the trend needs further investigation.","steps":["2025 profit ratio = 2,926 / 45,700 × 100 = 6.40%.","2024 profit ratio = 2,557 / 44,189 × 100 = 5.79%.","Profit per revenue dollar increased by approximately 0.61 percentage points.","Inspect the report’s significant-item explanation and operating cash before interpreting the increase as recurring operating improvement."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12}],"bank":[{"q":"Can ratios fully capture company performance without notes or context?","o":["Financial ratios have measurement and disclosure limitations.","One favourable ratio proves the company is a good investment."],"a":0,"why":"Multiple financial and non-financial sources are needed for a supported evaluation."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Multiple financial and non-financial sources are needed for a supported evaluation.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Financial ratios have measurement and disclosure limitations."},{"q":"In the model “Annual-report evidence”, what belongs to “Statements”?","o":["Performance, position and cash","Policies, movements and uncertainty","Comparable measures with conventions","Cost basis, estimates, mix and missing information"],"a":0,"why":"Statements: Performance, position and cash. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Annual-report evidence”, what belongs to “Notes”?","o":["Performance, position and cash","Policies, movements and uncertainty","Comparable measures with conventions","Cost basis, estimates, mix and missing information"],"a":1,"why":"Notes: Policies, movements and uncertainty. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Annual-report evidence","rows":[["Statements","Performance, position and cash"],["Notes","Policies, movements and uncertainty"],["Ratios","Comparable measures with conventions"],["Limitations","Cost basis, estimates, mix and missing information"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Can ratios fully capture company performance without notes or context?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can integrate ratios, cash flows and notes.</li><li>I can explain historical cost and comparability limits.</li><li>I can write a supported evaluation.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Integrate ratios, cash flows and notes","html":"<p>Assess position and performance using statements, notes, ratios and cash movements together. A rise in <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">profit</strong> may coincide with receivable growth and weak operating cash. A debt reduction may come from a share issue rather than operating success; identify the funding source.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain historical cost and comparability limits","html":"<p>Historical-cost asset amounts, different asset ages or estimates, business mix and disclosure limitations affect comparability. A <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">revaluation</strong> can change ratios without changing cash or operations. No single measure captures reputation, employee skills or every environmental risk.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Write a supported evaluation","html":"<p>A supported evaluation states numerical evidence, a case-linked explanation, a limitation and a conditional recommendation. Distinguish a fictional practice entity from a researched actual company. For a real-company extension, use its current published report and cite the exact page and year rather than inventing facts.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Australian company investigation · Wesfarmers FY2025","html":"<h3>Read an Australian reporting entity</h3><p>This dated case uses <a href=\"https://www.wesfarmers.com.au/docs/default-source/2022-full-year-results/2025-full-year-results.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wesfarmers FY2025 results, published 28 August 2025, page 4</a>. Follow its link to the accompanying annual and sustainability reports. It is a historical teaching case, not a current investment recommendation.</p><p>Check the report date, units, definitions and notes, then attempt the next screen. Explain conclusions using evidence and limitations.</p>"},{"kind":"question","title":"Real-report interpretation · exam practice","q":3},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain annual report analysis and limitations using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-external-audit","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"External audit and reporting confidence","subtitle":"Explain reasonable assurance · Distinguish management and auditor responsibilities · Evaluate an audit opinion’s limits","scope":"Explain reasonable assurance · Distinguish management and auditor responsibilities · Evaluate an audit opinion’s limits","outcomes":["Explain reasonable assurance","Distinguish management and auditor responsibilities","Evaluate an audit opinion’s limits"],"paragraphs":["External audit provides independent examination and reasonable assurance about whether financial statements are free from material misstatement and reported under the applicable framework. Management prepares the statements and controls; the auditor evaluates evidence and reports an opinion.","Reasonable assurance is high but not absolute. Sampling, judgement, collusion and uncertainty limit what can be established. An unmodified opinion does not guarantee future solvency, investment returns or that every immaterial error is absent.","Independence, competence and evidence support confidence for external users. Explain the role in a case: inspect records, assess risks and test material information. Distinguish external reporting assurance from internal audit’s review of business procedures and controls."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A shareholder says an unmodified audit opinion guarantees the company will remain profitable. Correct the statement.","steps":["The opinion concerns reported financial statements under the applicable framework.","Audit provides reasonable rather than absolute assurance about material misstatement.","It does not guarantee future profitability or investment returns.","Users still analyse risks, cash flows and business prospects."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-external-audit-q1"},{"prompt":"Management asks the external auditor to accept unsupported revenue because the trial balance agrees. Evaluate.","steps":["Equal debit and credit totals do not prove revenue occurred.","Auditor evidence must support material reported amounts.","Management is responsible for preparing reliable statements.","An unsupported material amount requires investigation and appropriate reporting consequences."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-external-audit-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “The auditor alone becomes responsible for preparing and owning all company records.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Management retains preparation responsibility; the auditor provides an independent opinion.","Independent assurance and management preparation are distinct responsibilities.","Example evidence: The opinion concerns reported financial statements under the applicable framework."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-external-audit-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does external audit remove management responsibility for financial statements?","o":["Management retains preparation responsibility; the auditor provides an independent opinion.","The auditor alone becomes responsible for preparing and owning all company records."],"a":0,"why":"Independent assurance and management preparation are distinct responsibilities."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Independent assurance and management preparation are distinct responsibilities.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Management retains preparation responsibility; the auditor provides an independent opinion."},{"q":"In the model “Audit responsibilities”, what belongs to “Management”?","o":["Prepare statements and maintain controls","Gather evidence and provide independent opinion","Interpret reports and remaining risks"],"a":0,"why":"Management: Prepare statements and maintain controls. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Audit responsibilities”, what belongs to “External auditor”?","o":["Prepare statements and maintain controls","Gather evidence and provide independent opinion","Interpret reports and remaining risks"],"a":1,"why":"External auditor: Gather evidence and provide independent opinion. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Audit responsibilities","rows":[["Management","Prepare statements and maintain controls"],["External auditor","Gather evidence and provide independent opinion"],["Users","Interpret reports and remaining risks"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does external audit remove management responsibility for financial statements?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can explain reasonable assurance.</li><li>I can distinguish management and auditor responsibilities.</li><li>I can evaluate an audit opinion’s limits.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain reasonable assurance","html":"<p>External audit provides independent examination and reasonable assurance about whether financial statements are free from material misstatement and reported under the applicable framework. Management prepares the statements and controls; the auditor evaluates evidence and reports an opinion.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish management and auditor responsibilities","html":"<p>Reasonable assurance is high but not absolute. Sampling, judgement, collusion and uncertainty limit what can be established. An unmodified opinion does not guarantee future solvency, investment returns or that every immaterial error is absent.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate an audit opinion’s limits","html":"<p>Independence, competence and evidence support confidence for external users. Explain the role in a case: inspect records, assess risks and test material information. Distinguish external reporting assurance from internal audit’s review of business procedures and controls.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain external audit and reporting confidence using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-s2-disclosure","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"AASB S2: governance, strategy, risk, metrics and targets","subtitle":"Explain four climate-disclosure areas · Connect risks to financial information · Distinguish disclosure from generic promotion","scope":"Explain four climate-disclosure areas · Connect risks to financial information · Distinguish disclosure from generic promotion","outcomes":["Explain four climate-disclosure areas","Connect risks to financial information","Distinguish disclosure from generic promotion"],"paragraphs":["AASB S2 climate-related disclosures address governance, strategy, risk management, and metrics and targets. Governance identifies oversight; strategy explains climate risks/opportunities and business effects; risk management explains identification and monitoring; metrics and targets show measurement and progress.","Connect the sections: a flood risk should have accountable oversight, a strategy response, a risk process and relevant measurable information. Scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions may be relevant metrics under applicable requirements and transition provisions. The course teaches the syllabus core, not a claim that every business has identical mandatory reporting obligations.","Describe a reporting boundary, method, baseline and uncertainty where relevant. A vague sustainability slogan cannot replace decision-useful disclosure. Use the current effective standard and official guidance for actual compliance, particularly where later amendments have future effective dates."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A company reports “we care about climate” but gives no responsibility, risk process or measured targets. Identify missing S2 areas.","steps":["Governance: identify responsible oversight and accountability.","Strategy: explain material risks/opportunities and business effects.","Risk management: describe identification, assessment and monitoring.","Metrics and targets: provide measured information, goals and progress."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-s2-disclosure-q1"},{"prompt":"A firm faces flood risk and plans resilient equipment. Give one connected disclosure for each core area.","steps":["Governance: identify board/management oversight of flood exposure.","Strategy: explain investment and expected financial effects.","Risk management: explain assessment and monitoring of site risk.","Metrics/targets: report relevant resilience milestones and measures with a stated basis."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-s2-disclosure-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “A general climate slogan replaces all S2 reporting information.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Decision-useful disclosures explain oversight, effects, processes and measured performance.","Users need connected evidence rather than unsupported promotion.","Example evidence: Governance: identify responsible oversight and accountability."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-s2-disclosure-q3"}],"bank":[{"q":"Does a climate slogan satisfy the four core disclosure areas?","o":["Decision-useful disclosures explain oversight, effects, processes and measured performance.","A general climate slogan replaces all S2 reporting information."],"a":0,"why":"Users need connected evidence rather than unsupported promotion."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Users need connected evidence rather than unsupported promotion.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Decision-useful disclosures explain oversight, effects, processes and measured performance."},{"q":"In the model “Four connected disclosure areas”, what belongs to “Governance”?","o":["Who oversees it?","How does it affect the business?","How is it identified and managed?","How is progress measured?"],"a":0,"why":"Governance: Who oversees it?. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Four connected disclosure areas”, what belongs to “Strategy”?","o":["Who oversees it?","How does it affect the business?","How is it identified and managed?","How is progress measured?"],"a":1,"why":"Strategy: How does it affect the business?. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Four connected disclosure areas","rows":[["Governance","Who oversees it?"],["Strategy","How does it affect the business?"],["Risk management","How is it identified and managed?"],["Metrics and targets","How is progress measured?"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does a climate slogan satisfy the four core disclosure areas?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can explain four climate-disclosure areas.</li><li>I can connect risks to financial information.</li><li>I can distinguish disclosure from generic promotion.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Explain four climate-disclosure areas","html":"<p>AASB S2 climate-related disclosures address governance, strategy, risk management, and metrics and targets. Governance identifies oversight; strategy explains climate risks/opportunities and business effects; risk management explains identification and monitoring; metrics and targets show measurement and progress.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Connect risks to financial information","html":"<p>Connect the sections: a flood risk should have accountable oversight, a strategy response, a risk process and relevant measurable information. <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Scope 1</strong>, 2 and 3 emissions may be relevant metrics under applicable requirements and transition provisions. The course teaches the syllabus core, not a claim that every business has identical mandatory reporting obligations.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish disclosure from generic promotion","html":"<p>Describe a reporting boundary, method, baseline and uncertainty where relevant. A vague sustainability slogan cannot replace decision-useful disclosure. Use the current effective standard and official guidance for actual compliance, particularly where later amendments have future effective dates.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain aasb s2: governance, strategy, risk, metrics and targets using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]},{"id":"acf-sustainability","year":"12","unit":"Unit 4 – Corporate reporting and regulation","title":"Interpreting sustainability claims, greenwashing and bluewashing","subtitle":"Evaluate boundaries and evidence · Distinguish environmental and social misrepresentation · Link sustainability and financial effects","scope":"Evaluate boundaries and evidence · Distinguish environmental and social misrepresentation · Link sustainability and financial effects","outcomes":["Evaluate boundaries and evidence","Distinguish environmental and social misrepresentation","Link sustainability and financial effects"],"paragraphs":["Greenwashing misrepresents environmental performance; bluewashing presents misleading social or ethical credentials. A selective claim may omit adverse operations, use vague terms or compare incompatible measures. Identify the exact misleading implication rather than labelling every imperfect initiative as fraud.","Check baseline, reporting boundary, measurement method and verification. A drop in emissions intensity can occur while total emissions rise as output grows. Analyse both absolute and intensity information, plus relevant Scope 3 exposure and limits.","For an Australian-reporting-entity extension, inspect a current published sustainability report with dates and page references. The worked case here is fictional. Connect claims to governance, costs, risks and targets; a positive story should be supported by complete, balanced evidence."],"questions":[{"prompt":"A fictional firm cuts emissions per unit from 10 to 8 kg while output rises from 1,000 to 2,000 units. It claims total emissions halved. Evaluate.","steps":["Initial total = 10×1,000 = 10,000 kg.","New total = 8×2,000 = 16,000 kg.","Intensity fell 20%, but total emissions rose 60%.","The halving claim is misleading environmental reporting/greenwashing."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":false,"rows":12,"id":"acf-sustainability-q1"},{"prompt":"A company advertises ethical labour but excludes subcontractors with documented unsafe conditions. Evaluate and recommend.","steps":["The claim may be bluewashing through selective social reporting.","The reporting boundary omits a relevant value-chain issue.","Disclose the boundary, evidence, adverse findings and corrective actions.","Use balanced targets and monitoring rather than unsupported ethical promotion."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12,"id":"acf-sustainability-q2"},{"prompt":"A student says: “A lower per-unit measure always proves total emissions fell.” Explain the mistake, state the correct principle and apply it to the lesson example.","steps":["Total emissions also depend on output volume and reporting boundary.","Growth can outweigh intensity improvements, so both measures must be analysed.","Example evidence: Initial total = 10×1,000 = 10,000 kg."],"allocations":[1,1,1],"marks":3,"practice":true,"rows":9,"id":"acf-sustainability-q3"},{"id":"acf-sustainability-q4","prompt":"Wesfarmers FY2025 results, page 4: combined Scope 1 and market-based Scope 2 emissions were 1,026.6 ktCO₂e in 2025 versus 1,132.4 in 2024. Calculate the percentage decrease and explain why this alone cannot establish the full value-chain footprint.","steps":["Reduction = 1,132.4 − 1,026.6 = 105.8 ktCO₂e.","Percentage decrease = 105.8 / 1,132.4 × 100 = 9.34%.","The stated combined measure excludes Scope 3 from this total.","Review reporting boundaries and methods before making a wider footprint claim."],"allocations":[1,1,1,1],"marks":4,"practice":true,"rows":12}],"bank":[{"q":"Does lower emissions intensity necessarily mean lower total emissions?","o":["Total emissions also depend on output volume and reporting boundary.","A lower per-unit measure always proves total emissions fell."],"a":0,"why":"Growth can outweigh intensity improvements, so both measures must be analysed."},{"q":"Explain the accounting consequence: which statement fits this lesson?","o":["Growth can outweigh intensity improvements, so both measures must be analysed.","The timing of cash always determines the timing of profit.","All debit entries reduce assets."],"a":0,"why":"Total emissions also depend on output volume and reporting boundary."},{"q":"In the model “Sustainability evidence test”, what belongs to “Baseline”?","o":["Compared with what period?","Which operations and value-chain sources?","Absolute total, intensity and method","Progress, adverse facts and limitations"],"a":0,"why":"Baseline: Compared with what period?. Trace this relationship through the worked example."},{"q":"In the model “Sustainability evidence test”, what belongs to “Boundary”?","o":["Compared with what period?","Which operations and value-chain sources?","Absolute total, intensity and method","Progress, adverse facts and limitations"],"a":1,"why":"Boundary: Which operations and value-chain sources?. Trace this relationship through the worked example."}],"model":{"title":"Sustainability evidence test","rows":[["Baseline","Compared with what period?"],["Boundary","Which operations and value-chain sources?"],["Measure","Absolute total, intensity and method"],["Balance","Progress, adverse facts and limitations"]]},"icon":"📊","screens":[{"kind":"reference","title":"Do Now · learning intentions","html":"<h3>Start with what you know</h3><p>Before reading, answer this retrieval question in your notebook: Does lower emissions intensity necessarily mean lower total emissions?</p><p>Allow 35–50 minutes. Work through the input, model, guided attempt and independent practice.</p><h3>Learning intentions and success criteria</h3><ul><li>I can evaluate boundaries and evidence.</li><li>I can distinguish environmental and social misrepresentation.</li><li>I can link sustainability and financial effects.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"learn","title":"Evaluate boundaries and evidence","html":"<p>Greenwashing misrepresents environmental performance; bluewashing presents misleading social or ethical credentials. A selective claim may omit adverse operations, use vague terms or compare incompatible measures. Identify the exact misleading implication rather than labelling every imperfect initiative as fraud.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"learn","title":"Distinguish environmental and social misrepresentation","html":"<p>Check baseline, reporting boundary, measurement method and verification. A drop in emissions intensity can occur while total emissions rise as output grows. Analyse both absolute and intensity information, plus relevant <strong class=\"sv-keyword\">Scope 3</strong> exposure and limits.</p>","model":true},{"kind":"learn","title":"Link sustainability and financial effects","html":"<p>For an Australian-reporting-entity extension, inspect a current published sustainability report with dates and page references. The worked case here is fictional. Connect claims to governance, costs, risks and targets; a positive story should be supported by complete, balanced evidence.</p>","model":false},{"kind":"check","title":"Check understanding · explain the choice","q":0},{"kind":"question","title":"Teacher model · attempt first","q":0},{"kind":"solution","title":"Teacher model · worked marking table","q":0},{"kind":"activity","title":"Guided practice · reconstruct the model"},{"kind":"question","title":"Independent exam practice","q":1},{"kind":"question","title":"Exam reasoning · correct a misconception","q":2},{"kind":"reference","title":"Australian sustainability investigation · Wesfarmers FY2025","html":"<h3>Read an Australian reporting entity</h3><p>This dated case uses <a href=\"https://www.wesfarmers.com.au/docs/default-source/2022-full-year-results/2025-full-year-results.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wesfarmers FY2025 results, published 28 August 2025, page 4</a>. Follow its link to the accompanying annual and sustainability reports. It is a historical teaching case, not a current investment recommendation.</p><p>Check the report date, units, definitions and notes, then attempt the next screen. Explain conclusions using evidence and limitations.</p>"},{"kind":"question","title":"Real-report interpretation · exam practice","q":3},{"kind":"reference","title":"Closure · retrieval and extension","html":"<h3>Exit ticket</h3><p>Without the notes, explain interpreting sustainability claims, greenwashing and bluewashing using the lesson example. Include the calculation or accounting entry and its effect on financial information.</p><h3>Extension</h3><p>Change one assumption in the practice task. Explain which entries, result or recommendation change, and which stay the same. Justify your answer with evidence rather than a definition alone.</p><p><a href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/humanities-and-social-sciences/accounting-and-finance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SCSA syllabus, specification booklet and official past papers</a> · <a href=\"ACCOUNTING-ATAR-LESSONS.md\">Full lesson coverage and teaching sequence</a></p>"}]}];
